{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost","id":1010387,"title":"Scion High Campaign Chronicle Repost","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-11-30T16:13:48-08:00","id":1010387,"title":"Scion High Campaign Chronicle Repost","content":"THE PLAN<br /> <br /> First I'll post the campaign premise quick sheet I handed out to players. It laid out the basic ideas of the campaign and of Scion. (All the players had played WW games before -- Vtm or Exalted -- but Scion was new to them.)<br /> <br /> Next I'll post the Character Creation and House Rules handouts. Since the characters were all teenagers, I thought it was appropriate to adjust character creation for people who had not developed the range and depth of skill of adults. Other house rules dealt with areas I thought Scion was weak or unclear. I also added a few new Knacks and Boons. If this doesn't interest you, go ahead and skip to the actual Chronicle of play sessions.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T16:20:50-08:00","id":1010391,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"left\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:48px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">SCION HIGH: STUDENT ORIENTATION</span></span></span></div></div> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> <br /> Part One</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">WHAT IS A SCION?</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">From time immemorial, Gods have mated with mortals. The children of such unions are Scions. Although Scions are mortal, they inherit a trickle of divine power. This enables Scions to perform amazing, superhuman or outright magical feats. With practice, a Scion gains power and gains a greater proportion of divinity. In time, a Scion can become entirely divine and leave the mortal World for the realms beyond.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">You are a child of the Gods, raised among mortals. Your divine parent \u2014 long absent \u2014 recently came back into your life and decided that you should know your heritage, develop your power and fulfill your destiny as a new generation of heroes to perform deeds worthy of legend.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">But first, you have to finish high school.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">WHY ARE YOU HERE?</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Long ago, the gods defeated their malevolent progenitors, the Titans, and bound them in the prison of Tartarus. The prison has crumbled, though, and the Titans are free. Their minions and spawn already commit badness in the mortal World. The Gods need Scions to do things in the World that they, for various reasons, cannot.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Oh. You mean, why are you here at <i>this school, </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">all together? Well, Scions need training at using their powers, but they also need practice at being people within the modern World. Your divine parents gathered their Scions in one place where it\u2019ll be easier to keep an eye on them. They also hope for new, cross-pantheon alliances between Scions. The old Gods have difficulty transcending the myths about them long enough to work together. Scions have no such trouble.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Thing is, Legend \u2014 the power underlying everything supernatural \u2014 attracts more of itself. Because you, a group of Scions, are here, other creatures of Legend come too: other Scions, minions of the Titans, creatures allied to neither side, you name it. Some of them came to the area long before you did, for Legend acts across time. If there\u2019s a dragon sleeping under the school gym, it\u2019s here because you\u2019re here. Or, you\u2019re here because it\u2019s here. It\u2019s confusing. Just expect all sorts of weird stuff to come out of the woodwork.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Your divine parents believe this isn\u2019t a bug, it\u2019s a feature. You can practice your powers and learn about the secret world of Legend while it tries to kill you, recruit you, destroy the World, or at least make you fail algebra.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">HIGH SCHOOL HEROES</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The ancient mythological epics describe heroes whose lives are an unending melodrama of passion and conflict. Nowadays, we call their emotional condition \u201cadolescence.\u201d Modern teens easily feel the wrath of Achilles, the love of Sigurd and Brunhilde, the friendship of Gilgamesh and Enkidu, or the betrayal of Arthur and Lancelot. Only, the grown-ups around them don\u2019t take them seriously. Adults know that the fate of the World doesn\u2019t really depend on who asks whom to the prom.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">What if those sensible grown-ups were wrong?</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion High </span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">takes the </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion </span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">game and filters it through modern high school. The style is that of TV shows such as </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Buffy the Vampire Slayer</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> or </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Smallville</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">: basically action-adventure, but with heaping helpings of teen angst and soap opera. Characters face titanspawn monsters and evil cultists, but they also face the challenges of sports, dating, afterschool jobs, parents and teachers. The big game may decide the fate of a soul; school cliques can represent supernatural factions; the mall is a goblin market of wonder and horror; a jealous classmate or a mean teacher can be a monstrous servant of the Titans.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Welcome back to school!</span></span></span><br /> <br /> (It looks like the forum doesn't accept very long documents, so I'll have to break posts into chunks. Part Two comes next.)<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T16:22:54-08:00","id":1010392,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"left\"><div align=\"left\"><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-size:48px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">SCION HIGH: STUDENT ORIENTATION</span></span></span></div></div> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Part Two</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The Scion Condition</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Once upon a time, Gods and the monstrous spawn of the Titans acted openly in the World. The Gods fought the Titans, and both sides fought amongst themselves. The Gods began as offspring of the Titans, but contact with humanity changed them. Titans have will and sentience, but they are not <i>people,</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> strictly speaking, and they have no use for people. Contact with humans taught the nascent Gods how to be people, and they liked it. The Gods wanted to continue being people, and their Titan progenitors objected. The Titans tried to destroy the Gods, and the Gods objected. Hence, the war between them.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gods are immensely more powerful than mortals, and Titans are more powerful than Gods. The power of Fate, however, constrains mortals, Gods and Titans alike \u2014 and Fate gives mortals a terrible power over Gods. When Gods use their supernatural powers in front of mortals, Fate may bind those mortals to them and the Gods to the mortals. The humans become part of the God\u2019s story in fixed roles, whether as friends or foes. All the mortals \u201cFatebound\u201d to a God try to push that God into playing certain roles in return. The Gods figured out that if they did not hide their power from mortals, they would lose their free will and become trapped in the stories that mortals tell about them. For at least 2,000 years, Gods acting in the World have disguised themselves as mortals and avoided using their full power, to avoid Fatebinding. At any given time, dozens of Gods live on Earth in mortal guises.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">One consequence is that Gods no longer appear to their mortal lovers as showers of gold or otherwise reveal their divinity. (Usually.) Sometimes they sire or bear children in their mortal guises. Before long, though, Gods must leave their mortal partners to resume their duties in the Overworld. Other times, Gods take the place of particular mortals for the express purpose of producing Scions and placing them in a particular family. The upshot is that by and large, Scions are raised by mortals and think they are mortal until someone tells them differently.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">When a divine parent reveals the truth to a Scion, it\u2019s called the Visitation. Gods usually tell their child directly, but they can do this through a messenger. For instance, the Norse Gods often delegate the Visitation to Odin\u2019s ravens, Hugin and Munin. Hey, Gods are busy.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Even before the Visitation, though, blood tells. Scions tend to show interests and aptitudes related to their divine parents. A Scion of Ares gets into fights; a Scion of Isis has a taste for esoteric knowledge. A Scion\u2019s name often hints at the divine parent\u2019s identity, if only because Gods themselves seem compelled to adopt revealing pseudonyms.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Six pantheons produce most of the extant Scions: the <i>Aesir</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> of Norse mythology; the Japanese </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Amatsukami</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">; the </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Atzl\u00e1nti </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">of Central America, most notably the Aztecs; the Greco-Roman </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dodekatheon</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">; the </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Loa</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> of Voodoo; and the </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Pesedjet </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">of ancient Egypt. Gods of other pantheons also produce Scions, though, and any of them might become students at Scion High.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">THE NITTY GRITTY</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Your Scion character has <i>Attributes</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> and </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Abilities</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> that represent his mortal skills and aptitudes. He also has </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Epic Attributes</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> that go beyond human limits. A Scion with Epic Strength can lift more than the greatest Olympic weightlifter; a Scion with Epic Manipulation can talk damn near anyone into damn near anything; and so on. </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Boons</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> represent explicitly magical powers such as flying or turning invisible. Boons are grouped in </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Purviews</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> such as Animal, Fertility, Justice or War.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">As part of the Visitation, a God typically gives the new Scion some presents, collectively referred to as <i>Birthrights</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">. A </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Creature </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">is a pet that can range from a kitty to Pegasus. </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Followers</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> can be summoned to fight on the Scion\u2019s behalf; for instance, einherjar called from Valhalla. A </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Guide </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">is an advisor that can range from a knowledgeable mortal to a talking scarab to some sort of spirit. A </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Relic </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">is a magical object. A Relic might have enhancements that make it more useful than a mundane object, such as a sword that inflicts extra damage. Other Relics summon Creatures or Followers, which prevents awkward questions about your pet unicorn. Most importantly, Relics enable Scions to channel the power of their Boons: No Relic, no Boon.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scions call on the power of <i>Legend</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">. In stories, heroes can do superhuman deeds. As a Scion, you are part mortal and part story. Your overall </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Legend rating</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> limits the power of your Epic Attributes and Boons. With experience, however, characters can raise their Legend rating and so acquire Boons of greater power and higher ratings in their Epic Attributes. Many Boons or Knacks require spending </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Legend points</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">. A starting character does not have many Legend points, so they are the primary resource to manage. Like your divine parents, however, each time you exploit Legend you risk mortals becoming bound to you, and you to them. Your parents request that you try not to let mortals know about the genuine supernatural world around them.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T16:28:46-08:00","id":1010394,"title":"","content":"This part was formatted like a manuscript submission, with headers indicated with codes such as &lt;1&gt; or &lt;sb1&gt;. The forum seems to have turned some of them into real headers; in others, the code indicators remain. Huh, weird, but probably not important..<br /> <br /> I should note that not one player could make head or tail of the rules for buying Boons, especially the different rules for regular Boons and PSP Boons. I am not sure whether this is because I was lousy at explaining them or the rules were intrinsically confusing.<br /> <br /> 1&gt;CHARACTER CREATION<br /> <b><i>Scion High</i></b> characters are heroes \u2014 Scions whose Legend ranges from 2 to 4. All traits work as described in <b>Scion: Hero</b>. There are, however, a few changes to character creation:<br /> \u2022 Characters start with 25 dots for Abilities instead of 30. <b><i>Scion High</i></b> characters are incredibly skilled for teenagers, but they are nonetheless younger and less experienced than the standard game assumes.<br /> \u2022 Dots of Legend cost more bonus points: Legend is <i>the </i>measure of a character\u2019s power, so a player who wants a character to start with higher Legend must give up a lot of potential other traits. It\u2019s probably best for play if characters all start at the same Legend.<br /> \u2022 To compensate, starting characters can have Epic Attributes and Boons rated up to their Legend, instead of being limited to (Legend \u2013 1). This continues up to Legend 3. At Legend 4 and higher, characters revert to the (Legend \u2013 1) rule.<br /> For the convenience of players, the complete but altered rules for character creation are given here:<br /> <br /> &lt;sb1&gt;Character Creation Summary<br /> &lt;sb2&gt;<b>Step One: Character Concept</b><br /> \u2022 Choose character concept, pantheon, parental God and Nature.<br /> <br /> <b>Step Two: Attributes</b><br /> \u2022 Note that all attributes start with one dot before you add any more.<br /> \u2022 Decide which categories \u2014 Physical, Social and Mental \u2014 will be primary (8 dots), secondary 6 dots) or tertiary (4 dots).<br /> \u2022 Choose Physical Traits: Strength, Dexterity, Stamina. Divide dots among these three Traits.<br /> \u2022 Choose Social Traits: Charisma, Manipulation, Appearance.<br /> \u2022 Choose Mental Traits: Perception, Intelligence, Wits.<br /> \u2022 No Attribute can go above 5.<br /> <br /> <b>Step Three: Abilities</b><br /> \u2022 Note the 6 Favored Abilities inherited from the Scion\u2019s divine parent.<br /> \u2022 Assign 6 dots among those Favored Abilities, however you like.<br /> \u2022 Assign 19 more dots among whatever Abilities you want.<br /> \u2022 No Ability ratings can go above 3 without using bonus points. <b>Step Four: Advantages</b><br /> <br /> \u2022 Record Legend (2, can raise with bonus points) and Legend Points (Legend value, squared).<br /> \u2022 Choose 5 dots of Birthrights: Creature, Followers, Guide or Relic. None can be rated over 3 without spending bonus points.<br /> \u2022 Choose 10 dots of Epic Attributes and Boons.<br /> \u2014 No rating for an Epic Attribute or Boon can exceed the character\u2019s Legend.<br /> \u2014 Epic Attributes cannot exceed their base Attribute.<br /> \u2014 Each General Purpose Boon has a separate dot cost. For instance, buying Storm Augmentation (Sky \u2022\u2022\u2022) does NOT mean a character also gets Wind\u2019s Freedom (Sky \u2022\u2022) and Sky\u2019s Grace (Sky \u2022). They are separate purchases.<br /> \u2014 Special Purviews or Pantheon-Specific Purviews, however, are bought like Epic Attributes: Just pick the number of dots you want in them, and you gain all subsidiary Boons (or one spell for each dot of Magic).<br /> \u2022 Select one Knack for each dot in an Epic Attribute.<br /> \u2022 Record the pantheon\u2019s Virtues and assign 5 dots among them. Every Virtue starts with one free dot. No Virtue can exceed 4 without spending bonus points, or exceed 5 in any case.<br /> \u2022 Record Willpower (sum of two highest Virtues).<br /> <br /> <b>Step Five: Bonus Points</b><br /> \u2022 15 bonus points may be spent at any stage of character creation, for any Traits:<br /> <br /> <b>[BEGIN TABLE]</b> <b>Trait Bonus Point Cost Per Dot</b><br /> <br /> Attribute 4<br /> Ability 2 (1 for Favored Ability of divine parent)<br /> Birthright 1 (2 if the Birthright is being raised above 3)<br /> Virtue 3<br /> Willpower 2<br /> Legend 12<br /> Epic Attribute 5 (4 if Epic Attribute is associated with divine parent)<br /> Additional Knack 3<br /> &lt;sb3&gt;Additional Boons or Spells 5 (4 if source Purview associated with divine parent)<br /> <b>[END TABLE]</b><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1175,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","user":"DShomshak","date":". 11-30-2016, 07:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T16:36:57-08:00","id":1010396,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;1&gt;HOUSE RULES, Part One</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion High </span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">does not follow the published </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion </span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">rules exactly. Some rules are tweaked for better play with younger characters who will not advance as far or as quickly as in a normal </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion </span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">game. Others are altered because they are unclear or do not seem conducive to the style of play I want.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;2&gt;Character Creation</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion High</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> characters are Heroes \u2014 Scions whose Legend ranges from 2 to 4. All traits work as described in </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion: Hero</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">. There are, however, a few changes to character creation:</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 Characters start with 25 dots for Abilities instead of 30. <b>Scion High</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> characters are incredibly skilled for teenagers, but they are nonetheless younger and less experienced than the standard game assumes.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 Legend will stay low, and cannot be bought up with bonus points or experience points. Everyone starts at Legend 2. To preserve play balance, the whole Band or characters will raise their Legend at once, when the Storyteller judges their deeds have been sufficiently epic.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 To compensate, starting characters can have Epic Attributes and Boons rated up to their Legend, instead of being limited to (Legend \u2013 1). This continues up to Legend 3. At Legend 4 and higher, characters revert to the (Legend \u2013 1) rule.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;2&gt;Amended Combat</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The following adjustments are made to the combat rules, to prevent some tactics from being either overwhelming, or bloody useless.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;3&gt;Amended Grappling</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Clinch Attack Roll</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> is ([Strength or Dexterity] + Brawl). It has no Accuracy bonus, and a character cannot use Parry DV while attempting a clinch.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Special DV vs. Clinch:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Very strong creatures and characters can shrug off attempts to clinch them \u2014 they are too strong to hold. A character\u2019s </span></span><b><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Clinch DV</span></span></i></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> is a static value equal to (Strength + [higher of Brawl or Athletics]) \u00f7 2. Epic Strength bonus successes add to it. Like other DVs, this is reduced by making attacks, and possibly other factors (you\u2019re off balance, can\u2019t use your strength most effectively, etc.) </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">However, </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">unexpected attacks do </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">not </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">render it inapplicable. Spending Legend for a \u201cdefensive do-over\u201d offers its normal bonus.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Held Character</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> immediately has Dodge DV and Parry DV drop to 0, but is not completely Inactive. He can still communicate, use Social or Mental Knacks, Boons that do not require physical actions, and so on.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Grappler\u2019s Options, </b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">once he succeeds in a clinch attack:</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Release the clinched target, who immediately regains his DV;</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Just hold the clinched target, preventing him from taking physical actions;</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Squeeze the clinched target, delivering Strength-based damage;</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Throw the target straight down, inflicting Strength-basd damage and leaving the target prone;</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Throw the target (Strength) yards away, forcing the target\u2019s player to roll vs. Knockdown.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Held Character\u2019s Next Action </b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">takes place at its normal time, regardless of being grappled.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>A Resisted Roll </b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">to control the clinch can take place any time either character, grappler or grappled, has an action. Both players roll ([Strength or Dexterity] + Brawl).</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Starting such a contest is a 6-tick action, just like attempting a clinch.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 The other character\u2019s resistance is reflexive.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Whichever character rolls the most successes takes control of the grapple and can decide what to do with the other character: release, hold, crush or throw. <i>But, </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">choosing to hold, crush or throw means starting a new Clinch action\u2026 committing yourself for the next 6 ticks.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 Ties leave the current status of the grapple unchanged.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2014 A character who breaks free from a grapple regains his DVs, but suffers the usual \u20131 DV penalty for taking a combat action.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;3&gt;Piercing Relics</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Relic Weapons</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> that carry the \u201cPiercing\u201d quality cut </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">all </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">a target\u2019s relevant soak in half, not just the portion that comes from armor.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Any Armor</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> with the \u201cBulletproof\u201d tag cancels this effect, protecting all of the target\u2019s soak.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;2&gt;Social Knacks and Unnatural Mental Influence</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The <b>Scion </b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">rules are not entirely clear on how and when characters can resist the effects of Social Knacks and similar effects such as medusa paralysis. Many Knacks come with rules in the text about who they can affect. There is also the rule about spending Willpower to resist a Knack, but no explicit statement about whether such an action is immediate and reflexive. If the latter, then Social Knacks are useless against Scions and other supernaturals: All you\u2019ve done by using a Knack is force the Scion to spend a Willpower point. Characters are unlikely to run out of Willpower points before other circumstances, </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">e.g.</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> killing the social attacker, renders the Knack\u2019s use moot. </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion High </span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">therefore adopts the following house rules:</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Legend Classes: </b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Social Knacks do not affect targets whose Legend is not just of a higher egend, but of a higher </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">class </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">of Legend. Thus, Heroes cannot Demigods and Gods. The same goes for creatures of comparable Legend ratings, so a Legend 4 lesser immortal cannot use Social Knacks on a Legend 6 lesser immortal. On the other hand, entities of lower Legend classes can still resist Social Knacks used by creatures of higher Legend classes. (Mortals remain the great exception. Ordinary mortals cannot resist Social Knacks at all.)</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Defined Resistance:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> If a Knack\u2019s description says it only affects targets of lower Legend, supplies a contested role, or otherwise supplies a defined restriction on who it affects or how it may be resisted, these limitations still apply.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Mental Defense Value Exception:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> To save on dice rolling, any roll contested by a target\u2019s (Willpower + Integrity + Essence) roll must instead roll more successes than half of that total dice pool. As with Dodge and Parry DVs, it is assumed that the source dice pool always rolls an exactly average result. However, an MDV can be boosted through stunts, defensive do-over, etc., just as with normal DVs.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>One Action Minimum:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> In the absence of some specially defined immunity or resistance, a character must act as a social Knack directs for at least one action. Usually, this is a 5-tick miscellaneous action, but if the Knack compels the character to some other action, use that instead. (</span></span><b><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Examples:</span></span></i></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Using Overt Order to command one person to clinch another results in a normal 6-tick clinch attack. Using Dreadful Mien to make someone run away forces them to take a 3-tick Dash action.)</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Resistance Action:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Spending Willpower is usually reflexive, but throwing off the effect of Social Knacks and other forms of supernaturally-enhanced mental influence takes a conscious effort of will. This involves a Speed 5, \u20131 DV miscellaneous action. If the Knack\u2019s effect lasts only one action anyway (such as Overt Order), tough luck. You acted on it.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Multiple Actions\u2026:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> A character can do other things while taking a Resistance Action; he simply suffers a multiple action penalty (see </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion: Hero</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">, p. 179). Throwing off a Knack\u2019s effect is a diceless action, so any rolled action done at the same time suffers a \u20132 dice penalty.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>\u2026Or Not:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Many Social Knacks would be meaningless if characters could take other actions while under their effect. (For instance, Serpent\u2019s Gaze necessarily implies that the target can\u2019t attack, run away, call for help, etc.) Therefore, for that initial action of effect, characters cannot engage in any other, simultaneous actions, unless they are compelled to attempt multiple actions.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Full Of Yourself:</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> This Charisma Knack offers advantages in resisting other social Knacks, but does not render them completely ineffective. See the Knack\u2019s description.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</span></span></span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T16:38:42-08:00","id":1010398,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;1&gt;HOUSE RULES, Part Two</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;2&gt;Modified Knacks</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;3&gt;Knack Selection</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Some Knacks are reserved for Demigod-and-beyond characters, because they just don\u2019t feel Hero-level to me. This includes all Knacks with prerequisites.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;3&gt;Altered Knacks:</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Appearance:</b></span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">My Eyes Are Up Here:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> This Knack now has no cost and is active whenever the Scion wants. While he\u2019s using this Knack, though, he cannot use any other Epic Appearance Knacks. Nor can his player reroll Social rolls until the character reveals his Epic Appearance to the world.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Dexterity:</b></span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Untouchable Opponent:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Duration of effect reduced from one scene to one action.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Intelligence:</b></span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Blockade of Reason:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Rewritten to function identically to Full Of Yourself (see New Knacks).</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">\u2022 <b>Intelligence:</b></span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Fight With Your Head:</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Duration reduced from one scene to one action.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;2&gt;Fatebinding</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The power of Fate to force mortals to enact mythic roles in relation to Scions \u2014 and to pressure Scions into acting the ways those mortals expect \u2014 is one of the <b>Scion</b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> game\u2019s most distinctive and thematic elements. Unfortunately, Fatebinding hardly ever occurs until characters become Demigods, or unless they cast spells. To bring Fatebinding into </span></span><b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Scion High</span></span></b><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">, therefore, Fatebinding rolls become </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">cumulative.</span></span></i></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">As characters expend Legend, have each player keep a running tally of the Fatebinding successes rolled for their character. When the tally exceeds five successes, note that one Fatebinding has occurred. Then the player starts the tally over again. (For simplicity, keep the Fatebinding difficulty at 5, whether or not characters spend Legend to alter the outcome of events, or whether it is to power Boons or Knacks. Different thresholds for different exercises of Legend is a needless complication.)</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">At the end of the scene, work out the strength of a character\u2019s Fatebinding based on how many times the character caused Fatebinding to occur, as in the standard rules. Then select a target for the Fatebinding. As <b>Scion: Hero </b></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">says, this can be a person, but also an object, place or other target. For a novel option\u2026</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">&lt;3&gt;Math Is My Kryptonite</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">While teachers are natural targets for Fatebinding, Storytellers might consider assigning Fatebound roles to particular subjects in school. Many Fated roles obviously do not apply very well to abstractions such as math, English or P. E., but negative roles such as Catastrophe, Jinx, Nemesis or Traitor should be easy to manage \u2014 the pop quiz on the subject you didn\u2019t study or the weird accidents that keep happening in Chem class.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-11-30T16:40:27-08:00","id":1010399,"title":"","content":"&lt;1&gt;NEW KNACKS<br /> I don\u2019t invent many new Knacks, but these came up in the course of <b>Scion High</b>.<br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;Strength: We Shall Not Be Moved<br /> <b>Cost: </b>1 Legend vs. surprise attacks; Reflexive<br /> <br /> Tremendously strong impacts can knock children of the Gods ass over teakettle\u2026 unless they know this Knack. The Scion reflexively uses her Epic Strength to brace against attacks. Her player subtracts the character\u2019s Epic Strength rating from the number of yards of knockback the character suffers: If this brings the number to 0, the character suffers neither knockback nor knockdown. The Knack costs one Legend if the attack took the character by surprise. If she saw the attack coming, We Shall Not Be Moved acts automatically, at no cost.<br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;Dexterity: Perfect Punch, Throwing Master<br /> These Dexterity Knacks function identically to Trick Shooter, but they involve (respectively) Brawl and Thrown. There is no analog for Melee, the Ability whose attacks gain the greatest possible bonuses from weapon traits and Epic Attributes.<br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;Charisma: Full of Yourself<br /> <b>Cost:</b> None; Instant; Reflexive or Miscellaneous Action<br /> <br /> Your boundless self-confidence (or obnoxious egotism) helps you resistant other social Knacks. When affected by social Knacks or other forms of unnatural mental influence, you spend Willpower to resist as a reflexive action \u2014 but after the Knack has its normal guaranteed one action of effect upon you. (After which, resistance may be too late.)<br /> <br /> Alternatively, you may spend Willpower immediately, to resist taking any action that is the result of unnatural mental influence \u2014 but this means you take your (Speed 5, -1 DV) resistance action instead of whatever action was compelled. Just as you cannot take other actions in the initial moments of social compulsion, in this case you cannot take other actions besides the struggle to assert your will against that compulsion.<br /> <br /> You still lose 5 ticks of free action either way. But, at least you don\u2019t do anything actively harmful to yourself or your allies.<br /> <br /> <b><i>Example:</i></b><i> Suttung Vetterson uses Overt Order to command Gus Sobeck to grab Holly Frost and rip her apart. Overt Order compels one action of obedience, and Gus\u2019 player decides he doesn\u2019t want Gus to obey the evil giant\u2019s command. So, Gus spends a Willpower point and says, through gritted teeth, \u201cScrew \u2014 You!\u201d His next attack on Suttung is delayed by 5 ticks, but at least he did not spend those ticks attacking a teammate.</i><br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;Manipulation: Forgettable<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Legend, sometimes; Reflexive<br /> <br /> Instead of making an impression on people, you manipulate people into ignoring you and forgetting what you said and did. If you talk to people, they remember conversing with\u2026 umm\u2026 somebody, about\u2026 it couldn\u2019t have been important. Even in a fight, your opponents remember only that they fought \u201csome guy,\u201d and can\u2019t give anything but the haziest description. The Knack doesn\u2019t change your looks in any way: You simply give cues that convince people you are not worth their attention.<br /> <br /> As long as you stay quiet and don\u2019t call attention to yourself, you can use this Knack any time. People\u2019s eyes just slide off you. If you want to interact with others but blur their memory of that scene, it costs a Legend point, paid before the interaction begins. (Sorry, you can\u2019t blur memories after the fact.)<br /> <br /> Scions and other creatures of Legend can resist Forgettable as normal, if they think of it at the time. After the fact, a character\u2019s player must succeed at a (Willpower + Integrity + Legend) roll, at a difficulty of the Scion\u2019s Epic Manipulation, for the character to spend the willpower and force herself to rmember the person she ignored at the time.<br /> <br /> Epic Appearance prevents a character from using Forgettable, unless the Scion also has (and uses) My Eyes Are Up Here.<br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;Manipulation: Power Groveling<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Legend; Miscellaneous Action<br /> <br /> By abasing yourself, whining and pleading (and spending a Legend), you convince a threatening person that you cannot possibly be a danger and might make a decent lickspittle lackey. The Knack works equally well on sapient titanspawn (who often really dig having lickspittle lackeys), schoolyard bullies and truant officers, making it a good way to avoid all manner of trouble.<br /> <br /> Strictly speaking, the Knack\u2019s effects last only one scene. After that, your target simply tends to think of you as a harmless wretch. It\u2019s up to you to persuade him of your continued usefulness, instead of eating you, beating you up or otherwise harming you \u2014 assuming you didn\u2019t have the sense to get away while you had the chance.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-01T16:04:36-08:00","id":1010854,"title":"","content":"NEW BOONS<br /> <br /> I didn't care much to create new Boons, either, but a few became unavoidable, or at least desirable to fill gaps in characters' capabilities. One simply seemed appropriate for the soap-oera-ish high school setting.<br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;Saltpeter in the Coffee/Viagra in the Water (Health \u2022)<br /> <b>Dice Pool:</b> Willpower + Legend <b>Activation:</b> Action<br /> <b>Cost:</b> 1 Legend <b>Duration:</b> Days<br /> <br /> With the slightest touch and expenditure of a point of Legend, the Scion can grant or withhold a very specific and highly valued form of health. One application of this Boon inflicts sexual impotence or frigidity. The other application grants increased (though not superhuman) sexual stamina and desire. (If you need a game mechanic, it adds +1 to the target\u2019s Stamina, only for purposes of this particular fatiguing activity \u2014 see <b>Scion: Hero,</b> p. 182.) Whether either application is a curse or blessing depends on circumstances and point of view.<br /> <br /> The roll to inflict this Boon has a difficulty of the target\u2019s Legend. The effects last one day, plus one day per threshold success. Use of either effect can cancel out the opposite application, leaving the target at her normal sexual capacity and interest.<br /> <br /> &lt;3&gt;My Knife is a Sword (War \u2022)<br /> <b>Dice Pool: </b>None <b>Activation:</b> Action<br /> <b>Cost:</b> One Legend <b>Duration:</b> Scene<br /> <br /> Weapons are not of equal worth in battle, but a Scion\u2019s force of divinity can make her a legendary warrior with any weapon she wants. Slaying enemies with a mace or a machine gun is ordinary. Slaying enemies with a pool cue, sickle or iron models of ducks\u2026 now, that\u2019s legendary!<br /> <br /> When a character acquires this Boon, she selects a signature weapon. This can be an ordinary type of weapon from the lists on pp. 202 and 203 of <b>Scion: Hero</b> or some novel weapon that can be represented using those standard weapon templates. (For instance, a pool cue is a macana; a sickle can be a kama; and darts stand in for those iron mandarin ducks.) The Scion spends a Legend point; this can be part of the same action spent readying the weapon. For the rest of the scene, the weapon\u2019s base Damage trait goes to +5 dice. The type of damage, bashing or lethal, does not change. Strength, Epic Strength, threshold successes and other factors then add to this base Damage as usual for weapons. Weapon Speed, Accuracy and other traits do not change.<br /> <br /> My Knife is a Sword works for relic versions of the character\u2019s signature weapon. In this case, the Boon changes the Damage rating of the weapon template; additional Damage bought through the relic\u2019s points add to this Damage of +5 dice.<br /> <br /> Obviously, this Boon is most useful when applied to weapons with low Damage ratings. It exists so Scions are not penalized for choosing signature weapons based on mythic appropriateness or sheer stylishness, instead of what kills enemies the most effectively.<br /> <br /> A character who buys My Knife is a Sword twice can apply its benefits to any weapon. Each new weapon so empowered, however, calls for a separate use of the Boon and a separate expenditure of Legend.<br /> <br /> NEW AND ALTERED PANTHEON-SPECIFIC PURVIEWS<br /> <br /> I won\u2019t give full texts of these because some of the Boons draw on other people\u2019s work. Ren Harvest is unchanged from the published version; Mana draws on the published version of Enech (the Irish Pantheon PSP) and the PSP in the Atua netbook. Runesinging is my own invention, but so simple that this capsule description is probably all anyone needs to nderstand the Chronicle.<br /> <br /> HEKU <b>(Pesedjet):</b> Power through names and the aspects of the soul.<br /> \u2022 <b>Ren Harvest:</b> Gain Legend points when people speak or write of your deeds.<br /> \u2022\u2022 <b>Bind the Heart: </b>Temporarily change another person\u2019s Nature.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>Shabti:</b> Transform images into temporay Creatures or Followers.<br /> <br /> MANA <b>(Atua):</b> Wield and exploit supernatural prohibitions.<br /> \u2022 <b>Know Tapu:</b> Sense supernatural entities and prohibitions.<br /> \u2022\u2022 <b>Warrior\u2019s Mana:</b> Assert your divine rank to enhance your weapons and armor.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 <b>Declare Minor Tapu:</b> Forbidden object brings bad luck to those who touch it.<br /> <br /> RUNESINGING <b>(Kaleva):</b> Wield supernatural power through song instead of Relics.<br /> \u2022 Use one-dot Boons from one Purview without the need for a relic; sing instead.<br /> \u2022\u2022 Use two-dot Boons from two Purviews without the need for a relic; sing instead.<br /> \u2022\u2022\u2022 Use three-dot Boons from three Purviews without the need for a relic; sing instead.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-01T16:07:37-08:00","id":1010855,"title":"","content":"Now, moving on to stuff that's more fun: the protagonists of the campaign! I thought everyone made excellent characters. I am grateful to have such good players.<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">THE PLAYERS\u2019 CHARACTERS</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Naturally, three of the five players wanted to play characters who didn\u2019t come from the official pantheons. So, I grabbed a quick\u2019n\u2019dirty Native American pantheon off the Scion Wiki, cribbed a few ideas from a fan-made Polynesian Pantheon (notably, I adapted the Irish PSP from the Scion Companion for a Polynesian PSP about taboos) and dashed off a simple Finnish pantheon and PSP. I hope you can figure out how the PSPs operate from context in the Chronicle.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">GENE BLACKFOREST, Scion of Coyote</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gene\u2019s mother was an aspiring actress who caught Coyote\u2019s eye. He took a false identity for a one-night stand; neither parent had parenthood in mind at the time. Gene\u2019s mother never found stardom, but she did eventually land a job as a local TV announcer. She gave Gene an interest in theater, and Coyote gave him a gift of gab and talent for deceit. Fortunately, Gene did not inherit his father\u2019s amorality.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The other Manitou forced Coyote to acknowledge and Visit his son, but they didn\u2019t have to force very hard. Coyote gave his son a belt that enabled Gene to channel Animal and Illusion Boons, and could also turn into a lasso. Gene also received a talking raven that could grow to great size.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Like his father, Gene tends to take impulsive, foolish risks and talk himself into trouble. With the help of friends, though, Gene is a little better than his father at talking himself <i>out </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">of trouble again.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">GUS SOBECK, Scion of Sobek</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus\u2019 great-grandfather Gotthelf Sobeck was a member of the Afrika Korps in World War Two. Circumstances lead to him dumped in the Nile, with crocodiles closing in on him. Only, the largest and fastest crocodile talked to him instead of eating him. <i>You bear my name,</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> the crocodile said. </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">For that, I will give you a chance to buy your life.</span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> Decades later, Sobek collected on that debt by taking the place of Gotthelf\u2019s grandson for one night.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus is tall and strong, blonde-haired and blue-eyed \u2014 a young Dolph Lundgren. Even before his Visitation, he became a star athlete (football and wrestling), as well as a junior-league parkeur champion. In his spare time Gus participates in the neighborhood watch and teaches junior self-defense classes at the Y. His family has money (his father runs the local dealerships for BMW and a few other high-end European cars). Gus\u2019 feeling of entitlement is tempered by a strictly meritocratic code: People with the drive to develop skill at something deserve respect for it; slackers do not.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">His Visitation pretty much confirmed Gus\u2019 attitudes about himself and the World. He became the most physically capable of the Scions, focusing on Epic Attributes, with Ren Harvest his only Boon. Shortly before he died, Gotthelf gave him a crocodile-tooth pendant; it is a Relic for channeling the Animal Purview, and allows Gus to summon a supernatural crocodile.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">HOLLY FROST, Scion of Louhi</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The Crone of Pohjola took the place of Holly\u2019s mother to place a daughter with a suitably aristocratic American family: The Frost family fortune goes back before the Revolution. Her father runs a commercial bank (and he didn\u2019t fall for the crazy schemes that crashed the financial system, thank you very much, the Frosts stick to the tried and true). Her mother is active in several charities. The Frosts are very nice people, and they taught Holly to be nice, too. Which she is. Which she works at\u2026 because when people make Holly truly angry, she destroys them with an icy fury and insidious, calculating genius.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Her Visitation revealed to Holly the source for those nastier aspects of her personality and amplified her agility and intellect to superhuman levels. Holly was a bit disappointed when her mother decreed that she should attend a public school on the other side of the country instead of a super-elite private school in the Hamptons, but no one argues with Louhi. Holly immediately decided that she would make Avalon High her oyster. She lives in one of the foreclosed McMansions, with two servants who don\u2019t know what\u2019s going on.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holly has straight, pale blonde hair and a slim, enchanting figure. Boys pay court to the ice princess, knowing she will never melt but hoping nonetheless. She is very much her mother\u2019s daughter: a witch, proficient in Magic, Mystery and getting her own way. Her chief tool of power is the Mirror of True Seeing, which channels Magic, Mystery and Moon, and lets her communicate with her Guide. To Holly\u2019s frustration, neither her own intellect, persuasion or Mystery enable her to divine the identity behind the voice that advises her on supernatural lore and urges her to temper her vengeful, manipulative streak.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">JACK THOMPSON, Scion of Lono</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jack Thompson always knew he was the son of a God\u2026 sort of. When she was younger, his somewhat hippyish marine biologist mother met gonzo journalist and counterculture icon Hunter S. Thompson on a beach in Hawaii, and the result was Jack. Hunter S. Thompson was fond of claiming that he was the mortal incarnation of Lono, the Polynesian God of a whole lot of stuff. Lono thought it was funny (and only fair) to take Hunter S. Thompson\u2019s form, that night on a Hawaiian beach. Later, Jack\u2019s mother moved to Washington and became an oceanography and marine biology teacher at a community college.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">As the son of a Polynesian War God, Jack grew up tall, strong and not looking a bit like either his mother or Hunter S. Thompson. Jack is an ace football player (people call him the Throwin\u2019 Samoan, really smart, highly sociable\u2026 and both lazy and self-sabotaging. He very much channels the trickster aspects of his father. He can take things seriously, but can\u2019t stand to be <i>seen </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">taking things seriously, or to have other people take </span></span><i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">him </span></span></i><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">seriously.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jack\u2019s Visitation strengthened all these aspects of his identity. He became even stronger, much quicker, and smarter. His father gave him the little tiki figure from an episode of <i>The Brady Bunch</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">, to use in channeling Chaos and Sky Boons (so far, Jack only shows interest in Chaos), and a hula dancer figurine that summons the ghost of a Polynesian warrior, as Jack\u2019s trainer. Jack\u2019s five buddies on the Avalon Knights offensive line spontaneously became his Followers.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">JAEDA MAGDALENA, Scion of Tlazolt\u00e9otl</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Mexican cabana boy Mateo Magdalena had no ambition higher than being a freelance gigolo to wealthy Anglo <i>touristas,</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> when he caught the eye of the most beautiful woman he\u2019d ever seen. Tlazolt\u00e9otl was posing as an actress in the, ahem, adult cinema industry at the time she visited the resort. She could have had any of the eager, good-looking young cabana boys; she chose Mateo. Nine months later, she reappeared just long enough to hand over the infant Jaeda and tell Mateo to raise their child well. Mateo did not. He ended up hopping the border to land in East Los Angeles, where Jaeda grew up in a world of poverty, gangs and drugs. When Tlazolt\u00e9otl next looked in on her daughter, she found Mateo drinking the stipend she\u2019d arranged to support Jaeda, and her daughter the heroin-addicted doxy of a gangbanger. Tlazolt\u00e9otl got her daughter cleaned up, told her about the Atzl\u00e1nti, and sent her to Avalon and the terrifying new challenge of high school.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jaeda now lives with her father in a Hooverdale trailer park. Her greatest love is the 1970 Roadrunner she restored herself. She is the most beautiful girl in school, as well as a swift and deadly knife fighter. Unfortunately, she still suffers severe self-esteem issues that make her cling to people around her, even while pretending to be a rebel who goes her own way.</span></span></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tlazolt\u00e9otl gave her daughter a pair of Tezcatlipoca\u2019s Smokin\u2019 Mirrorshades(TM) that enable her to channel Darkness Boons. (Nothing unusual in that; the Lord of Night hands them out to any Atzl\u00e1nti who ask.) She also gave Jaeda a Tepetlacalli \u2014 a ceremonial stone box used with bloodletting ceremonies; its only power is that using it in the Maguey Sting rite means the rite gives two Legend points instead of one.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tlazolt\u00e9otl was as surprised as anyone when Jaeda opened the box and found the First Flower inside. The First Flower is the obsidian dagger that Quetzalco\u00e1tl used when he sacrificed the other Atzl\u00e1nti to initiate the Age of the Fifth Sun. It is one of the pantheon\u2019s most potent Relics. Quetzalco\u00e1tl says the First Flower goes where Fate directs, and no one dares call him a liar. The dagger has \u20131 Speed and +1 Damage compared to a normal hadseax, its damage is Piercing, and channels Health and Illusion Boons \u2014 specifically, The Subtle Knife, so Jaeda can take it places where people would frown on such weapons. It has channeled other Purviews for other owners, but no owner receives access to more than two Purviews.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-01T16:09:25-08:00","id":1010857,"title":"","content":"Oh, hey, and you might like to know a little about where the campaign was set. Most of the setting was designed on the fly, but here's what the players knew at the beginning.<br /> <br /> AVALON, ETNA, HOOVERDALE<br /> <br /> The Scions live in and around the small town of Avalon. It is part of the exurban fringe that arcs around the east side of Seattle, from Monroe to Tukwila. Avalon\u2019s exact location is left undefined. Its nearest neighbor is another small town, Etna. The strip of unincorporated King County between Avalon and Etna supposedly holds the community of Hooverdale.<br /> <br /> Avalon is a nice, moderately affluent town very much like Monroe. Its \u201cdowntown\u201d consists of several blocks of trendy boutiques, restaurants and other small businesses, along with the town hall and public library. Around this spreads a halo of grocery stores and big-box stores, offices for dentists, realtors and other professional people, and a residential mix of condo blocks with older ranch-style homes, bungalows and the inevitable brand-new McMansions. Avalon High is part of this fringe. So is the very upscale gated community of Rowanfield, which has its own golf course.<br /> <br /> Etna is rougher, with fewer professional offices and more self-storage lots and car dealerships. The fanciest restaurant is a Red Robin\u2019s. What passes for a downtown consists of two intersecting main streets lined with fast food joints, auto parts stores, mini-mart gas stations and a Safeway. Both streets are always choked with people going through Etna on their way to and from somewhere else. The homes tend to be older, with more bungalows and apartments than condos or McMansions.<br /> <br /> A freeway runs through the unincorporated strip between Avalon and Etna. Offramps lead to both towns. This is allegedly Hooverdale, but Hooverdale does not actually exist. During the S&amp;L real estate bubble of the 1980s, developers laid out streets for extensive housing developments with names like \u201cQuail Run\u201d and \u201cCedar Vista.\u201d A mall was also built. Then the bubble burst, leaving an offramp to a mall and a whole lot of vacant lots. Instead of elegant upscale communities, Hooverdale got trailer parks. The recent real estate boom led to a crop of McMansions in Hooverdale (land was cheap), but most of those are now in foreclosure and the former residents live in the trailer parks. Hooverdale Mall was refurbished and expanded, though, and now survives on the business from Avalon, Etna and further afield.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T16:13:52-08:00","id":1011266,"title":"","content":"Here we go with the adventures! Since the accounts are a bit long, I'll break them up into smaller chunks.<br /> <br /> Episode One: Back to School<br /> <br /> The series begins with the first day of class in the new school year. The characters all received their Visitations over the summer. Their divine parents told them all they would go to school with other Scions: Some characters already lived in the town of Avalon, while others moved to the area during the summer. Only Gus and Jack, however, know about each other before school starts, because they are both on the football team and so find out during August practice. The Gods plan to introduce their Scions during a special lunchtime meeting.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Introductory Scenes</u></b><br /> As Gus, Jack, Gene, Holly and Jaeda arrive at Avalon High, they see the little dramas of the other students as kids renew acquaintances or meet the Scions for the first time. In no particular order\u2026<br /> <br /> <b>HOLLY</b>: The beautiful, obviously moneyed new girl attracts immediate notice by the Fashionistas, the school\u2019s self-appointed queen bee clique. Notable among them are golden-haired blonde cheerleader Delia Bright and red-headed Empress Bee, Sheena Delisle. They grill Holly about her knowledge of fashion, makeup, what\u2019s \u201cin,\u201d and other important subjects. Holly, of course, outdoes all the Fashionistas: Her father has more money; she not only has a Prada handbag, she bought it in New York on the same trip when she saw a Broadway show; etc.<br /> <br /> Sheena has other things on her mind, though. She sees s00per-hawt swim team star Derek Huckovich, whom she seems to regard as hers. She calls to him in a familiar manner, calling him over to the group. On the way, though, Derek bumps into another beautiful girl, not a Fashionista: Laine Farber. They seem to know each other, as Derek says Laine looks great without her braces. Indeed, they can\u2019t seem to take their eyes off each other.<br /> <br /> Holly tries to assess the social dynamics and gets it <i>disastrously </i>wrong. (The player botched the first roll of the game, woohoo!) Holly tells Sheena how good Derek and Laine look together, clearly they\u2019ve been a couple a long time. Sheena snarls, stalks over and yanks Derek away. Derek doesn\u2019t stick around, and his parting words are for Laine. Sheena rounds on Laine and hisses that <i>nobody </i>dates the star athletes except Fashionistas or girls who have their permission, and Laine is not to touch Derek, <i>ever.</i> Laine flinches and hurries away.<br /> <br /> <b>GUS</b>: As Gus walks down the hall toward his locker he sees Burt Broder, one of the school\u2019s more notorious bullies, shove and mock Landon Smith, one of the school\u2019s more victimized nerds. This time, however, Landon doesn\u2019t flinch, plead or try to get away. When Burt tries to shove Landon again, Landon neatly judo-flips him to the floor. Burt\u2019s sub-bully minions move in\u2026 but Vice-Principal Stone intervenes before a fight can start. Landon quickly says that Burt tripped, and Burt\u2019s buds reluctantly obey the No Squealing Code and back him up. VP Stone seems dubious \u2014 he knows this game \u2014 and tells Burt and Landon that he\u2019ll be watching them. Gus thinks Stone aims the warning at Landon more than Burt \u2014 but Stone is well known for partiality toward jocks (Burt is a wrestler, though not a very good one) and against nerds, geeks and other \u201cweakling\u201d types. Gus wonders: Is Landon one of us?<br /> <br /> <b>JACK</b>: Coach Reagan has gathered a number of the varsity players and is giving them a motivational speech before classes. He says this is the best team Avalon High has ever had, and this year he wants to <i>finally </i>beat their arch-rivals, the Etna Titans. Everyone will have to give 120% this season \u2014 110% just isn\u2019t good enough anymore! And that goes double for Jack. Talent isn\u2019t enough, he says gruffly. This year, Jack needs to buckle down and <i>work to win.</i> Team captain (and senior) Warren Mansfield says they will all do their best. Coach also warns the players to avoid distractions such as girls, afterschool jobs or grades. For the next three months, they need to keep their minds on what matters: football!<br /> <br /> <b>GENE</b>: While he mingles and shmoozes with the students he knows, Gene encounters another member of the drama club: Ashley Grace Kowalski, who prefers to use her middle name. Grace is a Perkygoth. She wears black, reads poetry and 19<sup>th</sup> century romances, baby-sits because it\u2019s the closes she can come to being a governess, and has spiked fuchsia hair that is certainly not suitable for an early 19<sup>th</sup>-century English country estate.<br /> <br /> Grace and her friends all carry various books in the \u201cTwilight\u201d series. Her friends chivvy Grace into admitting that Edward is hot, though Grace insists he still comes behind Heathcliffe and Mr. Rochester. Grace and Gene banter and flirt a bit before he moves on, working the crowd.<br /> <br /> <b>JAEDA</b>: The emigr\u00e9 from East L.A. immediately attracts a flock of admiring boys. After all, she\u2019s the hottest girl they\u2019ve ever seen, and her clothes say \u201cBad Girl Who Says Yes.\u201d The boys want to know who she is, where she\u2019s from, what she likes to do, her phone number, etc. Middle-aged janitor Mitch Osada shoos them away and gives Jaeda bit of faintly sinister advice. In many ways, he says, Avalon is a lot safer than East Los Angeles. No gangs, not much drugs or crime. On the other hand, it has dangers of its own that Jaeda might not expect. She should be careful \u2014 especially in how she chooses her friends. Enigmatic warning complete, he sends Jaeda off to the start-of-year school assembly.<br /> <br /> There is a brief assembly in the school auditorium before everyone goes to their classes. The assembly features a pep talk from Principal Flecker. It\u2019s exactly the same speech he gives every year and leaves everyone thoroughly depressed.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Meet and Greet</u></b><br /> At lunchtime, the Scions\u2019 divine parents meet them at the classroom door and escort them to a conference room. (Except for Holly, who merely received instructions from Louhi.) Matters become tense as the Gods argue who will chair the meeting, but Lono (resuming his guise as Hunter S. Thompson) and Coyote manage to soothe divine egos.<br /> <br /> Lono takes the podium. He reminds everyone of the war between Gods and Titans and the intent to have Scions get to know each other, learn to work together and form cross-pantheon alliances that the older Gods are too bound by Fate to manage easily. Each God then introduces his or her offspring, gives his or her own statement of what they want from their children, and has the Scion make a brief statement of his own.<br /> <br /> The Gods\u2019 introductions tell more about their characters than supply useful advice. Apollo tells the Scions to be beautiful and amazing. Huitzilopochtli tells them to fight and die bravely; their sacrifices will save the World. Tlazolte\u00f3tl tells Apollo to stop flirting, she\u2019s not interested. Oh, and the Scions should try not to get caught doing anything that would get them expelled. Sobek advised against leaving witnesses. Shango (well, he says he\u2019s Shango; he looks and sounds like James Earl Jones) tells the Scions to remember that they are to guide and protect mortals as well as fight threats to the World. Coyote spikes the punchbowl. Lono tells the Scions to remember to have fun and make friends. He notices Holly and has her introduce herself.<br /> <br /> The most dramatic incident, though, comes when Nergal introduces himself and his son, Leo. Nergal, Lord of Lions, God of night, death, plague and hunting, says he hopes the other Scions can make a man of his maggot of a son whom he never wanted anyway. When told to speak, Leo pulls out a cross and tries to rebuke and exorcise Nergal and the other \u201cpagan devils\u201d in the name of Christ. Nergal flips out. He throws Leo across the refreshment table, rips Leo\u2019s shirt off, carves a sketch of a lion on his back and summons a lion-demon. He tells the spirit that Leo is its assignment. The spirit fades and becomes a tattoo of a rearing lion across Leo\u2019s now-unwounded back. The other Gods do not interfere because they are in low-Legend Avatars and Nergal is not and could destroy the whole school in a moment of rage.<br /> <br /> The Gods then leave so the Scions can get to know each other on their own. In addition to the PCs, there are:<br /> <br /> <b>DELIA BRIGHT</b>, Scion of Apollo: Beautiful, vain, status-obsessed. Flirts with Gus because she\u2019s a Fashionista, he\u2019s a handsome star jock and it\u2019s the natural order for them to be together. She snubs Holly and Jaeda.<br /> <br /> <b>MARSHALL BYRD</b>, Scion of Huitzilopochtli: Brawny army brat, proud, patriotic and a real dick. He announces that as the Scion with the most combat training, he will be the group\u2019s trainer and war-leader. He also tells Jaeda, rather patronizingly, that if she sticks with him he\u2019ll take care of her. He mocks Leo and calls Gus \u201cDolph\u201d; Gus responds by dubbing him \u201cNiedermeyer.\u201d Marshall doesn\u2019t make any friends here.<br /> <br /> <b>BARBARA CROWNE</b>, Scion of Shango: Politically ambitious. She has her eye on the<br /> White House and sees student government as a necessary small first step. Barbara pumps the other Scions for information but gives away little about herself. Holly recognizes Barbara as her chief rival. They are carefully nice to each other.<br /> <br /> <b>LEO LEFEBVRE</b>, Scion of Nergal: Stays miserably to himself, rebuffs attempt to be nice to him. Even Gene can\u2019t get through to him. Gus, Jack, Gene, Jaeda and Holly agree that Leo will be a problem.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T16:15:44-08:00","id":1011268,"title":"","content":"Episode One: Back to School (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Malled!</u></b><br /> Barbara arranged a mass expedition to Hooverdale Mall to shop for school supplies and new outfits. Once at the mall, though, the Scions split up into individuals, pairs and trios. At the food court, the PCs find no groups of seats; Marshall sits with a group of fellow military brats, while Delia sits with fellow Fashionistas. The PCs decide to eat together on a set of benches in the main concourse.<br /> <br /> Near the benches, workmen have ripped up a section of mall floor to inspect an area of subsidence beneath the mall\u2019s foundations. They can\u2019t see a cause for the earth disturbance. A workman then falls into the ground with a scream, which is immediately cut off. Then a huge, pink, serpentine horror erupts from the ground \u2014 a lindwurm!<br /> <br /> Battle is joined. Everyone stunted like mad (too many to remember and list). Gus leads off with an attempt to slug the giant worm with his best haymaker. His fist bounces. Jaeda strikes with her Relic knife, and it bounces too. Jack, however, improvises a discus from the lid of a garbage can and charges it with Warrior\u2019s Mana. It slices into the lindwurm but is destroyed in the process. Holly uses Lightning Sprinter to pull the remaining workmen and other nearby people to safety in the nearby bridal shop. Gene produces his Relic lasso.<br /> <br /> Gene helps Jaeda strike a better blow by lassoing the lindworm. He knows he can\u2019t possibly hinder the massive (and massively-strong) monster, but his attack lets him employ Perfect Partner and coordinate with Jaeda, reducing the lindwurm\u2019s DV against Jaeda\u2019s next attack. Still, Jaeda doesn\u2019t hurt it much.<br /> <br /> Holly can do nothing to harm the lindwurm physically, so she pulls out her Relic mirror, catches the monster\u2019s reflection and glares fiercely while delivering the fiercest malediction a daughter of the Hamptons can inflict: \u201cPink? <i>Pink!?</i>\u201d Her Evil Eye lops five dice off the lindwurm\u2019s subsequent actions. The lindwurm strikes at Jaeda but misses badly.<br /> <br /> Gus realizes that hitting the lindwurm is futile. Instead, he wrestles it. Flexing and gathering his energies for a Crushing Grip, his shirt bursts off. (Just a bit of stunting.) He grabs the worm and starts ripping it apart. Jaeda keeps stabbing at the immobilized lindwurm while Jack takes a stab with a dropped pickax. Jaeda finally strikes the killing blow by stabbing at an existing wound, managing to sever an internal organ.<br /> <br /> All this took about 15 seconds. Just as the lindwurm falls, Marshall and Delia run up. Marshall is angry because he missed the battle. He says they shouldn\u2019t have fought the monster without him; the PCs\u2019 opinion of him drops further. (Particularly since he\u2019d bragged about his gun skills and seems conspicuously lacking in ordnance.) Delia just wants to help Gus clean off the lindwurm icky-stuff, preferably someplace private.<br /> <br /> The Gods told the Scions to hide evidence of the supernatural, so the Scions \u201cborrow\u201d a panel truck while Gene and Holly steal the security tape footage. They burn the body in the basement of one of Hooverdale\u2019s unfinished houses. Not knowing exactly what its Trophy is, they chop up the lindwurm before Holly checks her mirror (Mystery Purview) and learns they were supposed to collect its skin. Nevertheless, the Scions feel pretty good about defeating their first monster. They burn the body to prevent Titanic energies from turning any other creatures into monsters.<br /> <br /> Delia, meanwhile, makes herself useful by handling the media. When cops and reporters arrive at the mall, they find the tearful-but-trying-to-be-brave Scion of Apollo spinning a yarn about Mexican Mafia gangsters using a Fiendish Thingie to kill a workman and scare everyone away from their drug deal. It\u2019s more comforting to believe in gangster killings than giant worms, so the media buy this and nobody asks too many questions. (Later, the Scions investigate the lindwurm\u2019s hole and find that it collapsed just a few yards back, so they can\u2019t follow the tunnel to the lindwurm\u2019s source.)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Friday Night Fights</u></b><br /> That Friday, the Avalon Knights have their first home game against their arch-rivals, the Etna Titans. Coach Reagan is almost frantic with desire to finally beat the Titans.<br /> <br /> Gus and Jack are, of course, on the field. From what they\u2019ve been told about the Fateful Aura of Scions, Holly, Jaeda and Gene decide they\u2019d better attend a game against the Titans. In the parking lot, Jaeda\u2019s eye is drawn to a beautifully restored golden Ford Mercury convertible. Men would kill or die to have Jaeda look at and touch them the way she handles the car.<br /> <br /> The three easily wangle a place on the sidelines instead of up in the stands. (IIRC, Gene instantly got himself a place as the only boy on the cheer squad, not counting the boy who dons cardboard-and-tinfoil armor and waves a tinfoil sword as the team mascot, Sir Dance-A-Lot.) Delia is there too, as head cheerleader. (She gives Jaeda and Holly the stink eye.) If any other NPC Scions are there, the PCs don\u2019t see them.<br /> <br /> The Scions do, however, see a middle-aged, Nordic-looking couple in the stands. Who are about 10\u2019 tall \u2014 frost giants! There are also two rocky-looking, 8\u2019 high giants in Etna High letter jackets. The Titans themselves, however, just look like high school steroid junkie barbell boys.<br /> <br /> The Titans quickly establish themselves as a powerful team. Their defensive line is exceptionally tough, strong and fierce. Most of the team\u2019s strategies, however, revolve around getting the ball to #34, Gary Vetterson, who simply plows through all attempts to stop him and takes down any Knight he tackles.<br /> <br /> Gus and Jack know about Gary Vetterson. A recruiter from Alabama came to watch him play <i>last</i> year, when he was only a junior. He seems to have gotten even better. But can he stand up to Scions?<br /> <br /> Yes, he can. Gus first clashes directly with Gary when the Titan jinks from out of a screen of other players and lunges at Gus in an attempt to tackle. It was a good play, but wouldn\u2019t have worked against Gus\u2019 Epic Strength\u2026 except Gary clearly has Epic Strength as well. It\u2019s a near thing, but Gus goes down. Gus complements Gary on the play. Gary says to get used to it. The Titans take a narrow lead and hold it through that quarter.<br /> <br /> In the second quarter, Coach Reagan calls team captain Warren Mansfield over. Warren comes back with a new play. Gus and Jack are both to use a feint and try tackling Gary Vetterson. They have to stop him. Gus and Jack make their attempts \u2014 and as Gary reels, Warren dives at him low. This isn\u2019t just an attempt to knock him off his feet: The two Scions can tell that this is an attack meant to dislocate a knee and take Gary out of the game. And then the rest of the Knights\u2019 offensive line lands on Gary, concealing the dirty play from the referee.<br /> <br /> Gary is on his knees when he roars with rage. Suddenly, he is 8\u2019 tall with ice-white skin and rime on his pads and jersey. He lifts the entire mass of Knights piled on him and <i>throws </i>them like a handful of puppies. The crowd cheers at this amazing play, but only the Scions seem to see the giant who has suddenly manifested on the field. Seconds later, Gary is again merely a very large, strong teenage boy.<br /> <br /> The dirty play sets Gus seething with rage. He collars Warren and stalks over to Coach Reagan. Warren admits the coach ordered the assault. Coach Reagan starts trying to bluster his rationalizations and get Gus back out on the field, but Gus hits him with Game Face. Coach Reagan blanches and runs away. Gus snarls at Warren that win or lose, the Knights are playing the rest of the game clean.<br /> <br /> Well, sort of. Jack has other ideas.<br /> <br /> Gus and Jack no longer even pretend to follow Warren\u2019s leadership on the field. Jack commandeers his Followers on the offensive line to ordain a new strategy: Run around like maniacs, getting in the Titans\u2019 way, to cause as much chaos on the field as possible. Jack then takes the ball and runs down the field, using Eye of the Storm so the chaos of milling, confused boys doesn\u2019t affect him. By using Legend in this manner, Jack scores several touchdowns and pushes the Knights far into the lead. The Titans rally in subsequent quarters, with superb play by Gary and the defensive line (which always looks at him for approval before carrying out a play) \u2014 but they cannot overcome the substantial lead. The game ends with a solid victory by the Knights.<br /> <br /> (Gene, Holly and Jaeda don\u2019t find much to do on the sidelines, but they keep an eye on the giants. Gene also notices a remarkably hot Nordic glamazon in the Etna section of the stands.)<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-02T16:17:06-08:00","id":1011269,"title":"","content":"Episode One: Back to School<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Five: Aftermath</u></b><br /> Jaeda and Holly are first out after the game, followed closely by Gene, who wants to meet the leggy, golden-haired beauty he saw in the stands. Turns out, she had noticed him in the cheer section. She seems pleased to make his acquaintance, though she also wants to know about Jack and Gus, whom she calls the best players she\u2019s ever seen\u2026 besides her brother. She introduces herself as Reese Vetterson \u2014 Gary\u2019s sister. The Scions see the middle-aged frost giants drive off in a maximum-size SUV that should still be too small for them.<br /> <br /> Coming out of the locker rooms, Gus and Jack rendezvous with the other PCs. Gary has already met the other Scions at the Ford Mercury, which is, of course, his car that he restored himself. Jaeda impresses Gary by showing she can Talk Car. The two young rock giants also arrive at about the same time.<br /> <br /> Although Gary had seemed to respect Gus somewhat for Gus\u2019 strength and his sportsmanship (he\u2019d made sure to apologize to Gary for the dirty play), the young giant now scowls at Jack and Gus. \u201cScions,\u201d he snarls. Gary clearly wants to hit someone as he tells Gus and Jack how his parents told him what the two Scions were, and there can be nothing but war between giants and lousy, stinking God-brats. At the same time, he\u2019s arguing with Reese that she shouldn\u2019t get friendly with boys who \u201caren\u2019t our kind.\u201d Reese retorts that that limits her to Don and Dave \u2014 evidently, the two rock giants. She\u2019s had them both. In fact, she\u2019s had them both <i>at once,</i> and wasn\u2019t impressed. So, she\u2019ll look for her fun elsewhere. This does not improve Gary\u2019s temper any, and the two young rock giants look pretty pissed as well.<br /> <br /> Seeing that a completely pointless fight is about to erupt, Gene leaps up onto the hood of a car to get everyone\u2019s attention. (<i>Not </i>Gary\u2019s Mercury. Not being an idiot, he senses that would be <i>bad</i>.) Gene begins declaiming a paean about the game, giving full credit to all the great plays by the Titans as well as the Knights. Powered by Epic Manipulation and Center of Attention, he keeps everyone\u2019s attention long enough for tempers to cool. Holly records it all on her phone to show at the next school assembly. By the time Gene winds down, everyone feels pretty good about themselves and the game. Gary scowls that he\u2019s still no friend of Scions, but is no longer about to explode. He even asks Jaeda if she wants to Talk Car some more. Jaeda says, \u201cI\u2019m a Scion too,\u201d turns on her heel and stalks away.<br /> <br /> The Scions proceed to the post-game party. Jack is the hero of the hour, though there\u2019s enough talk about Gus for the Pesedjet Scion to recover his spent Legend through Ren Harvest. Naturally, Jack sabotages himself by getting stinking drunk and waking up the next morning with his pants worn in a non-standard fashion.<br /> <br /> Delia is at the party too, and she makes it clear she still has the hots for Gus. Gus would like to get hot and heavy with Delia, but his code of honor demands one thing from the Scion of Apollo: He tells Delia that she has to show Jaeda the respect due to a fellow Scion who\u2019s shown courage and skill in facing titanspawn. Delia blows up, calls Jaeda the \u201ctrailer trash daughter of a trailer trash god,\u201d and storms off. She clearly is angry at Gus now, as well.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Plans for the Future:</u></b><br /> Gene has no special plans except for looking up Reese Vetterson\u2019s phone number.<br /> <br /> Gus gives Warren a kindly-but-stern talk about fair play and winning clean.<br /> <br /> Holly sets out to befriend Laine and acquire a string of nerd followers as she joins the chess club, Knowledge Bowl and every other extracurricular activity where she can shine. Plus, she wants to start a rock band. Anything to raise her prestige.<br /> <br /> Jack, inspired by the lindwurm incident, starts looking for reports of other weird events.<br /> <br /> Jaeda pines for Gary\u2019s car, while getting lust-crazed boys to do her homework.<br /> <br /> -----------------<br /> And so the saga begins!<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T14:25:45-08:00","id":1011665,"title":"","content":"I like to imagine Norse Scions are the kids who somehow get access to alcohol, while being underage. :P<br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NCHq0m67lq8\" data-code=\"NCHq0m67lq8\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/NCHq0m67lq8/maxresdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T15:44:25-08:00","id":1011701,"title":"","content":"I'd thank you for the drinking song, but the library's computer didn't give me sound. Mute/Unmute button made no difference. Odd. I'll try again when I visit a friend and use his computer. Moving on...<br /> <br /> EPISODE TWO: AN EYE FOR BEAUTY<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Fast Times at Avalon High</u></b><br /> In the week after the game against the Etna Titans, the Scions all find ways to keep busy.<br /> <br /> HOLLY gets to know Laine Farber, the girl who outraged arch-Fashionista Sheena Delisle by catching the dreamy star of the swim team, Derek Huckovich. Laine is at once bedazzled and terrified at dating Derek, but things seem to be going well between them. Holly believes she can recruit them for a counter-clique against the Fashionistas.<br /> <br /> GUS AND JACK face Coach Reagan\u2019s ire: Gus for calling Coach Reagan on his dirty play and sending him fleeing in supernatural terror, Jack for winning the game in a way that offends Reagan\u2019s sensibilities. He rides them both through the week\u2019s football practice. Warren Mansfield also tries to avoid talking to them or meeting their eyes. Jack finds that not everyone on the team is happy with him blatantly winning the game on his own, either.<br /> <br /> Gus also has a chat with the enigmatic Barbara Crowne. He learns less than he hoped. Barbara makes it clear, though, that she sees her talents as chiefly social, and she doesn\u2019t expect to join any monster fights.<br /> <br /> At the same time, Jack looks for recent news stories of weird events in the area. He finds an odd coincidence, a UFO sighting, attacks on livestock, a murder victim\u2019s body in strange condition, and a sighting of a big pink snake \u2014 aha, a lindwurm! But was it the same lindwurm they slew, or another one? He puts pins in a map, hoping that a pattern will emerge that leads to, well, something.<br /> <br /> GENE looks up the Vetterson family\u2019s phone number. Lucky for him, Reese answers the phone. She remembers him from the game and post-game events, and she wouldn\u2019t mind seeing more of him. Reese suggests Gene join her and some of her friends on a picnic that weekend. Oh, and to bring a few more cute boys to keep her friends company. He can get her text and email contacts through her MySpace page. Gene looks. Along with the usual list of interests and activities, he finds a stunning picture of Reese in a bikini, and that her name is actually Rieste.<br /> <br /> JAEDA seems to be on the verge of making some mortal friends: Bob and Alice, a couple she meets in the lunchroom. Bob asks her to join them at a party Saturday night.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Skeleton and Stone</u></b><br /> Jack asks Gene for help in learning more about the murder victim found in a patch of Hooverdale woods. The news reports are sketchy. The body is that of a teenage girl from Etna High called Crystal Gould who disappeared about three weeks ago. The condition of the body, however, suggests she died long before that, so the cops have not officially identified her yet.<br /> <br /> Gene\u2019s mom is a local newscaster, so he asks her what the cops aren\u2019t allowing on the air. The cops genuinely don\u2019t know much, as the body was just a mass of bones in the earth. The boys decide this is weird enough to bring in Holly. Gene and Holly pose as school newspaper reporters, talk their way into the police station that\u2019s investigating the crime, and snaffle out a copy of the medical examiner\u2019s report; Holly also sees the body\u2019s location on a map.<br /> <br /> The ME found two blows to the head, neither fatal, and evidence of a slow and inexpert knife to the heart. Something also stripped the flesh from the bones, but he can\u2019t identify what. The bones were also jumbled in an area of churned, loose earth. Jack and Holly decide to inspect the murder scene.<br /> <br /> The body was found in a patch of forest left untouched by development, among a mass of Indian plum and near a large granite boulder. In the evening, the site becomes distinctly cold, shadowy and creepy. Jack decides to use his Atuan ability to sense the supernatural and finds that yup, there\u2019s dark evil chthonic supernatural power here, radiating from the boulder. When he looks at the boulder, it\u2019s like a \u201cMagic Eye\u201d picture: In the streaks and speckles of different minerals in the granite, he sees a grotesque, madly snarling face. He also steps on a banana slug that\u2019s way too big. Maybe he shouldn\u2019t have been barefoot.<br /> <br /> The characters decide to bring in the whole group of Scions. They even talk to Leo, who has done his best to stay alone; to their surprise, he joins the examination of the magic boulder. The Scions debate whether the murder victim was a sacrifice, whether the boulder generated the lindwurm, and what they should do about it. Leo decides not to debate. He tries to exorcise the rock with his cross but shows no surprise that it has no effect. Attempting a physical approach, he also whacks the boulder with his mace to no greater effect. \u201cI\u2019m useless,\u201d he announces, and leaves.<br /> <br /> Jaeda then decides to exorcise the rock using Atzl\u00e1nti techniques \u2014 which she does not actually know. She guesses that since blood is sacred, she\u2019ll gash her hand, press it to the rock, and command the evil power to depart. Instead, she loses all her Legend points, most of her Willpower, and is stunned for a few minutes\u2026 while earthworms in the soil nearby wriggle out of the ground, visibly growing into lindwurms! Everyone spends the next hour or so chasing and killing nascent lindwurms.<br /> <br /> Marshall then surprises the group by saying he might have a solution. He knows a guy who might be able to get some C4 \u2014 not <i>tonight,</i> but soon. The next day, he calls everyone\u2019s cell phone to say that yes, he <i>can </i>get C4! Gus winces, and suggests this is not the sort of thing to say over a cell phone. \u201cOops.\u201d Later, Marshall and the others get together to schedule a demolition. Friday night is out; Gus and Jack have another football game. Saturday afternoon is out; Gene has his date with Reese. Saturday night is out; Jaeda has her party with Bob and Alice. Eventually, they settle on Sunday morning.<br /> <br /> That Friday, the Knights play an away game against the traditionally weak team from Redmond High (those Microsoft babies spend too much time playing video games). Coach benches Gus so one of the second-stringers can play. Gus does not like this one bit, but he grudgingly accepts it. The Knights win anyway, and Coach seems satisfied that he has asserted his authority. Despite this, Gus hosts the post-game party at his house. Gus sees that Delia has picked Warren as her boyfriend. Delia smiles mock-sweetly, claps Gus on the shoulder, and tells him not to feel bad that the team managed without him. Sheena, though, takes the opportunity to come on to Gus and make it clear she\u2019ll go as far as he does. Gus eagerly accepts the invitation and finds\u2026 he can\u2019t perform. Thinking quickly, he makes sure Sheena doesn\u2019t notice. But he ends the evening in a foul mood and determined that someone\u2019s gonna suffer for this. Sheena, though, seems happy. She is already running for Homecoming Queen, with Delia as her only rival. She suggests Gus join her by running for Homecoming King, in competition with Warren.<br /> <br /> Saturday, Gene and some theater class buddies join Reese and her similarly sporty friends. They hold their picnic near a TV/radio transmission tower. Conversation turns to the urban legend that the radio transmissions from the dishes are so intense that they\u2019d cook or electrocute you. Reese announces that she\u2019ll test the story by climbing the tower, and she challenges Gene to join her. He calls and raises by making it a race to the top of the tower: If Reese loses, she has to be his date for Avalon High\u2019s Homecoming dance. Reese finds this acceptable. In return, though, if <i>she </i>wins, Gene must be <i>her </i>date at the Etna High dance. Gene agrees. They both climb with amazing speed (Epic Dexterity will do that), but Reese wins handily. It\u2019s as if she had a much longer reach than Gene does\u2026 which of course she does. <i>Giant,</i> Gene remembers.<br /> <br /> Saturday night, Jaeda goes to the party and dances with several boys. Bob is there. Alice is not: Bob says she couldn\u2019t make it. Then he draws her into a side room and explains that Alice couldn\u2019t make it to the party because he didn\u2019t tell her about it. If he can make out with Jaeda, Bob goes on, he\u2019ll do her math homework. Jaeda\u2019s tempted \u2014 she has trouble even with remedial math \u2014 but decides this would be a scummy betrayal of Alice, who has never been anything but nice to her. She turns Bob down. Fifteen minutes later, a tearful and angry Alice arrives at the party to accuse Jaeda of trying to steal her boyfriend: someone sent her a picture of them dancing together. Jaeda points out that she danced with a lot of boys, and this is between Alice and Bob. Bob starts groveling as Alice drags him away from the party, and Jaeda puts him out of her mind.<br /> <br /> Sunday morning, the Scions convene at the boulder. Marshall brought his guy with C4, a grizzled vet with a right-wing paranoid streak a mile wide. Gus pulls Marshall aside for a sotto-voce argument, reminding him that they aren\u2019t supposed to let mortals in on their activities. Marshall retorts that his guy doesn\u2019t know anything about Gods and Scions; all he knows is that he\u2019s demonstrating C4 demolition to a group of kids.<br /> <br /> The vet, Sgt. Curtis LeMay Maxon, drills holes in the rock, packs in wads of C4, and sets wires and detonators. Everybody moves back. Maxon sets off the charges. There\u2019s a great big boom \u2014 and when the smoke clears and gravel stops raining down, the boulder is a heap of rubble! Better still, Jack checks for mana and finds the rock\u2019s supernatural power rapidly fading. Whaddaya know, you <i>can </i>stop some supernatural threats with high explosives. The Scions depart before the authorities come to investigate an explosion at a crime scene. Everyone feels satisfied at a job well done. Gus even admits to Marshall that blowing up the rock was pretty damn cool. Jack merely pockets a fragment of granite for a souvenir.<br /> <br /> Monday morning, the school is abuzz, but not over the demolition of a boulder. Laine and Derek are in a local hospital. They were attacked; no one knows how, but Derek is maimed.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-03T15:47:26-08:00","id":1011702,"title":"","content":"Oh, and feel free to ask any questions about design and story choices, etc. Though I can't promise I'll remember, since this campaign happened some years ago. (Ye gods, MySpace was still a thing.)<br /> <br /> EPISODE TWO: AN EYE FOR BEAUTY (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: You Always Hurt The One You Love</u></b><br /> Holly quickly organizes a sympathy visit to Laine and Derek after school. The first thing they find is that something <i>ripped out Derek\u2019s eyes</i>. Laine merely suffered an emotional breakdown. Both teens say they don\u2019t remember what happened. Holly can tell that in Derek\u2019s case, this is traumatic amnesia. Laine, however, simply doesn\u2019t want to talk. After a conference featuring wild speculation about what happened, the Scions first assure Laine that they will believe whatever she says, then use Overt Order to start her talking.<br /> <br /> Laine says she was smooching with Derek, hoping that that night he\u2019d go all the way, when suddenly Derek screamed in terror. Two snakes then reached past Laine\u2019s head and bit out Derek\u2019s eyes. Laine screamed and reeled back. She saw a horrible monster in the room, humanoid but with scaly skin and snakes for hair, before she fainted.<br /> <br /> This narrows the field of speculation somewhat but opens other possibilities. For their next step, the gang decides to break into Laine\u2019s house. Gene opens the locked door with disconcerting ease. He also notices that he is not the only person to pick the lock recently. Jaeda and Gus keep watch outside while the others look for clues. All they find is that the living room where Derek and Laine snogged has a large wall mirror.<br /> <br /> (Outside, meanwhile, Gus mentions his \u201cperformance issues\u201d to Jaeda. She recognizes the effects of a Health Purview curse to which she, fortunately, knows the counter-curse. She performs it and, um, helps assure Gus that his problems are over.)<br /> <br /> Back at the hospital, Jack uses Know Tapu and confirms what the Scions already suspected: Laine is a supernatural entity. Questions about possible weirdness in her family lead nowhere, so Holly pulls out the Mirror of True Seeing and invokes the power of Mystery. Her questions to the Infinite reveal that Laine has been transformed into titanspawn; specifically, a lesser version of one of the legendary Gorgons. She doesn\u2019t know this has happened, though \u2014 or that she took Derek\u2019s eyes. The reason for her sudden transformation into a monster is \u201ctime.\u201d The Scions are still left with unanswered questions, though (in part because some questions were unanswerable as phrased).<br /> <br /> Holly sets out to learn more about Gorgons. She goes to the school librarian, Mrs. Weihs, and asks to see \u201cthe secret library.\u201d Mrs. Weihs raises her eyebrow and says there isn\u2019t one, but Holly can browse the clipping files in the back room for information you won\u2019t find on the internet.<br /> <br /> The back room holds the magazine collection and banker\u2019s boxes stuffed with manila envelopes full of photocopies and clippings on diverse subjects, as a research resource. Much of the envelopes are about diseases, foreign countries, and the other sorts of things that students write reports about, but Mrs. Weihs has some odd stuff too. In an envelope labeled \u201cMythology/Archeology,\u201d Holly finds a copy of an article from the <i>Journal of the Warburg and Courtald Institutes</i> (1923) with a picture of a Gorgon feeding a woman blood from its arm, Dracula-fashion.<br /> <br /> Holly finally breaks down and asks her Guide for details. The mysterious voice in the Mirror of True Seeing explains that as the Greek myths describe, a curse from the Gods turned the three Gorgon sisters into monsters (titanspawn, in fact) and Perseus slew one of them. After that, the two remaining Gorgons kept a low profile. They remained enemies of the Gods, though, and learned to transform mortals into lesser versions of themselves using their blood. The mortal becomes incredibly beautiful for one month before turning into a hideous monster. The only way the new \u201cmedusa\u201d can regain her beauty is to eat the eyes of an attractive mortal. That only works for a month or two, though, before the medusa must eat another set of eyes. The eyes of a Scion grant a longer period of beauty. From then on, however, the medusa can revert to her monster form at will.<br /> <br /> The only cure is speculative: If the transformation to monster is not complete, a second dose of gorgon blood (either from the true gorgons or the heart\u2019s blood of another medusa of higher Legend) might return the victim to humanity. Laine has eaten a mortal\u2019s eyes, but she didn\u2019t kill Derek and she acted in ignorance of her condition. Therefore, it is possible that she has not completed her transformation into a monster and can still be saved. No one has ever tested this cure, though. The only cases where medusas tried to keep from eye-eating ended within a year as the medusa either killed herself or broke down, ate someone\u2019s eyes, and became irredeemably evil.<br /> <br /> The gang asks Laine some more questions and connects the dots. A month ago, Laine had plastic surgery as her Sweet Sixteen birthday present \u2014 just a nose job and a little liposuction to pull out remnant baby fat. She thought she just got a lot more out of it than she expected. Her surgeon? Doctor Madelyn Sosa, of Vancouver, B.C. Everyone groans at the transparent pseudonym.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Radical Therapies</u></b><br /> The Scions decide to try saving Laine by forcing Dr. Sosa either to give up her supply of gorgon blood or by killing her and feeding her heart\u2019s blood to Laine. The latter course means that Laine has to come along. While they plan the expedition, Holly and Gene break the news to Laine that she\u2019s a monster and the one responsible for maiming Derek. Laine finds this believable only because of Gene\u2019s Knack for making people believe any true statement he makes. Laine thinks that drinking the heart\u2019s blood of a medusa sounds utterly disgusting, but she really, really doesn\u2019t want to be an eye-eating monster.<br /> <br /> Since Marshall proved he could be useful in the boulder demolition, the gang brings him into the plan. Marshall loves the idea of an expedition to kill a monster, since he missed out on the lindwurm. There\u2019s some concern about bringing his M-16 across the border, but Gene assures the others that this truly is not a problem.<br /> <br /> The Scions come up with some flim-flam about an educational field trip to the preparations for the Winter Olympics, then set out in Holly\u2019s and Jaeda\u2019s cars. Holly passes the border, no problem. Jaeda\u2019s beat-up car (and Jaeda herself) look suspicious to a border guard, though, and the drugs-and-explosives dog barks \u201cJ\u2019Accuse!\u201d at the glove compartment. (More likely for past drugs than for explosives, to be sure.) The border guard opens the trunk and sees Marshall\u2019s small arsenal. Gene immediately spins a line of bullshit about Marshall being a biathlon competitor, and you wouldn\u2019t believe how cheap the AOC is being with the less famous sports, making people drive themselves to the games, now the ice skaters and ski jumpers get flown in first class and they can carry drugs by the pound and nobody says boo\u2026 The guard is too dazed to realize that you don\u2019t use machine guns for biathlon, and he waves them through.<br /> <br /> In Vancouver, the gang easily finds Dr. Sosa\u2019s house and breaks in from the rear, making sure that nobody sees them or their vehicles. It\u2019s a nice house \u2014 and creepy as hell, because even though Dr. Sosa has supposedly lived here for years, there is no imprint of her personality. The interior is as impersonally pretty as a picture in <i>Architectural Digest</i>. The Scions hide and wait for Dr. Sosa to come home. Jack, however, decides to pose as a burglar for Dr. Sosa to discover: The gang is pretty sure she\u2019s a murderous medusa, but Jack wants to test her by her reaction to him (it\u2019d be bad to make a mistake and kill some innocent plastic surgeon).<br /> <br /> Doctor Sosa, a very attractive middle-aged woman, finally comes home. She has a bag of groceries in her hand. Jack, pillowcase of silverware in hand, tells her not to freak out, he doesn\u2019t want trouble. Sosa screams and drops her bag of groceries. As the Scions wonder if they\u2019ve made a mistake, she transforms into a scaled, clawed, snake-haired, hideous medusa and darts at Jack! Sosa hits him with the full force of her monstrosity, and Jack is paralyzed with terror. Before the others can act, she has grabbed Jack and holds her razor-sharp claws over his eyes. She snarls, \u201cI can smell you, Scions. Come out and surrender, or I take his eyes!\u201d<br /> <br /> Fortunately, the medusa doesn\u2019t know where everybody is. What\u2019s more, Gus can throw off the paralyzing terror through sheer force of egotism, Marshall is in another room using a mirror to aim his M-16 at her, Gene averts his eyes, and Jaeda is hiding behind the couch and behind Dr. Sosa\u2019s back. They manage to distract Dr. Sosa enough that she no longer has Jack\u2019s eyes covered. Gene lassoes Jack and pulls him to safety, while Gus grabs Dr. Sosa. This is possible only because Holly curses Sosa with the Evil Eye (\u201cYou should not cross a witch of the frozen wastes of the North!\u201d) This reduces Sosa\u2019s otherwise insanely high Dodge DV. (Sosa has three dots of Epic Dexterity. Uh-oh, that means she has higher Legend too\u2026)<br /> <br /> The gang pounds on Dr. Sosa a bit, to very little effect (she\u2019s very tough) but nevertheless enough to make her ask for parlay. (Even more than Gus threatening to rip her limb from limb, she seems to be unnerved when Marshall emerges to stick the muzzle of his rifle in her mouth and ask if she can bounce bullets from the inside, full-auto.) The Scions demand her supply of gorgon blood. She temporizes, saying it\u2019s back at her lab. Gene has a solution to this problem: He summons his great big raven Creature and gives it the instructions on how to retrieve the cursed blood.<br /> <br /> As the Scions debate options, they realize they can\u2019t let Dr. Sosa go. She is clearly an experienced medusa. She has killed many times before. She will undoubtedly kill again \u2014 they have no legal way to stop her. Sosa tries using Overt Order to make Gus let her go, but she has no escape from the group of Scions around her. She also tries resuming human form in hopes the Scions can\u2019t bring themselves to kill her. They can. Marshall makes good on his threat to shoot Sosa in the head, Gus starts ripping her apart, and Jaeda reaches into the wound in Sosa\u2019s abdomen and cuts out her heart. They call Laine into the room and manage to squirt the blood from the still-beating heart down her throat, without Laine fainting or throwing up. Within minutes, Laine fades from movie starlet-gorgeous to merely a rather pretty girl.<br /> <br /> (\u201cI\u2019ll always be beautiful to Derek,\u201d she rather unnervingly says. \u201cOh. Did I say that out loud?\u201d Well, being a titanspawn \u2014 even if not completely, and only for a short time \u2014 is bound to have some psychological effects.)<br /> <br /> That leaves a medusa corpse in a room with blood everywhere and bullets in the walls. Gus calls his crocodile Creature and feeds it the body, and the Scions clean up whatever other evidence they can think of. Doctor Sosa\u2019s house is still clearly the site of a messy homicide; they can only hope the Canadian cops can\u2019t trace it back to them. The Scions slip out the way they came, get in their cars and head back to Avalon.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Aftermath</u></b><br /> The adventure leaves Holly deeply bummed. They saved Laine from being a monster, but only by committing a brutal, cold-blooded murder. Gus tries consoling her with the thought that they are only at the beginning of their path to godhood. They\u2019ve found themselves in a dark new world with nasty creatures forcing nasty choices on them. When Holly comes into her power, though, \u201cthe frozen wastes of the North will melt at your command, and you can make a better world.\u201d Seems there\u2019s more to Gus than his tough-guy persona lets on.<br /> <br /> The Scions also acquired a few ampoules of genuine gorgon blood. It remains to be seen what they do with it, if anything. But, they also have unanswered questions \u2014 such as, how many other people did Dr. Sosa turn into medusas? They also still don\u2019t know who killed the girl at the cursed boulder, or why. They can only hope that inscrutable Fate will bring them answers in time.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T03:10:01-08:00","id":1011927,"title":"","content":"I vote they spike the dance's punch bowl with gorgon blood.<br /> <br /> Just for funsies!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T06:00:25-08:00","id":1011969,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1011927#post1011927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I vote they spike the dance's punch bowl with gorgon blood.<br /> <br /> Just for funsies!</div> </div> </div> </div>You do realize that this all already happened, right?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T09:08:39-08:00","id":1012032,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1011969#post1011969\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You do realize that this all already happened, right?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Wasn't thinking about that at the time of posting it. Came to mind immediately after, though.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T15:54:46-08:00","id":1012228,"title":"","content":"EPISODE THREE: HOMECOMING OF THE GODS<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Old Business, New Problems</u></b><br /> <br /> \\The Scions continue to settle into Avalon High and deal with the aftermath of the killing of Dr. Sosa.<br /> <br /> GENE is with the school\u2019s drama club. They debate what play to produce for Fall Quarter. This is the traditional season for \u201carty\u201d plays, since new members are at their least skilled and nobody comes to see the plays anyway. (Only the Spring Quarter musical gets significant audiences.) One student, Josh, suggests this kinda weird old play he found at the bottom of a trunk of costumes. Delia Bright suddenly marches in, grabs the slim, leather-bound playbook from his hand, swats Josh on the head with it and says, \u201cNo!\u201d Then, she marches out as abruptly as she came. Gene follows. He protests at Delia burning the play on the sidewalk, despite her insistence that she just saved Avalon High from a world of trouble. The playbook\u2019s covers survive the fire, and Gene \u2014 true son of Coyote that he is \u2014 decides that he <i>will </i>find another copy of <i>The King in Yellow.</i><br /> <br /> JACK is pulled from his Introduction to Ceramics class (better known to students as \u201cEasy A\u201d) by a school clerk who apologizes for the error in not putting him in the class Jack <i>really</i> wanted. Especially when his father wrote that nice letter asking for the class as well.<br /> <br /> The clerk leads Jack down a few halls and into another classroom, saying, \u201cHere he is, Doctor. Once again, we\u2019re sorry for the inconvenience.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cAnd so should you be,\u201d says the teacher. \u201cSit down\u2026\u201d (he consults a note) \u201cJack. Your last math teacher tells me you have a gift, if only you would apply yourself instead of\u2026 <i>coasting.</i> Well, in my class there shall be no\u2026 <i>coasting.</i> You will apply yourself. Or you will fail. <i>Ignominiously</i>.\u201d Welcome to AP Math with the formidable (and formidably eccentric) Dr. Demerit, who does not seem impressed with Jack\u2019s \u201cYes sir, you\u2019re absolutely right sir, I\u2019m looking forward to it sir\u201d ass-kissing routine.<br /> <br /> JAEDA overhears Bob telling a group of boys that Jaeda lured him to the party, came on to him and offered to trade sex for doing her homework \u2014 the complete opposite of what really happened. She calls him on it. Bob mutters that that\u2019s his story and he\u2019s sticking to it. (Likely to keep Alice from dumping him.) And anyway, Jaeda comes on to everyone male\u2026 Another boy tells Bob that trading schoolwork for sex was over-charging anyway, and Jaeda would probably settle for less. He pulls out a $20 and suggests that he and Jaeda do it after school. Whatever her self-esteem issues, Jaeda finds this sufficiently offensive that she punches the boy in the nose.<br /> <br /> Vice-Principal Stone moves in before matters can escalate further, and sends Jaeda to the Principal\u2019s office. Principal Flecker accepts Jaeda\u2019s version of events, but feebly protests that fighting in school is bad. Then he sends her to the school counselor. Lotus Ho (she\u2019s Chinese) tells Jaeda that while adolescence is a time to try out new identities and work out who you are, kids need to be careful not to make bad choices they can\u2019t take back. She then asks Jaeda to think hard about who and what she wants to be when she grows up. Of course, Jaeda can\u2019t say \u201cGoddess,\u201d so she says that she hadn\u2019t given it much thought, and didn\u2019t think she had many options beyond prostitution or porn. Ms. Ho firmly tells Jaeda that she has plenty of other choices if she\u2019s willing to work for them, and notes that Jaeda seems to do well in Mr. Oldfield\u2019s auto shop class. Ms. Ho also brushes aside Jaeda\u2019s doubts about the feasibility of girl mechanics, pointing to Barack Obama and the Chinese-American former governor of Washington/current Commerce Secretary Gary Locke as proof that old barriers and social stereotypes don\u2019t matter so much anymore.<br /> <br /> The speech doesn\u2019t entirely convince Jaeda. After school, though, she makes sure to intercept Bob in the school parking lot and give him a bloody nose too\u2026 and whammy him with Saltpeter in the Coffee. For a few days, Bob won\u2019t be doing it with Alice or anyone else.<br /> <br /> GUS feels pretty good about life and himself. How could he feel any other way, after enjoying brain-melting sex with the gorgeous Sheena Delisle? As they relax under a tastefully-draped sheet, Sheena says she\u2019s entirely satisfied as well. She also thinks that becoming Homecoming King and Queen will be just the start to an epic year, and they\u2019re gonna rule Avalon High. Sheena reports one worry, though: Her father wants to meet Gus. Not that he\u2019s an ogre or anything, it\u2019s just that she\u2019s happy with a relationship based entirely on sex. She fears to complicate it with anything else, and parents paying attention counts as complication. What if they have to hang out, do things together, develop common interests, etc.? Gus isn\u2019t worried, though. They clean up, get dressed and depart, Gus to football practice and Sheena to aerobics and other activities that Gus never sees.<br /> <br /> HOLLY finds a new acolyte. She sees a boy alone in the chemistry lab, mixing up chemicals. She finds that this is Maynard Salkind, junior pyrotechnician, making his own fireworks. (The chemistry lab has some chemicals he can\u2019t obtain on his own, for coloring the fireworks.) He\u2019s thinking of setting some off as an unsanctioned part of Homecoming celebrations. Holly thinks this is a fine idea.<br /> <br /> Holly has also decided to make Derek and Laine the Homecoming King and Queen. She lobbies relentlessly throughout the student body, presenting the two as the candidates for everyone who isn\u2019t in a \u201ccool\u201d clique. Later, she joins Laine in visiting Derek in the hospital. They wait their turn while another student, Alex, tells Derek about the latest swim team activities and his own weekend visit to Ocean Shores to swim in the Pacific. Then Alex realizes that Derek likely will not be on the swim team anymore and, awkwardly, makes things worse by saying how much everyone misses Derek and hopes he can join them on a team outing to Ocean Shores sometime soon, even if he can\u2019t\u2026 um. Once Alex leaves, Laine and Holly make their visit. Derek says the doctors say he can become an outpatient by the weekend. Holly suggests that he and Laine attend the Homecoming Dance that Friday. Derek\u2019s not enthusiastic.<br /> <br /> After the visit, Laine remembers that she asked her parents why they chose Dr. Sosa as her plastic surgeon. Turns out, her mother heard Sheena Delisle\u2019s mother say that Sheena had gone to Dr. Sosa for a few tweaks, as part of Sheena\u2019s ambition to become a supermodel. Mrs. Farber took that as a recommendation, and Dr. Sosa\u2019s prices turned out to be lower than the Farbers expected.<br /> <br /> So, Sheena Delisle was one of Dr. Sosa\u2019s patients.<br /> <br /> And over the summer, she went from \u201cbeautiful enough to appear in local TV commercials\u201d to \u201cbeautiful enough to turn heads in Hollywood.\u201d<br /> <br /> Oh, crap.<br /> -------------------<br /> Incidentally, one of my players actually had a teacher named Doctor Demerit. The real Doctor Demerit taught Advanced Latin at Boston Latin Academy, not math, but my friend says I captured his manner pretty well.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T15:56:29-08:00","id":1012229,"title":"","content":"EPISODE THREE: HOMECOMING OF THE GODS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Interlude: I\u2019m Sure He\u2019ll Thank Us, Really\u2026</u></b><br /> <br /> Gus told Jack and Gene about Sheena\u2019s trepidation about Gus meeting her father. When Holly passes on that Sheena was Dr. Sosa\u2019s client, the gang decides they need to learn more about Sheena\u2019s family. Did they know what Dr. Sosa was? Is Sheena trying to protect Gus?<br /> <br /> The two decide to handle the meeting for Gus. Jack easily collects a stray hair of Gus\u2019 for Gene to use as a focus for Stolen Face \u2014 and off they go to see George Delisle at his job (VP for Marketing at a large shipping/import company in Seattle). They easily talk their way in, since Mr. Delisle wants to meet his daughter\u2019s new boyfriend.<br /> <br /> They make a good impression. Mr. Delisle already knows about Gus as a local football star. They easily steer the conversation toward Sheena and her visit to Dr. Sosa. Mr. Delisle explains that Sheena wants a modeling career like her mother once had, but going beyond small-time local gigs. Dr. Sosa did a few tweaks to bring out her full beauty. It cost a bit, but nothing\u2019s too good for his princess. Sheena already appeared in a few local commercials, and Mr. Delisle thinks she could hit the big time.<br /> <br /> Mr. Delisle also suggests that \u201cGus\u201d might like to appear in a few local commercials too, or try for a summer internship. \u201cGus\u201d says he\u2019s interested, and Mr. Delisle offers to send him brochures. Gene agrees and gives Gus\u2019 home address.<br /> <br /> Jack and Gene are also somewhat unnerved, because Mr. Delisle is played by Ray Wise, with slick, diabolical cheer on full display. Delisle\u2019s explanation of his job in Marketing \u2014 \u201cI make people want things\u201d \u2014 comes out sounding just a bit sinister. The two Scionsleave Mr. Delisle\u2019s office filled with wild surmise.<br /> <br /> Back at Avalon, Gene and Jack tell Gus about the favor they\u2019ve done him. Gus doesn\u2019t see it that way. Whatever their motives, they <i>stole his identity,</i> and that is about the rudest challenge you can make to a Pesedjet. Jack gets off with a frown and a warning never to be party to such a thing again. To Gene, Gus snarls, \u201cAs for you\u2026 Run!\u201d and hits him with Game Face. Gene runs. As Gus tells Jack, this is him being forgiving and merciful. If Gene weren\u2019t more or less a friend, Gus would have fed him to his crocodile.<br /> <br /> Gus is also not exactly thrilled to find Holly campaigning against him and Sheena for Homecoming King and Queen \u2014 and with Gene\u2019s help. Holly soon lets Gus know, however, that he has much bigger problems than a trifling bit of high school celebrity.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Love Kills</u></b><br /> <br /> That still leaves the issue of whether Sheena is a medusa, and what connection (if any) she has to the murder at the Evil Boulder. Holly decides to make use of the smart kids she\u2019s cultivated as Followers. One of them, Bobby Kilmer (no connection to Bob the Lying Horndog) is the star of the school\u2019s computer club and robotics club. He says, sure, he can get Dr. Sosa\u2019s medical records along with the rest of the file from the Vancouver, BC police investigation. He just hacks into the FBI computer system and uses it as a false front for his request to the Vancouver PD. Sure enough, next day Bobby hands Holly a file of the investigation. Sheena was Dr. Sosa\u2019s patient exactly one month before Crystal, the girl from Etna High, was reported missing. Meanwhile, Holly talks to other Fashionistas and extracts accounts of Sheena\u2019s activities over the summer. The day before Crystal disappeared, Sheena phoned various friends and abruptly broke off planned events. No one saw her for two days.<br /> <br /> From there, the Scions easily reconstruct a possible sequence of events: Sheena gets her \u201coperation\u201d and achieves her current level of hotness. A month later, she turns into a monster. Somehow, she knew what to do to restore her beauty. She ambushed Crystal (likely a victim of opportunity), paralyzed her with fear, conked her on the head to knock her out, and dragged her to a secluded spot \u2014 the patch of brush around the Evil Boulder. There, she ate Crystal\u2019s eyes, killed her and buried the body. The killing activated the Evil Boulder to generate lindwurms, which skeletonized the corpse.<br /> <br /> But they aren\u2019t sure. Maybe Sheena\u2019s parents are monsters, or cultists, or <i>something,</i> and she\u2019s a victim of a more-than-usually-dysfunctional family. Maybe Mr. or the former Mrs. Delisle did the murder, or told Sheena to do it at the Evil Boulder.<br /> <br /> As the Scions ponder ways to fill in the blanks, and what to do about Sheena, Holly decides she should warn the other Scions. Marshall says he\u2019s in for any more medusa-killing. Barbara thanks Holly for the heads-up and promises to help with any cover-up. Holly doesn\u2019t tell Leo, who continues to avoid other Scions. Delia is outraged by the suggestion that a fellow Fashionista could be a murderous titanspawn. When Holly points out that medusas prefer the eyes of beautiful Scions, Delia icily says that she and Sheena are <i>Fashionistas,</i> and they stick together against the plain and the poor. <i>No matter what.</i> She further accuses Holly of trying to pull a jealous, evil trick \u2014 nothing less than trying to get Sheena killed to thin the competition for Homecoming Queen. Disgusted, Holly decides that Delia is beyond help and beneath contempt.<br /> <br /> Shortly thereafter, Holly gets a phone call from Laine. Laine says <i>she </i>just received a call from Sheena, saying, \u201cI know what you are and what you did to Derek. If you want to keep the police from knowing you maimed Derek, and thinking you killed the girl found in the woods, meet me at Holly\u2019s house at thus-and-such a time.\u201d And Laine found Crystal\u2019s ID card in her bedroom.<br /> <br /> At first the Scions think that Delia must have told Sheena, and this is Sheena\u2019s response. The timing seems awfully tight, though. It\u2019s more likely that this is Sheena\u2019s own plan, that just happens to come at the same time as the Scions\u2019 activities. But, it answers some questions and gives the Scions a chance to confront Sheena in circumstances of their choosing.<br /> <br /> Gus decides that if his girlfriend is to disappear, he needs an unbreakable alibi, and the only way to do this is for him to genuinely not be there at the confrontation. The Scions decide that he will protect Laine. They will drive around Avalon, be seen by people who know them both and by surveillance cameras, and (as the crowning touch) get a traffic ticket.<br /> <br /> Gene, on the other hand, will use Stolen Face to pose as Laine. Holly makes herself visible in an upstairs window at her rented house. Jaeda, Jack and Marshall take up hiding places near her, to ambush Sheena just as they did Dr. Sosa. Everyone carries hand mirrors.<br /> <br /> As \u201cLaine\u201d approaches Holly\u2019s house, Sheena slips out from some bushes and points a gun at her. She tells \u201cLaine\u201d to go into Holly\u2019s house. Holly answers \u201cLaine\u2019s\u201d hail by calling her to come upstairs. \u201cLaine\u201d and Sheena do so.<br /> <br /> Sheena says she knows there are Scions at Avalon High, and it wasn\u2019t hard to guess that Holly is one of them. Her eyes will keep Sheena beautiful for most of the school year. Laine will take the blame for it all, once she\u2019s dead. From hiding, Jack confirms (using Sense Tapu) that Sheena is in some way supernatural; the rest see it themselves as Sheena assumes monstrous medusa form. Battle is joined!<br /> <br /> Jack starts by throwing a kitchen knife to knock the gun from Sheena\u2019s hand. Sheena attacks Holly with her venomous medusa claws, but Holly dodges. Marshall shoots; Jaeda stabs. Their attacks are hindered by looking at Sheena only through mirrors. Sheena shows that she doesn\u2019t know the Scions undid the curse on Laine when she screams for Laine to help her, they can work together as fellow titanspawn! Gene does not disabuse her, which is probably why Sheena didn\u2019t run when she realized she was overmatched. Once Holly whammies Sheena with the Evil Eye, the Scions\u2019 victory becomes a foregone conclusion. Sheena can\u2019t touch them, but even without looking at her directly the Scions can still attack her. Holly coordinates the others so they can all attack Sheena at once: Jack throwing from the other room, Marshall shooting, Gene (still looking like Laine!) hooking his lasso to the ceiling fan to whip around and kick Sheena, and Jaeda doing the Limbo to avoid an attack from Sheena while stabbing up into her chest. Sheena dies in that second, with a bullet in her brain and her heart in Jaeda\u2019s hand. (Erm. Jaeda\u2019s getting <i>really good </i>at that\u2026 Must be an Atzl\u00e1nti thing.)<br /> <br /> After that, it\u2019s all over but cleaning up the mess. Bloody carpets are collected and burned. Marshall picks up his shell casings. They find that Sheen carried Crystal\u2019s wallet, watch and jewelry, evidently ready to plant on Laine. Later, Gus\u2019 crocodile disposes of the body. As far as the world shall know, Sheena Delisle simply disappears.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-04T15:59:47-08:00","id":1012233,"title":"","content":"EPISODE THREE: HOMECOMING OF THE GODS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Dansus Interruptus</u></b><br /> <br /> After the trauma of killing two medusas, the Scions are all ready for some fun. They have high hopes for the Homecoming Dance taking place that Friday.<br /> <br /> Several girls strongly hint to Jack that they\u2019d like him to invite them to the dance. Some angle for Gus, too. They don\u2019t seem to care what happened to Sheena, only that Gus seems to be available again. A few dozen boys ask Jaeda, but she decides this time, she\u2019ll give Marshall a chance: He\u2019s shown he\u2019s not a complete asshole.<br /> <br /> Holly tries to draw out Leo by asking him to the dance. She finds him in the gym after school, practicing with his mace. Leo\u2019s a lot more buff than Holly expected, and handles the mace well. He also seems to be instructed/hectored by his lion-demon tattoo, and not to be too happy with it. Leo turns Holly down. It\u2019s all lust and sin, and he\u2019s having none of it. Bobby Kilmer and Maynard Salkind ask Holly to the dance, but by then she can honestly say that some other boy (of higher status, and so better worth cultivating) asked her already.<br /> <br /> Gene already promised to be Reese Vetterson\u2019s date for the Etna High dance. He shows up with tuxedo, corsage, the whole hokey nine yards. Reese looks very nice, too. Once inside, of course, they run into Gary and the Sarsen twins, none of whom are pleased to see Reese on the arm of a Scion. (Yeah, Gary figured it out. Gene was with other Scions, and no mere mortal could have put on the oratorical display he did.) Reese, on the other hand, is none too pleased to have her big brother telling her who she can or cannot date.<br /> <br /> Gene decides the best way to avoid having three giants pound him to hamburger is to attract sufficient attention that the giants balk at making a scene. Anything worth doing is worth overdoing, so Gene asks Reese if she\u2019d like to be Homecoming Queen. The vote is about to take place: He and Reese jump up on stage and Gene presents them as last-minute candidates. All very irregular of course, but\u2026 Scion of Coyote. <i>Not </i>big on picky little rules. Like, Gene not being a student at Etna High. Nevertheless, he and Reese <i>both </i>turn on Center of Attention, and Gene hits the Etna High student body with the full force of his oratory (thank you, four dots in Expression!) In a few minutes, he has the crowd roaring its acclaim for the two as this year\u2019s Homecoming King and Queen.<br /> <br /> Gene also knows that minds will clear before long and people will realize that no one has actually seen him at Etna High. Gary and the Sarsens also still look mad enough to spit fire. After a few dances, therefore, he suggests to Reese that they depart to somewhere less fraught. Like, maybe, Avalon High. Maybe they can be Homecoming King and Queen at <i>both </i>schools! Reese thinks this sounds fun. Neither of them has a car, but look \u2014 there\u2019s a whole parking lot full of them! Even the Scion of Coyote balks at stealing Gary\u2019s beloved convertible (some things are truly unforgivable). Instead, they steal the Sarsen twins\u2019 pickup.<br /> <br /> They arrive too late for the election. Despite Holly\u2019s politicking for Laine and Derek, Delia and Warren win handily as the Homecoming royalty. Holly gets some satisfaction when Maynard\u2019s smuggled, thoroughly unsanctioned fireworks upstage the Scion of Apollo. Gene and Reese arrive to find the dance in full swing. Vice-Principal Stone stalks around as arch-chaperone, breaking up lascivious couples who try to go beyond what can be shown on network TV.<br /> <br /> Reese and Gene manage a few more dances before Gary and the Sarsens stalk into the Avalon High gym. Jaeda immediately tries intercepting Gary in hopes of preventing a fight (and seeing more of The Car, barely visible through the open gym door\u2026) Gus is ready to throw down and blow off some of his frustration at the week\u2019s events. The other Scions tense, expecting trouble.<br /> <br /> And then the lights go out.<br /> <br /> The large double-doors out of the gym crash down, squashing a student underneath.<br /> <br /> Dark, ominous music blasts out to replace the now-silenced DJ. (The main title music to <i>Omen III: The Final Conflict,</i> if you\u2019re interested.)<br /> <br /> And a huge, dark shape, four-rooted and furry, with eyes burning eerily in the faint light from outside the door, stalks in.<br /> <br /> <i>Hell </i>of an entrance. If a touch\u2026 theatrical.<br /> <br /> \u201cSCIONS!\u201d the creature growls. \u201cLearn the name of Bolvarg, heir of Fenris, for I am your doom! Not tonight do I seek your deaths, but other lives I hold in thrall. I know where you live, and whom you hold dear. If you would ransom them, give to me the growler\u2019s treasure, liquid purse-fire dropped from heaven! Come to me at midnight tomorrow, in the great hall of many merchants. In the steel steeds\u2019 pasture, give me what I seek. If you do not \u2014 THERE WILL BE BLOOD!\u201d And the creature backs out the gymnasium doors. The music ends abruptly with a click. Those near the fallen doors see a wolf the size of a Clydesdale pick up a CD player in its mouth and lope off\u2026 No, they don\u2019t. That would be insane. Later, the mortals say there was a <i>man </i>making incoherent threats (something about science?), accompanied by a big dog.<br /> <br /> First things first: The Scions manage to get enough people\u2019s heads together enough to call an ambulance for the student crushed under the doors. The Sarsens are still willing to fight, but Gary is sufficiently distracted by getting Reese home (this time, she does not protest) that he\u2019s willing to let pulping Gene slide for now. The dance breaks up. Delia is <i>furious </i>that her big night was spoiled.<br /> <br /> Some of the Scions are ready to chase Bolvarg right away (well, Marshall wants to get his guns first). Others hold off, thinking they should first figure out what the heck is going on. The resident Know-It-Alls (i.e., Holly and Jack) put their heads together to try deciphering Bolvarg\u2019s melodramatically cryptic speech.<br /> <br /> First, they recognize that Bolvarg used <i>kennings:</i> multi-layered metaphors common to Norse sagas. \u201cThe growler\u201d is the bear. \u201cPurse-fire\u201d is gold. Liquid gold is\u2026 um\u2026 honey? And honey could be a treasure for a bear. \u201cHoney dropped from heaven\u201d actually rings a bell with Holly: In the <i>Kalevala,</i> Lemminkainen\u2019s mother raises him from the dead by anointing him with honey obtained from God in Heaven. So, a big talking wolf threatens to kill the Scions\u2019 loved ones if they don\u2019t give it honey obtained from the Gods. Which they don\u2019t have.<br /> <br /> Holly decides she\u2019d better consult her Guide. The mysterious figure who speaks in her mirror says that Bolvarg is, as he said, a fenrir \u2014 the descendants of the Fenris Wolf from Norse mythology. Bolvarg has a record. Though he\u2019s known as a drama queen, he is also quite dangerous. Lately, he ravaged through the supernatural creatures of Finland, seeking <i>something</i>. Now, they know what: It\u2019s been rumored for a long time that the divine honey used to resurrect Lemminkainen wasn\u2019t used up, and some was left on Earth. The divine honey is also known as Amrit (the divine elixir of Hindu mythology) or Ambrosia (Greek). It is very potent. It must not fall into titanspawn hands, er, paws. Clearly, Bolvarg thinks someone is bringing it to the Scions \u2014 Holly, most likely.<br /> <br /> The Scions head home to sleep and plan their next move. Holly arrives home to find three small, humanoid creatures emerge from hiding under her porch. Two of them are about three feet high (and one carries a large handbag); the third is just two feet high. They are rotund, have large, round snouts, round eyes and short tails like those of a cow. \u201cThank the Gods we found you,\u201d the largest of them says. \u201cYou were the only person we could think of, and reach. Please \u2014 we need your help!\u201d Once inside, the creatures introduce themselves as Moomins. From her handbag, the other large Moomin pulls one of those bear-shaped plastic bottles of honey.<br /> <br /> \u201cThe honey from heaven,\u201d she says. \u201cDaughter of Louhi \u2014 we give it into your care.\u201d<br /> <br /> TO BE CONTINUED.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T09:29:37-08:00","id":1012623,"title":"","content":"<i>\u201cSCIONS!\u201d the creature growls. \u201cLearn the name of Bolvarg, heir of Fenris, for I am your doom! Not tonight do I seek your deaths, but other lives I hold in thrall. I know where you live, and whom you hold dear. If you would ransom them, give to me the growler\u2019s treasure, liquid purse-fire dropped from heaven! Come to me at midnight tomorrow, in the great hall of many merchants. In the steel steeds\u2019 pasture, give me what I seek. If you do not \u2014 THERE WILL BE BLOOD!\u201d</i><br /> <br /> <br /> <b>Me:</b> What the fuck are you talking about, dude!?<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/tongue.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Stick Out Tongue\" smilieid=\"5\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T12:40:26-08:00","id":1012720,"title":"","content":"Doctor Madelyn Sosa, of Vancouver, B.C. Everyone groans at the transparent pseudonym..<br /> <br /> <br /> Could someone explain the joke to me? i didn't get it:<br /> <br /> @DShomshak: I like this one when it came out years ago, i still like it now.<br /> <br /> PS: at the end can we have a pdf version of the story? also wasn't a second season of scion high planned?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2768-zorker","name":"Zorker","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T13:44:16-08:00","id":1012758,"title":"","content":"I'm liking the storytime here, its something nice to get me excited to play the game more. Thanks for sharing, Dean<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zorker</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1012720#post1012720\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Doctor Madelyn Sosa, of Vancouver, B.C. Everyone groans at the transparent pseudonym..<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>Madelyn Sosa -&gt; Maddy or Mads Sosa -&gt; Medusa","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12596,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12596-alpharius","user":"Alpharius","date":". 12-05-2016, 05:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12596-alpharius","name":"Alpharius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12596_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T15:02:54-08:00","id":1012803,"title":"","content":"Wow, what an awesome game! Can't wait for Scion 2E.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>My name is Colleen. My favorite song is &quot;Wildwood Flower.&quot; My ambition is to write the complete history of the White Wolf/Onyx Path universe.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11905-julianmoon","name":"JulianMoon","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"253"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T15:54:07-08:00","id":1012838,"title":"","content":"I suppose I could put together a .pdf of the entire saga to share around. Maybe I'll do that when I finish the repost. Oh, and for the .pdf I could include the picture og Gary Vetterson's car!<br /> <br /> While I considered the possibility of the Scion High &quot;TV show&quot; continuing for multiple seasons when I began it, by the end I was so knackered I was quite sure I would not continue it. While my players agreed it was the best campaign I ever ran, it was also the most work and the hardest -- some from wrestling with the Scion system, but more from juggling all the character subplots. The mythic side was easy; the high school side was more challenging! Anyway, I think it ended well enough that a second season probably would have jumped the shark.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1175,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","user":"DShomshak","date":". 12-05-2016, 07:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T15:54:49-08:00","id":1012839,"title":"","content":"EPISODE FOUR: I\u2019LL HUFF AND I\u2019LL PUFF\u2026<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Now What?</u></b><br /> <br /> This episode begins immediately after the end of \u201cHomecoming of the Gods.\u201d To recap: The homecoming dance breaks up in confusion after the fenrir Bolvarg\u2019s melodramatic entrance. EMTs come for the student that Bolvarg crushed when he broke down the gym door. Delia is furious and mortified at how events have ruined her big night. Holly is first to leave, intending to research more about the divine honey that seems to be the fenrir\u2019s goal. She finds a small family of moomins \u2014 Finnish house-trolls \u2014 waiting on (or rather, under) her doorstep. They give her the very item that Bolvarg seeks.<br /> <br /> As Episode Four opens, Holly thanks the moomins for bringing her the divine honey while wondering what she\u2019s supposed to do with it\u2026 and with a family of moomins that look at her expectantly with trusting eyes. She soon finds the moomins have no place to go: Bolvarg destroyed their home back in Finland, they\u2019ve been on the run ever since, and they don\u2019t know anyone in America. Sighing, she invites them to stay for a while until they can make other arrangements and gives them a spare bedroom.<br /> <br /> Back at Avalon High, Marshall is excited to see there are giants in the area. Jaeda is casual about it: She and the others knew about the Sarsens since the football game against the Etna Titans. (Marshall missed it. He was out looking for lindwurms.) The Vettersons, she says, are giants, too. Marshall wants to get his guns and go kill giants, but Jaeda points out that Bolvarg presents the more immediate problem. (Plus, the Vettersons and Sarsens don\u2019t seem that bad, as titanspawn go.) Gus, Jack and the other Scions agree. They also decide the problem can wait until morning. Chasing after a giant wolf at night, when only a few of them can see in the dark, strikes everyone as suicidally stupid. Instead, they agree to meet at Holly\u2019s for a council of war.<br /> <br /> Everyone comes to Holly\u2019s house bright and early. Even Leo answers the call, a bit to the surprise of the other Scions. The kids find a hearty breakfast waiting for them, courtesy of Moominmamma. (Holly gives her cook the day off.) Holly shows the rest the divine honey and fills them in about what she learned from her Guide.<br /> <br /> More than a thousand years ago, the mother of the demigod Lemminkainen used divine honey to resurrect her murdered son. Some of the honey was left over. It\u2019s kicked around the Finnish mythic world ever since. No one ever used its miraculous powers. Either they felt they didn\u2019t have the right to use something that belonged to the Gods, or they always thought something more important might come up later. After all, the divine honey can <i>raise the dead</i> and work many other miracles besides. The divine honey is also the Ambrosia of Greek mythology, the Amrit of Hindu myth, the Food of Life in Babylonian myth, and so on. It\u2019s rare and precious even by the standards of Gods. Just eating it will give anyone less potent than a God a significant, if temporary, power boost.<br /> <br /> The Scions then digress a bit, wondering why Bolvarg wants the honey, and what they should do with it. They suspect Bolvarg wants the power boost to obtain some other goal, but they decide that ultimately, Bolvarg\u2019s goals don\u2019t matter. After all, they absolutely will not give in to the fenrir\u2019s threats. If necessary, one of them eats the honey and uses the power boost to beat on Bolvarg! Most of them go along with the suggestion that if they can defeat the fenrir without using the honey, they should try using it to do some good instead of holding onto it for a \u201cjust in case\u201d that might never come. Most of the Scions think that restoring Derek Huckovich\u2019s eyes would be a good use for the honey.<br /> <br /> Delia and Barbara say they have minimal combat abilities, so they wouldn\u2019t be much use tracking and fighting Bolvarg. (Barbara admits that her father Shango gave her a pretty spiffy relic axe, but she knows zippo about fighting.) They will instead handle the inevitable cover-up, making sure mortals stay ignorant of things they are better off not knowing. Marshall, on the other hand, is stoked to go wolf-hunting. Leo agrees to come along, pushed by the lion-demon his father Nergal foisted on him. Leo\u2019s good with animals, sees in the dark, and has discovered supernatural prowess at tracking. Plus, he has a relic mace. The gang figures they have a pretty good chance of getting the drop on Bolvarg and fighting him on their terms.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshakj","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T15:56:21-08:00","id":1012842,"title":"","content":"EPISODE FOUR: I\u2019LL HUFF AND I\u2019LL PUFF\u2026 (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Wolf Hunt, Snipe Hunt</u></b><br /> <br /> Before the PCs set out (plus Marshall and Leo), they acquire some extra weaponry. Jack spent much of the previous night in the school\u2019s shop, grinding lengths of rebar into javelins. They might not be very good javelins (Jack doesn\u2019t have Craft: Metalwork, after all) but he figures that any lengths of pointy metal are better than nothing. The Scions also buy some cans of habanero pepper spray-gel: They figure it should work even better against Bolvarg\u2019s sensitive nose than against humans. As a final preparation, Jaeda calls her father and tells him to get the hell out of town for the day, as far away as possible and staying among crowds. She\u2019s the only Scion whose mortal parent knows about Gods, so she can warn him about supernatural threats in ways the others can\u2019t. (Though Barbara says she thinks she can get her folks out of town for the day, too.)<br /> <br /> Bolvarg\u2019s trail from the school gym leads into the woods east of Avalon. Bolvarg tried to hide his trail by splashing through a stream, making a long jump across a ravine, etc., but with both Gus and Leo using Predatory Focus, the troll-wolf has no chance of losing them.<br /> <br /> Still, the Scions stay on guard. As a result, when Bolvarg\u2019s trail leads through a narrow, muddy, weedy ravine whose sides are thick with brush and briars, more than one Scion thinks, \u201cHuh. Good place for a trap.\u201d And indeed, both Gus and Leo spot the fine tripwire running through the weeds. Simultaneously, they grab Marshall\u2019s arms and hold him back. A closer inspection leads them from the wire to the hand grenades hidden among the bushes.<br /> <br /> Ulp. Bolvarg may be clever monster, but there\u2019s no way he set a trap like <i>that.</i> The Scions realize they are up against more than one foe.<br /> <br /> From a distance they hope is safe, Jack throws a rock to trigger the trap. (The Scions don\u2019t want to leave something so lethal, when mortal kids or hikers could run into it.) Then they return to following Bolvarg\u2019s trail\u2026 but more slowly, taking greater care.<br /> <br /> About a mile further, the trail leads to a pass between two hills or ridges. There\u2019s a gap about 50 yards wide between the hills, with steep slopes covered in brambles, weeds and small trees and shrubs. An old railroad bridge crosses the gap from hill to hill. (Actually, it looks like someone cut out a section of a long, winding hill decades ago, maybe for a road or railway line that was abandoned or never built, and then bridged the cut for a different rail line.) Dirt and rocks cover the floor of the gap \u2014 fresh dirt from the large cave dug into one hillside. Bolvarg\u2019s tracks, and those of many people, are clearly visible in the dirt.<br /> <br /> Out from the cave come six of the Scions\u2019 fellow students from Avalon High. Jaeda recognizes Bob, her horndog semi-nemesis. Other Scions recognize students Adam, Clay, Davis, Elsie and Flicka. They\u2019re some of the larger and healthier boys and girls at Avalon High but nobody special. Though, they all look somewhat larger than before, and a whole lot stronger. And meaner, though that might be because they all carry machetes. The Scions realize that Bolvarg must have turned their classmates into thralls! (Or he has a giant accomplice who did this: At the moment, they aren\u2019t sure whether fenrir can create thralls.)<br /> <br /> The six thralls announce that their master Bolvarg told them to kill anyone who came looking for him. Bob further taunts Jaeda, suggesting that maybe she should reconsider his proposition, now that he\u2019s so strong. He\u2019s such a buff stud he can do better than Alice, and if Jaeda\u2019s <i>real nice </i>to him, maybe he\u2019ll give her a second chance\u2026 Jaeda feels even more disgusted than the other Scions.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s not much point in trying to negotiate with thralls, so battle is joined. The Scions hold back somewhat: Sharp-eyed characters spot the camera phone wedged in a bush, watching the fight. Jack leads off by knocking down the camera with a thrown rock.<br /> <br /> Jack then charges another convenient cobble-sized rock with Warrior\u2019s Mana and chucks it at one of the thralls. For his stunt, he tries throwing it so it bounces off one kid\u2019s head and smacks another, for Rockem Sockem Pinball Thralls. It works, injuring two of the thralls (though not much). Holly decides her best use in the fight is to activate Lightning Sprinter to zip forward and retrieve the Legend-charged rock so Jack can use it again.<br /> <br /> The other Scions close for a thrall brawl. Leo smiles as the thralls charge him with their machetes, and says, \u201cGo on, hurt me. It\u2019s my practice for Hell.\u201d<br /> <br /> The thralls are strong, but not very skilled. They have very little chance to hit anyone. The thralls try to surround a Scion so one of them can attack from behind, but the Scions separate them and defeat them too quickly. Gus\u2019 attempt to slam two thralls\u2019 heads together doesn\u2019t work very well, but it hardly matters. Jaeda starts by activating Lightning Sprinter, racing forward and kicking Horndog Bob in his previously broken nose. She follows this by kicking Bob in the nuts, which takes the fight out of Bob, and Bob out of the fight. Jaeda moves on to Davis. She lifts her shirt to flash the thrall. His Rapacity kicks in (on top of adolescent male hormones): As Davis grabs for her in the best anime-boy fashion, Jaeda puts him down with a single kick to the groin (<i>very</i> good damage roll; Davis might be singing soprano for life). Marshall has the presence of mind to use his rifle as a club, instead of shooting kids. For the first time, the other Scions see Leo in combat as he swings his mace at an already-wounded thrall and nearly pulps the unfortunate kid (Clay, IIRC). The other thralls are defeated with similar speed, and most of them are pushed into lethal damage.<br /> <br /> And inside the cave? The Scions find it empty, except for another fine tripwire at a bend in the short tunnel. Jack triggers it with another thrown rock. Instead of an explosion, a sign pops out of the dirt, saying: BOOM. And below that, Scared Yet? One or two Scions aver that yes, they are \u2014 not from the physical threats they\u2019ve faced so far, but from the way Bolvarg is toying with them.<br /> <br /> More importantly, though, the student thrall that Leo struck is dying. The others with any medical skill (mostly Jaeda) help him as best he can in trying to stabilize the crushed kid. Leo expends Legend and channels his Piety for a legendary deed while praying for the student not to die on account of Bolvarg\u2019s evil and Leo\u2019s own sin of wrath, and just barely saves the kid\u2019s life.<br /> <br /> The Scions rouse one of the unconscious thralls to interrogate (Elsie, one of the less wounded). Gene pretends that this was all a test from Bolvarg, and they failed. Elsie blubbers with shame, but the Scions manage to extract the information that Bolvarg grabbed them with the help of two human accomplices, and fed them his blood to make them strong and tough. Bolvarg is wonderful, she\u2019d do anything for Bolvarg and now she\u2019s disappointed him, boo hoo\u2026<br /> <br /> Gus looks for Bolvarg\u2019s trail departing the den. He doesn\u2019t find it: The fenrir apparently has some supernatural means of making himself impossible to track. Gus does find some men\u2019s tracks, though, leaving the site as well as arriving.<br /> <br /> So, Bolvarg led them to the den as another trap. But why? The fenrir had to know that a group of six student thralls would be no match for a comparable number of Scions. Thinking further, they suspect Bolvarg is dicking with them on more than one level. First, he warned them that other people would pay a price for the Scions\u2019 defiance. Second, he maneuvered them into inflicting grievous harm on a group of kids (even using bashing damage, the wound tracks circled \u2018round on most of the kids), nearly killing some of them, and <i>how do they explain this?</i> And\u2026 while they hunted Bolvarg, the fenrir has been free to do Gods know what.<br /> <br /> Leo is adamant that Clay and the other thralls absolutely must get to a hospital. The Scions call Delia and Barbara, who arrange for a medevac chopper to collect the wounded thralls. Leo stays at the den to protect the kids and ride with them back to the hospital. He is out of the hunt for Bolvarg, saying he\u2019s done enough damage already. He gives the other Scions his relic mace, though, in case they need it (and so the cops can\u2019t match it to wounds).<br /> <br /> The Scions followed the tracks of Bolvarg\u2019s henchmen to an old logging road, where they end at tire tracks: The two men and the fenrir got into a truck that drove away. The logging road leads to a rural road, which leads in time to a highway. Predatory Focus won\u2019t trace a truck through that much traffic.<br /> <br /> Along the way, the Scions call their parents and check online news feeds. Most of their mortal parents are okay \u2014 but Jack and Gene can\u2019t raise their mothers. Instead, Gene gets a cop, who tells him his mother was kidnapped shortly before. She\u2019s moderately well known, so people noticed when armed men bashed down the door of the Blackforest home and dragged her out. Jack\u2019s mother, they soon find, was similarly kidnapped. The wolf den wasn\u2019t just Bolvarg dicking with the Scions: It was a distraction.<br /> <br /> [<b>NOTE:</b> In retrospect\u2026 Why didn\u2019t the cops try calling Gene as soon as they learned about the abduction? I apologize for the hole in the plot, and the following necessary retcon: The Scions were in fact outside regular cell coverage. The camera phone in the bush, however, was a <i>satellite </i>phone. I would also accept that Holly has fully tricked-out satphone as well. Thus, the PCs could call out to Barbara, but the cops couldn\u2019t reach Gene or Jack until the Band got closer to town.]<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T15:57:29-08:00","id":1012844,"title":"","content":"EPISODE FOUR: I\u2019LL HUFF AND I\u2019LL PUFF\u2026 (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Yo Momma!</u></b><br /> <br /> Holly decides she\u2019s fed up with tracking Bolvarg on foot. It\u2019s time to bring out divine magic and, by the way, modern transportation. Jaeda easily hitches a ride with a middle-aged man in an SUV, who\u2019s a bit disappointed to find that the hot babe he picked up has a bunch of teenage friends who pile in after her. But, he\u2019s bedazzled enough by Epic Socials to drive the Scions wherever they want to go.<br /> <br /> Holly pulls out her Mirror of True Seeing and invokes Ariadne\u2019s Thread. For the next half-hour, she guides the driver in following Bolvarg\u2019s trail. When the trail splits, though, the Scions figure that Bolvarg is just tooling around, killing time and confusing his trail. (Bolvarg went down the same highway in opposite directions, creating an intersection in his trail.)<br /> <br /> The Scions check the phone they took from the den and find that, as they expected, the fenrir left a number. Maybe Bolvarg\u2019s henchmen keep a party line speed-dial; maybe the troll-wolf meant them to call. Either way, they phone Bolvarg.<br /> <br /> The fenrir answers the phone. He confirms that he\u2019s holding two of the Scions\u2019 mortal mothers for ransom: Their mothers aren\u2019t harmed yet\u2026 much\u2026 but if Bolvarg does not receive the honey and make a call to another of his thralls, the two women will die. The fenrir admits he took a chance. After all, maybe the Scions don\u2019t care about mortal kin since they learned of their divine parentage. The Scions sneer at Bolvarg for setting up a gotcha-last plan in case of his death, as it suggests a lack of confidence in his prowess. The fenrir responds with insults of his own, and the Scions cut the call.<br /> <br /> The Scions agree that tracking Bolvarg is pointless, and indeed unnecessary: His gotcha-last plan shows that he doesn\u2019t have Jack and Gene\u2019s mothers with him. Holly sighs, spends the Legend and invokes Ariadne\u2019s Thread again, to lead them (and their increasingly alarmed driver) to Ms. Blackforest. The mystic trail leads promptly to one of the trailer parks in Hooverdale. In fact, it\u2019s not far from where Jaeda lives. The Scions dismiss the relieved driver, and follow the trail to a specific house-trailer. Gene calls his raven, which spies through the windowsHoove. Sure enough, one room of the trailer holds the two women, unconscious, and a hard-bitten Nordic man in military gear. The thrall has a shotgun at the ready, a sword and various small arms.<br /> <br /> The Scions guess that the thrall might have orders to shoot the two women at the first sign of an attack. They must plan their attack so he doesn\u2019t have a chance. They also notice a lot of witnesses/injurable bystanders, such as children playing nearby. Jack fiddles with his smartphone a bit and finds an ice cream truck melody, hoping it will lure away at least some of the kids when the time comes. Mostly, though, the Scions decide to rely on swift and blinding violence.<br /> <br /> Gene goes and hotwires a truck while the other Scions move into position. At the signal, Gus charges through the wall of the trailer (he rips the aluminum wall like it was paper), grabs the two women and dives through the opposite wall, to deposit them in the truck. As Nils the thrall turns to face Gus, Jack and Marshall charge in through the hole Gus made and engage the thrall. Jaeda soon follows, and Gus returns as quickly as he can.<br /> <br /> The Scions then notice the bottles of gasoline scattered about the room, some of them knocked over by Gus\u2019 passage, at least one poised over a little Sterno lamp. In seconds, the trailer is ablaze. In between shooting at the thrall, Marshall says, \u201cIsn\u2019t there a Klingon proverb about fools fighting in a burning house?\u201d \u2014 but <i>he </i>won\u2019t be the first out the door. (Gus and Jack file the datum that Marshall might be a Trekkie for future blackmail or embarrassment.) Jaeda resolves the issue by flipping in, clinching the thrall\u2019s head with her legs, and flipping him out of the trailer. The thrall puts up a good fight (he\u2019s tough, skilled and has a bulletproof vest) but has no real chance: The Scions defeat him in seconds. In the process, Jaeda kicks Nils in the nuts. The male Scions uneasily see a pattern developing. Nils defeated and unconscious, the Scions pile him and themselves into the truck and make their getaway.<br /> <br /> Once again, the Scions call Bolvarg. They let him know his blackmail plan failed. What\u2019s more, they\u2019re through reacting to his cowardly ploys. They want to get this over with. They challenge the fenrir to meet them in battle in one hour and <i>take </i>the honey from heaven \u2014 because if he doesn\u2019t, one hour and five minutes from now, one of them will scarf it down. After a moment\u2019s thought, they decide the school athletics field makes a nice, private yet open venue for a battle, and they tell Bolvarg to be there or lose the divine honey forever. CLICK.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T15:58:35-08:00","id":1012845,"title":"","content":"EPISODE FOUR: I\u2019LL HUFF AND I\u2019LL PUFF\u2026 (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Sweet Victory</u></b><br /> <br /> Less than an hour later, the Scions are at the athletics field. They chase away some kids hanging out in the bleachers. Marshall spots the places where a sniper could shoot at them and makes sure they are not occupied. The other Scions look for other dirty tricks as best they can. They don\u2019t find any.<br /> <br /> As a final preparation, every Scion carries a bear-shaped squeeze bottle of honey \u2014 all but Gene, who carries the actual ambrosia. Exactly why they trust him with it is not entirely clear. But then, this happens a lot with Coyote and his kin.<br /> <br /> Bolvarg shows up on time. A panel truck drives onto the field and stops about 50 yards from the Band. The driver, Lars, gets out. He is another Nordic military man. The Scions note with some concern that he carries grenades as well as a shotgun. He covers the door as a third Nordic soldier (Ole) gets out, rifle at the ready, to cover the exit of Bolvarg himself. In the light of day, the Scions can see the fenrir wears an ornate golden torc. The two thralls spread out a bit from Bolvarg, but they keep their weapons ready.<br /> <br /> Bolvarg gives the Scions a final chance to give him the honey from heaven. They jeer and join battle\u2026 and Bolvarg starts his boom box to playing \u201cFinal Countdown.\u201d<br /> <br /> Some of the Scions take a while to reach the battle, but everyone gets their moment of spectacle. There were too many stunts to list (or remember \u2014 it was a stunt-heavy combat, enabling the characters to spend Legend, Willpower or both on almost every attack.)<br /> <br /> \u2022 Lars starts by throwing hand grenades at the advancing Scions, but he cannot score a direct hit. Indeed, Jack deflects one grenade with a thrown rock so it bounces back at Lars. Jack doesn\u2019t score a direct hit, either, and the tough, armored thrall is barely hurt by the blast. Jaeda races past him, though, and she manages to pull a pin on one of his grenades while yelling, \u201cYoink!\u201d Lars goes boom.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Holly, of course, tries using the Evil Eye on Bolvarg. When she does so, the torc briefly glows. Holly guesses that the torc cancelled her spell. (It did, but only in part: Instead of \u20135 dice on subsequent actions, Bolvarg was penalized only 2 dice.) So, she waits and looks for a chance to coordinate.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Ole the rifleman sees Holly trying to curse Bolvarg, and he takes a shot at her\u2026 successfully. Fortunately, guns do suck damage against Scions, so Holly is not badly hurt. Marshall spends much of the fight shooting at Ole, whittling him down.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Gene, a true Scion of Coyote, decides to keep Bolvarg occupied by annoying him. He races forward using Lightning Sprinter, throws his lasso\u2019s loop into the fenrir\u2019s mouth like a bit, and swings onto Bolvarg\u2019s back, whooping, \u201cGiddy-up!\u201d Bolvarg slams Gene against the truck, injuring him a fair bit, but at least the fenrir can\u2019t bite him and is too pissed to go after the other Scions. As the battle progresses, Gene decides that staying on Bolvarg is not the brightest thing he could do. He rolls forward, wrapping the indestructible Relic lass around Bolvarg\u2019s muzzle as he does so, leaving the troll-wolf briefly unable to bite anyone.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Gus uses the opportunity to try grappling Bolvarg. He finds the fenrir is as strong as he is and more dexterous. He can\u2019t hold Bolvarg for long, and this makes him the fenrir\u2019s preferred target once Bolvarg frees his muzzle from the rope. Bolvarg manages to bite Gus twice, but the first time scrapes off the Scion of Sobek\u2019s hide (I rolled super-suck damage for Bolvarg) and the second time was just a nip. Gus mocked the fenrir both times. On the other hand, Gus managed to punch Bolvarg in the face while using one of the pepper spray cans as a fist-load, crushing it in the process, and Bolvarg didn\u2019t like that one bit. Gus also managed to rip away the torc.<br /> <br /> \u2022 Jack uses his homemade javelins against Ole. Jaeda moves from the scattered chunks of Lars to Ole and kicks him in the nuts. Marshall shoots from a distance. Ole goes down.<br /> <br /> \u2022 With Lars and Ole gone and everyone closing on Bolvarg, Holly gets her chance to coordinate an attack. Bolvarg had already taken a number of wounds. Now everyone lays into him at once, going all-out with stunts, Legendary Deeds and Willpower channeled through Virtues. The results are spectacular. Gus clinches Bolvarg again and does a \u201cVidar,\u201d wrenching open the fenrir\u2019s jaws so he can\u2019t bite anyone. Jaeda runs straight at Bolvarg, drops to slide under him, and uses the First Flower to gut him from below. Marshall follows, diving to thrust his M-16 (and hands, and head) between the fenrir\u2019s jaws to empty the clip down the fenrir\u2019s throat. This is quite enough to kill Bolvarg, but right as Jaeda and Marshall scramble away, Gene runs over the dying fenrir with the beast\u2019s own truck.<br /> <br /> By now, the Scions know a bit more about titanspawn in general, giants in particular, and fenrir in special particular. Jaeda and Gus skin the fenrir (Jaeda wants to cure the pelt and use it as a bedspread), but first Jaeda cuts out the heart and drains the fenrir\u2019s eitr into some convenient emptied pop bottles. (The others think this trophy is yucky, but we\u2019ll see if anyone is willing to use it.) Gus, Jack and Marshall wrench out the fenrir\u2019s teeth. Gus thinks he\u2019d like to make them into a set of brass knuckles, to make his punches Piercing. After that, the Scions dispose of what\u2019s left of Bolvarg\u2019s corpse.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Aftermath</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions are left with many strange and violent events to explain away. Barbara starts the process with the story she and Delia cook up to explain the six injured students out in the woods, who babble about serving a giant talking wolf. The two Scions plant the notion that the kids were obviously kidnapped by a drug gang and subjected to some sort of experimental designer drug hallucinogen that left them raving mad. Then a rival gang beat them up, or maybe there was a falling-out among gang members. The other Scions think this sounds pretty good. The six young thralls will spend varying times in the hospital, and more time likely in a mental institution, but they remain a loose end. Thralldom doesn\u2019t wear off.<br /> <br /> It helps that Nils, Lars and Ole all racked up long and violent criminal records during their thralldom to Bolvarg. Everything can be pinned on them, and Lars at least is not going to disagree. The Scions are not willing to execute the other two thralls in cold blood, but Nils and Ole apparently keep clam.<br /> <br /> (Barbara further reports, however, that Leo wanted to take the blame for beating up the six thrall-kids, out of guilt for nearly killing one of them. Delia blew her top at this plan and gave Leo an amazing tongue-lashing. \u201cLittle Miss Prettyboots has a mouth like a Marine drill sergeant. Who knew?\u201d Oddly, Leo seemed to like the verbal assault, even as he crumpled.)<br /> <br /> The Scions then call in Laine and Derek. They can see that Laine is a little apprehensive, but she doesn\u2019t try to stop them from restoring Derek\u2019s eyes. Holly is a little put out to learn from her Guide that her Epic Intelligence won\u2019t help her use the ambrosia: It all depends on medical skill and raw divine force. (I.e., the roll is Legend + Medicine.) This is not great for any of the Scions, but Jaeda turns out to have the best chance. She feeds most of the honey to Derek, anoints his eye sockets with the rest, and bleeds on him a bit for good measure. (It\u2019s an Atzl\u00e1nti thing \u2014 her way to channel her Loyalty and spend Legend for a Legendary Deed.) It works, and Derek\u2019s eyes are miraculously restored.<br /> <br /> Which leaves the Scions with another strange event to explain away. Once again, Barbara has a suggestion: Create a story that the earlier report of Derek\u2019s eyes being ripped from his head was media exaggeration, and that actually his eyes were merely injured. Change hospital records to match. The doctors who attended Derek will know better, but who will believe them? Derek\u2019s parents will know better, but will they argue with a miracle? Not if the social Scions persuade them that they don\u2019t want to be the focus of a media circus. This sounds good to Derek, but it\u2019s up to the other Scions to decide whether to back the plan. (Note: They did.)<br /> <br /> Also: Much to Laine\u2019s relief, Derek seems to find her as beautiful as before. The Scions still haven\u2019t said that Laine was the monster who maimed him in the first place. And Holly\u2019s Guide commends the Scions on finding a good use for the honey from heaven, after it has caused so much trouble over the centuries.<br /> <br /> On a not-so-warm and fuzzy note, Marshall stakes a claim to the Trophies from Bolvarg. He argues that since he and Jaeda dealt the most damage and were the ones who actually killed the monster, they should receive the Trophies and disburse them as they see fit. It is up to the PCs to decide how to respond to this claim.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-05T22:10:46-08:00","id":1013004,"title":"","content":"Bolvarg just wanted something besides jelly to put on his peanut butter sandwiches! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-06T15:30:12-08:00","id":1013430,"title":"","content":"Incidentally, the &quot;TV show&quot; of Scion High has theme music to run with the credits at the start of each episode: &quot;Icarus Dream Fanfare,&quot; from Yngwie J. Malmsteen's Concerto Suite for Electric Guitar and Orchestra. Me big tech dummy so I'm afraid I can't post a link, but I think it captures the feel of Scion well. Theme stated first by bombastic full Wagnerian orchestra, then repeated by modern electric guitar; orchestra and guitar then conduct a dialogue through the piece.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-07T05:30:12-08:00","id":1013696,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UIN1BHbkpXA\" target=\"_blank\">Icarus Dream Fanfare</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-07T15:30:55-08:00","id":1014007,"title":"","content":"Ah, thank you, Resplendent Fire!<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-07T15:32:43-08:00","id":1014010,"title":"","content":"Thanks for posting your chronicle! I haven't read it all yet but it looks pretty cool.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2367-resplendent-fire","name":"Resplendent Fire","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2367_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1887"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-07T15:34:52-08:00","id":1014014,"title":"","content":"Gene's player was not available for the next adventure. So, I made that the center of the adventure. I'd already read the &quot;Coyote Stories&quot; section from a book on Pacific Northwest native mythology, so that was convenient as well. No specific myths were referenced, just a general style of trickster tale.<br /> <br /> EPISODE FIVE: COYOTE STORIES<br /> <br /> <b><u>Old Business</u></b><br /> <br /> After the battle with Bolvarg, Marshall made himself annoying by questioning the division of the spoils. He argues that he and Jaeda should receive all the fenrir\u2019s trophies (including the enchanted torc) to distribute as they choose, because they struck the killing blows. The other Scions aren\u2019t too keen on his high-handed attitude, but they reluctantly agree they need some system to decide who gets loot from their adventures. They agree that if one person clearly defeated a particular foe, that Scion gets the enemy\u2019s stuff. Where a trophy is highly divisible, the people involved in the victory get roughly equal shares, if they want them. Most of the Scions also point out that by Marshall\u2019s reasoning, Holly should receive the trophies because they couldn\u2019t have slain Bolvarg without her coordinating their attacks. Holly, however, doesn\u2019t want much.<br /> <br /> The Scions have nine doses of Bolvarg\u2019s heart\u2019s-blood. Some of the Scions don\u2019t want any of it (they don\u2019t see much use for an exceptionally yucky Berserker Potion). Jaeda and Marshal get two doses (they inflicted a lot of damage, it\u2019s blood, they\u2019re Atzl\u00e1nti, it fits their idiom). Gene gets two doses, since he was the first to engage Bolvarg. Holly, Jack and Gus take one dose each.<br /> <br /> Gus ripped the torc off Bolvarg, so he holds it for now.<br /> <br /> Gus also receives Bolvarg\u2019s fangs. While he has visions of gloves with ripping claws, a la Wolverine, he finds that isn\u2019t practical. He can, however, fit the fangs into a set of brass knuckles that give his punches +2 damage and make damage Piercing (against all soak, because they\u2019re a relic weapon).<br /> <br /> Jaeda claims Bolvarg\u2019s pelt to turn into a rug, or maybe a bedspread. Nobody argues.<br /> <br /> Marshall clearly wanted to get more. The socially perceptive Scions can tell that he feels uncomfortable and under-appreciated as part of the nascent Band. He expected to lead because of his parentage and pre-Visitation combat training, and he isn\u2019t. On the other hand, he doesn\u2019t have an established leader to follow, either. He also hasn\u2019t been the clearly superior fighter that he thought he\u2019d be. (Guns just aren\u2019t so useful against the supernatural.) Atzl\u00e1nti warriors expected to receive promotions and honors for their victories. Plus, well, he\u2019s a jerk. But he accepts the settlement of Bolvarg\u2019s trophies.<br /> <br /> In other matters, Holly works on acquiring a kevlar trench coat (chic <i>and</i> bulletproof!) and a Wild Kingdom-issue trank pistol. She also builds a dossier on Leo and his mortal parents. As expected, they are very conservative members of the very evangelical and very political local megachurch, the Glory of God Ministry. (It\u2019s the sort of place that shows movies of American fighter jets during the church service.) Mr. Lefebvre doesn\u2019t just put a fish symbol in his Yellow Pages ad, he advertises specifically as a \u201cChristian Electrician.\u201d Poor Leo.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Family Matters</u></b><br /> <br /> A few weeks have passed since the events of \u201cI\u2019ll Huff And I\u2019ll Puff\u2026\u201d The Scions have planted the story that Derek\u2019s eyes were <i>wounded, </i>not removed. They have changed hospital records and convinced many key people to doubt their own memories. Derek, his parents and Laine know better, but they are all grateful for the miracle. Derek rejoins the swim team; he and Laine are still dating and seem very much in love. Meanwhile, the six students whom Bolvarg turned into thralls undergo therapy at a secure facility.<br /> <br /> The Scions feel pretty good about themselves. So, naturally it\u2019s time for something bad to happen. Their parents come to town \u2014 all on the same day. At first, this doesn\u2019t seem like it will spoil their Saturday morning\u2026<br /> <br /> GUS gets a call from his dad Sobek, saying he\u2019s passing through the area on the way to a job and to meet him at the liquor store in Etna. Sobek quizzes Gus about school, friends, how he\u2019s adjusting to being a Scion, and progress in forming a Band. He also asks Gus if he wants something from the store: Gus asks for two of the strongest-tasting liquors (Jalape\u00f1o Vodka and Garlic Schnapps, or something like that). They continue chatting in the parking lot.<br /> <br /> Gary Vetterson cruises by in his convertible. He spots Gus and does a fast turn into the parking lot and accosts the Scion. He demands to know where Gene is, or where Reese is, that he still owes the Scion of Coyote a beat-down for the events of Homecoming, and he is <i>not </i>letting some filthy Scion make time with his sister!<br /> <br /> Gus plays it cool. He swears he doesn\u2019t know Gene\u2019s or Reese\u2019s location, and he wouldn\u2019t protect Gene if he did. He then offers Gary a swig from his bottles \u2014 both at once, of course, if he\u2019s a <i>real </i>man. Gary accepts the challenge, takes a long pull from both at once, and smiles. He tells Gus that he ought to try drinking something <i>strong,</i> not this Kool-Aid. Which means Gus has to drink them together too. The supernatural jocks part, having established their mutual machismo.<br /> <br /> As Gary drives away, Sobek wants more information on Gary. He didn\u2019t see Gary as a giant. He\u2019s never heard of giants hiding their nature from Gods that well, but he promises to ask Thoth about it. Sobek also asks why Gary isn\u2019t dead. Gus says that for a giant, Gary and Reese aren\u2019t bad. Finally, Sobek asks if Gus would take occasional small jobs for the pantheon if anything should turn up in the region. Gus says he\u2019d be glad to. Sobek drives off to do wetwork in Russia. Gus thinks a moment and decides he\u2019d better tell the others that his divine parent was in town. Oh, and Gary\u2019s on the war-path over Gene and Reese.<br /> <br /> JAEDA gets a surprise visitor when her mother Tlazolt\u00e9otl walks into the trailer Jaeda shares with her father, Mateo. Who is stunned. Tlazolt\u00e9otl ejects Mateo for this mother-daughter talk. Her gone-to-seed former lover now clearly disgusts her.<br /> <br /> Tlazolt\u00e9otl quizzes Jaeda about school, friends, how she\u2019s adjusting to being a Scion, and progress in forming a Band. They are briefly interrupted by a phone call from Grace, one of the kids in Drama Club with Gene. Grace says Gene didn\u2019t show up for a read-through of a play and asks if Jaeda knows where he is? Jaeda does not.<br /> <br /> They are interrupted again when they hear Mateo say, \u201cHey, you can\u2019t go in \u2014\u201c SMACK, and a bump against the side of the trailer. And then Nergal walks in. He wants Jaeda to bed his son Leo, and offers to pay. Tlazolt\u00e9otl, however, tells Jaeda never to let a man think he can <i>buy and own </i>her. (A man paying for the privilege of sex is another matter.) Nergal sneers and says that\u2019s rich, coming from her. Turning back to Jaeda, he says that if she doesn\u2019t want money, he can pay in other ways \u2014 relics, slaves, etc.<br /> <br /> Tlazolt\u00e9otl does not like being brushed off \u2014 and when Tlazolt\u00e9otl gets mad, she gets sexy. <i>Really</i> sexy. She turns her Epic Appearance up full-blast and aims it at Nergal. His eyes flame with lust, and he reaches for her. (Jaeda finds herself thinking impure thoughts about her own mother, just from the sidesplash.) Tlazolt\u00e9otl says that this time, <i>she </i>didn\u2019t tamp down her divinity. She pouts prettily at Nergal, strokes his face and coos, \u201cOh, go down for me, honey,\u201d and pushes. The Lord of Lions folds like a cheap suit until he lies flat on his back on the floor. Tlazolt\u00e9otl puts her foot on his face with her spike heel in his mouth. \u201c<i>This </i>is how you deal with men like him,\u201d she tells Jaeda, as she dials down her Epic Appearance again. (Jaeda thinks, \u201cI have got to learn how to do that.\u201d)<br /> <br /> Nergal exits, fuming. Before he goes, though, he tells Jaeda that his offer stands\u2026 and that he is better to have as a friend than an enemy.<br /> <br /> Jaeda worries at that. As they bring the unconscious Mateo into the trailer, though, Tlazolt\u00e9otl assures her that while Nergal might have some of his minions harass her, he won\u2019t attack her himself. First, his pantheon keeps him busy on the front lines, fighting the Titans. Second, in this time of crisis his pantheon can\u2019t afford to open a war on a second front against the Atzl\u00e1nti \u2014 and that\u2019s what\u2019ll happen, if he murders an Atzl\u00e1nti Scion. Her mother then tells Jaeda to bed Leo Lefebvre if she wants him or thinks it\u2019s the right thing to do, but it should be <i>her </i>choice, not influenced by Nergal.<br /> <br /> JACK lounges around home, enjoying a late breakfast while his mother goes shopping. A sudden gust of wind shakes the house and the roar of a motorcycle engine resounds from the kitchen. It\u2019s Hunter S. Thompson, a.k.a. Lono, astride the sort of insanely fast Italian motorcycle that nobody\u2019s crazy enough to drive unless they\u2019re hopped up on amphetamines, or a God. (The real Hunter S. Thompson loved them.)<br /> <br /> Lono wants breakfast. Jack finds the tag end of a box of Wheaties; Lono pours the cereal in a bowl and douses it with whiskey from his hip flask. While they eat, Lono quizzes Jack about school (\u201cHow do you like math?\u201d), friends, football, how Jack\u2019s adjusting to being a Scion, and progress at forming a Band. He notes that Jack has never consulted the Polynesian warrior whom Lono gave him as a Guide. Would Jack prefer some other Guide? Lono insists that Jack needs a Guide to teach him the skills he\u2019ll need in the Atua, both for fighting the Titans and establishing his place in the political hierarchy. (Academics for laws and ceremonies, Melee for close combat, etc.) To Jack, this all adds up to \u201cMore Homework,\u201d and worse, \u201cA Career Plan.\u201d<br /> <br /> They are interrupted by a call from Drama Club guy Josh, looking for Gene. Jack can offer no help.<br /> <br /> Lono finishes his cereal. Jack promises he\u2019ll invoke his Guide and try learning some new skills. Lono mounts the motorcycle, guns the engine, and vanishes in a blast of wind that makes a mess of the entire house.<br /> <br /> HOLLY enjoys another excellent Saturday morning breakfast cooked by Moominmamma when she receives a text from her mother, Louhi. Holly is to present herself at Gate 6 of Sea-Tac International Airport at 11 a.m., sharp. Holly is there on time. A large, unsmiling man in a very well-tailored suit escorts her to the private jet of Louisa Northland, owner and CEO of Northland Holdings LLC, the Witch of Wall Street, officially worth around a cool billion, actually worth at least ten times as much.<br /> <br /> Over wine, cheese and caviar, Louhi quizzes Holly about school, friends, how she\u2019s adjusting to being a Scion, and progress at acquiring minions to exploit. The Crone of Pohjola finds Holly\u2019s answers satisfactory. She is also intrigued by the unusual items of power the Scions have collected. Louhi offers her daughter a trade: a single use of a very special assassination service in return for each relic of interest that Holly provides. She would like to start with the Gorgon Blood and Bolvarg\u2019s torc. Holly says she\u2019ll see what she can do. Louhi tells Holly to expect more supernatural creatures to visit Avalon, and more chances to acquire relics: \u201cThe vortex of Legend is building, as I foresaw, and it draws in more of itself.\u201d As she leaves, Holly sees Nils and Ole (Bolvarg\u2019s surviving thralls), strapped to gurneys, being wheeled to the plane. The Crone of Pohjola has use for experienced, supernaturally-touched killers, and a little thing like the U.S. legal system won\u2019t keep them out of her hands.<br /> <br /> Driving back to Avalon, Holly receives a call she did not expect. It\u2019s Gary, flushed, angry and mortified at asking a Scion for anything, wanting to know if Holly knows where Reese is. Holly doesn\u2019t. Gary presses: He knows that Holly can find out where Reese is. Holly admits that she can. (She does not bother asking how Gary knows this. Gene knew, and it is no stretch to imagine that Gene might have told Reese, and Gary might have learned that way.) Holly tells Gary to meet her at her house. Along the way, Holly receives the phone and text messages from Gus, Jack and Jaeda, telling of their divine parents\u2019 visits and, btw, where\u2019s Gene? Holly (no fool) realizes that Something Is Up.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-07T15:36:15-08:00","id":1014015,"title":"","content":"EPISODE FIVE: COYOTE STORIES (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Rendezvous With\u2026 No, Not Destiny</u></b><br /> <br /> At her house, Holly throws Gary off balance by immediately agreeing to help him find Reese. (And he had some good bluster and threats prepared.) She also gets background: Reese was off to meet friends of hers at Hooverdale Mall, but she didn\u2019t show up. They called him; he called everyone else Reese knows; he suspects Gene is having his vile Scionish way with her.<br /> <br /> Holly calls the others and has them join her and Gary. She also calls Marshall and gets a quick, strained, \u201cNot a good time now!\u201d Barbara is also occupied. She doesn\u2019t bother calling Delia or Leo. Gary doesn\u2019t like any of this, but he\u2019s over a barrel.<br /> <br /> The gang piles into assorted cars to start the trail in Etna. Holly casts Ariadne\u2019s Thread. They follow Reese\u2019s path almost to the mall, where it detours off to a bar (where the barman saw Reese, Gene and two Perfectly Legitimate IDs) and, in time, to the El Sleazo Motel (Cheap Hourly Rates \u2014 Magic Fingers in Every Room). Gary\u2019s fury grows. Some of the Scions feel less respect for Gene for taking Reese to such a place.<br /> <br /> The motel room door isn\u2019t locked. Well, it isn\u2019t locked after Gus pulls it out of the wall. Inside are the incongruous remains of a very elegant and romantic luncheon (grilled sole, a bottle of fine wine, a box of Godiva chocolates, etc.) There are also Reese and Gene on the Magic Fingers bed, just about to get to the good part.<br /> <br /> Gary roars with rage and leaps forward before anyone else can act. (He used a Legendary Deed and channeled Willpower to absolutely definitely win the Join Battle roll.) Gary also briefly flickers into his 8\u2019 tall, ice giant form as he pulls Gene from the bed and slams him down with all his strength across the lunch table. The table becomes splinters, which is to be expected. The grilled sole becomes half-eaten Fishwiches, the wine becomes Night Train, and the chocolates are just Reese\u2019s Pieces. (Everyone mentally groans at the pun.) And Gene turns into a coyote who rolls and is out the door fast as lightning, yipping in panic.<br /> <br /> Yup, Gene got a visit from his divine parent too\u2026 and Coyote decided to get some of his son\u2019s girlfriend. Once she realizes what happened, Reese is nearly as angry as Gary.<br /> <br /> Only, <i>what happened to Gene?</i><br /> <br /> Gary doesn\u2019t care. Reese does, some, but lets Gary talk her into going home.<br /> <br /> Holly sighs as she realizes she must spend another Legend point to track Gene. His path starts from his home and ends in the Avalon High parking lot. More precisely, it ends at the side of the parking lot in an area of beauty bark and some scrubby little pines.<br /> <br /> There are tracks. The Scions examine them, apply a little Epic Attribute super-sleuthing, and reconstruct the events: Gene and a man wearing cowboy boots came to this place. The man walked several paces across the beauty bark and then suddenly went to one knee, scuffing the bark. Gene stepped forward. Then, Gene\u2019s tracks stopped \u2014 but re-start from the place the cowboy boots were, and leave the bark. But Holly knows from Ariadne\u2019s Thread that those are not really Gene\u2019s tracks. From what happened at the motel, they must be Coyote\u2019s, heading out to intercept Reese for a little naughty nookie. The real Gene seems to have vanished.<br /> <br /> Jack decides that Holly shouldn\u2019t do all the mystic mumbo-jumbo and activates Sense Tapu. He finds that yup, there\u2019s something still magical at the point where Gene\u2019s trail stops. In fact, he knows exactly what it is, because there\u2019s a magical prohibition: It\u2019s a portal to somewhere else, but it\u2019s closed and not to be opened except by the right people, in the right way.<br /> <br /> Jaeda wonders if she can\u2019t use the First Flower to re-open the portal because, well, it\u2019s magic and it cuts stuff. So she stabs the ground where Gene\u2019s footsteps end. To her surprise, it works. Whatever tapu Coyote put on his dimensional portal, it isn\u2019t stronger than a blade that sacrificed an entire pantheon.<br /> <br /> The Scions have come this far. Down the rabbit hole they go!<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-07T15:37:55-08:00","id":1014021,"title":"","content":"EPISODE FIVE: COYOTE STORIES (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: The Even Wilder West</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions land in another copse of scrubby pine trees, but these are older than the ones at school. The air is also hotter and thinner; the sun is brighter, though it seems to be the same time of day. Looking around, they see mountainous country covered with more pine and chaparral. Gus looks for tracks and sees, yup, Gene was here. He mooched around a while, then wandered off to explore his surroundings.<br /> <br /> Jack, meanwhile, checks with Know Tapu to find how they get back. The Boon seems to work, but the answer is insane: They need to climb up a hollow reed. (Must be a big reed\u2026) He and Holly put their Obscure Trivia Master heads together, though, and remember that various Native American cultures say their ancestors came to the present, Fourth World from their former habitation in the previous, subterranean Third World \u2014 now the Underworld \u2014 by climbing up the inside of a hollow reed. To get back, the Scions must find a hollow reed, stick it in the air, and invoke their divinity (<i>i.e.,</i> spend Legend) to recapitulate the myth.<br /> <br /> The Scions follow Gene\u2019s trail. It soon intersects the tracks of a group of horses, at least some of them with riders. These people seem to have forced Gene to travel with them. After another few miles, though, the trail of hoofprints becomes a chaotic muddle, suggesting a struggle \u2014 that left blood. They don\u2019t have to wonder what happened, though, when a Native American-looking dryad steps out of a tree and asks the Scions who they are. They are not <i>real </i>people (<i>i.e.,</i> Native American \u2014 though the dryad isn\u2019t sure about Jaeda). And, they feel sort of godlike but not like <i>real, </i>proper Gods. As the suspicious and hostile spirit eyes them, wolves and bears stalk out of the trees and bushes.<br /> <br /> The Scions don\u2019t want a fight; they want to find Gene. Fortunately, some of them have enough Social Fu to disarm the situation and get the dryad to talk to them. Her name is Hoyalet, and she is a Canotila (different name, but still basically a dryad). She attacked a group of cowboy white men because they held a son of the real Gods captive. Gene escaped, but he quickly moved beyond the patch of woods Hoyalet claims as her own, and so she doesn\u2019t know Gene\u2019s current location. At least the Scions know where to pick up the trail again.<br /> <br /> Hoyalet also clarifies where the Scions are. This is not the Underworld; it is the Ghost Dance Country that Coyote and other Gods from several pantheons created more than a century ago as a refuge for the hard-pressed Native American tribes. The Ghost Dance did not, as promised, magically expel the white man and bring back the buffalo. Instead, it shifted the dancers to this Terra Incognita, wedged between the Third and Fourth Worlds. Unfortunately \u2014 and Hoyalet can\u2019t imagine why \u2014 hundreds of white (and black, and Asian) people somehow got into the Ghost Dance Country as well. They have their own town and are a great nuisance, but the spirits and tribes keep them largely confined to their \u201creservation.\u201d<br /> <br /> Back on the trail, the Scions follow Gene for many miles. Along the way, Gene\u2019s tracks show he encountered another group, traveled with them a ways and then broke away, running, with the others in pursuit. The trail leads to a mountainside with a huge slab of rock set in the side like a door. Six figures prowl around the door: two Native American men, two coyotes, and two hybrid coyote-men. The group\u2019s experts on the arcane and mythic immediately recognize them as Skinwalkers (shapeshifter-witches from Navaho folklore).<br /> <br /> The Skinwalkers grin to see this group of young white people wandering the wilderness. They say they want to help rescue Gene from inside the mountain, but they are so obviously preparing to jump the Scions that it\u2019s almost insulting. A few super-strength tricks convince the Skinwalkers to back off. (So much for the great Navaho bogeyman.) But, the Skinwalkers keep watching the Scions from a distance.<br /> <br /> The rock-door is so heavy that Gus and Jack must work together to move it, but it does move. The Scions hurry inside. The rock slides back into position as the Skinwalkers dart forward.<br /> <br /> Behind the rock is a huge cave lit by a bonfire and light coming through chinks in the rocks. Twelve immense figures hewn of living rock dwell in the cave. They look about 12 feet tall and really strong. Gene is there, too, sitting on a high pile of rocks at an oversized, rough stone table. As the Scions watch, one of the giants tries to give Gene a handful of fruit: He misjudges and slams the fruit onto the table, spraying juice and pulp everywhere. Another giant lumbers forward with a big stone bowl of water, anxious to clean up: He spills it. A third giant, turning to look at the Scions, bumps into a fourth giant: They both fall flat on their butts, confused expressions on their faces. The rest waddle around, similarly clumsy, like big stone babies.<br /> <br /> \u201cHey guys,\u201d Gene says. \u201cMeet my new friends. Please rescue me.\u201d<br /> <br /> Once again, the Scions decide to find what\u2019s going on before joining battle. The giants readily introduce themselves as the A-Senee-Ki-Wakw, or Stone People. The Northeastern tribal God Glooscap made them, but he found them so stupid and clumsy that he destroyed them and made humans as a replacement. He didn\u2019t destroy all the Stone People, though, and Coyote put the survivors in the Ghost Dance Country.<br /> <br /> The Stone People somehow realized that Gene was the Scion of Coyote. They want to keep him. They think that Coyote will eventually come looking for his son. If they treat Gene nicely, maybe Coyote will reward them by turning them into something else. <i>Anything </i>else. They don\u2019t like being Stone People! As the Scions see one Stone Man accidentally set fire to the bed of dry grass they made for Gene, they agree the Stone People have a point. Holly declares, \u201cThese are the most pathetic creatures I have ever seen.\u201d<br /> <br /> The Scions do not like the plan to wait for Coyote. They are pretty sure they could get Gene away from the stupid, clumsy giants. However, they also agree they would feel rotten leaving the Stone People to the sad fate of being Stone People. So, they agree to try taking the Stone People to Coyote and persuading him to grant the giants\u2019 request.<br /> <br /> This is easier said than done, because the Stone People are almost as stubborn as they are stupid. It takes Social Fu just to pound the idea into their heads that <i>We can find Coyote.</i> Eventually, though, the Scions emerge from the cave with the Stone People in tow. The Skinwalkers are still waiting. Once again, the Skinwalkers decide not to make trouble.<br /> <br /> The journey back to the copse where the Scions entered the Ghost Dance Country takes more than a day. It\u2019s like taking a mob of toddlers on a cross-country hike. The Stone People wander off. They fall down. They ask, \u201cAre we there yet?\u201d every five minutes. Still, nobody messes with a group accompanied by twelve immensely strong Stone People. Along the way, the Scions find a dried-out pond and collect a hollow reed.<br /> <br /> Back at the copse, Jack thrusts the reed above his head and spends Legend the way Know Tapu said he should. The reed pierces the sky and sucks Jack right up. The Scions follow, each spending a Legend as they herd the Stone People to the reed. They are sucked up as well.<br /> <br /> The mob emerges back at the parking lot. It seems very little time has passed while they were in the other world. The Drama Club is just leaving the school. They see the Scions surrounded by Stone People. Then they decide that no, they didn\u2019t see any such thing. The Scions manage to get the Stone People to play the Let\u2019s Sit Very Still Game, so the giants look like a somewhat strange rock formation, and then they go looking for Coyote.<br /> <br /> Holly doesn\u2019t want to spend any more Legend, but the Scions suspect they won\u2019t need Ariadne\u2019s Thread to find Coyote. They just hit every seedy bar they can find, starting near the El Sleazo motel. They find Coyote, back in human form, in the third bar they try.<br /> <br /> Coyote insists he was trying to <i>help </i>Gene in his relationship with Reese, since his son seemed to be a bit slow in getting some. Coyote was \u201cbreaking the ice\u201d with her (Get it? Get it?) Coyote starts to suggest that what the Scions <i>really </i>want to do is \u2014 BRRRAAAAAP! He farts, loudly, interrupting whatever scam he was about to attempt.<br /> <br /> Coyote doesn\u2019t want to talk about it, but the Scions fill in some blanks and discreet texting to Reese fills in the rest. Coyote made another play for Reese, <i>still</i> pretending to be Gene \u2014 this time, come to apologize for his father\u2019s misdeeds. Coyote dared to sneak into the Vetterson house to do this. Only, Reese suspected this still might not be the real Gene. She got away long enough to alert her parents. Her mother cast a spell to reveal the truth. Then her mother attacked Coyote with her best curse while her father attacked Coyote with his best battle-axe. Her father missed. Her mother did not.<br /> <br /> At this point, Coyote is getting the story of the Scions\u2019 adventures in the Ghost Dance Country. Jaeda initially threatens him with the First Flower, and he seems to take the threat seriously. Then he turns wheedling and suggests that Jaeda needs his advice on using such a powerful item \u2014 BRRRAAAAAP! However Coyote hoped to snow Jaeda, it doesn\u2019t work as Coyote cusses a blue streak and tells the Scions to take revenge on the Vettersons or he will!<br /> <br /> Finally, Coyote agrees to help the Stone People. This has been a bad day for him and he wants to end it as Coyote the Great Master of Change instead of Coyote the Bumbling Idiot. He inspects the Stone People and says that sure, he can change them into anything, what do they want? Jack suggests big, floppy-eared puppies. So, Coyote does a little shuffling dance and chant, then whips out a huge mallet and bashes the Stone People in their heads, right down the line. There\u2019s a flash of light. The Stone People crack and collapse into heaps of gravel with big gray-brown puppies in the middle of them. \u201cMy work here is done,\u201d Coyote says. He turns into a bird and flies away, gassily.<br /> <br /> Jack decides to keep the puppy that looks most confused. The Scions spend the next few days making sure the other 11 puppies also get good homes. It isn\u2019t hard. The Scions end their adventure feeling pretty good about themselves. They rescued Gene, didn\u2019t have any horrible battles to the death, did a good deed for the Stone People, and saw a real jerk victimized by the most hilariously humiliating curse of all time.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Addendum One</u></b><br /> <br /> Jaeda didn\u2019t forget Nergal\u2019s demand/promise/threat. She goes to see Leo at one of his lone workouts in the school gym. She doesn\u2019t try seducing him, though. Instead, she tells him about what Nergal wanted \u2014 and says she isn\u2019t doing it. The conversation segues into a debate on whether the Scions\u2019 parents are really the Gods of myth, or demons. Leo asks what\u2019s more likely: That all these different, inconsistent mythologies are true \u2014 with no explanation for each others\u2019 existence \u2014 or that devils have simply posed as different Gods to delude humanity into sin? Tlazolt\u00e9otl\u2019s encounter with Nergal could have been staged to make Jaeda think her mother isn\u2019t evil.<br /> <br /> Jaeda gives up trying to help Leo feel good about being a Scion, at least for now. However, she persuades him to practice combat with her as well as other Scions. As she points out, some of the real, no doubt about it, evil monsters out there are female. Facing them, it\u2019ll be kill or be killed, and a lot of innocent bystanders being killed as well. So, Leo has to learn how to hit a girl.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Addendum Two</u></b><br /> <br /> And what of that flash of light when Coyote wielded his full divine power to transform the Stone People?<br /> <br /> Montage: The \u201cContainment Breach\u201d music from <i>Ghostbusters</i> starts playing.<br /> <br /> A view from above the school as a circular wave of light expands quickly from the parking lot. Cut to a higher view as radiance sweeps over Avalon, Hooverdale and Etna; cut again to show the wash of Legend spread and fade over the entire western half of Washington.<br /> <br /> Cut back to a series of images.<br /> <br /> Eyes glitter in a rocky crevice.<br /> <br /> <i>I believe it\u2019s magic (magic)</i><br /> <br /> A serpentine monster\u2019s head rises from a lake.<br /> <br /> <i>I believe it\u2019s magic (magic)</i><br /> <br /> Two trees in a forest, their branches intertwined like the lintel of a doorway: The view between the trunks bends like a painted scene on an opening door. Light spills out from beyond.<br /> <br /> <i>I believe it\u2019s magic (magic)</i><br /> <br /> View of an ornate old mansion; the viewpoint swoops in through a bay window to focus on a very old man in a wheelchair with a little tube up his nose. He spasms in the chair and cries, \u201cNo \u2014 no!\u201d A nurse hurries over and anxiously asks, \u201cSir? What\u2019s wrong?\u201d as she fusses and checks his oxygen cylinder and heart monitor. He waves her away irritably, snapping, \u201cNothing. It\u2019s nothing you\u2019d understand.\u201d She retreats. The old man stares bleakly out the window at the manicured grounds and whispers, \u201cIt\u2019s started again.\u201d<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-08T15:27:57-08:00","id":1014482,"title":"","content":"EPISODE SIX: DAY OF THE DEAD<br /> <br /> <b><u>Flashback: Gus Sobeck\u2019s Visitation</u></b><br /> <br /> The scene is the previous summer. Gus visits his great-grandfather in the nursing home. His great-grandfather had always been old to him, but only in the last year had he become frail. Nobody in the family wants to talk about it, but they know that Gotthelf Sobeck will die within days. Now, old Gotthelf wants a private word with the great-grandson whom he taught never to accept failure.<br /> <br /> Gotthelf has a final present for Gus: a souvenir from his Afrika Korps days. The burnished wooden box holds a crocodile tooth on a fine chain. \u201cIt has brought the family luck,\u201d Great-Grandpa says with a peculiar smile. \u201cMay it serve you well. I repay a debt in giving it to you. And I hope the debt is at last now paid in full.\u201d<br /> <br /> Gotthelf also gives Gus a ticket to the Underground Seattle tour. \u201cIt is worth seeing. I would not like to think you missed it.\u201d He smiles and shrugs. \u201cIndulge the foolish worries of an old man.\u201d Gus is quite sure his great-grandfather never had a foolish moment in his life: He knows something is up. Gotthelf sends Gus on his way, but asks him to send the pretty redhead nurse in to fluff his pillow. At 92, he\u2019s still not too old at least to look\u2026<br /> <br /> Gus takes the tour the next day. It\u2019s sort of interesting, walking under the downtown sidewalks and seeing the old subcellars, though not especially so. Perhaps his inattention causes Gus to stray from the tour. He finds himself alone in a damp tunnel with only the faintest light from a distant bit of glass set in the tunnel ceiling. Only, there are two other small lights ahead. Gus hears the scratch and rustle of something big moving. The lights come closer. They are eyes, the eyes of a crocodile that is much larger than any that Gus has seen in the zoo\u2026 and its claws are adorned with gold.<br /> <br /> <i>Hello, son,</i> the crocodile says. <i>It\u2019s time you knew something important about your family.</i><br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Disturbing Behavior</u></b><br /> <br /> It\u2019s been a quiet couple of weeks in Avalon. Gus has his Bolvarg-fang cesti made and has put in an order for a chain mail hauberk. Holly has her kevlar trenchcoat and dart gun; now she wonders what she should put in the darts. She sent Louhi Bolvarg\u2019s torc and the Crone of Pohjola has returned it, along with a card that bears a phone number. Jack puzzles Dr. Demerit by simultaneously slacking and doing well in his class. Progress on housebreaking his new puppy goes slowly. Jaeda will be lucky to manage Ds in anything except PE and Auto Shop, where she\u2019s doing well. The Knights have won every game. Coach Reagan mutters to himself, \u201cThis year\u2026 finally, this year\u2026\u201d The Etna Titans have also won every game except the one against the Knights. The championship games have begun and it seems inevitable that the final game will be between the Knights and the Titans.<br /> <br /> Gene, alas, is gone. The kidnapping of a news anchor attracted national media attention. She rode the attention to a network position. Gene, perforce, followed his mother. He\u2019s now in the other Washington. Reese is sad.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s the week before Halloween. This year, Halloween falls on Saturday. There\u2019s a dance scheduled for Friday night, with the next football game on Halloween itself. The theme for the school\u2019s Halloween decorations and dance is Mexico\u2019s Day of the Dead for a bit of easy multiculturalism. Jaeda knows, of course, that D\u00eda de los Muertos is really all Atzl\u00e1nti. So, she\u2019s perhaps the first to notice when people start behaving oddly. Well, more oddly than usual at Scion High.<br /> <br /> JAEDA is in Remedial Math, and she\u2019s bored. She pays less attention to the teacher, Ms. Fanshaw, than to Kristin, a girl a few seats away, who proudly tells another student how drunk she got the weekend before. Fanshaw, a middle-aged burnout, tiredly asks Kristin if she could at least pretend to pay attention, or would she like to come up and finish the math problem on the whiteboard? Kristin sing-songs, \u201cNo, Ms. Fanshaw. Sorry, Ms. Fanshaw,\u201d and giggles. Ms. Fanshaw says, \u201cIn that case, Kristin\u2026 Die!\u201d And she leaps to the startled girl and tries to wring her neck.<br /> <br /> Jaeda realizes this is not normal behavior for a teacher, and she\u2019d better stop it. (The other students are evenly divided between fleeing the classroom and pulling out camera phones.) Jaeda pulls her first punch. Ms. Fanshaw shrugs it off, sniffs in Jaeda\u2019s direction and hisses, \u201cAtzl\u00e1nti!\u201d Jaeda decides she\u2019d better fight harder. She soon knocks out the teacher.<br /> <br /> Principal Flecker and Vice-Principal Stone arrive a few minutes later. Stone is grimly pleased: He now has a reason to expel Jaeda. The remaining kids say how Ms. Fanshaw attacked Kristin, though, and have pictures of the teacher throttling the girl. Principal Flecker decides that Ms. Fanshaw clearly needs a rest. No doubt the attorneys will work something out between the school district and Kristin\u2019s parents.<br /> <br /> (INCIDENTALLY: Earlier, Marshall told Jaeda what he did last episode when the Gods all visited their children. Huitzilopochtli took Marshall on a hunting trip. They found a lindwurm, and Huitzilopochtli challenged Marshall to kill it by himself. Which Marshall did, though he got pretty thrashed in the process. As a reward, Huitzilopochtli told Marshall how to skin a lindwurm and let him know that the skin could be cooked into a magical soup. So, um, could Jaeda do the cooking? Because she\u2019s a girl. Jaeda says, \u201cNo.\u201d Only, she says it a bit more colorfully than that.)<br /> <br /> GUS hears a fight as he walks through the halls and checks it out. Unusually, only a few students are staying nearby to watch and cheer. Gus finds Ben Rasmussen, a big, blonde member of Marshall Byrd\u2019s Army brat posse, beating the tar out of another kid. This isn\u2019t kid-stuff fighting, either: Ben is trying to punch and kick the other boy to death. Gus grabs Ben\u2019s arm and pulls him away. Ben rages and struggles, but he can\u2019t escape us\u2019 grip.<br /> <br /> Marshall shows up a moment later and demands to know why Gus is manhandling his friend. Marshall is in no mood to listen to explanations, though: Ben is Army (well, plans to be), Gus is not, and as far as Marshall is concerned, that puts Ben in the right no matter what. Ben snarls that what the other kid said to him was unforgivable, nobody calls him a Nazi baby-killer, etc.<br /> <br /> Amazingly, the victim of the beating briefly lifts his head, looks at Gus and says, \u201cNo\u2026 It\u2019s you\u2026 <i>murderer</i>\u2026\u201d before falling unconscious.<br /> <br /> Vice-Principal Stone arrives shortly thereafter. Stone likes Gus because Gus is a star athlete, but Stone also likes Marshall and Ben because Stone\u2019s ex-military himself. So, he orders Ben suspended for a week (legally, odds are he ought to be expelled and placed in juvie) and calls Nurse Montague to take care of the other kid.<br /> <br /> Marshall is still angry at Gus, and Gus can see the anger is far deeper than this one incident. Marshall has seen Gus as a rival since the start of the school year. Gus tells Marshall that if he wants to have it out, fine, they can fight after football practice behind the gym. Marshall accepts the challenge.<br /> <br /> Naturally, when the beaten kid regains consciousness, he has no memory of what happened.<br /> <br /> HOLLY is talking with her new guitarist. She wants to form a rock band (it\u2019s a prestigious high school thing). She already talked the Student Council into choosing her band to play the Halloween dance, with member Barbara\u2019s collusion (it\u2019s a Scion solidarity thing).<br /> <br /> Now Holly just needs the band. Holly can sing, excellently (it\u2019s a supernaturally significant Kaleva thing). Jack volunteered to play drums and shows competence (though not because it\u2019s an Atua thing). That, however, exhausts the musical possibilities of the school\u2019s Scion population.<br /> <br /> Holly found another girl, Abbie McConnel, who\u2019s competent enough with a bass. She also found the school\u2019s best guitar player: Aaron Bindahna, a highly assimilated son of Arab immigrants. His family comes from Oman (\u201cThe Arab country you never hear about\u201d), so it sort of makes sense that his surname means \u201cSon of the Desert.\u201d (\u201cDahna\u201d is another name for the Rub al Khali or Empty Quarter in Arabia. Gosh, the stuff you know with Know-It-All.) Aaron plays excellently and can even improvise lyrics, but he is kind of shy and doesn\u2019t want to front the band. That suits Holly just fine. So, they just need to arrange a few practice sessions and get some songs down before the end of the week: Holly is confident she can get the band in shape in a few days. How hard can it be for a daughter of the Gods? She calls the band Winter\u2019s Grace.<br /> <br /> Thing is, she poached Aaron from another band. As Holly, Abbie and Aaron work out a practice schedule, Rick, a boy from the other band, barges in on them. He\u2019s not happy at Aaron\u2019s dropping out to join Ice Queen\u2019s band. He tries to make Aaron change his mind. So, it\u2019s social combat between Rick and Holly. (Well\u2026 contested Manipulation + Presence rolls.) Holly crushes Rick. Despondent, Rick says he should have known he didn\u2019t have a chance, he never has a chance, and starts banging his head against the wall. Blood starts flowing. Holly finds herself in the unusual position of participating directly in combat to restrain Rick, though she gets Abbie and Aaron to help her. Rick passes out. When he comes to (just before the ambulance arrives), he doesn\u2019t remember how he got hurt.<br /> <br /> Holly, of course, instantly suspects possession.<br /> <br /> JACK goes to Arby\u2019s after football practice for a late afternoon snack. So does team captain Warren Mansfield, though that\u2019s coincidence: Warren has become notably cool to Jack and Gus as the two Scions have replaced him as stars of the team. Two Polynesian warriors interrupt Jack by smashing his table with their stone war-clubs and shouting, \u201cDefend yourself!\u201d One warrior tosses Jack a third club. Perforce, Jack defends himself. Everyone else in the Arby\u2019s flees.<br /> <br /> The two warriors manage to hit Jack pretty good a couple times. They also kibitz as he fights back and confirm that Lono sent them to assess Jack\u2019s progress at learning weapons combat. The warriors are strong, skilled and channeling Virtues, but Jack is strong and a Scion, so he wins in the end. The warriors fade away as he knocks them out. (They were Atuan analogs to Einherjar.)<br /> <br /> At which point, the clerk who\u2019d cowered behind the counter looks at Jack and says, \u201cYou. You\u2019re one. Don\u2019t let them get the key. I can\u2019t hold them any longer! The damned worm!\u201d And then the clerk falls unconscious.<br /> <br /> This is odd, but first things first: The warriors left Jack a club. He uses it to smash the restaurant\u2019s security camera and recorder in hopes there won\u2019t be any proof that the nutty people who smashed up the Arby\u2019s were after him specifically.<br /> <br /> Jack also notices a slender plastic tube under the table where Warren sat. It holds an unused hypodermic needle. It isn\u2019t hard to figure out that Warren is juicing.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-08T15:30:28-08:00","id":1014483,"title":"","content":"EPISODE SIX: DAY OF THE DEAD (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Haunted High</u></b><br /> <br /> The fight between Gus and Marshall wasn\u2019t actually played out because it would take a looong time and leave the other players with nothing to do. Suffice to say they beat on each other for several minutes, each inflicting a few levels of bashing damage. Then Mitch Osada, the school\u2019s janitor who gives an uncomfortable impression of Knowing The Score, comes out of the school and tells them to break it up. They part, Marshall still angry. But a few minutes later, Jack contacts the other Scions about his odd experience, and Gus agrees they have more important matters to think about.<br /> <br /> Holly, Gus, Jack and Jaeda compare notes and realize they have a ghost problem. Well, the events are consistent with ghosts, anyway. Most likely spectres, considering the murderous or kill-the-host behavior \u2014 except for the ghost who possessed the Arby\u2019s clerk. The Scions brainstorm what the enigmatic message might mean. They also contact those remaining NPC Scions with whom they are all still on speaking terms.<br /> <br /> Barbara hasn\u2019t had any strange encounters, but Leo says he\u2019s seen ghosts in the school and around town. In fact, he describes a woman whose spirit bore the marks of slit wrists, whom the Scions suspect was the ghost that possessed Rick. (Oh. Yeah. Since they brought it up, Leo admits that he can see ghosts, at least while he holds Nergal\u2019s mace.)<br /> <br /> Jack and Holly go into research mode. For the last month or so, Jack has randomly collected any news report that seemed in any way strange. Holly goes to the school librarian, Mrs. Weihs, and pits her against the Internet. Between them, they find plenty of local murder victims, suicides and killers who might be potential ghosts.<br /> <br /> They also find a crackpot\u2019s account of a strange cult that was active in the greater Seattle area back in the 1970s. This cult supposedly wanted to open a gateway to Xibalba, the Mayan Underworld, to release a horde of malevolent ghosts on the World. They were stopped, however, by a group of mystery men (and women) such as Michael Balboa, Randolph Sobeck and Sister Elyse Prosser. The key to the cult\u2019s intended gate was a sword.<br /> <br /> \u201cHey, I know where there\u2019s a magic sword,\u201d Jack says. When he went through the school with Know Tapu running, noting everything that registered even a twinge of magic, one thing he spotted was Sir Dance-A-Lot\u2019s sword. But it\u2019s just spray-painted wood, cheap glass jewels and aluminum foil! Isn\u2019t it?<br /> <br /> The Scions break into the school\u2019s trophy display case and fetch the school mascot\u2019s sword. Sure enough, when they peel away the foil (the foil with the \u201cdon\u2019t detect the real magic\u201d enchantment, which stood out particularly to Know Tapu because it was magic of prohibition), the tacky prop turns into a Stormbringer-style black metal Doomsday Sword.<br /> <br /> Gus asks Jack to <i>please </i>let the others know about other supernatural stuff he finds, <i>promptly, </i>and promises him $100 in pizza for each revelation. No, each <i>provable </i>revelation.<br /> <br /> \u201cOh,\u201d Jack says. \u201cLike the guitar player for our band being magical?\u201d Though, since Know Tapu is so much less selective than Unlidded Eye, he can\u2019t say <i>how </i>Aaron is magical.<br /> <br /> Gus also notes the name of one of the cult-breakers in the crackpot\u2019s story. Thinking back on the time when his dying great-grandfather gave him his crocodile-tooth Relic and the ticket to the Underground Seattle tour where Gus met his divine father, Gus realizes that his ancestor hinted that Gus might not be the first Scion in the family tree.<br /> <br /> That aside, it seems like something has disturbed the doomsday death-portal that the cult created, and it\u2019s opening again. They have the key; now where is the gate?<br /> <br /> The ghost\u2019s warning to Jack gives a clue: \u201cThe damned worm!\u201d Could a lindwurm have disturbed whatever seals were placed on the gate? One of the Scions whom Marshall hasn\u2019t alienated calls the Scion of Huitzilopochtli and asks where his fight with the lindwurm took place. Marshall says it happened in an old graveyard. It was a hell of a fight, too. When the lindwurm tried burrowing away, he threw C4 down the hole to flush it out.<br /> <br /> Well, it makes sense that a doorway to Hell would be in a graveyard. And, Jack\u2019s \u201cWeird File\u201d includes a recent story about unknown vandals knocking over tombstones and disturbing the ground in that very cemetery. <i>And </i>not just a giant burrowing worm, but C4 going off underground. Oh, joy.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-08T15:32:35-08:00","id":1014485,"title":"","content":"EPISODE SIX: DAY OF THE DEAD (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Tomb of Moderate Unpleasantness</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions figure they have no time to lose. It\u2019s already night. They drive to the old Avalon cemetery and go looking for Marshall\u2019s fight scene. Jack decides to bring the Doomsday Sword because gosh, something that dangerous shouldn\u2019t be left unattended. But he also brings the war-club, having found (through direct experience) that <i>weapons do more damage than fists.</i> What a concept!<br /> <br /> Jack also immediately notices the mausoleum overgrown with brush and rowan trees, because the \u201cDon\u2019t Pay Attention To Me\u201d ward on it pings on the Know Tapu he has active. Holly\u2019s use of The Unlidded eye gives a more detailed dossier on the enchantments meant to confine deathly forces and centered beneath the heavy stone building. The name above the tomb door is \u201cMcLann.\u201d (\u201cMictlan. Right,\u201d the Scions note with a collective roll of the eyes.) But, the Scions are not the only people at the mausoleum. There are lights on inside.<br /> <br /> The Scions with Lightning Sprinter race ahead. That means Jaeda and Holly are first in and the first to see Don and Dave Sarsen (the stone giant twins) lifting the bottom out of a sarcophagus, with Gary and Reese Vetterson nearby. Oh, and there\u2019s a hot Goth chick neither of the Scions recognize. Gus and Jack are not far behind, so battle is joined.<br /> <br /> Holly immediately uses Overt Order to make one of the Sarsens drop his side of the massive concrete slab. It falls into the sarcophagus and, apparently, down a shaft to crash at the bottom. The other Sarsen picks up the concrete sarcophagus lid and throws it at the two girls. It misses, breaks in the doorway, and falls in pieces before Gus and Jack. The Goth chick makes a devil-horn gesture at Holly, hisses \u201cMaleficium!\u201d and Holly feels herself struck with the Evil Eye.<br /> <br /> The battle quickly resolves to Gary and Reese against Gus, and the Sarsens and the Goth chick (the Scions soon hear her called Acacia) against the rest of the Scions. Jaeda attacks Acacia before the Goth witch can do much more. Acacia stays in the rear, behind the four giants, but Jaeda leaps off the rim of the sarcophagus, uses the First Flower to pivot off the ceiling, and lands by Acacia. The witch squeaks, \u201cNo, I\u2019m human!\u201d but Jaeda stabs her anyway. Acacia isn\u2019t dying (yet), but she is badly wounded. Holly uses the Evil Eye against Dave Sarsen, but she only curses him for a short time due to the curse Acacia placed on her. She settles for telling Don Sarsen to punch Dave Sarsen, wasting the giant\u2019s actions. Jack and Jaeda both try attacking the cursed Dave, but even the First Flower can barely scratch the stone giant: Jaeda just isn\u2019t strong enough, and the giant\u2019s soak is too high.<br /> <br /> Gus and Gary go at it hammer and tongs. Gus briefly manages to grapple the frost giant and crack a few of his ribs; but Gary slips free and haymakers Gus in return. Gary tries throwing grit in Gus\u2019 eyes, but Gus has Blindfighting, negating the tactic. Wounding Gary also enrages Reese, and she gives Gus a kick that knocks him around pretty good. Reese abandons the fight, however, to carry away Acacia; and while Gary proves to be as strong and skilled a fighter as Gus \u2014 and has several more health levels \u2014 the Scion of Sobeck\u2019s new fenrir-enhanced cesti pound the giant again and again until Gary lies unconscious on the floor. But it was still a close fight.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, the other Scions finally devise a strategy to deal with the Sarsens. The next time Holly has Don punch Dave, Jaeda quickly interposes the First Flower, converting the giant\u2019s punch from bashing damage to piercing, lethal damage, hurting Dave a great deal. The Scions tell the giants to surrender, but the Sarsens are too angry or too stupid to do so. So, Jack pounds on the First Flower\u2019s hilt with his war-club, and at last Dave collapses on the floor and begins crumbling into mud and gravel. Once Reese has fled with Acadia and Gary is unconscious, the Band finishes off Don in the same way. Gus doesn\u2019t mind this, because the Sarsens initiated lethal force by throwing the sarcophagus-lid and did not surrender when they had the chance. He lets Gary live, though, as the frost giant was unarmed and never tried to move past fisticuffs.<br /> <br /> The shaft in the sarcophagus leads down to an identically-shaped crypt about 25 feet underground. This vault, however, is not a fake like the one above. Inscriptions in Greek, Cuneiform, Mayan and Egyptian Hieroglyphics cover the walls, interspersed with images of various gods. There\u2019s another sarcophagus, though the designs are Mayan rather than Egyptian. A few stone blocks of the wall are pushed a little out of place, cracking a few paintings and inscriptions, while a few statuettes of gods fell over.<br /> <br /> A clay figure \u2014 a <i>shabti </i>\u2014 starts talking to the Scions. It introduces itself as the ghost of Michael Balboa, Scion of Mictlantecuhtli. Michael notes how much Gus looks like his old comrade Randolph. He also asks what the hell the Scions are doing, bringing the key so close to the gate.<br /> <br /> Michael explains that the only way his Band of Demigods could seal the cult\u2019s gate was for one of them to sacrifice himself and become its eternal guardian. Michael had the right supernatural powers, so he volunteered. It\u2019s his body in the sarcophagus. His corpse became the portal to Xibalba; it must stay there, undisturbed. Michael spends most of his time battling the spectres that try to slip through and can leave that duty only for short times. The damage to the crypt weakened the wards that assist him, though, and so several spectres managed to slip past him and into the World. The spectres were looking for the key. Fortunately, the Scions found it first, right where the earlier Band hid it.<br /> <br /> The only way to stop more spectres from forcing their way past is to repair the vault. Michael feels pretty sure that if the stone blocks are pushed back into position, the paintings touched up and the Greek, Mayan, Mesopotamian and Egyptian funerary prayers recited by Scions of the proper pantheons, the wards will repair themselves and lock the portal shut again.<br /> <br /> Gus and Jaeda can handle the Pesedjet and Atzl\u00e1nti rites. The only Dodekatheon and Anuna available, however, are Delia Bright and Leo Lefebvre. The Scions call Leo: The Scion of Nergal isn\u2019t happy at receiving phone calls in the wee hours of the morning that he can <i>not </i>explain to his mortal parents, but he is easily persuaded to help keep damned souls in Hell. (He\u2019s not quite so happy that they want him to perform an Anuna rite.) Delia is a harder case. To the argument that the Xibalba-gate is a threat to the entire World, she retorts that then the other Scions should be willing to give her just about anything for her help. The Band bargains her down to Gus and Jack playing below their full capacity for the remaining few games of the football season: not throwing the game or humiliating themselves, but letting Warren shine as the star player while the college recruiters are watching. Gus very reluctantly agrees.<br /> <br /> The Scions spend an hour or so repairing the tomb chamber. Gus, Jaeda, Leo and Delia perform the funerary rites. Jack and Holly confirm that the wards are indeed repairing themselves.<br /> <br /> That still leaves the problem of what to do with the Vettersons and Acacia. They could still re-open and wreck the crypt to let spectres loose again. Even if the Scions were willing to kill all three, what if they already told someone else?<br /> <br /> Conferring with Michael, the Scions decide to fill the shaft with cement so the only way down would be to dig in with heavy machinery \u2014 which could not be done quickly or inconspicuously. Gus and Holly have money: They hire a cement mixer to prepare a truckload before dawn and deliver it to them, no questions asked.<br /> <br /> Jaeda volunteers to take Gary home before he regains consciousness. The Scions load him into her car, then dump him on the Vetterson front porch. Jaeda rings the doorbell, jumps in her car and speeds away.<br /> <br /> Finally, the Scions quickly fashion a copy of Sir Dance-A-Lot\u2019s sword for the display case. Hopefully, no one will realize that the silly prop of wood and foil is not the same silly prop that was in the case the day before. What to do with the real sword is a problem they leave for later.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T14:36:38-08:00","id":1014961,"title":"","content":"EPISODE SEVEN: THE BIG GAME<br /> <br /> <b><u>Old Business</u></b><br /> <br /> It\u2019s two weeks after the last adventure. A number of possibly significant things happened in the meantime:<br /> <br /> <b>Acacia\u2019s Survival:</b> The story is that Acacia was out late with her friend Reese. They ran into a mugger who knifed Acacia. Reese took her to the hospital. A warning to girls not to stay out late! (But nothing about the cemetery or the Scions.) But the wound wasn\u2019t as deep as it initially appeared, and Acacia is out of the hospital in eight days. She might say she\u2019s mortal, but she heals like a Scion.<br /> <br /> <b>Thralls Return:</b> Four of Bolvarg\u2019s student thralls returned to Avalon High, including Bob. Two of them seem to be still insane from the \u201cdrugs\u201d the \u201cgang\u201d gave them, but the doctors think these four can return to normal life \u2014 at least, they aren\u2019t raving about giant wolves anymore. Their minds are still damaged, but they might be able to function.<br /> <br /> Horndog Bob sought out Jaeda and said he hasn\u2019t forgotten her for the insults she gave him. He now thinks school will be a lot better, though, now that he\u2019s so strong: He can push other kids around, and he bets he can get more and better girls than Alice, too. Jaeda\u2019s neither intimidated (she reminds him how she beat him up at Bolvarg\u2019s den) nor impressed with his thrall physique (she hangs out with Gus, after all).<br /> <br /> <b>Halloween Dance: </b>Winter\u2019s Grace performed adequately. They had time to learn only a few songs, but by taking requests (from shills in the crowd) they could recycle a lot. Jack sang \u201cMonster Mash\u201d three times. Plus, Aaron can improvise a guitar line around anything Holly sings, and bass and drums are, well, bass and drums. As the night wore on and Aaron loosened up, Holly persuaded him to deliver a rather otherworldly cover of \u201cCemetery Girls.\u201d<br /> <br /> Leo did not attend. (Not that he intended to.) Barbara convinced him to patrol around the school and parking lot with his mace, swatting any ghosts that showed up. (And they did.)<br /> <br /> ++++++++++++++++<br /> Aaron Bindahna<br /> <br /> Since the dance, Holly and Aaron have done acoustic-and-vocal gigs at the sort of clubs that play that sort of music. Holly can tell Aaron is getting sweet on her. She also did a background check on him, since he is in some way supernatural. The Unlidded Eye does not reveal in what way he is supernatural \u2014 merely that he <i>is.</i><br /> <br /> Aaron\u2019s name means \u201cLofty Son of the Desert\u201d (the Dahna, the eastern side of Arabia\u2019s \u201cEmpty Quarter\u201d). His parents, Abdurrahman (\u201cSlave of the Merciful One,\u201d <i>i.e.</i> Allah) and Hafsa (name of one of Mohammed\u2019s wives) Bindahna, became US citizens in 1993, just before Aaron was born. Holly\u2019s hacker lackey cannot find their immigration records. Aaron has a younger sister, Janet (or Jannat, \u201cGarden\u201d), in eighth grade. Abdurrahman runs a copy shop in Avalon. Hafsa keeps house.<br /> <br /> Aaron\u2019s parents are devout Sunni Muslims; Aaron, not so much, but he does go to the mosque now and then. Their mosque is mainstream, active in interfaith activities, and definitely not wackadoodle.<br /> ++++++++++++++++<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Pre-Game Workout</u></b><br /> <br /> As has been clear since the end of September, the state championship football game is between the Avalon Knights and the Etna Titans. The Knights are undefeated; the Titans lost only once, to the Knights. In the week before the big game, Coach Reagan is near frantic with anxiety, driving the players to practice longer and harder, so intense is his desire to finally win the championship from the Titans. Nevertheless, Gus and Jack find time for a few other things; so do the other Scions.<br /> <br /> HOLLY receives a visit from Delia Bright. The cheerleader Scion of Apollo says she thinks the two got off on the wrong footing at the start of the year, and she\u2019d like to start over. This isn\u2019t just personal: Delia thinks that she, Holly and Barbara should work together to manage the other Scions, since they are the three with the social-fu and the sense to apply it. Holly agrees to what Delia says. Holly also figures out (it isn\u2019t hard) that Barbara put the idea in Delia\u2019s head. We shall see what becomes of this hidden alliance.<br /> <br /> GUS receives a text message from Thoth regarding the Vettersons: Sobek asked Thoth why Gary usually looks human to divine eyes, when giants normally look human only to clueless mortals. Thoth says it\u2019s because Gary (and Reese) have more practice pretending to be human than at being giants, and some of the humanity rubs off. The two young hrimthurssar also must have mixed Virtues \u2014 some Dark Virtues of the Titans, some Divine Virtues. This probably makes them less reflexively malevolent, but no less dangerous.<br /> <br /> Gus also has a talk with the late Sheena\u2019s father. Mr. Delisle spots Gus at a service station. He asks Gus, again, if he\u2019s heard anything from the disappeared Sheena or can remember any hint that something was wrong with her, that she had enemies, etc. Gus says he does not. His answers seem to satisfy Mr. Delisle. Sheena\u2019s father says, though, that he remains convinced there is something to be discovered in Sheena\u2019s disappearance, and he isn\u2019t waiting for the cops: He\u2019s hiring private detectives. If anyone harmed his little girl, he <i>will </i>find them, and they <i>will </i>pay for it.<br /> <br /> Gus also has a talk with Leo. He has no better luck than Jaeda in persuading the Scion of Nergal that being part pagan deity is a good thing; but he tells Leo that whatever Nergal really is, God or demon, Leo doesn\u2019t have to be like his father. He can assert himself and be his own man. Leo\u2019s resolve hardens and says he has an idea how to do this.<br /> <br /> Gus has also acquired his suit of chain mail. It cost hundreds of dollars. This and paying for half of the cement fill for the tomb leaves his savings significantly depleted.<br /> <br /> JACK keeps an eye on local reports of weird events. The latest is the death of a shi tzu belonging to a Mrs. Wickersham, of Etna. The dog\u2019s barking woke her up. She looked outside to see what riled the dog and saw it killed by what she insists was a leopard. Animal Control says it must have been a cougar (they sometimes come out of the nearby hills and forests and attack pets, and no big cats have escaped from local zoos). Jack moseys over to Mrs. Wickersham\u2019s and applies Instant Investigator. He comes away convinced that was no cougar, and that the putative leopard consciously slew the dog. He can\u2019t tell why, though.<br /> <br /> A day or so later, Jack arrives at school to find that someone broke into the school at night and stole Sir Dance-A-Lot\u2019s sword from the trophy display case. (More precisely, they stole the sword the Scions made at the end of \u201cDay of the Dead.\u201d) This was not a Master Burglary, as the case was jimmied open. The thief didn\u2019t take anything else.<br /> <br /> Coach Reagan is furious. He believes kids from Etna High took the sword as a prank. He calls Jack and Warren into his office and sternly tells them that it would be <i>very wrong </i>for them to avenge this insult to the honor of the school and the team by stealing or despoiling the stilts and \u201cgiant suit\u201d worn by the Titans\u2019 team mascot, or the plaster volcano in which the cheer squad sets off fireworks when the team makes a goal. <i>Do they understand</i>? Warren does. Once they leave Coach\u2019s office, he asks Jack if he\u2019s coming along to prank Etna High.<br /> <br /> Jack joins him in the prank (slipping into Etna High, spraying Liquid Heat and Nair inside the \u201cgiant suit,\u201d setting off a messy baking soda-and-vinegar eruption in the plaster Mount Etna). Along the way, though, Warren makes no secret that he resents being overshadowed by Gus and Jack, and he thinks they must be juicing \u2014 some new designer steroid. Warren also saw how Horndog Bob and the other thralls came back to school significantly bigger and stronger than they were before. He tells Jack to \u2018fess up and say what he and Gus are using, because Warren thinks he\u2019ll find out anyway.<br /> <br /> Jack makes a big decision: He makes sure no one else can see them, picks up a small car and says that no, he doesn\u2019t use steroids, and what he <i>does </i>have, Warren can\u2019t possibly get. Warren is bummed despite Jack\u2019s attempts to buck him up with praise of Warren\u2019s hard training and discipline. Warren can see that no matter how hard he trains, he will never equal Jack and Gus. (What are they, mutants? Aliens? \u201cSomething like that. Does it matter?\u201d No, not really.) But, Jack stresses that he and Gus will be team players. In fact, they will do their best to keep Warren central to the big game.<br /> <br /> JAEDA thinks she\u2019s getting through to Leo when he asks her for pointers on knife fighting. Not that he intends to fight with a knife, but he wants to know how to defend himself. They meet in the school gym after classes. Jaeda shows Leo the proper ways to evade and disarm. While they practice, though, Leo suddenly twists to hurl his back against the First Flower. Jaeda tries to pull the knife away, but the God-slaying dagger sinks deeply into Leo\u2019s back. Leo falls, almost mortally wounded.<br /> <br /> Jaeda manages to stabilize Leo before getting him to an emergency room. Before shock sets in, Leo explains that he was trying to cut the tattoo Nergal put on his back as a way to sever the lion-demon\u2019s connection to him. He thinks it worked but he\u2026 kind of\u2026 overdid it\u2026 <i>ow!</i><br /> <br /> At the emergency room, Jaeda says she entered the gym and found Leo there, already wounded. Leo says he didn\u2019t see who stabbed him. Of course, the hospital has to call Leo\u2019s parents; Jaeda quickly calls Barbara in to run interference, using her Social Fu to make sure nobody says something that could get Leo in trouble, such as, \u201cWhat\u2019s with the big lion tattoo on your son\u2019s back?\u201d<br /> <br /> The cops also quickly note the wound\u2019s resemblance to Acacia\u2019s stab wound. Coming on top of the disappearance of Sheena and murder of Crystal (back in Episodes 1-3), the cops are convinced there\u2019s a serial killer in Avalon.<br /> <br /> Barbara needs more Social Fu to prevent the doctors from asking awkward questions when Leo heals in two days. (Most of the health levels lost were \u20130, which Scions heal in 6 hours each.) She convinces the relevant people that the wound was not as bad as it looked. Yeah, it only <i>looked </i>like it went clear through his body and punctured a lung\u2026<br /> <br /> When Leo gets out of the hospital, though, the lion-demon comes off his back, gives Leo a beat-down, and says that if Leo ever tries to do this again, the demon will kill Leo\u2019s parents. Leo can\u2019t stay wounded forever; he has to take the threat seriously.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T14:37:59-08:00","id":1014963,"title":"","content":"EPISODE SEVEN: THE BIG GAME (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Game Plan</u></b><br /> <br /> Gus has another conversation. Gary Vetterson parks his car in front of the Sobeck driveway so he can confront Gus. Gary has an axe. Gus gets his fenrir-fang cesti from the glove compartment of his car, but Gary says the axe is just in case Gus decides to start something. Gus used a weapon in their last fight, so Gary is prepared to use weapons right back.<br /> <br /> Getting to the point, Gary says the Knights cheated in their game against the Titans. They used supernatural powers, beyond their own strength, speed and skill. The Titans played an honest game. Well, that won\u2019t be the case in the championship. Gary\u2019s team will use whatever supernatural advantage they can swing. Anything to win. That\u2019s the way children of the Gods do it, after all.<br /> <br /> This declaration worries the Scions. They know Gary\u2019s mother Hyndla can cast at least one curse. She probably knows the Evil Eye as well. Acacia certainly does \u2014 that\u2019s two witches to hex the Knights. Jack and Holly then watch game footage of the Titans from throughout the season. They figure out that Gary isn\u2019t the only supernatural player: A running back, Kwame \u201cThe Cat\u201d Morrison, has Epic Dexterity. Once he gets the ball, no one can catch him or tackle him unless he lets them. (Which he does, much of the time, so he seems to be merely skilled and lucky. Hey, the Titans don\u2019t need a touchdown every play to win games.) The Titans typically use Kwame late in the game as a sort of \u201csecret weapon.\u201d The five players on the Titans line who take their direction from Gary \u2014 almost certainly thralls \u2014 collectively are worth one Scion. That gives the Titans effectively three supernatural players to the Knights\u2019 two. (Oh, and the Scions are pretty sure they\u2019ve found their hypothetical were-leopard.)<br /> <br /> The Scions brainstorm various ways they might sneak Marshall into the team or otherwise even the odds, but they eventually decide that A) there are limits to how much they\u2019re willing to cheat, and B) they can\u2019t think how to do it anyway. They settle on a two-stage plan\u2026 but it means going against their divine parents\u2019 instructions to hide the supernatural from mortals.<br /> <br /> First, Holly and Jaeda will stalk the stands, trying to prevent Hyndla Vetterson and Acacia from cursing the Knights. If they can\u2019t, Holly will curse Gary and Kwame to even the playing field as best she can.<br /> <br /> Second, Jack will feed Bolvarg\u2019s eitr to his buds on the Knights\u2019 defensive line. This will make them stronger and tougher, at cost of temporarily stupider and more easily enraged. The eitr\u2019s effect doesn\u2019t last long, so he\u2019ll do this in the last quarter. Of course, this means telling the five players something about the supernatural: They are not among the great minds of the Western world, but there\u2019s no way to hide that they\u2019ll be drinking blood and feeling a surge in strength. Well, Jack already told Warren\u2026<br /> <br /> Jack talks to his five buds and convinces them that he is not insane. He stresses that the boost from the magical blood is a one-time thing: There ain\u2019t no more, and they can\u2019t obtain eitr by themselves. (He is not strictly truthful: The Band has 12 more doses of eitr taken from the Sarsen twins.) They will know things they can never tell, make commitments they might not be able to renounce. They will be in the Big Game of Gods and monsters. And the five agree, confirming their place as Jack\u2019s Followers.<br /> <br /> And that\u2019s all the Scions can do until the game itself.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Gods of the Gridiron</u></b><br /> <br /> The state championship game takes place in Husky Stadium. Lots of people want to watch the two most exciting and powerful teams Washington State high schools have ever produced.<br /> <br /> The first quarter, the Titans start with an immediate hard press against the Knights \u2014 especially Gus and Jack. In the stands, Holly and Jaeda immediately spot the Vetterson family, but they can\u2019t find Acacia until the young witch curses Gus with the Evil Eye. Then she\u2019s obvious to Holly, who sees her with The Unlidded Eye. Acacia has disguised herself by coloring her hair, removing her piercing jewelry, covering her tattoos with makeup and wearing a turtleneck over her Relic necklace. Holly counter-curses Gary while she and Jaeda try to catch Acacia. Both cursed, Gary and Gus experience weak first quarters. Overall, however, the Knights do well thanks to the Scions keeping Warren in the center of plays instead of themselves, with able direction of the rest of the team by Warren and Coach Reagan.<br /> <br /> Holly and Jaeda spend the second quarter slowly chasing Acacia through the stands. Acacia tries a few stratagems such as insta-flirting with big guys in hopes they can obstruct the two Scions, or accusing the two of purse-snatching in hopes they\u2019ll be thrown out. Jaeda and Holly counter them with their own Epic Appearance and Social Fu. In the end, they corner Acacia, rip her necklace away and tell her to get lost.<br /> <br /> Then the two girls turn to the Vettersons. Hyndla can indeed cast the Evil Eye, and Holly can tell that Hyndla can do it more times than she can thanks to her higher Legend. So, Holly uses Overt Order to make Hyndla curse Etna\u2019s cheerleaders instead. Gary\u2019s father, Suttung, catches wise at once: He watches Holly, and when Holly tries to stop Hyndla from cursing the Knights, he interrupts and tells Holly not to. He has Overt Order too! But, Jaeda trumps that gambit by plopping on Suttung\u2019s lap and\u2026 <i>distracting </i>him. Hyndla ripostes by casting the Illwind Curse on Jaeda; but Jaeda defends with Hapless Cool, making her sudden gas attack funny instead of disgusting. Hyndla looks ready to escalate to tearing Jaeda limb from limb \u2014 or maybe Suttung \u2014 but Holly points out that if the Vetterson\u2019s make a scene, they will attract enough attention that they can\u2019t curse the Knights anyway. Gary\u2019s parents grumpily accept the stalemate. Reese, who was also present, went from looking daggers at the two Scions to belly-laughing at the spectacle.<br /> <br /> The two witches neutralized, Holly and Jaeda settle down to watch the rest of the game. Holly gives Reese the necklace to return to Acacia after the game. (It isn\u2019t as if either Scion could use it themselves.)<br /> <br /> Down on the field, Gus and Jack start spending Willpower to counter the few curses they receive, and enjoy a bit of luck besides. The Titans coach realizes he needs to counter all the Knights, not just the two Scions, and this gives Gus and Jack a few chances to score.<br /> <br /> After halftime, the Titans start relying more on Kwame and make up some lost points. Gary also starts playing harder, further pushing the boundaries of human possibility. The Knights maintain their lead, but just barely.<br /> <br /> In the last quarter, Jack\u2019s defensive line Followers drink their eitr. Now they are a match for Gary\u2019s thralls, and match them they do. Gary starts spending Legend: Up in the stands, Holly can see the flashes of power. At those moments, the Scions see him as an eight-foot-six giant with frost on his jersey. Gus and Jack, however, can counter Gary\u2019s strength with their own. The Titans shift their strategy almost completely to passing the ball to Kwame. Once \u201cthe Cat\u201d has the ball, neither of the Scions can catch him. So, they adjust their own strategy: Keep the ball from Kwame.<br /> <br /> As the minutes tick down, Gary has the ball. A single touchdown for the Titans could put the game in a tie. Checked by Gus and Jack, Gary throws a high pass to Kwame \u2014 far too high for anyone to intercept. Warren shouts, \u201cThrow me!\u201d and runs at Gus. Gus makes stirrups of his hands, Warren jumps on, and Gus hurls Warren after the football. Warren fails to catch it \u2014 but his hand slaps against the ball, sending it away from Kwame. The Titans don\u2019t get their touchdown. Warren face-plants on the field, but his pads and helmet (and the <b>Scion</b> soak rules) mean he\u2019s hardly bruised. The Knights win the game with the most spectacular play ever seen in high school football! Coach Reagan manages not to gloat too obviously as he shakes hands with the Etna High coach\u2026 whom the Scions now can see looks enough like Coach Reagan to be his brother.<br /> <br /> After the game, Gary confronts Gus again. Of course he is angry at losing, but he\u2019s also ebullient at the bitchingest game he\u2019s ever been in. Football season is over, but\u2026 \u201cWinter quarter. Wrestling?\u201d Gus says that was his plan, too. Gary says that one-on-one, he\u2019ll beat Gus. Gus says he looks forward to seeing Gary try.<br /> <br /> The post-game party at Gus\u2019 house is suitably epic. The experienced revellers revel. Holly lets her hair down enough to drink just a <i>little,</i> and then a little more. Some people are angry drunks, happy drunks or crying drunks: Holly turns out to be a pontificating drunk. Gus orders the family chauffeur to take Holly safely home (yes, the Sobecks are rich enough to have a chauffeur), but Jaeda intercepts him. Instead, Holly ends up sleeping naked in Gus\u2019 bed. Gus is gentleman enough not to take advantage, though, and sleeps on the couch.<br /> <br /> The next morning, the four Scions wake up feeling\u2026 different. Good. <i>Powerful.</i> Each of them has risen to Legend 3.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-09T22:04:04-08:00","id":1015160,"title":"","content":"So, what happened to Gene's player? Did I miss something?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T07:28:47-08:00","id":1015244,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1015160#post1015160\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, what happened to Gene's player? Did I miss something?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> He wasn't present during the session. So they had to make do.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T13:43:24-08:00","id":1015357,"title":"","content":"I think he was meaning Gene plain relocating across the US suddenly, that smacks of a player who had to drop the game suddenly due to schedule things or something.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12596-alpharius","name":"Alpharius","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12596_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"306"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-10T16:10:13-08:00","id":1015398,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Alpharius</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1015357#post1015357\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think he was meaning Gene plain relocating across the US suddenly, that smacks of a player who had to drop the game suddenly due to schedule things or something.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Oh, right. D:","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-11T10:01:27-08:00","id":1015709,"title":"","content":"Alpharius is correct: Gene's player dropped out. Turns out a personality problem between players was more serious than Gene's player let on at the time.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-11T10:04:23-08:00","id":1015711,"title":"","content":"EPISODE EIGHT: ROAD TRIP<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: A New Quarter Begins</u></b><br /> <br /> The Big Game was also the end of Fall Quarter. With new classes and newly raised Legend, the Scions await new challenges\u2026<br /> <br /> In no particular order:<br /> <br /> GUS has not had to clash with Burt, the school\u2019s most incorrigible bully, and his cronies. They know better than to challenge him. One day, though, he sees one of Burt\u2019s friends try to score brownie points by tripping Leo as the Scion of Nergal hurries through the hall. Leo, however, has Epic Dexterity and Cat\u2019s Grace: He keeps his footing and doesn\u2019t even <i>notice </i>the attempt. This annoys Burt and his friends. As luck would have it, Landon Smith passes by at that time. Burt remembers how Landon humiliated him on the first day of school. He and his buds suddenly surround Landon and start beating him down. Landon isn\u2019t enough of a judo fighter to defend himself against three bigger, stronger boys attacking him at once and from surprise.<br /> <br /> This offends Gus. He catches Burt\u2019s two cronies by their thumbs and twists, forcing them to their knees. Burt stops kicking the fallen Landon. Gus notes that three on one is not fair, and he asks everyone how they want to resolve the situation. Landon says he\u2019ll fight all three, one at a time, if that\u2019s what they want; but it comes down to Burt vs. Landon behind the gym after school. Gus promises/threatens to act as referee.<br /> <br /> The fight is pretty even: Burt is bigger and stronger than Landon, and driven by fear of humiliation before his cronies \u2014 but he has never genuinely trained at fighting or self-defense. Burt ends up lying on the ground with Landon\u2019s foot on his throat. The three bullies slink off in defeat; Gus feels he has seen justice done.<br /> <br /> JAEDA keeps an eye on the gym after school, watching for a day when Leo trains. When this happens, she enters to do some sparring. First, though, she belts Leo across the face and tells him that if he <i>ever </i>tries to throw himself on her blade again, she will make him regret it. Did he think for one moment what could have happened if he\u2019d died? He could have started a war between the Anuna and the Atzl\u00e1nti! Leo crumples and says he\u2019s sorry\u2026 but Jaeda gets a weird vibe. She Reads Motivation and realizes that in a perverse way, Leo likes being punished. He thinks he deserves it, to the extent that this is the center of his sense of self. (<i>I.e</i>., his nature is Wretch.) He is the perfect abuser\u2019s victim, and she has just reinforced this. Jaeda tries to get back to the impersonal practicality of training, but she realizes that Leo is even more messed-up than she thought.<br /> <br /> (Though it didn\u2019t really matter in this adventure, the Scions also found that Leo\u2019s Legend rose to 3 as well, thanks to his nearly-suicidal attempt to cut himself free of his lion-demon Guide. Leo\u2019s Legend may be one of torment and self-loathing, but it\u2019s still a Legend.)<br /> <br /> Jaeda also teaches a pair of Fashionistas not to mess with her. After PE, she opens her locker and finds that someone has stolen her customary trampwear and replaced it with the dowdiest, most old-fashioned dress imaginable. Two Fashionistas are instantly ready with camera-phones to spread the image of her with the dress, and to mock her with snide questions about who\u2019s taking her to the barn dance.<br /> <br /> Jaeda doesn\u2019t play along. She glares coldly at Vanessa, the Fashionista who is closest to her size, and says she will take <i>Vanessa\u2019s </i>clothes instead and Vanessa can wear the dress. And she will <i>hurt </i>Vanessa if she resists. Jaeda offers to return Vanessa\u2019s clothes when her own clothes are returned to her regular locker. Vanessa decides that discretion is the better part of survival, and so she puts on the horrible dress. Jaeda then plucks up Vanessa\u2019s phone, snaps a picture, and sends it to everyone on the Fashionista\u2019s friends list. She finds her own clothes hanging by her hallway locker before the day is out.<br /> <br /> JACK feels pretty good. Doctor Demerit gave his first \u201cquizzybubble\u201d (or pop quiz) of Winter Quarter. Holly competes fiercely with Jack to get better grades in AP Math \u2014 but this time, Jack surpasses her. Both answered every question correctly, did the extra credit\u2026 but Jack gave every answer in base 8 as well as base 10, for <i>extra </i>extra credit. (Doctor Demerit noted in the margin that for a real challenge, though, Jack should have used base 7.) Jack tells Holly not to feel bad at only getting A+ instead of A++; everyone knows that girls aren\u2019t good at math.<br /> <br /> Jack feels not so good when his mother tells him that now that football season is over, he should get an afterschool job. The bank has unexpectedly bumped up the mortgage payments on the house; Jack eats a lot; a community college salary only goes so far. Jack does the math (those classes from Dr. Demerit sure help) and says he\u2019ll start looking right away.<br /> <br /> Hooverdale Mall holds the greatest local concentration of entry-level jobs, and Jack knows where he wants to start: Stick It, in the food court. He\u2019s already semi-famous for eating one of every item on the menu of Food-Like Products On Sticks in a single meal. Jack gives the manager his most heartfelt speech about how much he wants to work at Stick It. When the manager says he already has enough employees, Jack offers to intern: He will work free for a week, to show his skill and dedication.<br /> <br /> Hyndla Vetterson walks by and hears this exchange. She stops and adds, in a friendly, supportive tone, that Jack\u2019s almost certainly over that head lice infestation. Jack puts on his best bright, false smile, greets Mrs. Vetterson, and says the new girdle suits her, you can hardly tell how much weight she\u2019s gained\u2026 Hyndla barely restrains herself from burying Jack in the rubble of the food court and stalks off.<br /> <br /> Jack gets the internship. Woohoo, he gets to wear a hat that looks like he has a stick thrust through his head! And he\u2019d better do well in the internship, because when he leaves the mall he finds a ball of crumpled metal that used to be his car.<br /> <br /> HOLLY learns a few things.<br /> <br /> Derek asks her if ghosts are real. Holly says yes. Derek says that\u2019s a relief, because he\u2019s seen a few. Yup, his divinely-regenerated eyes give him Death Senses. He is also now a legendary mortal.<br /> <br /> Holly also learns about Jack\u2019s new internship. Thinking this is just too rich, she buys the Stick It franchise at the mall so that she, in fact, is now Jack\u2019s boss. Of course she doesn\u2019t tell him. Jack gets his entry-level job. When the time is right, though\u2026<br /> <br /> Most importantly, Aaron tells Holly some bad news. Suddenly, his father is trying to sell the house and business. The Avalon Slasher makes the town just too dangerous, so he wants to move the family somewhere else.<br /> <br /> Holly wangles an invitation to have dinner with the Bindahnas and arranges to be alone with Aaron\u2019s dad. She tells him that she knows there\u2019s some other reason why he wants to move the family, and she knows they are not what they seem. Mr. Bindahna blanches, and Holly can tell this is the sort of fear that can make people do violent, desperate things. She quickly reassures Mr. Bindahna that she is no enemy, declares herself as a Scion, and says she wants to help. Mr. Bindahna still won\u2019t tell her much, but he says the spectacular game against Etna High will attract attention to the area \u2014 attention that could be very bad for his family. They are refugees \u2014 runaway slaves, in fact \u2014 living in hiding as mortals. He pleads with Holly not to tell Aaron or his little sister Janet the truth, as it\u2019s safer for them not to know what they really are. All this reinforces Holly\u2019s suspicion that the Bindahnas are a family of jinn. She accepts Mr. Bindahna\u2019s reasons for moving the family, though, and says she won\u2019t interfere.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Let\u2019s Try That Again\u2026</u></b><br /> <br /> Jack subscribes to a \u201cWeirdNet\u201d service that collects and distributes reports of strange events. During a morning class, his phone pings to let him know of a new, local WeirdNet report: a pink python seen at the sanitary landfill that serves Avalon, Etna and nearby regions.<br /> <br /> Ever since the lindwurm attack at Hooverdale Mall, people in the area have occasionally reported seeing big pinkish worms or snakes. The Scions successfully deluded the authorities into dismissing the mall lindwurm as a bizarre stunt by a drug gang, intended to frighten people away from a major drug deal. Since then, opinion has been divided whether there is an actual \u201cpink python\u201d in the area, whether the reports indicate a continuing hoax by the same gang, copycat hoaxes by local people, or whether the whole thing is mass hysteria. Undeniably, pets and local farm animals continue to disappear.<br /> <br /> In the ensuing months, the Scions did encounter hoax lindwurms (some kid paints a length of dryer outlet pipe pink, attaches a cardboard head, and poses it in some bushes). On the other hand, Marshall slew the lindwurm in the graveyard that held the Xibalba-portal crypt. So, Jack alerts the rest of the Band and they plan to cut the next class and hunt the lindwurm before it kills anyone at the landfill. Jack also notifies Marshall, since he wants to make nice and figures the Band can use all the fighters they can get. Holly, however, decides to stay in class: She\u2019s sure the rest can handle a lindwurm without her.<br /> <br /> As planned, the Scions leave school and rendezvous at the dump. (Jack needs to beg a ride with another Scion.) They find Marshall already there with his five army-brat buddies, and they\u2019re already battling a lindwurm amid the stinking garbage. As the Scions hurry toward the fight, another lindwurm erupts from a mound of garbage and attacks them.<br /> <br /> The fight does not take long, since the four Scions have a better idea about monster-killing than they did at the mall. Some attacks bounce \u2014 even, to Jack\u2019s frustration, his new stone war-club (lindwurms have high Bashing soak). A few decent stunts later, though, Jaeda manages to strike the Nemean worm inside its mouth\u2026 and the lindwurm <i>swallows </i>the First Flower. The inexorable peristalsis of the lindwurm\u2019s gut sends the knife cutting through its flesh from the inside until the knife drops out the worm\u2019s side and the monster falls dead. (Jaeda\u2019s last attack pushed the lindwurm into Dying health levels anyway; might as well make the death cool.)<br /> <br /> Marshall swaggers over, prepared to boast about killing the lindwurm before the others showed up. The PCs point to the worm they just slew. Their debate on how to dispose of the corpses is interrupted by the arrival of Ms. Dortwade, the school district\u2019s truant officer. Ms. Dortwade, a pickle-faced, middle-aged woman, smiles with grim delight at the chance to get Jack and Jaeda in trouble again. She is disappointed to see \u201cgood kids\u201d like Gus and Marshall drawn into their shenanigans.<br /> <br /> And then a <i>third</i> lindwurm erupts from the ground, right in front of Officer Dortwade, confronting her with a horror undreamed of in her narrow, spiteful world. She faints.<br /> <br /> The five Scions (and five gun-toting army brats) make short work of the lindwurm, so it doesn\u2019t get a chance to eat Officer Dortwade. Jack cleans her up as best he can under the circumstances and carries her to her car. He wakes her up and tells her she had a fainting spell, then asks if they should they call an ambulance. Dortwade still seems dazed but focuses on the stable, familiar and undeniable fact that Jack and the others are <i>skipping class.</i> She hauls them back to Avalon High. They get detention. \u201cBig whoop,\u201d Jaeda says. She gets detention almost every week.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-11T10:06:19-08:00","id":1015712,"title":"","content":"EPISODE EIGHT: ROAD TRIP (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: When In Doubt, Add Ninjas</u></b><br /> <br /> A few days later, Gus is giving his evening Self-Defense For Young People class at the Y. It\u2019s the usual mix of people who want to learn to protect themselves and girls who want to spend time close to a brawny young studmuffin. Suddenly, the skylight shatters as a man in a trenchcoat falls through it. Thinking quickly, Gus grabs a wrestling mat and sweeps it over the heads of his students, protecting them from the rain of glass shards. \u201cDon\u2019t let them get the package,\u201d the man gasps; he presumably refers to the package he clutches in his arms. Immediately thereafter, several ninjas jump down through the opening, swords drawn!<br /> <br /> Thinking quickly again, Gus roars, \u201cRun!\u201d to the stupefied students. He then grabs the stranger and jumps up through the broken skylight to the roof, and starts following his own advice.<br /> The ninjas leap back up and out as well. They hurl several shuriken at Gus, but they miss. Gus finds he can run faster and leap much farther than the ninjas, so he gets away.<br /> <br /> As he runs, Gus wonders who in this situation are the good guys and who are the bad guys, the wounded stranger or the ninjas. He decides that ninja assassins aren\u2019t usually the good guys. Besides, these ninjas made <i>no sound at all,</i> which is uncanny, and makes them seem even less likely to be on a noble mission.<br /> <br /> At first, Gus thinks he should take the mystery-man to a hospital. The man protests that the \u201cshinobi\u201d who were after him would think to check every hospital nearby. So, Gus calls Jaeda, since she has some first aid skills, and takes the mystery-man to Holly\u2019s house. Along the way he calls in Jack as well. The mystery man has a further surprise, though: He asks Gus, \u201cYou\u2019re a Scion, right? Good.\u201d Or, maybe not such a surprise. After all, a man chased by creepy silent ninjas is probably in the Big Game.<br /> <br /> Jaeda stabilizes the wounded man and the Scions get as much of the mystery-man\u2019s story as he\u2019s willing to tell. Contrary to their first suspicion, the man who calls himself Riley Krieger is not a Scion. He is just a highly skilled mortal who works for various Gods, mostly as a courier. He <i>has </i>to be highly skilled, since anything a God would use a courier to deliver is something other people \u2014 or entities \u2014 would like to take for themselves. Krieger says he doesn\u2019t know what he\u2019s carrying: What he doesn\u2019t know, he can\u2019t be made to tell. He just picked up the package from one operative and set out to deliver it somewhere else. Krieger also explains that the ninjas are from a special cult that all other ninjas shun. The cult serves Mikaboshi, the Titan of Darkness, and its prophet, the Black Bodhisattva. These \u201cshinobi\u201d gain minor supernatural powers from their service, but they lose their souls.<br /> <br /> Holly decides she needs more information before committing to anything, so she pulls out her mirror and applies Mystery. She learns that Krieger carries the Bell of Endless Night. Remarkably, Holly has heard of this! (Bunch o\u2019 suxx on Intelligence + Occult gets you this.) The bell in some way reveals every place where the power of the Titan of Darkness bleeds through into the mortal World. It would be very useful to the Gods and a great loss to the Titans. Krieger is meant to deliver this powerful Relic to \u2014 <i>Classified.</i> Well, it\u2019s someone powerful enough to block supernatural inquiries, and it would sure suck for the Gods if servants of the Titans could pull their secrets out of the ether\u2026 But Krieger is sincere, and Holly decides she has enough information to trust his mission. (For now.)<br /> <br /> The Scions know they can\u2019t just turtle up and try to fight off the shinobi. Krieger assures them that while only a dozen are after him at the moment, dozens more can come within a day or two, plus Gods know what else. He needs to complete his mission and disappear.<br /> <br /> Krieger is in no condition to travel on his own, so the Scions offer to help him deliver his package if it\u2019s not too far. Where was he going? Krieger says he was to deliver it to Jewel Cave in the Black Hills of South Dakota. The Scions check a map, do a little quick math and figure they can drive all night and reach the cave by mid-morning. It will of course mean missing a day of school before they can drive back, but what the hell.<br /> <br /> Gus calls his father to say he just met two girls who are really, really big fans of his football exploits and he won\u2019t be home for\u2026 well, who knows. His father accepts this with a smug \u201cThat\u2019s my boy.\u201d Jaeda\u2019s father knows the deal. Holly does not need to ask anyone\u2019s permission. Jack simply decides to seek forgiveness later.<br /> <br /> Off Krieger and the Scions go in Jaeda\u2019s Roadrunner. They switch roads frequently, choosing randomly at intersections, in hopes this may confuse any supernatural or divinatory pursuit. They know, however, that they have limited choices of passes through the Cascade and Rocky Mountains. If the shinobi can attack, they\u2019ll do it at those choke-points. Krieger adds that the shinobi can\u2019t act during the day, so the Rocky Mountains will be the supernatural ninjas\u2019 last chance to take back the package.<br /> <br /> The Scions get through the Cascades without incident. As they predicted, though, the shinobi catch up to them in the Rocky Mountains. (Specifically, the Big Belt Range, while they drive from Bozeman to Livingston, Montana.) Jaeda drives. (She\u2019s the one with Control: Car.) As they speed through the pass a truck slowly pulls up behind their car, with shinobi in the back.<br /> <br /> Jack decides to deal with the truck. He climbs out the window of their car and onto the roof. As the truck approaches, he jumps, already swinging his war-club at the truck\u2019s engine. He strikes a tremendous blow: The truck\u2019s engine starts sputtering. Jack uses the momentum of the truck and his impact to bounce him cartwheeling back up the road to the Scions\u2019 car.<br /> <br /> At the same time, two shinobi appear on the hood of the car, weapons ready. The Scions, however, are readier. Before the shinobi can cut through the windshield, Gus snarls at one and hits him with Game Face. Holly taps the windshield in front of the other to draw his attention, and shouts, \u201cJump!\u201d through the open window \u2014 using Overt Order. By the revised \u201cUnnatural Mental Influence\u201d rules, they must obey for one action before they spend Willpower\u2026 The two shinobi bail. Their truck, meanwhile, falls behind. Jack didn\u2019t wreck the truck, but he caused enough damage to slow it.<br /> <br /> Holly suspects this won\u2019t be the end of the shinobi\u2019s attempts, even if the Scions get a chance to switch roads. (She guesses the shinobi cult might have Prophecy about what roads the Scions will take, or multiple people with Mystery working in sequence.) She calls on Smoking Mirror to watch the road ahead for further attacks, and she finds one: a car speeding toward them, with an eighteen-wheeler approaching at a more leisurely pace. The speeding car is almost certainly an attack; the Sea-Land truck might just be ordinary commerce.<br /> <br /> The Scions ready themselves. This time, Gus climbs out the window and onto the hood of the car. If necessary, he intends to try pushing the shinobi\u2019s car away from the Scions\u2019 car. The shinobi\u2019s car moves to the middle of the road. Maybe the driver is playing chicken with Jaeda, or maybe he just intends to crash his car into the Scions.<br /> <br /> It doesn\u2019t matter, though. Jaeda looks like she\u2019s playing chicken right back, but at the last second she swerves to pass the shinobi\u2019s car with less than an inch between them and two wheels up on the embankment. (Legendary success at Control.) The shinobi driver does not do as well. He swerves, trying to hit the Scions anyway, but loses control of the speeding car and misses the next turn on the mountain road. The shinobi\u2019s car goes over the edge in best Hollywood fashion.<br /> <br /> As for the eighteen-wheeler? Jaeda faces another challenge to pass by it at high speed on the curving road, but she does so with ease. The surprised truck driver also keeps control of his vehicle. It wasn\u2019t another shinobi attack, just normal traffic.<br /> <br /> The sun rises half an hour later, and the Scions hope they are in the clear. They arrive at Jewel Cave a few hours later.<br /> <br /> They join the first tour of the day. The cave is well worth seeing \u2014 magnificently decorated with crystals and strange, gleaming gypsum formations. At a certain point, though, Krieger nudges the Scions and they leave the path for a tunnel opening that no one else on the tour seems to notice. They enter a cavern hung with thousands of slender stalactites that resemble long soda straws.<br /> <br /> A human figure steps out of the wall, like a person-shaped silhouette or cutout made of the stone surface. The figure asks for the package. Krieger hands it over. The Scions tense for the sudden but inevitable betrayal or revelation that they\u2019ve been played for suckers. It doesn\u2019t happen. The figure merely thanks them.<br /> <br /> Jack and Holly both try using their Epic Mental Knacks to find some clue to the unknown God\u2019s identity. (They\u2019re pretty sure it must be a God.) With his Instant Investigator, Jack spots the figure tapping his fingers irregularly against his thigh while he talks. It\u2019s Morse code for, \u201cGood work, kids. Say hello to Jaeda.\u201d<br /> <br /> The mystery God offers to get them back to Avalon. The Scions accept. The God opens a passage through the rock back to the surface. Krieger stays in South Dakota; the God takes the wheel of the Roadrunner. He steers it down into the ground. A few minutes later, the car enters a huge cavern. The God drives through the tunnels and caverns for about half an hour (and the Scions catch glimpses of strange things off to the side), then steers the car back through the rock to the surface and the Avalon High parking lot. He thanks them again and sinks back into the ground.<br /> <br /> When Jack tells Jaeda what he noticed, Jaeda guesses the mystery God was Quetzalco\u00e1tl. He\u2019s an intellectual showoff, has some earth associations, and knows about Jaeda \u2014 after all, she carries his knife. He\u2019s also certainly magician enough to block anyone else\u2019s use of Mystery. But the Scions can\u2019t really be sure. The Scions go in to their classes. Everyone gets detention for coming in late without notes from their parents, but nobody much cares.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-11T10:07:41-08:00","id":1015713,"title":"","content":"I might not be able to post again for some days, but have no fear: Scion High will continue!<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-16T15:32:07-08:00","id":1018235,"title":"","content":"EPISODE NINE: P.S., This Is A Trap<br /> <br /> A shorter episode this time.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Interludes with Gary</u></b><br /> <br /> It\u2019s been a quiet week in Avalon, at least up to now. The Scions go about their daily lives. So do their rivals. Sometimes, they intersect\u2026<br /> <br /> HOLLY is not especially surprised that Friday when Aaron Bindahna asks her out on a date. She knew he was getting a crush on her. (So are her Followers Manny Salkind and Bobby Kilmer, and half the boys in the Mathlympics, Chess Club and Knowledge Bowl.) She lets Aaron take her to a concert (small and arty, not stadium rock) and to dinner afterward at Olive Garden.<br /> <br /> Gary also shows up at the restaurant with his current girlfriend \u2014 who won\u2019t be his girlfriend any longer. Whatever they planned for their evening, they are now having a fight. Girlfriend is upset that Gary looks at other girls and wants more of his time. Gary says she\u2019s clingy and needy (likely true), and that he can get what he wants from plenty of other girls (well\u2026 also likely true). A waiter tells Gary and Girl to lower their voices; Gary throws the waiter across the room.<br /> <br /> Holly decides she really doesn\u2019t like Gary. She walks over and tells him he\u2019s a pig\u2026 so eat like one! Gary obeys the Overt Order, and promptly drops his face into the antipasto tray. Girl giggles. Aaron goggles. Holly decides they\u2019d better get the hell out before Gary recovers. Seconds after they reach the street, they hear an angry roar from inside the Olive Garden and a whole table comes flying through the window.<br /> <br /> Holly thanks Aaron for a nice evening and gives him a peck on the cheek. Aaron believes the evening was a disaster.<br /> <br /> JACK now spends 20 hours a week working at Stick It. This means his football team Followers (Moose, Bull, Bear, Ox and Gnu) hang out there as well, hoping to score free food. (Sometimes they do, especially at day\u2019s end when stuff would otherwise be thrown out.)<br /> <br /> Thanksgiving is coming, so Jack\u2019s supervisor shows up to train him on the holiday special, Thanksgiving On A Stick: turkey mini-sausages, cranberry jigglers, candied yam cubes, stuffing cubes and mashed potato spheres with a hard gravy-like shell!<br /> <br /> Today, Jack and his pals are joined by Gary and his crew of football team thralls. They hassle Jack by taking forever to order, complaining about the food and wanting replacements, intimidating other patrons, etc. Jack\u2019s pals are getting pretty steamed, and Gary and his buds seem ready to oblige them with a fight.<br /> <br /> Jack decides to preempt things by pulling out a box of sticks, tripping and \u201caccidentally\u201d throwing them at Gary &amp; Co. as a hundred little javelins. They stick in Gary\u2019s thralls pretty good, and Jack basks in the cries of pain and surprise. For Gary, though, they are barely an annoyance. He starts talking lawsuit. Jack finds this an occasion for more insults. In a battle of zingers, Gary is truly unarmed, so Gary pithily responds by attacking Jack.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s not much of a fight. Jack is agile, but Gary lands a lucky punch that staggers Jack. (Luck plus Legendary Deed plus channeled Virtue, that is.) Jack hits back, but doesn\u2019t do much. Then Gary switches to wrestling, so he can use his Strength instead of his Dexterity to attack, and immediately gets Jack in a clinch. He squeezes; Jack struggles to no avail and passes out. Meanwhile, Gary\u2019s thralls duke it out with Jack\u2019s pals.<br /> <br /> Jack wakes up with mall cops around him. Gary and the thralls are gone, but they left broken tables and chairs and \u201cSCIONS SUCK\u201d written on the wall in ketchup. Jack has the dubious comfort that security cameras probably caught the scuffle and Gary will probably have to pay some kind of fine.<br /> <br /> GUS is teaching one of his self-defense classes that evening. Instead of ninjas attacking, this time Gary swaggers in to interrupt the class. He tells the students that what a guy needs for self-defense is muscle, so work out until you can do THIS: whereupon he haymakers a punching bag, which explodes in a shower of sawdust. And for the gals\u2026 get a boyfriend who can do that.<br /> <br /> Gary isn\u2019t there just to be obnoxious, though. He challenges Gus to arm wrestle. Gus, of course, agrees. At first, Gary seems stronger \u2014 stronger than Gus remembers \u2014 but Gus grits his teeth and vows that no way is any giant going to beat him at anything! Legend spent, Virtues channeled on both sides\u2026 and Gus feels a surge of strength like he\u2019s never felt before. And the table cracks beneath the strain. Gus not only slams Gary\u2019s arm down, the table splinters and he flips Gary to the floor! Gus has gained his third dot of Epic Strength.<br /> <br /> (Gus\u2019 player rolled amazingly hot. In fact, he would have won without the extra dot of Epic Strength. As it happens, though, this coincidentally happens to fit very well with the Fatebinding between Gus and Gary\u2026)<br /> <br /> Gary is astonished. \u201cYou got a power-up too?\u201d he demands. \u201cMorning after the game. You too?\u201d \u201cDitto.\u201d They part, warily on Gary\u2019s part, but Gus exultant.<br /> <br /> JAEDA spends Sunday at the local wrecking yard, looking for spare parts for her Roadrunner. She encounters Gary and Kwame \u201cThe Cat\u201d McMorris. While the giant and suspected were-leopard look for parts for Kwame\u2019s car, they argue about what to do about Gus. Kwame suggests ambush. Gary protests that beating Gus won\u2019t mean anything unless he wins fairly. \u201cYeah, that\u2019s why you\u2019re losing,\u201d Kwame retorts. At which point they come around a stack of cars and see Jaeda.<br /> <br /> Despite the argument and his defeat the night before, Gary seems to be in a fairly mellow mood. He Talks Car a bit with Jaeda and suggests parts she could grind down a bit to fit her Roadrunner \u2014 he thinks he saw one such car. Kwame comes on to Jaeda so crudely that even Gary the Entitled Male Chauvinist Pig Jock rolls his eyes in embarrassment. Which makes Kwame ask if Gary is sweet on Jaeda.<br /> <br /> They reach the car. Gary rips it open. At that moment, a strange winged beast drops from the sky and attacks. Kwame springs back from the creature. It slashes at Jaeda with its claws. She slashes back with her knife. Gary, a little slow off the mark, grabs the beast in a bear hug. (Yes, with Epic Strength 3 he\u2019s really, really good at that.) Jaeda soon slices open the beast and pulls out its heart.<br /> <br /> \u201cThanks for all the help, pussycat,\u201d Gary tells Kwame. \u201cHey, there could have been more,\u201d Kwame protests. \u201cAnd why are you helping a Scion, anyway? You really are sweet on her, aren\u2019t you?\u201d He makes loud kissy-noises. Gary points out, however, that he couldn\u2019t tell who the beast was really after \u2014 it might have attacked him next \u2014 and, \u201cI can hurt you Kwame.\u201d ZIP! Kwame gone.<br /> <br /> Which leaves the matter of the beast. It looks sort of like a griffon, with a leonine body and eagle-like head and sings, but parts of it are scaled like a lizard. Jaeda calls Holly to ask what it is. Thanks to her humungous erudition, Holly recognizes the beast as a creature from Mesopotamian artwork that archeologists call a \u201cgriffon-dragon\u201d for lack of a better name. It\u2019s a pantheon beast, not titanspawn, so it doesn\u2019t have a Trophy. A brief discussion between Jaeda and Gary about disposing of the carcass ends with Gary taking it home for his mom to cook. He says he\u2019ll let Jaeda know if griffon-dragon steaks taste good. Jaeda asks if he\u2019ll keep some of the long wing-feathers for her, and he amiably agrees to this as well. The beast might not have any supernatural Trophies but\u2026 hey! Griffon-dragon! That\u2019s worth keeping a memento.<br /> <br /> Thinking about it, Jaeda decides that Mesopotamia mythic beasts don\u2019t just drop from the sky to attack random Scions. Almost certainly, Nergal sent the beast to show his displeasure at Jaeda refusing his demands and Tlazolteotl humiliating him.<br /> ---------------<br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-16T15:34:24-08:00","id":1018236,"title":"","content":"EPISODE NINE: P.S., This Is A Trap (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Jinn and Panic</u></b><br /> <br /> Holly knew from last episode that Mr. Bindahna planned to leave Avalon as soon as possible, because he feared that the Scions would attract the attention of people he wants to avoid. So once again, she is not especially surprised when Aaron calls her a few evenings later to say that his family is in trouble, he doesn\u2019t understand what\u2019s happening, but he needs her help. Aaron wants to meet her at the remains of an unfinished big-box store in Hooverdale. Oh, and she should come alone and not tell any of her friends about this.<br /> <br /> Aaron might as well have said, \u201cThis is a trap.\u201d <i>Of course</i> Holly phones the rest of the Band. While she walks boldly up to the ruin, Gus, Jack and Jaeda slip through the brush and weeds toward another side of the store.<br /> <br /> Construction of the store never got past laying the concrete foundation and the start of some walls and yard-wide concrete support pillars. Aaron waits in the middle of the leaf-strewn concrete floor. When Holly approaches, he says he\u2019s sorry, he had no choice. Three hulking, ogre-like figures appear out of thin air and attack.<br /> <br /> Holly recognizes them as jinn: humanoid but near giant-size, with hooves and horns like cattle, long, unkempt black hair and tusked jaws. A female with dark green skin appears on top of a pillar and fires a pistol. A male, also green-skinned, wears incongruous modern riot gear and carries a big spear. The third, male, largest of all and massively built, has dark red skin and carries a big scimitar. Big Red \u2014 who announces himself as Abu Harb al-Irami (\u201cFather of Enmity, inhabitant of Iram\u201d) \u2014 commands Aaron by the oath he swore to assume his true form and join in the attack. Looking surprised, Aaron also turns into a 7\u2019 tall, green-skinned jinn and tries to grab Holly. Irami also says that Holly will make a fine addition to some jinni lord\u2019s harem. (Yuck!) The other Scions take this as the signal to start running toward the store.<br /> <br /> The fight takes less time than expected \u2014 certainly less time than Irami expected. The green-skinned Bindahnas are completely ineffectual: The gun and spear might deal some damage if the jinn could hit any of the Scions, but they can\u2019t. The Scions can tell that the Bindahnas are not especially good fighters, and they are not going all-out. (IE, they are not channeling Virtues or attempting Legendary Deeds.) That leaves Irami as one against four. Holly curses him with the Evil Eye the first chance she gets. Irami tries to trap her by materializing an iron shell around her, but he misses. He also misses when he tries to disintegrate Jack\u2019s war-club.<br /> <br /> Irami is tough, but he\u2019s not tough enough. When Jack disarms Abdurrahman Bindahna of his spear \u2014 spectacularly \u2014 Jack sends the spear hurling in a high arc to strike Irami. His aim is true and the spear neatly spits the hulking jinni. That takes a lot of the fight out of Irami, and a punch from Gus knocks him out. After that, it\u2019s just a matter of time (and not much of it) to put a stop to the Bindahnas\u2019 half-hearted attacks by knocking them out, too.<br /> <br /> Holly remembers the fourth member of the Bindahna family, the younger daughter Janet. Gus finds Janet fettered and gagged with duct tape in the shadow of a pillar. He calms her down (showing an unexpectedly gentle side) and frees her. The 13-year-old Janet gets an instant case of hero worship.<br /> <br /> The Scions wake up Irami, carefully, with Jaeda, Jack and Gus ready to knock him out or kill him at a moment\u2019s notice. Holly tells the jinni to release the Bindahnas from whatever oath was forcing them to obey him. Irami says he can\u2019t do that until the other jinn are awake so they can hear him. The jinni gets a crafty look in his eyes then \u2014 but before he can attempt whatever cunning plan he just thought of, the Scions knock him out again.<br /> <br /> Then, they revive the unconscious Bindahnas along with Irami. With a little prompting (having the First Flower at his throat and a great big spear in his back makes for good persuasion), Irami commands the Bindahnas to regard their oath to obey him as null and void. The Scions then let him go with a warning never to return to Avalon. Irami leaps into the sky in a burst of wind and dead leaves, and vanishes.<br /> <br /> Filling in the details, the Scions learn that Irami had been in Avalon for days, following the Scions invisibly and immaterially. That\u2019s how he found the Bindahnas. Irami is a Titan-worshipping subject of Iblis, the king of evil, unbeliever jinn. The Bindahnas had been slaves in the same jinn community as Irami before Abdurrahman and Hafza converted to Islam and escaped. Irami thought that by capturing some runaway slaves he could increase his own prestige, and more by capturing the Scions. First, Irami captured Janet: He then used the threat of harm to force her parents to swear obedience in the name of Sulayman bin Da\u2019ud, the oath no jinni can break. Then they caught Aaron. Fortunately, Abdurrahman has experience at manipulating arrogant masters, and he maneuvered Irami into trying to trap Holly in this manner instead of just appearing in the Scions\u2019 bedrooms and attacking them in their sleep. Aaron caught wise as well and made sure that his attempt to lure Holly was obviously fraudulent.<br /> <br /> This incident, however, convinces Abdurrahman that he and his family can\u2019t wait to sell the house and business. They need to leave town immediately. The Bindahnas, all in human form once more, hurry off to do just that.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T15:50:07-08:00","id":1018599,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 10: Kids Having Kids<br /> <br /> Another short adventure. This one examines the Serious Social Issue of teen pregnancy. Gosh, it\u2019s just like one of those earnest Afterschool Specials they used to have on TV. Well, maybe not.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Prequel: Hunting Shadows</u></b><br /> <br /> After the Scions rescue the Bindahna family in the last episode, father Abdurrahman declares that Avalon is way too dangerous for his family: They are leaving town and going into hiding that night. On the next school day, though, Holly finds a box tied with ribbon at her home room desk. An unsigned card says, \u201cI will never forget you.\u201d The box holds a coronet of golden holly leaves with pink pearl berries. It is exquisite. Other girls\u2019 jaws drop. The gossip over the coronet completely overshadows the disappearance of Aaron Bindahna.<br /> <br /> After that, the Scions get a few quiet days until a shadowy horror starts killing people. This creature looks like a weird fusion of bug and snake and is made of black smoke. It is called a Lucifuge, or Fly-the-Light. It isn\u2019t solid, but its claws and mandibles cut flesh.<br /> <br /> The episode begins at the ending of a different adventure, like a James Bond movie: The Band has already tracked the Lucifuge to its lair, a rusty old pipe that leads into the ground. They\u2019re pretty sure it leads to some sort of old storage tank. The vaporous Fly-the-Light cannot pass through solid matter, but it seems immune to all physical damage. As far as the Scions can tell, nothing hurts the Lucifuge except bright light. When they figured that out, they armed themselves with road flares and bundles of laser pointers and chased the Lucifuge to its lair. There\u2019s no way to dig up the tank quickly and inconspicuously; morning is hours away, and they can\u2019t risk the creature getting away to find another lair. They also aren\u2019t sure how strong the creature is, so corking the pipe isn\u2019t a sure thing, either. How to kill it?<br /> <br /> Jack starts by dropping a flare down the pipe. The flare hits a bend and sticks before it reaches the tank.<br /> <br /> Holly remembers her Follower Manny Salkind, amateur pyrotechnician. It\u2019s late at night when she calls him, but Manny\u2019s always up for anything that involves fire, explosives or doing something for Holly, no matter how peculiar. He immediately has some ideas and tells Holly to come over right away. They sneak into Manny\u2019s basement lab. There, he whips up a ball of thermite wrapped in a shell of squibs set to go off over the course of several seconds. The ball will bounce around in all directions, enabling it to move around corners and into the buried tank. Then the thermite will burn brightly for more than a minute, even underwater.<br /> <br /> It works. The Scions drop the ball down the pipe. It fizzes, crackles and ricochets beyond their sight. Seconds later, a horrible alien screech echoes up the pipe. The Lucifuge tries to escape up the pipe, but Jack jams the massed laser pointers into the end. Caught between two intense light sources, the Lucifuge dies.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Hooking Up</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions get on with their lives.<br /> <br /> GUS is playing in a pick-up basketball game when Acacia finds him and comes on to him. She\u2019s not at all coy: She says she wants him, so how about it? Acacia\u2019s evil, but she\u2019s very hot, and he\u2019s a red-blooded 16-year-old male. Gus says yes. (Never mind that he\u2019s supposed to be dating a Fashionista now. That\u2019s business, for public appearances.) They leave together to the admiring hoots of the other players.<br /> <br /> Gus enjoys his tryst very much. He uses protection. Acacia seems to enjoy it as well. She uses Epic Appearance and natural talent to be the most intoxicating lover Gus has had since, well, Sheena. Acacia suggests Gus might be even better than Gary, which further pleases Gus.<br /> <br /> JACK has no such luck. He\u2019s officially going steady with Holly, and he fears to test the limits of that pseudo-relationship. (Too bad, the pretty girl at Orange Julius seems interested.) Instead of steaming up the windows of his car (he doesn\u2019t have a car, grr) he\u2019s in the mall at Cinema Giallo Rentale, looking for a weird indie movie to fill his lonely evening.<br /> <br /> Cinema Giallo Rentale specializes in foreign and indie flicks. Quentin, the movie-geek proprietor, has shrines to Italian gore-fest directors Dario Argento and Mario Bava in the back room. While Jack browses, a customer asks Quentin if he has the DVD to the first season of the popular TV show, <i>Cassie the Vampire Slayer.</i> \u201cNot even at gunpoint!\u201d sneers Quentin, who then rattles off a list of vampire movies the customer <i>ought </i>to watch. Jack, watching the scene, sees a DVD case float across the aisle from one shelf to the other, just like the book did in the opening scene of <i>Ghostbusters.</i> Quentin doesn\u2019t notice.<br /> <br /> Oookay, so the video store is haunted by a ghost who likes reshelving DVDs. Jack notes this bit of weirdness as another chance to get free pizza from Gus, and returns to Stick It.<br /> <br /> An hour later, a customer screams, flings her Sausage on a Stick to the floor, and gags. \u201cWhat are you trying to do, kill us?\u201d she gasps. \u201cI\u2019ll call the board of health! I\u2019ll sue!\u201d<br /> <br /> She thought the sausage was full of maggots. Jack picks it up and sees no maggots. Neither does the woman, now. She departs, confused. Around the mall, though, screams of fear erupt from other customers and employees. Jack runs down the mall to see what\u2019s going on. He notices DVD cases flying out the door of Cinema Giallo Rentale. Inside, Quentin is grabbing cases from the shelves, shrieking \u201cNo! Get out! Get out! <i>No Titanic!\u201d</i> Jack tells him to get a grip, but Quentin sinks to the floor and huddles in a corner, weeping and softly singing, \u201cI Will Get By.\u201d<br /> <br /> Jack concludes there\u2019s an evil ghost making people hallucinate their greatest fears. His guess is confirmed when Holly walks in and announces they should get married. AAAUGH! But no, it\u2019s only a hallucination, Jack firmly tells himself, and the terrifying vision disappears. Jack calls Holly (with some nervousness) to let her know of the occult peril. She says she\u2019ll get there as soon as possible.<br /> <br /> Then, Jack wonders if he can\u2019t handle the situation by himself. After all, he knows a lot of random trivia. He wracks his brains for everything he knows about exorcism, and he remembers that even mortals can do successful exorcisms: The trick is to convince the ghost that you have the power to send it back to the Underworld. If the ghost believes it, it happens. So, Jack quickly scours the mall for tiki torches and other paraphernalia to conduct a Polynesian exorcism. (It\u2019s best to use your own mythology. If you believe in the power of your symbols, it\u2019s easier to make the ghost believe in them, too.) Fortunately, Jack always carries the little Brady tiki that is his Relic. Jack goes all-out to psych out the ghost, and it works. When Holly arrives, the people in the mall have stopped going nuts. Quentin, though, needs a rest after his trauma.<br /> <br /> HOLLY has her own little adventure a day or so later. While walking in the late afternoon, she notices two men following her. They look Native American, though dressed in jogging sweats. A motorcycle whizzes past. Holly starts walking faster. So do the men. Suddenly, a third Native American man in jogging clothes steps out from an alley as the two behind her speed up. The third man grins evilly and starts turning into a coyote\u2014<br /> <br /> \u2014And falls to the ground, out cold, struck by the \u201ccobra\u201d extenda-club in the hand of the teenage boy who just sped by on his motorcycle. The boy screeches the Harley around to stop near Holly and shouts, \u201cGet on!\u201d Holly does, and they speed away from the dismayed Skinwalkers.<br /> <br /> The boy introduces himself as Cole Thomasun. He takes Holly to a burger joint. When he takes off his helmet, Holly sees that Cole is a cutie with cleft chin, blue eyes and short, spiked golden hair. Cole explains that he saw her as he drove past and turned around to take another look: Holly is the prettiest girl he\u2019s ever seen. Then he saw her apparently threatened by the three men, or whatever they were, and thought he should do something about it. \u201cI have poor impulse control,\u201d he explains. \u201cIt gets me into trouble.\u201d But his grin suggests that Holly\u2019s the sort of trouble he likes.<br /> <br /> As Cole and Holly talk, it comes out that Cole is an orphan who lives with his uncle. He goes to Etna High. There\u2019s something wrong at Etna, he says. Students keep disappearing. People say they ran away. Cole\u2019s not so sure. Now, with all the talk about the Avalon Slasher, maybe people will finally realize that something is up and do something about it!<br /> <br /> By the end of the meal, Cole asks Holly for a date, and she says yes. And yes, she checks him for magic with her mirror. The Unlidded Eye shows that Cole is mortal... but a Legendary Mortal \u2014 not supernatural himself but tangled in Fate, like Krieger the courier. One way or another, he\u2019s destined for adventure and involvement in the Mythic World.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T15:51:21-08:00","id":1018600,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 10: Kids Having Kids (Continued)<br /> <br /> GUS, AGAIN: One evening, he stops by the mini-mart in Etna where kids get their beer. (The store doesn\u2019t card. You got the cash, they got the beer.) He encounters Gary, leaving with a metal keg under one arm. They exchange a few wary words. Then Gary\u2019s forehead explodes in a spray of blood and brain matter, and he drops. A moment later, though, he exclaims, \u201cOW! What the\u2026 Who hit me?\u201d and gets up. He feels his forehead and the back of his head. His fingers come back bloody, though the big holes that should be there are gone. \u201cSobeck,\u201d he snarls. \u201cSetting me up, huh? Won\u2019t face me in a fair fight?\u201d<br /> <br /> Gus protests (and gets a little mad at the accusation), but he notices that Gary\u2019s outrage at him is fake. He\u2019s just talking while circling around and scanning the nearby rooftops. Suddenly, Gary hurls the keg of beer like a 50-pound football at something Gus doesn\u2019t see. \u201cI think I missed,\u201d Gary complains. \u201cBut whoever it is, he ran.\u201d He goes on to say that even if Gus is a filthy Scion, Gary knows Gus would at least face him if he tried to kill him. Gus promises that this is so, and he buys Gary a replacement keg of beer. As they leave the mini-mart, Gus wonders at the identity of the sniper. Was it Marshall Byrd\u2026 and was he trying to kill Gary, or Gus? That was a well-aimed shot, but from a distance the Scion and the giant actually do look a lot alike. Gus finds a spent shell casing on the roof, but there\u2019s no way to track the gunman based on that; and since the gunman fled by motorcycle, there\u2019s no way to track him through the town\u2019s busy streets.<br /> <br /> That Friday, JAEDA gets an invitation to party with Kristin, the girl she saved from strangulation by the possessed Mrs. Fanshaw. They and several other girls and boys, from both Avalon and Etna, meet in an abandoned barn outside town to drink, dance, drink, talk, drink, make out and drink. For heat they have a fire in a trash barrel.<br /> <br /> Lauryn, from Etna, blithers on about how much she wants a baby. (Babies are adorable, it\u2019d prove she\u2019s an adult, blah blah.) Jaeda, who has seen what sort of life unwed teenage mothers get, tries to tell Lauryn this is a Bad Idea. The argument reveals that Lauryn isn\u2019t alone: She and a group of other girls have a pact to all get pregnant by the end of winter quarter. Acacia is one of the girls, and in fact it\u2019s her idea. Lauryn is dubious about Acacia\u2019s Newage talk about \u201cEmpowering Female Mysteries,\u201d but she wears the charm that Acacia gave her to help her get pregnant and ensure the health of the potential baby. Oh, and her dreamy star athlete boyfriend and sperm donor is Kwame \u201cThe Cat\u201d McMorris, suspected were-leopard.<br /> <br /> Jaeda realizes at once that the situation is much worse than some dippy girls making bad life decisions. She applies all her passion (and Willpower, and Legend) to convincing Lauryn that she must not have this baby and to get rid of the talisman. As Lauryn wavers, a drunken boy shudders and says, in a voice not his own, \u201cMe. It must be me. You are the gate by which I live again.\u201d The boy lunges at Lauryn, but a drunken boy possessed by a specter is still a drunken boy and Jaeda easily knocks him out. This bit of creepiness helps Jaeda cement the deal with Lauryn, and the feckless girl hands over the talisman.<br /> <br /> Jaeda calls Holly and the rest of the gang. Holly confirms that the talisman really is magic, intended, somehow, to channel Titanic energy into the wearer. This is likely none too healthy for mortals but not enough to turn them into titanspawn. On the other hand, if a developing embryo already had a touch of the supernatural, the talisman (and other rituals Acacia bamboozles the girls into performing) could ensure that a child of titanspawn parentage would be born as pure titanspawn, while a child of Scion parentage could be born corrupted into a sort of Titan-Scion. Reincarnating specters is another possibility \u2014 children born already evil and bound to the Titans.<br /> <br /> Gus, when he hears this, somewhat diffidently brings up his tryst with Acacia but points out that he used a condom. Holly says (with a touch of grim amusement) that she wouldn\u2019t count on mortal protection thwarting what was obviously a magical rite. Condoms are never absolutely certain, and magic turns the rare fluke into the absolute certainty. She\u2019s morally certain that Gus is soon to be a daddy.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-17T15:53:16-08:00","id":1018602,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 10: Kids Having Kids<br /> <br /> Finally getting to the actual adventure...<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Blessed Event</u></b><br /> <br /> Holly takes charge. She decides that first, any of the girls in the Baby Pact must be checked for pregnancy and any fetuses aborted. Jaeda has already softened up Lauryn; Holly gives her the full Social Fu whammy to extract the names of the other girls in the pact and get them all to a doctor that she knows will do the job and not tell the girls\u2019 parents. (Well\u2026 between Holly\u2019s money and her Scion Mind Tricks this is any doctor she wants, really.)<br /> <br /> Next morning, the Band informs the other Scions about what\u2019s happening. Turns out, they\u2019ve individually noticed weird goings-on too, generally of a ghostly nature. Barbara quickly devises a plan. She suggests that the Band go after Acacia. The other Scions will hunt the specters that still seem to be running loose. (They are likely left over from \u201cDay of the Dead,\u201d but it\u2019s not impossible that Acacia has somehow alerted other specters loose in the World that there\u2019s a chance to live again.) As Barbara points out, only Leo can see and hit the evil ghosts; Barbara and Delia will make sure the mortals don\u2019t pay attention to anything strange the Scions must do; Delia has oracular powers with which she can find the specters (where they are, or where they will be); Barbara can order specters to leave mortals they possess, so Leo can swat them; and Marshall can protect them from any material threats. The Band agrees this is a sensible plan (though Holly, alert to nuances of Motivation, can tell that Barbara has realized she and the others have fallen behind in Legend, and acting in a Band is the way to gain epic deeds.)<br /> <br /> The Band goes looking for Acacia. They somewhat reluctantly bring Reese Vetterson into the search, since Acacia is her friend. Fortunately, Reese wasn\u2019t part of the Baby Pact and finds news of it worrying: Reese likes Acacia as an amorally fun person to hang with, but she worries that Acacia will get hurt in her zeal to see the World ground beneath the mighty heels of the Titans.<br /> <br /> As they search, Jaeda gets a disturbing phone call from her father. One of Jaeda\u2019s bottles of Something I Don\u2019t Want To Know About is missing. Jaeda knows, of course: These are the bottles of eitr the Scions collected when they slew the Sarsen twins. Reese further worries the Scions by revealing that even while she had taken the gut-stabbed Acacia to the hospital, the witch had insisted Reese return to the crypt and, while the Scions were busy in the lower crypt, gather some of the clay-and-pebble remains of the Sarsen twins. Acacia has them, now.<br /> <br /> By this time, none of the Scions are surprised when Ariadne\u2019s Thread leads them to the McLann crypt. The doors haven\u2019t been replaced yet. Inside, they find Acacia lying in the central sarcophagus, naked beneath what they recognize as a flayed lindwurm skin, the empty bottle of Sarsen eitr dropping from her hand. Her belly already is grossly swollen. As the Scions enter, she gasps, \u201cTuonela, I am the gate\u2026\u201d (Holly recognizes the name of the death-realm in ancient Finnish mythology: the Greater Titan that is a special foe of her pantheon, the Kaleva.) Acacia\u2019s body stretches even further and a horrible Thing <i>rips </i>its way out of her, leaping for the door in a spray of blood. Fortunately for the World, the Scions are in its way.<br /> <br /> The Tomb-Spawn is humanoid, about 7\u2019 tall, with ropy, knotted muscles beneath its stony, somehow dead-looking skin. Its head is vaguely crocodilian (grandchild of Sobek, after all), while maggots drip from the pits where its eyes should be. Battle is immediately joined.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s not really a lot to say about the fight:<br /> <br /> * Holly tries shooting the Tomb-Spawn with her dart holding the last of the Honey of Heaven. The dart hits and eats away a patch of the creature\u2019s skin, but it does not inflict a great deal of damage. She also curses it with the Evil Eye but can tell the hellish thing is somewhat resistant.<br /> <br /> * The creature snarls horribly at Jack, who is compelled to run by its Dreadful Mien. Sure, it\u2019s only five seconds of effect before he forces himself to stop and come back, but he\u2019s effectively out of the fight.<br /> <br /> * Gus manages to grab and squeeze the Tomb-Spawn. It\u2019s terrifically strong \u2014 as strong as he is \u2014 but the Evil Eye on the creature, even with only partial effect, is enough to tip the balance and Gus can keep it held\u2026 as long as he can keep channeling Willpower and Legendary Deeds. Gus finds he is motivated to do so. He manages to damage the abomination with each squeeze, but it takes a lot of killing.<br /> <br /> * Jaeda stabs and slashes with the First Flower, but the tomb-Spawn is horribly tough (and the player repeatedly rolled suck damage). Again, the thing takes a lot of killing.<br /> <br /> * Unable to escape or bite, the Tomb-Spawn sends a wave of vile power out into the graveyard. Five hungry corpses \u2014 the fast, brain-eating sort of zombie \u2014 rise from the graves and swarm into the crypt to try helping their master. Two of them manage to grab Gus (his DV is fairly suck for holding onto the Tomb-Spawn), but though the undead horrors gnaw at him they can\u2019t hurt him. Nor can the hungry corpses manage to strike Jaeda or Holly.<br /> <br /> * Unable to think of anything else she can do to hurt the Tomb-Spawn, Holly consults her Guide. Her mysterious mentor in the mirror warns her that creatures of Tuonela are super-charged with the essence of death and disease. If the Tomb-Spawn is destroyed inside the crypt, the release of death-energy from Tuonela will disrupt the magic of the upper crypt that hides the lower crypt from divination. If that happens, it would be easy for other servants of the Titans to find the lower crypt and destroy it, opening the gate to all the specters of Xibalba.<br /> <br /> * Gus manages to haul the Tomb-Spawn outside before the killing blow is struck. As the Scions hastily back away, the cadaver shrivels in seconds, then explodes in a cloud of necrotic dust. (It\u2019s the Mathean Template for Crom Cruach, used for Tuonela\u2019s creatures instead.) The hungry corpses remain active, but they are easy for Gus, Jaeda and Jack (now recovered and returned) to destroy. Holly does her part by using Center of Attention to keep the mortals in the cemetery paying attention only to her while Scions battle zombies in the background.<br /> <br /> Right as the Band finishes off the hungry corpses, the other Scions arrive. Barbara, Delia and Marshall are jubilant; Leo, as usual, is miserable. Barbara says her hunting plan worked, and they got to fight their own squad of hungry corpses. Field work is fun!<br /> <br /> Reese does not partake in the general good cheer. Acacia is dead. Reese says her last words were, \u201cI didn\u2019t think dying would hurt.\u201d Reese carries Acacia\u2019s body away to bury in secret. The lunatic witch will just have to be another runaway or victim of the Avalon Slasher.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T15:26:04-08:00","id":1019449,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 11: Ninja High School<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Just the Usual Adolescent Crises</u></b><br /> <br /> Over the next week or so after Acacia\u2019s death, the Scions deal with nothing worse than the everyday teen-age issues of relationships, hall scuffles, transportation and mysterious ninjas. Okay, maybe not <i>exactly </i>the usual adolescent crises\u2026<br /> <br /> HOLLY has been dating Cole the Etna High biker boy, sort of, though she\u2019s careful not to do anything that would seriously compromise her for-public-consumption \u201cgoing steady\u201d with Jack. Manny Salkind, though, has been paying more attention to her since she asked his help in dealing with the Lucifuge, and his crush on Holly is pretty clear for everyone to see. OTOH, Holly\u2019s work to make nice with the Fashionistas might be paying off: She notices Vanessa, #3 girl in the Fashionistas, apparently talking to Manny in a civil manner \u2014 something that never would have happened before.<br /> <br /> The shoe drops at the end of one school day. Kids stream out to the buses and their cars, including Holly and most of the Fashionistas. Manny is already waiting. He tells Holly that he needs to tell her how he feels, and he triggers a pyrotechnic display that forms a heart with her name in it. And in the shocked silence (it\u2019s pretty spectacular), Vanessa\u2019s voice rings out in amused contempt. \u201c<i>You?</i> And <i>her?</i>\u201d She starts laughing. Other Fashionistas join in, followed by other kids, until half the school is laughing at Manny\u2026 and Holly.<br /> <br /> Holly has a choice. She can laugh at Manny\u2019s declaration of love and place herself wholly with the Fashionistas; or, she can stand by Manny and accept the moment of humiliation. Holly smiles, kisses Manny on the cheek and tells him he\u2019s very sweet. Then she turns to Vanessa and the Fashionistas. Delia is already white as a sheet, eyes wide in horror. Holly\u2019s smile and words to Vanessa are pleasant, but the more perceptive kids catch the venomous fury behind them and start edging away from Vanessa. The Fashionista knows her scheme to humiliate Holly through Manny has misfired, but she doesn\u2019t know exactly how. Or what the daughter of Louhi will do to her for it.<br /> <br /> She\u2019ll find out.<br /> <br /> Holly has a talk with Manny afterward, applying all her Social-Fu to let him down gently. He\u2019s a good friend, and she appreciates his gesture of affection, but she\u2019s already in a relationship, etc. Manny confirms what she suspected, though: Vanessa convinced Manny that he had to make a grand gesture to Holly, or he\u2019d lose her to the handsome and glamorous Cole.<br /> <br /> JAEDA has an unpleasant week. First, she comes to one of her afterschool training sessions with Leo to find Marshall already there. He tells her that he\u2019ll take over from her, Leo is a member of his Band, not hers. Jaeda questions this (rather cogently turning Marshall\u2019s own words against him in the process), but she feels she can\u2019t push Leo too hard to defy Marshall. Leo doesn\u2019t, even though \u2014 or possiby because \u2014 Marshall adopts a classic \u201cYou maggots!\u201d Marine drill sergeant attitude to Leo. The situation also confirms that to Marshall, at least, the Scions have conclusively split into two separate and rival Bands.<br /> <br /> A few days later, Jaeda sees Horndog Bob, the masterless thrall of the late Bolvarg, hassling a girl in the hallway. This is nothing strange. Bob relishes the size and strength he gained by becoming a thrall, and he thinks he\u2019s now entitled to take what \u2014 or who \u2014 he wants. He\u2019s cornered the girl, groping her and telling her to go along with it, she\u2019ll like it. The girl clearly feels otherwise. So, Jaeda intervenes. Per her usual practice with Bob, she kicks him in the groin and punches him in the nose. Bob drops. At which point, Vice-Principal Stone arrives. He gloats about the chance to expel Jaeda, finally, but the girl and the also-arriving Janitor Mitch stand up for Jaeda, pointing out that she was protecting the girl from sexual assault (and likely saving the school from a lawsuit). So, Jaeda just gets detention (again, ho hum).<br /> <br /> More importantly, Jaeda\u2019s father receives another phone call about the reason for her detention, and it comes during one of his drinking bouts. It turns him self-pitying and maudlin. He cries in his tequila about how he\u2019s failing as a father, Jaeda\u2019s mother was the only good thing that happened to him and he screwed it up, etc. Jaeda finds it all very uncomfortable.<br /> <br /> JACK, on the other hand, faces a simple, straightforward problem. He made enough at Stick It to afford a motorcycle (an elderly Kawasaki that definitely does not enhance his coolness). He\u2019s riding it home from work when a Dumpster comes flying at him. It hits. He manages to skid with the blow enough that the ton or so of metal and garbage doesn\u2019t hurt him, but it\u2019s not good for the bike. Jack crawls out in time to see a huge, lumpish hag who seems made of stone charging at him. She carries one of those metal pipes set in a concrete plug that are used as movable dividers or fence supports, and she swings it like a club. Jack is unarmed. The giantess sobs about how the Scions killed her boys, and she\u2019s going to kill them in revenge. Jack realizes this must be the Sarsen twins\u2019 mother.<br /> <br /> The giantess can\u2019t hit Jack with the club (his DV is too high). When she gets wise and tries grappling instead, Jack remembers what happened with Gary and decides to run, though it offends his pride (and his Courage Virtue). Sometimes, though, discretion is the better part of staying alive. The giantess hurls her steel and concrete club at Jack, but she misses. She settles for crushing his bike and throwing that at him, too.<br /> <br /> The other Scions are not too surprised at this attack. Actually, they are more surprised that it took this long for Mama Sarsen to try killing one of them.<br /> <br /> GUS continues his neighborhood watch activities. One night, a mugger accosts him and the old lady he escorts home after dark. The mugger has a gun. Gus grins and prepares to make the mugger regret his choice of profession. Then a ninja drops silently from the trees between them. With one hand, the ninja motions for Gus to stay back. With the sword in his other hand, he slices through the mugger\u2019s gun. Behind his ski mask, the mugger\u2019s jaw drops. The ninja wiggles his fingers at the mugger in a \u201ccome and get me\u201d gesture. The mugger, slave to every movie and TV show in which a dumb thug confronts a clearly superior hero, throws the remains of his gun at the ninja. The ninja darts forward, catches the mugger\u2019s arm and judo-flips him to the ground. Then the ninja holds the tip of his sword to the mugger\u2019s throat and waves an admonitory finger at him. The mugger lifts his hands and says he gives up. The ninja salutes Gus and the old lady, steps into a shadow and is gone. The ninja never made a sound.<br /> <br /> Gus lets the other Scions know the shinobi are back in town. At least, there\u2019s one shinobi, and he\u2019s fighting street crime. Huh? None of the other Scions can make sense of it either.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T15:28:12-08:00","id":1019451,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 11: Ninja High School (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Revenge of the Ninja</u></b><br /> <br /> While the Scions ponder these developments, Jack and Holly go on a date that Friday night. Like the rest of their relationship, this is duty rather than pleasure \u2014 something to cement Holly\u2019s place among the Fashionista clique and Jack\u2019s place among the alpha jocks. They go dancing at The Public Pool, the hottest (and only) club in Avalon and Etna. As its name suggests, The Public Pool used to be a swimming pool before city budget problems forced its closure. Now people dance in the empty pool, with the deep end as the mosh pit. Naturally, Cole shows up. Matters briefly get a little tense between him and Jack.<br /> <br /> After their public appearance as a couple, Holly drives Jack home in her Lotus. Jack has his window down so the icy breeze on his face reminds his adolescent hormones that he really truly should not try anything romantic, no matter how beautiful Holly is. (Stupid hormones.) Which is why he and Holly hear the scream.<br /> <br /> A block away, they find a girl screaming and three shinobi silently kicking a figure on the ground. Jack joins battle with them. The shinobi are not trivial to defeat, but Jack outmatches them and Holly uses Overt Order to make them stand still and take Jack\u2019s blows. Jack takes the sword from one shinobi and uses it against the rest. First one shinobi falls and disintegrates to leave only a black shuriken behind; then the second. The third grabs the girl who screamed and holds a blade to her throat. The Scions let him retreat.<br /> <br /> The shinobis\u2019 victim turns out to be Landon Smith. He is seriously wounded (a couple shuriken in him, which soon disappear) and unconscious. Jack and Holly perform what first aid they can and rush him to a hospital.<br /> <br /> Landon isn\u2019t conscious again for hours, and it\u2019s next evening before the Scions can manage to talk to him without doctors or his parents listening in. (In play, this timing got a little compressed; everything from the assault on Landon to the final fight should have taken longer than one night.) Holly runs interference while Jack slips in to talk to him. Jack activates his Know Tapu and detects that Landon has become supernatural, with some sort of ban against sunlight. It isn\u2019t hard to infer that Landon has become a shinobi, and Jack soon gets him to admit this. Which leaves two big questions.<br /> <br /> How? Master Chiba, the new sensei at the dojo Landon attends, led him in a special rite to gain powers of martial arts mastery, stealth and shadow. Becoming a shinobi hurt a lot the first time as darkness ripped Landon apart and remade him, but now he just needs to drip a little blood into a shadow to become a shinobi again.<br /> <br /> And why? To make a difference in the world. Landon took up judo so he could defend himself against bullies, but he found he wanted to stop bullies from picking on other people too. So, he joined the neighborhood watch (like Gus). Master Chiba saw that desire and offered him a chance to become part of something greater: a secret order of supernatural ninjas that fight to save the world from evil in the night. Stopping a mugger was his own idea, though, as a way to test his new powers (and show off to Gus).<br /> <br /> Landon won\u2019t be doing any more ninja-ing for a while. The Scions leave him at the hospital and head for home while they ponder what to do next. First, though, they decide Landon needs a guard in case more shinobi try to kill him. The choice is really between Barbara and Leo. (Delia lacks sufficient physical prowess, and Marshall\u2019s a prick.) While Leo would probably do it, Barbara volunteers: She has less combat skill than Leo, but she can fly Landon to safety if the hospital comes under attack.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: My Car!</u></b><br /> <br /> For the next order of business, the Scions decide to learn more about Master Chiba. Holly uses Mystery to ascertain that Master Chiba serves the entity Shosa-no-Mikaboshi, AKA the Black Bodhisattva of the Endless Night Clan, and that he\u2019s turned 20 local people into shinobi. The center of their power is a place called the Black Mirror Shrine, which they can reach through the dojo Landon attends, but this otherworldly shrine is an extension of the Black Bodhisattva: He can withdraw it from the World any time, and it disappears if the Black Bodhisattva dies.<br /> <br /> The Scions then visit United Martial Arts (Judo \u2022 Aikido \u2022 Health \u2022 Sport \u2022 Defense) to see a class late that Saturday afternoon. Jack has Know Tapu active and finds that the receptionist is a shinobi. So are most of the students in this advanced class. So is their teacher, Master Chiba. (And yes, he looks just like Sonny Chiba). Shaken, the Scions leave.<br /> <br /> Night falls as the Scions drive away in Jaeda\u2019s Roadrunner. They argue whether they should try confronting Master Chiba right away or wait until morning when the shinobi won\u2019t be able to use their powers. And a huge, shadowy claw rips the engine from the car, while hidden shinobi knock out the streetlights nearby. (The shinobi don\u2019t join the fight, though.)<br /> <br /> The Lucifuge is back. (Or it\u2019s another one. Does it matter?) None of the Scions\u2019 regular weapons can hurt it. Jack tries throwing the shuriken left by the two dissolving shinobi and hits with one of them, doing a fair bit of damage, but retrieving black shuriken at night in the middle of battle is slightly problematic. He then punctures the car\u2019s gas tank, and Gus coats his hands in the gasoline to strike at the Lucifuge with flaming fists. (He\u2019s tough enough the fire barely stings.) Jaeda gets flares from the back of her car and lights them to harm the shadow-creature; she even uses one like a knife. Ultimately, she ignites the pool of gasoline, sacrificing her own car to generate another light source with which to harm the Lucifuge. Holly commandeers a passing driver\u2019s car to use its headlights as another weapon. For its part, the Lucifuge manages to bite Gus; its shadowy mandibles inflict deep yet eerily unbleeding wounds. At last they manage to kill the unholy thing. Between fire and the damage to the engine, though, Jaeda\u2019s car is trashed.<br /> <br /> Naturally, the fight blocks traffic, and who should trot forward to see what\u2019s going on than Gary Vetterson? The frost giant is appalled that the monster destroyed a classic muscle car. He tells Jaeda that he wants to help take down the sonuvabitch who sent the Lucifuge: This crime must be avenged! Jaeda accepts Gary\u2019s offer with gratitude. Besides, it\u2019s a chance to ride in Gary\u2019s fabulous Mercury Cougar XR-7\u2026<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T15:29:43-08:00","id":1019452,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 11: Ninja High School (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Heart of Darkness</u></b><br /> <br /> Gary drives the Scions back to United Martial Arts, with brief side-trips to collect weapons and to buy flares and gasoline for improvised Molotov cocktails. When the Scions reach the dojo, they start by throwing Molotov cocktails through the windows in hopes the light of the burning building will inhibit the shinobi \u2014 or at least prevent Dark Master Chiba from summoning another Lucifuge.<br /> <br /> Then they all charge in. The students are running out a side door as the Scions (and giant) run in the front door; Dark Master Chiba is already gone, but Holly knows exactly which way he went thanks to Ariadne\u2019s Thread. The trail leads the same way the students went \u2014 but they find 20 shinobi blocking their way!<br /> <br /> Gary doesn\u2019t care. When Holly says the trail leads to Chiba\u2019s office on the other side of a workout room, he charges straight through the mass of sword-wielding ninjas. He gets past most of them unscathed\u2026 but a few of the shinobi manage to cut him with their blades, nearly killing him by the time he reaches the office. Jaeda is right behind him, and the few shinobi who try attacking the little girl instead of the charging giant miss her by a mile. Holly likewise slips past into the office. Gus and Jack battle their way through as well, leaving a trail of fallen shinobi behind them.<br /> <br /> In the office, Gary\u2019s first act is to grab the tank of Dark Master Chiba\u2019s water cooler and crush it over his head. The water freezes as it drenches him (and for a few seconds he is visibly a giant), then cracks and falls away to show Gary partially healed from his wounds. (Gus mutters that it isn\u2019t fair that giants can heal themselves better than Scions can.)<br /> <br /> The office has a fire door to the alley outside, but it\u2019s one of those doors that can\u2019t close quickly, so they don\u2019t think Dark Master Chiba left that way. Besides, Ariadne\u2019s Thread points to a silk wall hanging. Sure enough, there\u2019s a door behind it\u2026 though the Scions saw no door on the other side of the wall. The door turns out not to be locked.<br /> <br /> Gus guards the door to the office while the others go through the mystery door to the Black Mirror Shrine. The shrine looks like the interior of a Shinto temple \u2014 a large, rectangular hall with a peaked roof \u2014 but the walls and pillars are all made of black mirrors set at angles to create a confusing kaleidoscope of reflections. The only light comes from twisting \u201cblack light\u201d tubes whose weird, purple illumination conceals as much as it reveals.<br /> <br /> At the far end of the hall, Dark Master Chiba kneels before a figure who sits on a throne on a dais. This figure rises to his feet as the Scions enter. He appears to be a man in robes of black silk edged with shards of black glass. His face is a black porcelain mask of a serene Buddha, with three eye-slits filled with opal. They have found Shosa-no-Mikaboshi, the Black Bodhisattva, master of the Endless Night Clan!<br /> <br /> The Black Bodhisattva makes a desultory attempt at intimidation with the old \u201cYou have come to me as I planned so that I can kill you\u201d routine, but the Scions don\u2019t buy it. They and Gary attack. Dark Master Chiba dons a demon-face mask and fights at his master\u2019s side as a high-powered (Legend 3) shinobi. The Scions, however, have sussed how to block the greatest power of the shinobi and their master: They light flares, filling the Black Mirror Shrine with light so there are no shadows through which Chiba or the Black Bodhisattva can teleport. Dark Master Chiba snuffs the first two flares with well-aimed shuriken, but the Scions promptly light more.<br /> <br /> Nevertheless, it\u2019s a hard fight. Jack manages to lose his shinobi shuriken (though Holly retrieves them). No one seems able to lay a finger on the Black Bodhisattva: He activates a War Boon, Warrior Ideal, that penalizes all the attacks upon him. The Black Bodhisattva attacks by slashing with the razor-sharp black glass shards of his robes, though he doesn\u2019t have much better luck against the Scions than they do against him. Holly curses him with the Evil Eye, but they can\u2019t tell if it\u2019s working or not. Overt Order clearly fails, and Holly ascertains that the Black Bodhisattva is Legend 5 \u2014 a Demigod-equivalent titanspawn.<br /> <br /> Holly\u2019s powers <i>do </i>work against Chiba. The ninja sensei remains nearly as agile as his master, but eventually Gary grapples him and Jaeda cuts out his heart in revenge for her car. He dissolves, leaving a shuriken and his demon-mask.<br /> <br /> Out in the office, meanwhile, Gus holds the door against waves of shinobi. Only two can attack him at a time, but they stagger their attacks so two attack every tick. Fortunately, Gus has cover from the door and an improvised shield, and he wears his new chainmail. The few shinobi who manage to hit him with their swords cannot harm him. Gus, on the other hand, finds that he can simultaneously punch one shinobi with his fenrir-enhanced cesti while using Game Face on another. Even with the cesti, he can\u2019t one-punch the shinobi, but every use of Game Face means another ninja running in terror.<br /> <br /> In the Black Mirror Shrine, Gary manages to clinch the Black Bodhisattva and tear off his mask. This seems eminently sensible, since, dude, creepy mask worn by creepy supernatural ninja leader? Gotta be a Relic, right? Well, yes, but the Black Bodhisattva responds with a chuckle and a statement that now the Scions get to see his <i>true</i> form\u2026 And Shosa-no-Mikaboshi swells into a gleaming, black-skinned horror bigger than Gary\u2019s giant form, with huge claws and a mass of tentacles where its face should be! The horrific Thing emits a ghastly ululation, throws off Gary with ease, and resumes battle with the Scions. Fortunately, although Shosa-no-Mikaboshi is now much stronger, it seems not quite as agile as before.<br /> <br /> Holly decides she can do nothing in this particular battle and goes to help Gus. About that time, the shinobi manage to cut the power to the building (or maybe it\u2019s the fire), making shadows by which they can teleport into the office; but only a couple manage it before Holly enters and lights flares. Between Gus and Holly\u2019s use of Overt Order, the few remaining shinobi are either knocked out or flee. Of course, the mind-controlled shinobi recover their senses within 10 seconds or so, but the fire is spreading through the building. The two Scions see one fanatical shinobi try to reach them by charging through the flames, only to catch fire and die, silently screaming.<br /> <br /> Gary gives up trying to grapple the Black Bodhisattva and goes back to chopping with his axe. Jack has a shinobi blade and manages to cut the Black Bodhisattva a time or two. Jaeda, however, guesses that light is the creature\u2019s true weakness. After wounding the thing in the head with the First Flower, she lights two flares and jams them into the wound, flame first!<br /> <br /> The Black Bodhisattva throws her off and screams, a sound more horrific and terrifying than any the Scions have heard before. It affects them and Gary like Dreadful Mien, affecting everyone at once. They must flee in blind terror for at least one action; and so they all charge out the door. Moments after they leave, the door vanishes. They decide it would be prudent to leave the building and be far away before police and the fire department show up.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T15:31:16-08:00","id":1019454,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 11: Ninja High School (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Aftermath: Jaeda\u2019s Choice</u></b><br /> <br /> United Martial Arts burns to the ground. No bodies are found, but more than a dozen students and other people around Avalon and Etna mysteriously disappear. Burt Broder and his two cronies disappear as well. At a guess, Dark Master Chiba found them and made them shinobi, too, but the three bullies went off on their own to revenge themselves on Landon. Jack likely killed two of them, and the third likely died in the conflagration at United Martial Arts.<br /> <br /> The Scions come out of the battle with three Trophy shuriken, from the two shinobi that Jack killed and from Dark Master Chiba (they couldn\u2019t collect any others during the fight because they did not actually kill anyone else at the dojo: All the other shinobi either ran away or were merely knocked out and died in the fire.) These shuriken deal double base damage against titanspawn. The Scions also got Chiba\u2019s mask, which is a Relic for channeling the War Purview. Unfortunately, Gary lost the Black Bodhisattva\u2019s mask during the fight (he was more interested in keeping hold of his axe).<br /> <br /> Earlier, Gus had promised to help Jaeda find another classic muscle car. Jaeda\u2019s first thought was another Roadrunner, but she decides that she\u2019d learned all she could from restoring one already; she should move on to some other car. Whatever she gets, she plans to hang the shuriken from Dark Master Chiba from the rear view mirror as a personal trophy.<br /> <br /> Gus also promised Jaeda a night of incredible, superhuman sex as consolation for losing her car. Here, however, Jaeda faces another choice. Gary joins the Scions in their post-battle wind-down party. (Are they going to say no, when he fought so bravely and effectively? The Scions are all pretty sure that without him, the shinobi, Chiba and Black Bodhisattva would have killed at least one of them.) When Gary notices Gus paying <i>that sort </i>of attention to Jaeda, he comes on to her too and promises her the best night of her life.<br /> <br /> Way back in the first episode, Gary had expressed his opposition to giants dating Scions, or at least to his sister dating Scions, but:<br /> <br /> * Gary\u2019s drunk (well, as drunk as someone with two dots of Epic Stamina can be);<br /> <br /> * Jaeda\u2019s not only incredibly hot, she appreciates fine automotive engineering;<br /> <br /> * Gary\u2019s incredibly competitive, especially with Gus. (An actual Fatebinding of rivalry between them.) And\u2026<br /> <br /> * Holly detected, with Unlidded Eye still running, that Gary came out of the battle with his Legend subtly altered. It\u2019s more like his sister Reese\u2019s now. Make of that what you will.<br /> <br /> Jaeda finds it difficult to choose between these two gorgeous young Nordic supermen. She fears the Scions are dispersing into factions, and sees sex with Gus as a way to keep a group together (or at least keep Gus with her). OTOH, Gary is also strong, brave, fought on her behalf and shares her automotive interests. Quite apart from physical attraction, she\u2019d like to get to know him better. Anyone who loves classic muscle cars can\u2019t be all bad, right?<br /> <br /> Gus, however, spots Jaeda\u2019s inner conflict. While Gary visits the bathroom to recycle some of the beer, Gus assures Jaeda that he won\u2019t think less of her if she boinks Gary this night, instead of him, and backs off.<br /> <br /> So, Jaeda spends the next several hours with Gary, first in The Car and then in a motel room. And the giant didn\u2019t lie. (In fact, he channeled his Rapacity Dark Virtue and spent a Legend for a Legendary Deed; I presume Jaeda contributed through Perfect Partner.) Morning finds them snuggling in bed (under a sheet that miraculously survived to preserve <i>Scion High</i>\u2019s suitable-for-network-TV status), still awake but extraordinarily relaxed and Talking Car. Jaeda\u2019s next car will have to be a fixer-upper, but Gary has promised his help in repairing it.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Addendum: Jack\u2019s New Sword</u></b><br /> <br /> Using the shinobi\u2019s sword agains them worked so well that Jack decides he wants a sword of his own. (Strangely, he doesn\u2019t want to use the perfectly serviceable relic Sword of Xibalba that would doom the World if it fell into enemy hands.) Real, battlefield-quality swords are not that easy to obtain, though. Jack turns to Jaeda. He knows Holly could obtain the sword for him, but she would very pointedly ask nothing in return, and Jack\u2019s pride won\u2019t stand for that.<br /> <br /> Jaeda phones some of her old gang contacts in Los Angeles. She finds someone who can supply a katana. (Some of those gang-bangers like that sort of thing.) They\u2019ll bring it in two days. It will cost $1000, and that\u2019s a discount for Jaeda\u2019s sake.<br /> <br /> When he hears the cost, Jack starts dithering and wondering if they\u2019d accept payment by installments. Jaeda knows that they don\u2019t. She firmly says, \u201cIt\u2019s a deal\u201d and leaves the problem to Jack. Who despite his vast intelligence cannot think of a more-or-less legal way to make $1000 in less than two days. Gritting his teeth, he turns to Holly. Who gives him the money without hesitation and, as Jack feared, asks nothing in return.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T18:00:58-08:00","id":1019527,"title":"","content":"A thousand dollars for a usable katana?<br /> <br /> Holy shit, that's dirt cheap!<br /> <br /> They typically go for hundreds of thousands of Yen.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T19:18:12-08:00","id":1019561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1019527#post1019527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A thousand dollars for a usable katana?<br /> <br /> Holy shit, that's dirt cheap!<br /> <br /> They typically go for hundreds of thousands of Yen.</div> </div> </div> </div>You're assuming this is an authentic, Japanese-made using Traditional Folded Steel, rather than a cheap knockoff. :V","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1596-horngeek","name":"horngeek","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1596_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"241"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T20:12:42-08:00","id":1019580,"title":"","content":"I seem to have missed something, because I don't know why Holly and Jack are pretending to be in a relationship together.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12300-crying","name":"Crying","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12300_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"151"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-19T21:05:07-08:00","id":1019596,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>horngeek</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1019561#post1019561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're assuming this is an authentic, Japanese-made using Traditional Folded Steel, rather than a cheap knockoff. :V</div> </div> </div> </div>Be interesting if it turns out to be the cheap knockoff at a really bad time...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12628-yukikaze","name":"yukikaze","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12628_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"83"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T04:49:17-08:00","id":1019713,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>horngeek</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1019561#post1019561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You're assuming this is an authentic, Japanese-made using Traditional Folded Steel, rather than a cheap knockoff. :V</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Somebody's getting punched if that's the case.<br /> <br /> Also, I just looked it up, and apparently 468,000 yen is only 4,000 dollars. So maybe 1,000 isn't as cheap as I thought.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T15:56:01-08:00","id":1020023,"title":"","content":"Well, since the player sprang this on me I pulled a number out of my ass for &quot;More money than Jack can obtain,&quot; yet &quot;Within the means for a moderately wealthy person,&quot; And then said it was a discount, to make it clear this was not a carefully researched number. Jack isn't getting a 100% traditionally forged katana, but he's still getting a serviceable sword, not crap that's just meant to look pretty.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T16:05:52-08:00","id":1020029,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crying</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1019580#post1019580\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I seem to have missed something, because I don't know why Holly and Jack are pretending to be in a relationship together.</div> </div> </div> </div>At some point, Delia insisted on this pact between her and Holly, maintaining the alliance between the two established mortal cliques (jocks and Fashionistas) while keeping the Scions tied to both. Gus dates a Fashionista to shore up that clique's prestige; Holly dates Jack, the football team's other star player, to assure the Fashionistas that she's one of them and not really trying to build a rival clique. Even though that is exactly what Holly is doing!<br /> <br /> Remember, I wrote these to help the players remember what happened in previous adventures. If something didn't make it into the chronicle, feel free to ask and I'll fill in as best I can.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-20T16:07:04-08:00","id":1020030,"title":"","content":"And now a bit of background I wrote up for the players after the Scions' meeting with the Bindahnas. Most of this is authentic jinn lore.<br /> <br /> Regarding Jinn:<br /> <br /> Having met the Bindahnas, the Scions might want to learn more ablut jinn. Here\u2019s what they can find out through basic research, Know-It-All and their Guides.<br /> <br /> These lesser immortals have no connections to any specific pantheon. They instead form a supernatural nation of their own with commoners, an aristocracy and several rival kings. Many jinn are evil and follow Iblis, an avatar of the sky-titan Ehekatoyaatl. Other jinn converted to Judaism, Christianity or Islam. Very few of them worship the Gods of Legend, despite frequent contact with these beings \u2014 or rather, because of that contact. As with mortals, most jinn believe there must be something greater than themselves. The jinn decided long ago that the all-too-human Gods were not that \u201csomething greater.\u201d<br /> <br /> Middle Eastern mythology says that whereas God shaped humanity from clay, he made the jinn from a scorching wind or from black, smokeless fire. Jinn who follow Abrahamic faiths accept this myth of their origin. On the other hand, the followers of Iblis claim the jinn are the whirlwind-demons that the Babylonian Epic of Creation lists among the chaos-Titan Tiamat\u2019s army of monsters. Whichever account is true \u2014 if either \u2014 the jinn are moral free agents, responsible for their actions in ways that titanspawn usually are not. Jewish, Christian and Muslim jinn follow divine Virtues. Iblisite jinni follow Dark Virtues.<br /> <br /> As with Gods, a jinni has a body of ichor but can dematerialize completely. In this immaterial state, jinn may dwell in the atmospheric heights, the Earth\u2019s surface, underwater or underground, at their whim. The surface-dwellers favor abandoned buildings, wells, ruins, tombs, deserts and desolate places for their homes, though some jinn prefer river banks, gardens or the foot of great trees. A few Legend-touched mortals \u2014 unusually perceptive children, the holy, the mad and the dying \u2014 can see immaterial jinn. Middle Eastern tradition gives other names for jinn \u2014 jann, ifrits and marids \u2014 but an ifrit is merely a powerful jinni, and a marid (meaning \u201ccontumacious\u201d in Arabic) is merely a hostile jinni. They are all one race.<br /> <br /> Many jinn choose to live in the World in a territory stretching from Morocco to Central Asia. These jinn also hold a few Terrae Incognita. Most notably, Iblis built his capital on Mount Qaf, an otherworldly peak or mountain range in the Middle East. Otherworldly palaces also lurk at the bottom of desert pits full of fluid, quicksand-like dust. On the other hand, Istanbul folk say the king of the sea-jinn has a palace in the Bosphorus near Leander\u2019s Tower.<br /> <br /> Jinn can manifest in various ways. They can gather whirlwinds about their immaterial forms. They also materialize as animals, humans or huge, ogre-like creatures. Jinn can be male or female: jinni or jinniyah, ifrit or ifritah. The ogre-form is hideous, with tusks and horns, but human forms are often notably attractive. Jinn have children, grow old and die, though they live for thousands of years.<br /> <br /> These lesser immortals can have many other powers too, from inflicting disease to possessing poets, but are notable for vast strength, rapid movement around the World, and creating and transforming matter. Strictly speaking, even the mightiest jinn cannot grant a mortal\u2019s every wish the way folktales portray them \u2014 but noble jinn may wield so many powers that they can perform almost any task a mortal can imagine.<br /> <br /> Only the mightiest magic can compel a jinni, but that spell was cast long ago. According to legend, King Solomon bound the entire race of jinn in the name of God. Henceforth, jinn must fulfill any pledges that they make in the name of Suleyman bin Da\u2019ud. The oath to Solomon binds even the Iblisite jinn.<br /> <br /> In addition to the Iblisites, the jinn have several other nations with kings of their own. The great Masitash, Caliph of the Muslim jinn, has 300,000 vassals, each of them a lord of other jinn. Muhammed himself converted Masitash and the kings Abdur-Rahman and Abdul-Kadir (and the Koran specifically says that God sent Mohammed as his messenger to both humans an jinn). Malik Afshan rules the Muslim jinn of Persia. Masidus rules Christian jinn (Orthodox: They don\u2019t hold with Roman Catholicism or newfangled Protestant doctrines); Tututash rules Jewish jinn. The jinn monarchs have their differences, but the Abrahamic kings all oppose Iblis.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1175,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","user":"DShomshak","date":". 12-20-2016, 07:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T15:26:49-08:00","id":1020677,"title":"","content":"Regarding Landon<br /> <br /> Holly\u2019s mysterious Guide \u201cmade inquiries\u201d about Landon Smith\u2019s becoming a shinobi. Here\u2019s what he reports:<br /> <br /> Landon is a shinobi, now and forever. He chose this, and such choices are irrevocable \u2014 even if they were made based on false information.<br /> <br /> Nevertheless, Landon is not necessarily bound to the Titan of Darkness that empowered him. Long ago, the Japanese Pantheon (called the Amatsukami) worked a signing statement into the pact that turns a mortal into a shinobi. Most shinobi are bound to the Darkness Titan and serve horrors such as the Black Bodhisattva of the Endless Night Clan. Some shinobi, however, are bound to serve the Japanese Pantheon as high-end Followers. All shinobi have a degree of free will for the affairs of their everyday lives, but when their masters command, they must obey.<br /> <br /> Landon is currently in an undefined state because the leaders of the Endless Night Clan have never commanded him. If a senior shinobi such as Dark Master Chiba should get to him and give him an order, he would have great difficulty resisting. If he kills at their command, he belongs to the Titans and becomes evil.<br /> <br /> There are only two ways to prevent this happening:<br /> <br /> * Make sure the Endless Night Clan (or other agents of the Darkness Titan) never command him. This means abandoning his life and family. It still might not work if the Titan\u2019s agents truly want to find him.<br /> <br /> * Bind him to some other power. The Amatsukami know how to bind shinobi to themselves, but you\u2019d need someone from the Amatsukami to do this. Other pantheons might have their own methods. The closest the Kaleva have is a spell \u2014 which only Gods can cast \u2014 for transferring patronage of a latent Scion from one God to another. The Anuna might have better and easier methods, since binding covenants with Gods are one of their big schticks. You\u2019d have to ask a knowledgeable Anuna. (If you check, the lion-demon bound to Leo doesn\u2019t know.)<br /> <br /> As a new shinobi, Landon has Legend 1. He has no powers in his human form. When he turns into a shinobi, he has increased combat prowess, minimally superhuman agility, makes no sound at all, and can teleport between shadows. As Dark Master Chiba showed, shinobi can gain greater power through practice and experience. Dark Master Chiba had Scion-comparable combat prowess, and his Relic mask suggests he could channel War Boons. If Landon can avoid enslavement to the Endless Night Clan, it might be possible for him to become more powerful as well.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-21T16:29:28-08:00","id":1020701,"title":"","content":"I take it that being able to shift between human and shinobi form is a house rule mechanic, either to make things easier, or to enhance the story?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:20:45-08:00","id":1021035,"title":"","content":"IIRC, the Shinobi writeup in Scion: Hero mentions dripping blood into a shadow to become a shinobi, but it doesn't say whether this is a one-time thing or whether shinobi spend most of their time as normal people and carry out this ritual to activate their powers for a time. I thought the latter seemed more plausible. I'd have to dig out the book to check.<br /> <br /> I guess you'd have to say it's a house rule, but it's to fill a gap in the RAW. There's no problem if shinobi appear *only* as Followers, but what if some mortal tries to become a shinobi in play?<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:28:45-08:00","id":1021040,"title":"","content":"Now a long adventure. Conveniently for this posting schedule, it's the Scion High Christmas episode! It also brings in several new characters and conflicts that become important in later episodes.<br /> <br /> And there's Aten. (Ducks as people throw things.) You have been warned. ADDENDUM: Also some speculations about the metaphysics of the World which I am quite sure have no support in the game as written.<br /> <br /> EPISODE 12: SOLD MY SOUL FOR CHRISTMAS<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: It\u2019s Raining Shoes</u></b><br /> <br /> In the final week before Christmas, the Scions all find their lives getting more complicated.<br /> <br /> GUS is somewhat surprised, though not worried, when a police lieutenant accosts him after school and asks \u2014 in a friendly way \u2014 if Gus could come down to the station and help the police reconstruct the last few days of a missing student. Gus agrees.<br /> <br /> Down at the station, nice old Lieutenant Picinich wants to know about Gus\u2019 relationship with the missing Acacia. Gus says it was limited to a fairly incredible one-night stand. (Plenty of people saw Acacia proposition him, so there\u2019d be no point in lying.) Picinich presses for more information, though, on how well Gus knew Acacia before that night. Then he asks where Gus was on the night after Acacia was last seen (the night when Gus saw her fatally give birth to the Tomb-Spawn, in Episode 10) and where he was on the night Acacia was stabbed (trying to open the Gate to Xibalba, back in Episode Six). Gus lies that he doesn\u2019t remember. He uses God\u2019s Honest to make Picinich believe him when he lies, or at least must leave out important aspects of the truth. Picinich is a hard case, though. He also asks Gus about his relationship with Sheena Delisle. Gus can guess that the lieutenant has suspicions about the rich young combat-trained athlete who has had two girlfriends disappear without a trace, but Gus feels confident that he can keep the law off his back.<br /> <br /> His confidence is unexpectedly vindicated when the precinct captain walks into Picinich\u2019s office with news that they\u2019ve found a witness for where Gus was the night Acacia disappeared: Another girl with whom Gus had another one-night stand. Which is news to Gus. Picinich lets Gus go, but no longer pretends to be friendly. He tells Gus he thinks the Sobecks are trouble, and money won\u2019t protect them from everything.<br /> <br /> Outside the station, a black stretch limo with tinted windows waits. A window rolls down. From inside, a very old man calls to Gus. The old man says he arranged for Gus\u2019 alibi this time, but he wants the Scions to leave town. Their force of Legend is calling disaster onto them and everyone around them. The old man rather pointedly refuses to give his name, saying he\u2019s sure Gus can find him if he wants, but why make it too easy? After a few more vague-yet-unhelpful ominous warnings, the old man signals his unseen driver and off they go. Gus memorizes the license plates.<br /> <br /> Back home, Gus mentions his brush with the police to his father. Mr. Sobeck is properly concerned, but he says he\u2019s not surprised at Picinich\u2019s suspicion and ire. Gus\u2019 father and his older brother Gav had run-ins with Picinich when they were young.<br /> <br /> Gus urges his father to say more: His father hardly ever talks about Gav, the uncle who joined the Marines and died in the Beirut barracks bombing. The elder Sobeck merely notes that Gav had some pretty wild adventures, got into trouble because of them, and sometimes got his younger brother involved. Gav joined the Marines because it was that or jail. Gus\u2019 father asks him to please be careful so he doesn\u2019t end up like Gav. Gus would like to learn more about his mysterious uncle, but Gus\u2019 father must go supervise the decorators who\u2019ve come to install the Sobecks\u2019 usual spectacular and soulless light display. This year\u2019s will be even flashier than usual to impress the gorgeous twentysomething redhead who will soon become Gus\u2019 latest stepmother.<br /> <br /> Gus asks Holly to ask her hacker minion to track down the old man\u2019s license plate. To no one\u2019s surprise, there is no such license.<br /> <br /> HOLLY has family issues as well. Her parents phone to say that instead of having Holly fly home for Christmas, they will fly out to visit her. That way, they can meet her new friends. She should throw her friends a Christmas party!<br /> <br /> This gets Holly pondering how to hide her resident Moomins from her parents. (The Moomins, meanwhile, say they\u2019d like to look for a place of their own or some honest way to earn money for plane fare back to Finland.)<br /> <br /> Of greater concern to Holly is her campaign against Vanessa, the Fashionista who tried to humiliate her. Most of the time, Holly tries to be a very nice person\u2026 because she doesn\u2019t like the person she becomes when she is truly angry. Not that that stops her from channeling her mother Louhi, and the Kaleva\u2019s Virtue of Vengeance\u2026<br /> <br /> Between episodes, Holly already acted to ruin Vanessa\u2019s friendships and place in school. She also sets out to ruin Vanessa\u2019s family. To this end, Holly sneaks into the gated community of Rowanfield, where Vanessa lives, and curses Vanessa\u2019s father with the Evil Eye. At the moment Holly casts the spell, though, she feels a stabbing migraine. She also realizes her curse was not as effective as it should be. Many of the trees around her \u2014 rowan trees \u2014 suddenly stand out to her sight with painful, hallucinatory intensity and she remembers that rowan is a traditional protection against witchcraft. Holly decides that she should make a strategic retreat. On the way out, she sees an unusual number of Rowanfield\u2019s security guards tooling around in their little golf carts, and in the back of the cart they carry\u2026 flamethrowers? <i>Eep.</i> Clearly, there is something very strange about Rowanfield!<br /> <br /> But such inquiries must wait until after Holly\u2019s date with Cole. They go dancing at a Seattle nightclub that features techno music and flashing lights. The three skinwalkers from Episode 10 interrupt them and demand that Holly come with them. Holly doesn\u2019t like this idea. Neither does Cole, who pulls out his Cobra and tells the skinwalkers to get lost if they don\u2019t want to get whacked in the head again. (Yes, he recognizes them from their first encounter.) The skinwalkers sneer, start shifting into coyote-man form, and attack.<br /> <br /> Holly curses one skinwalker. Cole has trouble striking his skinwalker foe \u2014 they are too nimble. A skinwalker claws Cole across the chest; he manages to strike the were-coyote once but does no damage. Holly manages to judo-flip one skinwalker into a bank of hot lights, and that skinwalker runs away yipping with his fur on fire.<br /> <br /> At that point, though, Holly decides that discretion is the better part of valor, and running away is the better part of discretion. She grabs Cole and they skedaddle, Holly dragging Cole along while she uses Lightning Sprinter. She drives him to an emergency room for treatment.<br /> <br /> Afterward, Holly admits she doesn\u2019t know why the skinwalkers want her so badly. She also finds that Cole believed his eyes when he saw the skinwalkers change (but he already realized there\u2019s weird stuff going on in Avalon and Etna). The evening ends with Holly rewarding Cole\u2019s courage with smoochies (though nothing beyond that). All in all, Cole thinks the evening is a win.<br /> <br /> As she thinks back on the fight, though, Holly remembers one odd detail she missed at the time: One person on the dance floor <i>did </i>notice the fight, seemed mildly surprised when the skinwalkers turned into coyote-men, but did <i>not </i>freak out. He merely moved further away from them. Holly resolves she must make further inquiries, later.<br /> <br /> Holly also covertly casts The Unlidded Eye to make sure the skinwalkers did not infect Cole with lycanthropy (coyotanthropy?) when they slashed him. They did not \u2014 but she sees that Cole is not only a heroic mortal, he is also now strongly Fatebound to her \u2014 and not just because he was the subject of her spell. <i>Hmm.</i><br /> <br /> JACK has another wants-to-slit-his-wrists day working at Stick It. This being the week before Christmas, Stick It is of course selling Christmas On A Stick (don\u2019t ask).<br /> <br /> Getting to work, Jack has to push past a crowd of people gathered by Reverend Stringer, the preacher at the Glory of God Ministries, the church Leo\u2019s family attends; Leo is there. Stringer has a Nativity scene and a big cross with a golden sunburst in the center. He makes a speech about bringing Christmas to the mall, the schools and City Hall, challenging the Godless Liberal Secular Atheist War on Christmas, forcing everyone to choose between Jesus and Satan, yadda yadda.<br /> <br /> At Stick It, Jack again does not try to get better acquainted with MariLee, the Orange Julius girl. She smiles when she looks at him, but every time he thinks about asking her for a date, Holly appears in his mind\u2019s eye, he thinks about what she could do to him (and MariLee), and he decides to stay faithful to his \u201cgirlfriend.\u201d<br /> <br /> Jack\u2019s Follower Moose stops by to complain about Christmas shopping. His four younger siblings all want either Wii or Cuddle Me Eggbert (the season\u2019s \u201chot\u201d toy), and he doesn\u2019t have that kind of money. Heck, his <i>parents</i> don\u2019t have that kind of money! He grouses that he wishes his family was Jewish, like Jack\u2019s, so they wouldn\u2019t need to deal with this crap.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s worse that two teens are smooching in one of the booths nearby, reminding Jack of what he isn\u2019t getting. The two kids are interrupted when a very pretty, well-dressed girl stalks up to them and exclaims, \u201cJason? You\u2019re cheating on me? With <i>her?\u201d</i> Jason replies that he isn\u2019t cheating, he\u2019s breaking up with her because she\u2019s vain, spoiled, demanding, etc. and he can\u2019t imagine what he ever saw in her. He leaves with his new girlfriend, and the rejected girl crumples on the floor, sobbing.<br /> <br /> Jack recognizes the girl: Vanessa, a Fashionista. He leaves his post to try comforting her (at least enough that she stops crying and attracting Mall Security \u2014 that\u2019s all Vanessa would need, to get thrown out of the mall for making a scene). While doing this, Moose stops by again, jubilant that a store at the mall had a Cuddle Me Eggbert, and for a lot less than he thought! Moose barely seems to notice Vanessa.<br /> <br /> JAEDA, on the other hand, feels that life is going pretty well. Yes, she lost her car and she expects another pathetic, impoverished, barely-making-the-effort Christmas from her father, but she\u2019s having fabulous sex with Gary as his secret girlfriend. Though she doesn\u2019t like the \u201csecret\u201d part.<br /> <br /> Gary explains, however, that his father hates, HATES Gods and their Scions because he was the only survivor when the Gods exterminated his family. Suttung Vetterson hasn\u2019t moved against the Scions only because he hasn\u2019t thought of a way to murder them and get away with it. If he found that Gary was dating a Scion, he\u2019d probably go nuts and try to kill them both. So, Gary can\u2019t hang out with Jaeda where anyone from Etna might see, lest word get back to his father. Still, he\u2019s also helping her look for another classic muscle car to restore.<br /> <br /> (Gary also tells Jaeda about how frost giants celebrate the Winter Solstice. It\u2019s their biggest holiday. They extinguish a bonfire, pray that the Sun similarly dies, and feast because if their prayers are answered it\u2019s their last meal, so they should make the most of it.<br /> <br /> (The Vettersons also exchange presents. Gary caused that innovation. When he was eight, he learned about human kids getting presents at Christmas, and he threw a tantrum when his father refused to adopt the custom. So, Gary burned the house down. He insists it was an accident: He brought a fir tree into the house, tried to set lit candles in it just like the Olde Tyme Christmas scenes on cards, and\u2026 <i>he was eight.</i> Fortunately, his father took it for revenge. Beat Gary within an inch of his life, but proudly. Since then, though, they\u2019ve had Solstice presents.)<br /> <br /> One night, though, Jaeda wakes up to crackling sounds and yellow light coming in through the trailer\u2019s windows. Jaeda immediately realizes this is an attack. The whole exterior of the trailer is on fire. She grabs the First Flower and her father, dives out the nearest window and runs \u2014 right before a car smashes down on the burning trailer. If she\u2019d been a few seconds slower, they\u2019d both be caught in the flaming wreckage. Jaeda sees a female giant silhouetted against the fire, poking at the wreckage. She suspects it must be Cailleach Sarsen, the mother of the late Don and Dave: She knows that Mama Sarsen is out for revenge, and she doesn\u2019t think Gary\u2019s mother would use fire as a weapon (frost giants and fire don\u2019t get along).<br /> <br /> Jaeda\u2019s father wakes up enough to see that A) their home is destroyed, and B) Jaeda is stark naked. He quickly gives her his shirt. Jaeda runs back to check on the trailer\u2019s propane tanks, but the impact of the car broke the lines from them and the tanks are venting harmlessly into the flames. The giantess is gone, though not before pushing the car (a neighbor\u2019s old Ford Taurus) out of the flames so it looks like it crashed into the trailer, not that it dropped on top of it.<br /> <br /> The police soon arrive. The cop in charge is, of course, Lieutenant Picinich. He notices that Jaeda is carrying a rather unusual obsidian-bladed knife. The cops also remove her Tepetlacalli from the remains of the trailer.<br /> <br /> Later, Picinich asks Jaeda some probing questions. The burning of the trailer was pretty clearly arson, and a background check turns up Jaeda\u2019s gang past. He wants to know more about Jaeda\u2019s gang connections \u2014 such as possible enemies \u2014 and what she\u2019s doing with Mesoamerican antiquities. Jaeda says (truthfully) that the knife and box are presents from her mother. Her answers seem to satisfy Picinich for now, but she can tell the shrewd cop has mentally filed her as a \u201cperson of interest.\u201d For the moment, he keeps the Tepetlacalli as \u201cevidence,\u201d and Jaeda does not protest. When she gets a chance later, she retrieves her sunglasses and the shuriken of Dark Master Chiba\u2019s soul from the ashes of the trailer.<br /> <br /> Of course, all this means that the Magdalena family has lost everything. They get new clothes and Red Cross vouchers from the cops so they can live a while at a motel, but they had no bank account and no insurance. Such matters, however, must wait. Jaeda calls the other Scions and lets them know about this deadly escalation by Cailleach Sarsen.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1175,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","user":"DShomshak","date":". 12-22-2016, 06:35 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:30:05-08:00","id":1021042,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 12: SOLD MY SOUL FOR CHRISTMAS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Interlude: The Christmas Party</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions get to meet Holly\u2019s parents. As requested, Holly throws a pre-Christmas party. She first introduces her parents to Gus, letting them see the dangerous, sexy, well-fed beast she hangs out with. Then she introduces Jack as her boyfriend, making the big goofy lug look much better, or at least much safer, by comparison. Moominmamma stitches together an outfit for Jaeda that looks suitably presentable.<br /> <br /> The Scions\u2019 mortal parents come too, though it\u2019s a challenge making Jaeda\u2019s father Mateo not look like a bum. He tries flirting with Jack\u2019s mom and is not entirely rebuffed. (Jack frowns.) Gus\u2019 father brings his fianc\u00e9e. If Mr. and Mrs. Frost have qualms about their daughter\u2019s friends, or their parents, they are too polite to say anything.<br /> <br /> Holly, Gus and Jaeda also give gifts to the other Scions. Holly\u2019s and Gus\u2019 are tasteful and/or useful, and not so expensive as to be embarrassing. Jaeda must settle for writing out coupons for services, such as detailing Holly\u2019s car. A good time is had by all, and Holly\u2019s parents do not see anything that would result in awkward questions.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: A Very Old Man With Enormous Secrets</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions gather and compare notes. Once again, obviously, Something Is Up. They guess it probably has a connection to the approaching Winter Solstice (a mythically potent date). After a bit of discussion, Holly pulls out her magic mirror and uses Mystery to find out what\u2019s going on. Fate gives her seven questions:<br /> <br /> * <i>What\u2019s the real name of the old man who accosted Gus? </i>Aristotle Faustus Dee. Really.<br /> <br /> * <i>What disaster does Dee fear, that he thinks the Scions\u2019 presence makes worse or more likely? </i>Eruption of the Titans into the mortal World.<br /> <br /> * <i>Did Cailleach Sarsen attack Jaeda\u2019s trailer?</i> Yes.<br /> <br /> * <i>Who, if anyone, goaded or manipulated her into doing this?</i> Guibourg. (Jack and Holly recognize the name of the hideous defrocked priest-turned-Satanist who conducted Black Masses for the notorious Madame de Montespan, back in pre-Revolutionary France. But it might just be a guy using the same name.)<br /> <br /> * <i>What is Guibourg\u2019s ultimate goal? </i>Eruption of the Titans into the mortal World.<br /> <br /> * <i>Where is Guibourg\u2019s primary base of operations on Earth? </i>Etna High Principal\u2019s office.<br /> <br /> * <i>What is Guibourg\u2019s plan for the Winter Solstice?</i> Summon a Hand of Aten.<br /> <br /> <i>Well.</i> Again, the two occult-literate Scions can fill in the rest. Aten is the Titan of Light, once worshipped by the heretic Pharaoh Akhenaten, whom some call the inventor of monotheism. Aten\u2019s servants, the Hands of Aten, are beings of light who may have inspired legends of angels, or (equally plausible) were shaped to exploit such legends. Hands of Aten range from minor cherubs to archangels who can battle Gods. All Hands of Aten are fanatically \u2014 even suicidally \u2014 loyal to the Titan and inspire similar fanaticism in other beings.<br /> <br /> Such a summoning would need a Relic as a focus. Something like, maybe, a big cross with a sunburst in the center? With a crowd of people consumed with self-righteous anger and zealotry as a power source? Suddenly, Reverend Stringer\u2019s little crusade looks a lot more sinister.<br /> <br /> But that is not the only game afoot. What of the so-mysterious Mr. Dee? Holly uses Ariadne\u2019s Thread to find him. To no one\u2019s surprise, the trail leads to Rowanfield. Secluded from the surrounding McMansions by a screen of oak and rowan trees, there is a real, no kidding mansion, clearly much older than the rest of the gated community. Holly sees that it\u2019s warded up the wazoo.<br /> <br /> A male nurse answers the door. When the Scions say who they are and they want to talk to Mr. Dee, the nurse says (with an embarrassed shrug), \u201cI won\u2019t invite you, but I won\u2019t stop you. Enter if you can.\u201d The Scions can. After a brief wait in the opulent-but-creepy vestibule (Is that a stuffed griffon? Why yes it is. Is it watching us? Could be) the nurse guides them to Mr. Dee.<br /> <br /> Dee is not surprised to see them. The Scions\u2019 first question is why he thinks they are bringing disaster onto Avalon. Dee explains that Legend calls more to itself. Because the Scions are gathered in Avalon, other mythic entities are drawn to the region as well. Eventually, Avalon could become so mythic that it drops out of the Mortal World and becomes a legend of its own.<br /> <br /> Another danger, though, is that the Gods are not the greatest force protecting the mortal World: More than any God, the Titans are barred from the mortal World by humanity\u2019s collective belief that such things cannot exist. People say they believe in Gods and devils, but they don\u2019t <i>really </i>think the sky might rip apart, the seas give up their dead, that armies of flaming giants could burn the World to ash, or other sorts of apocalyptic cataclysms. Not <i>here.</i> Not <i>now.</i> Such stories are\u2026 just stories.<br /> <br /> The Titans and their servants, however, want people to believe in Gods and monsters. The more people believe in small intrusions of the mythic or horrific, the easier it becomes for greater entities to invade the World and further erode people\u2019s sense of stable reality. If people come to believe that anything can happen, why, it shall be so \u2014 and the Titans will be loose in the World, as they were in the ancient Mythic Age.<br /> <br /> Most Gods would rather this not happen. They like the World as it is. Some Gods disagree. As the Scions think back on their adventures, they suspect that Coyote might have given the mythic cascade described by Dee a jump-start by his transformation of the Stone People into puppies \u2014 and that might have been his goal. After all, Coyote\u2019s irresponsible enough to do something like that just for shits and grins.<br /> <br /> Dee thinks the Scions are right that Guibourg is using Reverend Stringer as his pawn. An \u201cangel\u201d appearing before hundreds of people, filmed and broadcast to the world on the news, would serve the Titans\u2019 goals very well. Dee has heard of Guibourg: a powerful warlock, a mortal servant of the Titans given magical powers as Acacia was. Dee still wants the Scions to scatter as the best hope to quell the building vortex of Legend, but he agrees that for the moment, they are the best hope to stop this particular scheme.<br /> <br /> When the Scions ask Dee for his own story, he merely says that he is a mortal who once worked for various Gods. Dee learned more about them than is usual for their servants \u2014 enough to recognize that their interests were not always the same as humanity\u2019s, and so he stopped working for them. Since then, he has used what little power he has acquired to protect the World from supernatural intrusions by both Gods and Titans. Dee is aware of the irony that sometimes he uses (and abuses) people as ruthlessly as he says the Gods and Titans do. The difference, he says, is that he does so because of weakness, rather than strength.<br /> <br /> Dee also semi-confirms that Rowanfield is more than just a personal fortress. \u201cDon\u2019t go digging in Rowanfield,\u201d he says. \u201cIt would be bad.\u201d<br /> <br /> The Scions leave Dee\u2019s mansion fairly convinced that he is not their friend. In fact, Gus now wonders if it wasn\u2019t an agent of Dee\u2019s who shot Gary. However, they think he sees them as more useful than a liability, at least for the moment. They also think he probably knows a great deal about past Scion activity in Avalon\u2026 such as, maybe, previous Scions in Gus\u2019 family. For now, though, they need to focus on preventing a Christmas miracle.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:31:40-08:00","id":1021043,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 12: SOLD MY SOUL FOR CHRISTMAS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Angels We Haven\u2019t Heard on High</u></b><br /> <br /> Back at Dee\u2019s, the Scions decided they needed to reduce the number of people at Stringer\u2019s Solstice zeal-fest. They continue discussing ways to achieve this. The Scions know that secular society activists will show up for a counter-demonstration, which will only make Stringer\u2019s Evangelicals feel that much more persecuted and zealous (and the zeal of the counter-protesters might feed into the summoning as well).<br /> <br /> After a lot of brainstorming, the Scions simplify their plan down to visiting the leaders of such counter-protest groups, making them believe they need to protest something more important somewhere else, and having Marshall Byrd shoot out their tires once they arrive at the supposed White Power Against Gays Anti-Feminist rally, so they can\u2019t get back to Stringer\u2019s shindig. Then, Holly, Barbara and Delia do whatever they can to distract the Evangelicals and break the mood. While everyone\u2019s distracted, Gus runs in (wearing a ski mask), carries off the Relic cross, and chucks it in the back of a pickup Jaeda\u2019s hotwired. She drives away as fast as she can. Then they hope the cross must be <i>present </i>at the resulting explosion of holy outrage when people realize what happened. Jack, meanwhile, will stand ready to protect the girls from Leo in case the pious Scion of Nergal flips out and tries to kill them.<br /> <br /> Alone among the Scions, Jaeda thinks Leo should be told what\u2019s going on. When she tries telling him that his church is being used in a Titan plot, though, he won\u2019t listen, cuts her off and angrily stalks away. But he doesn\u2019t know the Scions\u2019 plan.<br /> <br /> As another ploy to reduce the worst-case damage, the Scions decide to make the Solstice a skip day since that\u2019s the day Stringer chose to protest at Avalon High. Fewer students mean fewer possible casualties. (Or hostages.) Everyone needs to mobilize their cliques: Holly for the brains, Gus and Jack for the jocks, Delia for the Fashionistas, Barbara for the student government types, Marshall for the military brats and Jaeda for boys who have a pulse.<br /> <br /> Holly puts the plan to Barbara and Delia. Barbara\u2019s okay with it, but Delia says no. Not unless Holly calls off her vendetta against Vanessa. Delia understands why, and it was the dumbest thing Vanessa\u2019s ever done, but\u2026 Vanessa is a Fashionista and hence more or less Delia\u2019s friend. Holly can\u2019t carry this any further without Delia taking umbrage on Vanessa\u2019s behalf. Holly says she will cease her vengeance when Vanessa gives a sincere apology. Delia says it shall be done.<br /> <br /> And it is. The next day, Vanessa tells Holly she is very sorry for trying to embarrass her. Holly can tell that Vanessa speaks with a sincere repentance born of stark terror. Holly smiles a Disney Princess smile, hugs Vanessa and says all is forgiven. And that is that.<br /> <br /> The plan goes off without a hitch. Between a third and half the students skip class (even a bunch of the brains and grinds who normally don\u2019t even hear about such plans, let alone follow them). Principal Flecker bites his lip; Vice-Principal Stone growls that he\u2019ll expel whoever\u2019s behind it all; most of the teachers don\u2019t give a damn.<br /> <br /> As noon approaches (the time the Scions think the summoning is intended to take place), only half the expected counter-demonstrators have shown up at Avalon High. Holly uses Unlidded Eye to confirm that the cross is indeed a powerful Relic of the Titans. Stringer begins his rant about bringing God back to the schools\u2026 and Holly and Delia start singing while hitting the crowd with Center of Attention. (It was never said what they sang, and it doesn\u2019t really matter, but I suggest \u201cJesus Loves Me.\u201d The Evangelicals can hardly object to it, making it hard for them to resist; and it\u2019s one of the most pacifying hymns ever written.) Leo is there among the line of burly young parishioners Stringer has recruited as guards for himself and his cross, but even Leo is caught up by the girls\u2019 blast of Epic Socials. Gus, fortified by Full Of Yourself, recovers his presence of mind first, dashes through, and carries the cross away. It\u2019s in the pickup and heading away from the rally before anyone in the crowd knows what\u2019s happening.<br /> <br /> As Jaeda and Gus make their escape, the other Scions attempt further damage control. Leo is white with fury at what he can see only as a deliberate insult to his faith. The Scions try to talk him down, but he thinks their story sounds preposterous. At least he isn\u2019t pulling out his mace and laying into them.<br /> <br /> Jack turns to Reverend Stringer, who sees the theft of the cross as proof! PROOF!!! of his paranoia. Jack\u2019s deft questioning, however, strips away the layers of false memory implanted in Stringer (or confabulated by him to explain inconsistencies in his own thoughts and actions) until he blurts out that he received the cross from Mr. Burgen, the principal of Etna High, along with the idea for his confrontational crusade.<br /> <br /> At this point, even Leo has to admit that something is hinky. He doesn\u2019t know that Burgen is (allegedly) the warlock Guibourg, but he saw how someone must have tampered with Stringer\u2019s memory.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, Leo isn\u2019t entirely convinced by the Scions\u2019 story either. Jack puts it to Leo that the proof lies in whether the cross turns out to be a Relic. Leo believes that Christ and the Gods have nothing in common; therefore, if the cross is a Relic of the Gods or Titans, it cannot be a holy vehicle for the power of the true God. Leo agrees that is reasonable, and demands the Scions take him to the cross.<br /> <br /> The Scions rendezvous at the abandoned barn where Jaeda partied back in Episode 10. They now face the problem of what to do with the Relic cross, which like all Relics is nigh-indestructible. Gus figures that now the Scions have saved the day, it\u2019s time for their divine sponsors to pull their weight. His smartphone has an app for hieroglyphics; he leaves a message on his own Facebook page in hieroglyphics, asking for the Pesedjet to deal with the cross. He figures that Thoth or someone is keeping an eye on him, or at least on his online presence. Sure enough, he soon gets a message back from the artisan-God Ptah, saying he\u2019ll be right over.<br /> <br /> On seeing the cross, Leo prays that God will forgive him for striking His symbol. Then he pounds it with his mace as hard as he can. He doesn\u2019t even scratch the gold. Leo turns away, even more miserably shut in on himself than usual, and the Scions leave him be.<br /> <br /> Ptah arrives in a flying saucer. He examines the cross with some sort of mixed supernatural/high tech scanner goggles, and confirms all the Scions\u2019 guesses about how it works. It\u2019s still armed, but Ptah can deal with that. He is, after, all, the builder of, you know, THE UNIVERSE. (Leo stiffens when Ptah says this, but says nothing.) A simple Relic is nothing for him. Ptah calls a squad of little robots out of his saucer, which quickly assemble into a magical buzz-saw whose blade is edged with little diamond ankhs. (\u201cPtah always has the <i>best</i> toys,\u201d Gus mutters.) Ptah saws the cross in half and announces it is now powerless. His work done, Ptah reloads his gear into his saucer and zooms away.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:33:11-08:00","id":1021044,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 12: SOLD MY SOUL FOR CHRISTMAS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Santa Troll Is Coming To Town</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions\u2019 job is not done, though, for they learned that another threat was building. When Jack talked to Moose about the skip day, the jock (and loyal Follower) worried about his grades and not doing what he was supposed to. <i>That isn\u2019t Moose!</i> So Jack activated Sense Tapu to find if Moose was under some supernatural influence. He wasn\u2019t. Well, not exactly. Jack found a Moose-shaped hole in the ambient supernatural substrate of the World. Jack had never sensed that substrate before, because he had never experienced its absence.<br /> <br /> Sense Tapu, however, is only a yes/no detector for any supernatural influence beyond mystic prohibitions. So, Jack calls in Holly. With Unlidded Eye, she can diagnose the void more precisely: Moose has lost his soul. Jack remembers how blas\u00e9 Moose had been to Vanessa\u2019s breakdown, and mentally kicks himself for not recognizing the plot hook at the time.<br /> <br /> Once the Scions have dealt with the immediate threat of Reverend Stringer, they can look for Moose\u2019s soul. Jack already knows to start looking at the mall. What did Moose do between griping with Jack and ignoring Vanessa? Why, he found a Cuddle Me Eggbert on sale at a seasonal store called Santa\u2019s Little Helpers.<br /> <br /> Cuddle Me Eggbert is the \u201cfad\u201d toy of the season. It\u2019s a foot-high plush egg with googly eyes, a smile and a computer chip and speaker inside. It asks to be hugged, rocked, etc. and rewards these actions with coos and gurgles. There aren\u2019t enough to go around, and parents are clubbing each other like baby seals to get them.<br /> <br /> Alas, the Scions are scattered at the time. Jack phones everyone and tells them to get to the mall PDQ. Jack and Holly get there first; Gus will be delayed by picking up Jaeda at the motel where she and her father stay. So, Jack and Holly head for Santa\u2019s Little Helpers themselves.<br /> <br /> Santa\u2019s Little Helpers is a nexus of concentrated, sugar-coated kitch. It sells holiday-themed candy, tree ornaments, house decorations and toys \u2014 and, every day, a few Cuddle Me Eggberts. The employees are all short, elderly men in elf costumes. In the back of the store, kids line up to sit on Santa\u2019s lap, tell him what they want and have their picture taken. Holly, using the Unlidded Eye, can see that the employees are really goblins and Santa is a huge, grotesque troll, hidden by illusions. And one hassled but happy mother is about to buy a Cuddle me Eggbert.<br /> <br /> Goblins are bad news. They are minor creatures in most ways, but they possess one great magical power: the Deal. A goblin can seal a deal with a curse of bad luck on the person who breaks it. What\u2019s more, goblins can take intangible qualities in payment, such as memories, skills, personal beauty, luck\u2026 or souls. They are also extremely cunning and persuasive salesmen. The Goblin Market is one of the enduring evils of the supernatural World, and it has come to Hooverdale Mall in the guise of Santa\u2019s Little Helpers.<br /> <br /> The two Scions waste no time. Holly shouts that a toy store at the far end of the mall has Cuddle Me Eggberts for sale at reduced price. That clears out most of the shoppers. Jack moves in and stops the mother from selling her soul for a toy, but draws the attention of the goblins and Santa Troll. They recognize him as a Scion and attack.<br /> <br /> The five goblins use props in the store as weapons. Two of them grab big candy canes and use them as maces. (They hit like maces, too.) Another two throw handfuls of entangling tinsel. The fifth mans a gumball machine and makes it shoot hard candy like a machine gun. Santa Troll, his cover blown, rips out a section of support pillar and tries to swat Jack with it.<br /> <br /> Fortunately, Jack is now extremely hard to hit, what with Untouchable Opponent and Fight With Your Head (used to counter the troll\u2019s high Epic Attributes) and he brought his new katana with which to fight back.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the number of opponents means he must turn his back to someone and give a goblin a free shot.<br /> <br /> Fortunately, Jack can pick which one, so he is only struck by candy cane-maces, and the goblins aren\u2019t strong enough to hurt him much.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the troll finally realizes that swatting Jack with a heavy object might not be as effective as trying to grapple.<br /> <br /> Fortunately, Holly cursed Santa Troll with the Evil Eye.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, the goblins know the Evil Eye as well, so they can\u2019t be cursed with that spell, and they can curse right back, reducing Jack\u2019s chance of hitting.<br /> <br /> Goblins are also tough enough that Jack can\u2019t kill one of them with a single blow. Jack realizes that if he doesn\u2019t get backup, the goblins and troll will eventually get lucky or he\u2019ll run out of Legend despite all his stunting: He\u2019s burning through huge amounts of Legend, what with using two Legend-intensive Knacks every action.<br /> <br /> Holly gets Santa Troll off Jack through the simple expedient of using Overt Order to make the troll run away. She herds him to the nearest mall entrance and holds him there through repeated use of Overt Order, but she can\u2019t force the troll to run into the sunlight (which would turn him to stone).<br /> <br /> About the time that Jack is spending his last Legend points, Gus and Jaeda show up. Gus shoulder-blocks the troll out the door of the mall. The troll turns to stone, falls over and breaks open. Since the troll is still dressed as Santa Claus, this traumatizes several onlooking children. At the store, Jaeda knifes a goblin or two.<br /> <br /> When Gus and Holly return, some goblins try to surrender. The most severely wounded goblin runs for the back room, but Jaeda kicks the door open before the goblin can lock it. The door smacks the goblin in the face and flings it against the wall. He, too, surrenders. The goblins all grovel and whine piteously, pleading for mercy \u2014 in fact, using the Power Groveling Knack.<br /> <br /> First, the Scions want the souls back. The goblins plead to keep them: Souls are incredibly valuable in the right markets. Can\u2019t they just cut the Scions into the scheme? Jack reminds the goblins that <i>this </i>time, the Deal is: Give the Scions everything they want, swear to leave Avalon and never, ever return to Washington State. Otherwise, the goblins all die on the spot. <i>This is not a negotiation.</i><br /> <br /> The goblins hand over the souls. These turn out to be white moths trapped inside little glass balls, packed in egg cartons. After suitable prodding, the goblins explain that the way to return someone\u2019s soul is to break the ball near them (20 feet or so) and tell the soul to return to its proper home. As a final piece of good fortune, the goblins kept careful records of whose souls they bought.<br /> <br /> The Scions also think to ask about the Cuddle Me Eggberts. They were goblin-made knock-offs, after all. Even here and now, the goblins can hardly keep from hugging themselves at the ingenuity of their scheme. See, the Cuddle Me Eggberts were magical, too. They will talk to the children, befriend them\u2026 and persuade the children to sell their souls as well! And there\u2019s nothing, <i>nothing,</i> more profitable than a child\u2019s soul\u2026<br /> <br /> Jack tells the goblins to get gone, <i>now,</i> before he reconsiders the Deal and kills them anyway. The goblins fade away.<br /> <br /> So, the Scions \u2014 well, mostly Jaeda \u2014 spend the remaining nights before Christmas breaking into people\u2019s houses, returning souls and stealing Cuddle Me Eggberts, saving at least three dozen children by ruining their Christmas.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-22T15:52:39-08:00","id":1021053,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1021035#post1021035\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">IIRC, the Shinobi writeup in Scion: Hero mentions dripping blood into a shadow to become a shinobi, but it doesn't say whether this is a one-time thing or whether shinobi spend most of their time as normal people and carry out this ritual to activate their powers for a time. I thought the latter seemed more plausible. I'd have to dig out the book to check.<br /> <br /> I guess you'd have to say it's a house rule, but it's to fill a gap in the RAW. There's no problem if shinobi appear *only* as Followers, but what if some mortal tries to become a shinobi in play?<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> It does say that's how one becomes a shinobi, but then it goes onto say that the ninja will <u><i><b>never</b></i></u> make another sound again. Also, the ritual is used by those who have chosen to surrender themselves to the Titan of Darkness, as opposed to it being something they're manipulated into.<br /> <br /> I think it's supposed to be a permanent transformation.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1021044#post1021044\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> So, the Scions \u2014 well, mostly Jaeda \u2014 spend the remaining nights before Christmas breaking into people\u2019s houses, returning souls and stealing Cuddle Me Eggberts, saving at least three dozen children by ruining their Christmas.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>This is the greatest ending to an episode I've ever heard.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 12-22-2016, 08:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T15:41:22-08:00","id":1022488,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1021053#post1021053\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It does say that's how one becomes a shinobi, but then it goes onto say that the ninja will <u><i><b>never</b></i></u> make another sound again. Also, the ritual is used by those who have chosen to surrender themselves to the Titan of Darkness, as opposed to it being something they're manipulated into.<br /> <br /> I think it's supposed to be a permanent transformation.</div> </div> </div> </div>It does. (And thanks for saving me the trouble of rummaging through my basement.)<br /> <br /> I don't remember my precise reasoning for making the change, but one of my campaign design goals was to treat at least some titanspawn as characters rather than as disposable monsters. I also wanted to fit in as many sorts of supernatural people as possible as fellow students, teachers and other associates of the Scions. It's difficult to do that with someone who can't speak, is part of a hive-mind and burns in sunlight.<br /> <br /> So I changed it. While I'm cautious about house-ruling mechanics, I'll change setting elements freely. I have very little respect for published settings: Having been on the inside of the game-writing biz, I know just how slapdash it all is. (And Scion was more slapdash than most.) I don't even consider it house-ruling, really.<br /> <br /> Another important change that people may have noticed: Giants with mixed Dark and Divine Virtues, and how this makes their true nature visible only when they expend Legend. I didn't want the PCs to know instantly that Gary and Reese were giants -- so I invented a reason they wouldn't, then made it part of the ongoing story.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-26T16:21:08-08:00","id":1022510,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DShomshak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1022488#post1022488\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It does. (And thanks for saving me the trouble of rummaging through my basement.)<br /> <br /> I don't remember my precise reasoning for making the change, but one of my campaign design goals was to treat at least some titanspawn as characters rather than as disposable monsters. I also wanted to fit in as many sorts of supernatural people as possible as fellow students, teachers and other associates of the Scions. It's difficult to do that with someone who can't speak, is part of a hive-mind and burns in sunlight.<br /> <br /> So I changed it. While I'm cautious about house-ruling mechanics, I'll change setting elements freely. I have very little respect for published settings: Having been on the inside of the game-writing biz, I know just how slapdash it all is. (And Scion was more slapdash than most.) I don't even consider it house-ruling, really.<br /> <br /> Another important change that people may have noticed: Giants with mixed Dark and Divine Virtues, and how this makes their true nature visible only when they expend Legend. I didn't want the PCs to know instantly that Gary and Reese were giants -- so I invented a reason they wouldn't, then made it part of the ongoing story.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Actually the house ruling on Giants isn't that strange. I think the 1E book explicitly states that they have some kind of perception altering effect on mortals so that they don't see their true forms. I know they said that most people don't believe the Cyclops really has one giant eyeball in its face, but chalk it up to being a trick of the light, or something.<br /> <br /> In fact, I think a lot of supernatural creatures have such an ability, where only beings of Legend can see them in their true forms unless they allow you to do so.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 12-26-2016, 09:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T16:06:36-08:00","id":1023468,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1022510#post1022510\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Actually the house ruling on Giants isn't that strange. I think the 1E book explicitly states that they have some kind of perception altering effect on mortals so that they don't see their true forms. I know they said that most people don't believe the Cyclops really has one giant eyeball in its face, but chalk it up to being a trick of the light, or something.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, that's the RAW for ordinary giants. The change I made: If the giants have mixed Virtues, Scions don't see the giants' true nature any more than mortals do, unless the giant asserts his or her supernatural nature by spending Legend.<br /> <br /> For an example, look at the football game back in Episode One. The Scions instantly spot the Sarsen brothers and Vetterson parents, though the mortals don't notice the giants among them. Gary, however, looks human until he picks up and throws the mass of Avalon Knights football players. Then the Scions see him as a frost giant.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T16:09:38-08:00","id":1023470,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 13: Odds and Ends and New Beginnings<br /> <br /> Another short episode. Gus\u2019 player couldn\u2019t game, and there were no huge plot points to pursue, so the remaining PCs dealt with various issues in their lives, wrapped up a loose end and learned a little more about the dark forces gathering around Avalon and Etna.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Post-Christmas Blues</u></b><br /> <br /> Winter break is mostly quiet for the Scions.<br /> <br /> GUS and his family leave to visit his grandfather in Florida \u2014 and, Gus hopes, learn more about his uncle Gav and great-uncle Randolph. Stories from his grandfather and other relatives leave Gus even more convinced that Gav and Randolph were both fellow Scions of Sobek.<br /> <br /> HOLLY takes pity on Jack and lets him break up with her or, more accurately, publicly ends their sham relationship. (He asks to break up; she replies, \u201cRun free, young stallion.\u201d) This leaves Holly free to make Cole her public boyfriend. Though she doesn\u2019t fill her parents in on these details, she does introduce Cole as \u201ca friend\u201d when he comes to visit her. Edgy Biker Guy Cole is on his best behavior to Holly\u2019s parents. Holly\u2019s father, meanwhile, reveals that he enjoyed motorcycles when he was young, asking Holly\u2019s mother with a smile if she remembers how they met. Holly\u2019s mother blushes.<br /> <br /> Cole\u2019s purpose in visiting, though, was not merely social. He tells Holly about the latest Etna High student to disappear. Supposedly, he and his parents moved out of town suddenly \u2014 but Cole thinks there\u2019s something fishy, seeing as how they left all their personal belongings behind. Cole says he\u2019s asking around, trying to find more information, and he\u2019ll let Holly know if he finds any leads.<br /> <br /> Just before her parents return to New York, Holly\u2019s mother warns her that the bank the family has run for 150 years is under siege. Holly\u2019s father didn\u2019t make foolish investments, but the bank did business with people who did business with people who did, and the dominoes are still falling. The Frosts will never be poor, but they might need to cut back: There\u2019s no guarantee they can continue to rent Holly a house and servants. Holly decides to turn her formidable intellect to the problem of becoming independently wealthy.<br /> <br /> Holly also ponders how the Moomin family can afford to return to Finland in legal, respectable fashion. The likeliest plan is a small catering or tailoring business, fronting for Moominmamma; Moominpappa is a skilled builder, but he would have trouble working as a carpenter without anyone realizing he is not human.<br /> <br /> Barbara tells Holly that her band is going after Guibourg. Leo is furious at Guibourg for using Christians and Christianity as cover for his evil scheme. He demanded that Delia use her oracular powers to find more about the warlock principal of Etna High. Leo further let Delia, Barbara and Marshall know that if they did not back him, he would confront Guibourg by himself. Barbara would rather the PCs stayed out of this for now. If they all die, the PCs can avenge them.<br /> <br /> On the first day back in school, Barbara tells Holly how the confrontation went. There was no fight. Guibourg was ready for them, meeting them in his office. The warlock said that his death would release a powerful demon that would ravage Etna. There would be no possible cover-up. Barbara and Delia think Guibourg was telling the truth, too.<br /> <br /> \u201cThat man has a Blofeld complex like you wouldn\u2019t believe,\u201d Barbara said. \u201cHe did everything but stroke a white cat on his lap.\u201d Leo was ready to try killing Guibourg anyway, but Marshall saw reason (for once) and dragged him away. Leo is not taking any of this well.<br /> <br /> In a possibly unrelated matter, Holly dissects an earthworm in biology class. The intense young teacher, Mr. Clive, discourses a bit on the biology of annelids and notes that some close-minded fools in the scientific establishment still deny the existence of the giant Palouse earthworm despite more than a century of sightings by reputable witnesses. When his own researches are complete, though, he will silence the mocking tongues of the Academy.<br /> <br /> JACK has a quiet vacation from school, though he continues working at Stick It. On the first day back at school, Dr. Demerit has a quizzybubble to see how well his students remember the subjects he lectured upon in the last week before Christmas. The quiz consists of 10 math problems for 5 points each, and one question for 50 points: \u201cWhat is my favorite color?\u201d Clearly, the man is taking petty revenge for losing a third of his class to the Scions\u2019 \u201cskip day.\u201d<br /> <br /> Holly realizes that she cannot possibly suss the math teacher\u2019s favorite color from his clothes or other clues; he likely did not even tell the class the truth. She pulls out her mirror. Demerit instantly notices and tells her she does not need to primp in his class. Holly catches Jack\u2019s eye. He gets the hint and begs Demerit to let him go to the bathroom. Demerit responds by pulling a bedpan from his desk and tossing it to Jack. Jack says that well, okay, but it\u2019s Number Two\u2026 Demerit rolls his eyes and tells Jack to go. This byplay sufficiently distracts Demerit that Holly can perform a quick Mystery session. After asking what Demerit had said his favorite color was (\u201cAquamarine\u201d), she asks some quick questions about Guibourg and Cailleach Sarsen. The most disturbing answer comes when she asks who Sarsen plans to attack next: It\u2019s her.<br /> <br /> Jack, meanwhile, is lucky enough to find one of Demerit\u2019s students from an earlier class. To Jack\u2019s chagrin, that class received a question about Demerit\u2019s favorite piece of music (the Ricercare from Bach\u2019s <i>Musical Offering</i>, if you must know). Jack grinds his teeth at being outwitted by the wily math teacher. Hoping to get a little of his own back, he writes the answer to the other class\u2019 question. The next day, his test paper comes back with a notation of, \u201cNot clever enough, Mr. Thompson.\u201d But Holly gets 100% and a notation of \u201cImpressive.\u201d<br /> <br /> JAEDA has a miserable holiday. Worst of all, losing the trailer seems to have broken her father\u2019s spirit. They need work, but her father seems unable even to try anymore. Jaeda finds herself forced to take charge and look for jobs for both of them. She eventually chivvies her father into taking a job as a waiter at a Mexican restaurant. Jaeda herself takes the quickest, easiest, most immediately profitable job she can think of: bikini barista. Given her stunning beauty, she gets a lot of tips. Between the two of them, they manage enough to take a room at a Motel 6.<br /> <br /> The only bright spots come from Gary. As part of his Solstice present for her, he takes her shopping to get some nice clothes instead of the police and Salvation Army donations.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T16:11:07-08:00","id":1023471,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 13: Odds and Ends and New Beginnings (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: The Lion Snarls</u></b><br /> <br /> While heading for the cafeteria, Jack hears a boy ask loudly, \u201cHey, Bible Boy, how\u2019d your big rally go?\u201d followed by laughter. Then a group of boys start singing, \u201cE-vil, In league with Sa-tan,\u201d followed by more laughter \u2014 abruptly ending in a squawk. Jack hurries to the scene and finds Leo holding another boy off the ground, in one hand. Leo is glowing as he says, in quiet fury, \u201cGod is not mocked.\u201d The other boys are cowering. Gawking students fall silent. Jack realizes that Leo is on the edge of doing something very foolish that will expose him as a Scion beyond any hope of cover-up.<br /> <br /> Jack defuses the situation the best way he knows how: He gets Leo to punch him instead of the hapless mortal boy.<br /> <br /> Holly arrives a few seconds later and uses Center of Attention to distract the other students. Leo drops the other boy, hunches in on himself, and turns away. Holly and Jack run a quick scam on the kids, suggesting the whole thing was a trick that Jack arranged (planting a glow-stick on Leo, talking the boys into making fun of him, etc.) To cement the hoax, Holly tells the boy Leo lifted off the ground to admit that he was in on the plan. Compelled by Overt Order, the boy says just that. The crowd breaks up, muttering with disgust at how Jack has \u201cplayed\u201d Leo.<br /> <br /> Jaeda arrives last to the scene with Leo. She follows him as he leaves and has yet another talk with him in an empty classroom. Possibly tired of coddling Leo, she bluntly tells him that he likes hating himself. A little probing about Leo\u2019s home life leads to the revelation that he was adopted by the Lefebvres (and he has no idea of his birth-mother\u2019s identity). More importantly, Jaeda is not surprised to learn that the Lefebvres raised Leo with strict discipline. In fact, she suspects most people would call it child abuse. Leo insists he deserved it all, and everything the Lefebvres did was out of love and concern for his soul. Jaeda ends the conversation no wiser about how to untwist Leo\u2019s self-loathing, but at least she tells him that he doesn\u2019t have to be alone in opposing the absolute, definite, no doubt about it evil of Guibourg and the Titans. Leo says he will fight them \u2014 even if it kills him.<br /> <br /> Somewhat later, Jack has his own talk with Leo. Leo admits that he feels not just defiled by his parentage, but utterly useless. The Scions say he should try to use his Scion powers for good, but he can\u2019t even <i>fight</i> Guibourg. Jack responds that none of them can fight Guibourg \u2014 yet \u2014 but they can fight lesser evils and do their best to be good people. Leo has to admit that while he cannot consider Jack a virtuous person, he has never seen Jack hurt another person on purpose. It\u2019s at least a start.<br /> <br /> Afterward, Jaeda thinks Jack may have been more successful than she was in getting through to the self-tormenting Scion of Nergal. (Maybe it\u2019s a guy thing.) Holly, for her part, despairs of ever helping Leo. She believes he will explode soon, and all the Scions can do is try to contain the damage.<br /> <br /> All this time, the Scions ponder how to go after Cailleach Sarsen. They feel pretty sure Guibourg is advising her, and that he likely has Prophecy with which to learn their plans. How do you fight someone who knows whatever you finally end up deciding to do?<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Bad Traffic</u></b><br /> <br /> After school, Holly gets in her Lotus to drive home. Fortunately, she has keen eyes and sees the car approaching on a ballistic arc, silhouetted against the clouds. It barely misses her. It\u2019s the attack by Cailleach Sarsen. Holly peels out and calls Jack and Jaeda to let them know the conflict has heated up again: The stone giant isn\u2019t even trying to hide her attacks from other people.<br /> <br /> Jack drafts the nearest one of his football buddy Followers, Gnu, to run the Stick It in his absence. Holly picks him up at the mall. Then they head for the espresso stand where Jaeda works. A quarter-mile from the stand, another car sails toward them. Holly slams on the brakes and bails; Jack grabs the wheel from across the front seat, steps on the gas, and steers the car away from the automotive missile. Hurled sections of Jersey barrier follow in quick succession.<br /> <br /> Holly runs the rest of the way to the espresso stand to find Jaeda. She hits the motorists with an Epic Social-backed warning that the Crips are on a rampage and coming down the freeway, getting the mortals (she hopes) to drive away from the fight. Jaeda then joins her in running back to the confrontation. Jaeda does not like running through a very cold January night wearing nothing but a micro-bikini, but two dots of Epic Stamina mean she can endure it for the moment.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Jack looks for Mama Sarsen. He finds her. He first tries a bit of automotive jousting, driving past her at high speed while slashing with his sword. It doesn\u2019t even nick her. (Stone giants are tough.) Then he rams her with the Lotus. That manages to at least rattle her a bit. (Four levels of bashing damage.) The car, however, is crushed, Jack took damage too, and he failed to knock the giantess back. She responded by smashing down with the section of Jersey barrier in her hands, knocking Jack for a loop. Holly and Scion reach the fight just as Mama Sarsen smacks Jack again, knocking him out and breaking bones. (The bashing damage wrapped around to lethal.)<br /> <br /> Holly concentrates on getting the unconscious Jack away from the wreck of her car. Jaeda uses the remaining concrete barrier and wrecked car as springboards to run up the giantess\u2019 back and swing around to stab her in the eye with the First Flower. And\u2026 something unexpected happens. Jaeda made a necklace from the shuriken left when Dark Master Chiba died; she wears it now. She feels Legend flow as she attacks the giantess who destroyed her home, and she wishes that she had a sword instead of a dagger. To her surprise and delight, the First Flower deals more damage at once than Jaeda ever saw before. Cailleach Sarsen falls backward, unconscious.<br /> <br /> Holly, using The Unlidded Eye, sees that Dark Master Chiba\u2019s shuriken is more potent than the usual Shinobi trophy: It is also a Relic for channeling War Boons. Jaeda has just done this, spontaneously learning and using a new Boon that the Scions dub My Knife Is A Sword. Such investigations, though, come after Jaeda carves up Cailleach Sarsen to kill her and extract her heart and its freight of eitr. First things first! Mama Sarsen\u2019s body quickly crumbles into a heap of clay and pebbles.<br /> <br /> The three Scions decide they should leave quickly before the accidents caused by hurled cars and Jersey barriers brings the authorities. Jaeda hotwires an abandoned car, and they zip away. First stop: Holly\u2019s house, so Jaeda can get something besides a microbikini to wear.<br /> <br /> <b>Some Slight Addenda:</b> Jaeda calls Gary to tell him about the fight. She figures it\u2019s best he hears about it from the Scions themselves. To her relief, Gary isn\u2019t mad: Cailleach Sarsen attacked first, it was a fair fight, and what the <i>hell </i>was the giantess doing, showing her powers so blatantly in public? And Gary is disappointed that he didn\u2019t get to see Jaeda fight in a microbikini.<br /> <br /> Jack calls Gnu to check on the Stick It. Gnu says he had no idea running a Stick It could be so challenging. Aside from the usual difficult customers, Gary stopped by to do his usual hassling of Jack and saw no reason to let up because Jack wasn\u2019t there. There was also a creepy old lady who, upset for some reason, croaked out a curse on the Stick It.<br /> <br /> The gang drives out to the mall (it\u2019s a good place to ditch the stolen car anyway). Holly examines the Stick It and finds no curse on it \u2014 just the mall\u2019s new Fatebinding to Jack from all the Legend he spent fighting Santa Troll. On the way out, though, with Unlidded Eye still running, she sees that magic still pervades the retail space that held Santa\u2019s Little Helper. A school and office supply store has moved into the space and is conducting its grand opening sale, but the place still carries some sort of Psychopomp connection leading beyond the mortal World.<br /> <br /> Holly and Jaeda investigate. The store\u2019s contents seem ordinary enough. As they look around, though, the pimply-faced teen at the cash register asks the manager, \u201cSir, What does this light mean?\u201d The manager looks at the register, looks around, spots the two Scions and approaches them, thanking them for coming to the sale and assuring them the store can serve all their school supply needs, however unusual. Holly sees the man is a lesser immortal: specifically, an <i>akh </i>or one of the justified dead of Duat, the Pesedjet Underworld.<br /> <br /> The manager leads them through a door to the back room where he keeps the special products for special customers: reams of authentic papyrus, ink and pens made from Nile water and reeds, laptop computers and PDAs in hieroglyphics, cuneiform, Hebrew, Ancient Greek, and other languages, and similar items. A cardboard cutout of Thoth waves cheerily. \u201cWelcome to Scribal Depot!\u201d the manager gushes.<br /> <br /> The only item the Scions find that is actually magical is a box of Blue Books of Thoth, enchanted to prevent cheating. It is addressed to Avalon High. Holly buys some papyrus and a laptop with a Hebrew keyboard, just because. It all seems harmless, but it undeniably shows that the Avalon region is getting weirder.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-28T16:12:54-08:00","id":1023473,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 13: Odds and Ends and New Beginnings (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Fried Worms</u></b><br /> <br /> As the Scions wonder what to do next, Cole calls Holly: He has a lead on the kid who disappeared. The lead led to the Etna High science club, and from there to an abandoned brewery. The science club is meeting at the brewery very soon! The Scions decide to investigate.<br /> <br /> Since the Band doesn\u2019t have Gus, and they suspect they\u2019ll have to fight a bunch of science nerds turned cultists, the Scions decide to get some mortal backup. Jack asks his football buddy Followers if they\u2019d help him, and of course the high-spirited jocks say yes. Maynard Salkind, Holly\u2019s pyrotechnical Follower, hears about the preparations and asks to help as well. The Scions agree, thinking he can supply a good distraction and maybe an infernal device to burn monsters, the way he did against the Lucifuge.<br /> <br /> Cole carries Holly to the old brewery on his motorcycle while Jack, Jaeda, Maynard and the Defensive Line pile into cars and trucks. The motorcycle arrives first.<br /> <br /> The old brewery is a big corrugated steel and concrete box with a loading dock, two side doors, large, blacked-out windows and ninja-port skylights. Cole jimmies open a window. He and Holly sneak into one of the old offices, and from there to a point where they can spy on the science club.<br /> <br /> The main room of the brewery is just a big empty two-story space with some large pits in the floor and various girders crossing it up above. As for the science club\u2026 The kids are wearing sinister black hooded cultist robes. On a dais by the larges pit stands a tall, elderly, distinguished-looking if somewhat cadaverous man with a shock of white hair, dressed in fancier black robes. He carries a weird contraption that looks like a cross between a caduceus and Egon Spengler\u2019s ecto-meter. \u201cThat\u2019s Mr. Thesiger, the biology teacher!\u201d whispers Cole. Next to him on the dais\u2026 \u201cOh, God, let me go blind! That\u2019s Dirtwad!\u201d The pickle-faced, middle-aged Truant Officer Dortwade is <i>not </i>the sort of person who should wear transparent silk and leather kinkwear. Next to <i>her </i>is a winch for the rope that suspends a struggling, gibbering boy over the pit. The heads of three lindwurms rise out of the pit, mouths working eagerly.<br /> <br /> Thesiger chants, \u201cI\u00e4, Chthonia! Great worm mother of the Earth, accept this sacrifice!\u201d Dortwade starts lowering the kid into the pit.<br /> <br /> Holly decides she\u2019d better stop this right now, or at least buy time for Jack and Jaeda to arrive. She saunters out, kicking on Center of Attention, and delivers a few choice insults to Thesiger, Dortwade and the assembled cultist nerds. Thesiger reacts predictably by shouting \u201cDefilers! Destroy them!\u201d The kids charge at Holly and Cole, who retreat to an office and bar the door with a convenient broom. The cultist kids bang ineffectually on the door for a bit, but then they get wise, come around the side of the building and start breaking the office windows. Holly and Cole then fling open the door, push their way past the few cultist kids left on guard, and run up a flight of narrow steps to an office overlooking the brewery floor. Once again, they bar the door, and there\u2019s no way up except the stairs.<br /> <br /> Looking out the big plate-glass window, however, they see Thesiger has begun the ceremony again while Dortwade has drawn a gun. Dortwade shoots out the window; Thesiger points his widget at Holly and shouts a curse in Ancient Greek. Holly feels the Evil Eye on her: She knows that curse, though, and so she can cast it herself to counterspell Thesiger\u2019s use. But the captive kid is dropping toward the pit again.<br /> <br /> Well, there\u2019s nothing for it but to take the fight to the evil priest and priestess. Holly jumps down from the office (trivially easy for a Scion with her Epic Dexterity and Athletics) and runs over to the dais. Dortwade shoots at her, but can\u2019t hit the nimble Scion of Louhi. Thinking quickly, Holly grabs one of Dortwade\u2019s discarded shoes and jams it in the winch. Cole is not a Scion, but he\u2019s physically capable and has guts: As soon as he can, he loops his belt over a girder, slides across the brewery and drops onto the dais beside Holly. He draws his Cobra.<br /> <br /> Thesiger snarls, \u201cCurse you, interfering Scion!\u201d but he only had one Legend point so he won\u2019t actually be doing any more cursing. Cultist kids grab at Holly and Cole but miss (they lack the tactical sense to surround anyone and so get an unexpected attack). Cole swats a kid or two. Thesiger declares that the children of Chthonia will slay at his command. He waves his caduceus-meter widget, holds his other hand dramatically to his forehead, and a lindwurm lunges up and out of the pit to attack Holly. Cole smacks it good with his Cobra but can\u2019t do a thing to the giant, rubbery worm.<br /> <br /> Jack and Jaeda arrive just as the lindwurm snaps at Holly again \u2014 and hits. The bite would have killed her if she hadn\u2019t worn her kevlar trenchcoat. As it is, the bite grievously wounds her (reducing Holly to her \u20134 health level). Holly decides she can\u2019t keep doing this. She runs out of the building, trailing blood, as Jack and Jaeda run in. Jack just crashes through a window; Jaeda leaps after him. The Defensive Line, clad in their football pads and wielding baseball bats, round up any cultist kids left outside and guard Maynard while he pulls gear out of his trunk.<br /> <br /> Outside, the lindwurm concentrates on the wounded Holly and ignores the other kids. (Lucky for them.) Even wounded, though, Holly has no trouble keeping her distance (thank you Lightning Sprinter). Inside, Jack and Jaeda engage Thesiger and Dortwade. Like Cole, Jack wishes he could go blind when he sees Dortwade. Cultists kids try to grab them but haven\u2019t a prayer of succeeding. The first attempt to knock Thesiger into the pit of lindwurms fails, but then Jack cuts off the hand of the biology teacher that held the widget. As the cult leader reels in shock, Jack pitches him into the pit.<br /> <br /> As for Jaeda, she enters by leaping over the pit, catching the kid intended as a sacrifice, and carrying him across to the dais as she cuts the rope. Fortunately, she\u2019s agile enough that Dortwade and the cultists kids can\u2019t touch her as she cuts the sacrificial boy free. He\u2019s still pretty out of it, but when Jaeda tells him to run for his life, he runs. That done, Jaeda tosses Dortwade into the pit. Lindwurms eat her and Thesiger.<br /> <br /> The Defensive Line bashes open another door and Maynard strides into the brewery. He\u2019s dressed in heavy leather coveralls and carries a flamethrower. He clears a path to the pit by the simple expedient of shooting a jet of flaming gasoline: Cultist kids scatter. The Defensive Line spreads out, bats raised menacingly, to round up the cultist kids. The Scions agree there\u2019s no better way to deal with the two adult lindwurms still in the pit, and the dozen or so young, than for Salkind to hose down the pit with his flamethrower while they attack any lindwurms that try to escape. Salkind does so with pleasure, singing:<br /> <br /> <i> King Salamander, that\u2019s his name.</i><br /> <i> A desert maker, that\u2019s his aim.</i><br /> <i> The benign cremator, branding iron in his hand,</i><br /> <i> Eager and willing to torch the land.</i><br /> \u2014 Siouxsie and the Banshees, \u201cBurn Up\u201d<br /> <br /> Jack thinks that Thesiger and Dortwade were <i>not </i>the scariest and craziest people here tonight.<br /> <br /> Outside, Thesiger losing his widget, and his death immediately thereafter, breaks his mental control of the lindwurm. This makes no difference for Holly: The lindwurm still wants to eat her. As the other lindwurms fry, though, Jack and Jaeda come to her aid. The three Scions quickly kill the lindwurm and throw it into the pit of fire.<br /> <br /> That leaves the question of the cultist kids. On the one hand, they are accessories to multiple murders. (The Scions are pretty sure this was not the first sacrificial ritual, and the missing kid and his parents are still missing.) On the other hand, proving this to the authorities without revealing supernatural shenanigans would be difficult, and the Scions don\u2019t feel like murdering the kids in cold blood. (Another 20 or so disappearances at once would also lead to official inquiries they want to avoid.) So, the Scions just give the kids a stern lecture. Well, more of an eloquent threat, backed by Epic Socials, baseball bats, swords and a maniac with a flamethrower, about what will happen to the kids if they step out of line or breathe a word of what happened to anyone.<br /> <br /> Jaeda gives Holly first aid, then drives her to a hospital. Finally, the Scions have Salkind burn down the brewery. He\u2019d like to try demolition charges, but the Scions decide that simple arson will raise fewer questions with the authorities.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Epilogue</u></b><br /> <br /> And it is so. Or, nearly so. When the cops and fire department investigate, they find unidentifiable chewed bones \u2014 remains of the lindwurms' victims \u2014 in the pit of a burned building. Thesiger\u2019s and Dortwade\u2019s remains are intact enough to identify as human, but that detail is small enough for Scion Mind Tricks to cover up. The official story becomes that homeless people squatted in the abandoned brewery, and that a gang used them as guards for a meth lab. The meth lab caught fire and burned down the building. Thesiger and Dortwade are rumored to have eloped. People who knew them shudder.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T14:53:10-08:00","id":1023949,"title":"","content":"At around this point, the Scion High campaign went on hiatus for a few months. While it was a lot of fun, it was also a lot of work and I needed a rest. When we started up again, I began with a pair of handouts about what the players said their PCs had been doing, and about what the rival Scions had been up to.<br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">SCION HIGH UPDATE: Activities of the PCs</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">While the Scions have gone a month without great adventures, they have kept busy with school, friends, family and assorted inquiries.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">GUS talked to the <i>akh </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">who manages Scribal Depot. Gus wants to know if any of his older relatives have arrived in the Pesedjet Underworld. Pediamen-nest-tawy \u2014 AKA Pete Nestaway \u2014 says Duat and the Field of Reeds still receive new residents (though not many). Pete doesn\u2019t remember Gotthelf, Randolph or Gav Sobeck among the new arrivals who coached him for his role in the modern World, but that doesn\u2019t mean much. Pete has no quick way to inquire about Gus\u2019 kin, but he can set the wheels in motion. On the other hand, Pete says that if Gus visits the Field of Reeds himself, he will almost certainly meet any close relatives who made it there. Fate, you know.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus also talked to Landon about gathering a cadre of kids with drive. Landon isn\u2019t sure exactly what Gus wants or how to recognize it. He does have one notion, though: Landon thinks that some of his fellow nerds might be willing to work out if they knew they wouldn\u2019t be laughed at. Jocks are not kind to the weak. At least, some nerds think the jocks would make fun of them if they turned up at the weight room. (Landon feared this, though most of the jocks have treated him with respect since he won his fight with Burt Broder.) So, one possibility might be to set up weight training or other fitness classes with a promise that this will be a mockery-free zone.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Home life has been hectic. Gus\u2019 father and his soon-to-be-stepmother Brittany have decided to hold the wedding at a Cascade Mountain ski lodge on President\u2019s Week (mid/late February), when school is cancelled for the entire week. That way, the Sobecks can have a week-long honeymoon and Gus can enjoy a ski vacation. Dad will even pay to bring one of Gus\u2019 friends to keep him company. Not that he has trouble finding company of his own, chip off the old block\u2026</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">HOLLY spends most of her time recuperating from her injury. Continuing to attend school would drastically slow her recovery, but she can send her laptop with a camera and microphone to watch classes and have a friend deliver her homework assignments. Cole visits frequently.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holly wants to become independently wealthy in case her parents can no longer afford to rent a house for her. This turns out to be harder than Holly thought. Mystery gives no simple answer: Asking how she can make money with an internet business (without porn) is too broad. She needs to ask narrower, more precise questions.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holly has a lot of time to ponder Guibourg\u2019s claim that killing him would release a terrible demon. The claim seems plausible. The problem with trying to sabotage such a Gotcha Last enchantment is that it doesn\u2019t come from Guibourg: A patron Titan had to set this up, and the Titan\u2019s power will send the demon to Earth. Guibourg is like the tripwire of a land mine: His death is the trigger, but it isn\u2019t the explosive. Breaking the Fateful connection between Guibourg and his patron undoubtedly takes greater power than Holly is likely to achieve soon. A God of magic could do it, but this might bring the God directly into conflict with the Titan.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">JACK: The dude abides.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Okay, and the Defensive Line wants more adventures. Busting a murderous cult was the biggest, most exciting, most <i>important </i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">thing they\u2019ve ever done. They want more.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jack\u2019s Guide warns him that anyone he brings into his life will probably become part of the Big Game between Gods and Titans. That\u2019s not safe. Then again, just living in the same town can be enough to place mortals in the crossfire. His only alternative is to be a useless, womanish coward (Jack asks his Guide, \u201cHave you seen Jaeda?\u201d), turn his back on his Legend and let the Titans do their worst. This would displease Lono. But if that\u2019s his choice\u2026?</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">JAEDA can arrange a few secret trysts with Gary. He keeps his eyes open for likely cars for her, too. So far, they have had no luck. Every car they\u2019ve found has been too expensive or too wrecked. So, she has to ride the bus to school. AIEEE!</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holding a steady job, though, seems to help her father stay sober. He seems proud that he\u2019s keeping a roof over Jaeda\u2019s head instead of the other way around.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tlazolt\u00e9otl stopped by briefly on her way through to somewhere else. She thinks Jaeda is right, though, about her father\u2019s need to feel useful, so she isn\u2019t going to give the family more than she does already. She also discusses how to change a person\u2019s fundamental Nature. It isn\u2019t easy, but she\u2019s done it. She thinks Jaeda and her friends could too, if they tried.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Tlazolt\u00e9otl also has a little to say about saving Landon from being enslaved by the Darkness Titan that empowers him. The Amatsukami aren\u2019t the only pantheon with ways of binding mortals to them. This usually involves God-level magic. It does in the case of the Atzl\u00e1nti, at least. (It would likely involve some form of sacrifice, such as cutting out Landon\u2019s heart. Don\u2019t worry, he\u2019d get better.) Other pantheons might have better methods. Binding covenants with Gods are sort of an Anuna thing, so Leo might be able to do something \u2014 but that would likely bind Landon to Nergal, not to Leo. Maybe if Landon accepted a Tapu from Jack or an Atua of greater power? Or if a Pesedjet magically changed Landon\u2019s name? At this point, though, Tlazolt\u00e9otl is guessing. A real God of Magic would know more.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Finally, Tlazolt\u00e9otl asks if Jaeda has found her nagual \u2014 the animal that is the external half of her soul. This is a big thing for Atzl\u00e1nti, since they can wield some unusual powers through their nagual and by no other means.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T14:55:29-08:00","id":1023951,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">SCION HIGH UPDATE: Activities of the Rival Band</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">January was a quiet month for the PCs in part because the rival Scions chased every hint of Titanspawn activity they could find. That included Delia using the prophetic powers she inherited from Apollo to find where creatures <i>would</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> be. The other Scions are quite sure they have finally forced every last specter back to Xibalba and exterminated the lindwurms. (Including the baby lindwurms the Avalon High biology teacher Mr. Clive was raising in a terrarium under the misapprehension that they were Giant Palouse Earthworms. The Scions even spent a weekend freezing their butts off in Eastern Washington, finding real Giant Palouse Earthworms so Mr. Clive would have something non-supernatural with which to silence the mocking tongues of the Academy.) And yet, Barbara, Delia and Marshall find their Legends stubbornly stuck at 2. Only Leo has Legend 3 \u2014 the result of his attempt to free himself from his demon by throwing himself on he First Flower.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The rival Scions also have their own activities:</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">BARBARA stays busy with Student Government, Future Business Leaders of America, and a few other clubs and extracurricular activities. Just as Holly has staked out the brainy, gifted kids as her Followers, and Delia has the Fashionistas (for what they\u2019re worth), Barbara cultivates a cadre of potential business people, politicians and activists. She has already used them to run a fundraiser for the school band that got her picture in the local newspaper: Barbara\u2019s Followers and the Fashionistas just hit up their parents for donations.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Barbara also talked her FBLA cronies into starting an investment club. With Barbara\u2019s Epic Intelligence (and a prophetic tip from Delia) the junior investors are outperforming the market 3 to 1. Admittedly, for most of them this is not a lot of money \u2014 they don\u2019t have much to invest.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Oh, and Barbara has learned how to use the battle-axe her father Shango gave her. When she goes all-out, she can chop things up pretty good. And she can fly.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">DELIA has similarly worked to become a competent archer so she isn\u2019t completely limited to social activities. As a top cheerleader, she\u2019s already a decent athlete, so this isn\u2019t a big leap for her. Still, she\u2019s limited by her quite ordinary strength. She\u2019s looking into explosive arrows, or maybe some sort of Boon to supercharge her attacks.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Beyond that, Delia remains smug and happy as leader of the Fashionistas. She even feels secure enough to be nice to Laine and Derek, letting them be accepted within the school\u2019s roster of Beautiful People.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">LEO is at least no longer walking around with a giant invisible Kick Me sign over his head. He hates Guibourg more than he hates himself. Leo now spends as much time training and working out as Marshall or Gus, in hopes he can become strong and skilled enough to kill whatever monster Guibourg has as his Gotcha Last. So, instead he walks around with a giant invisible I\u2019m Going To Snap sign. People instinctively stay away from him.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">MARSHALL should be happy. Barbara lets him think he leads their Band as its \u201cWar Leader.\u201d He has his military brat Followers helping the Band as much as mortals with guns can. Marshall\u2019s gun shop mentor gave his M-16 an underslung shotgun barrel, increasing his tactical options. Better still, one of his Band\u2019s adventures (something about a cyclops) led to him acquiring a magic bayonet that makes the whole weapon a Relic: Marshall\u2019s shots are now Piercing versus all soak, not just armor. But, he has never performed some deed glorious and amazing enough to raise his Legend. His frustration is obvious.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-29T15:43:24-08:00","id":1023972,"title":"","content":"So out of the whole bunch, the one who hates being a Scion is moving up in the ranks, while everybody else is stuck at the bottom?<br /> <br /> <br /> Fate is weird, like that. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T16:08:47-08:00","id":1024531,"title":"","content":"Well, at the time I let my players vote whether Leo should get a Legend bump from his deed. Assent was unanimous.<br /> <br /> More than any other of the rival Scions, Leo is building a story. It isn't a happy story, but it's pushing him further -- and he's pushing himself -- more than the others.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T16:09:39-08:00","id":1024532,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 14: Bad Options<br /> <br /> The Scions had a quiet January and part of February, in part because the rival Band chased after anything that hinted at any possibility of adventure. They stay busy with school and everyday life as they look forward to President\u2019s Week break in February.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Just Watching Life Go By</u></b><br /> <br /> JACK is glad that Holly ended their sham relationship. That means he can pay attention to MariLee, the girl who works at the mall food court\u2019s Orange Julius stand. She seemed interested in him. Sure enough, when he asks her for a date, she says yes.<br /> <br /> They go to a movie at the mall\u2019s multiplex. Afterward, they return to the food court for a meal. Jack thinks the date is going well. MariLee is pretty. She\u2019s nice. she laughs at his jokes. He thinks that after a few more dates, she might put out.<br /> <br /> And then Kwame McMorris, suspected were-leopard of Etna High, notices them. Kwame saunters over and asks Jack if he\u2019s on a date. He compliments Jack on dating such a pretty girl, smiles and says that MariLee looks good enough to eat. Jack smiles back and says that that reminds him, this <i>friend </i>of his was saying that if Kwame came sniffing around his girlfriend and did anything to hurt her, or even touched a hair on her head, he would break every bone in Kwame\u2019s body and kill him. Kwame gets the message and backs off.<br /> <br /> Jack gets the message, too. He remembers what his Guide said about entangling mortals in his life. So he self-sabotages, playing the buffoon enough that MariLee no longer takes him seriously as boyfriend material. No kiss at the end of the date, and MariLee shows no sign of wanting a second date.<br /> <br /> Jack later confesses to Gus that his choice isn\u2019t entirely to protect MariLee. She\u2019s pretty. She\u2019s a nice girl. She\u2019d make a wonderful girlfriend. But she\u2019s so <i>boring\u2026</i><br /> <br /> GUS avoids his own feminine entanglements. His father and fianc\u00e9e, Brittany, plan to get married on Sunday of President\u2019s Week, at a ski lodge in the nearby Cascade Mountains. That way, they can have their honeymoon and Gus can enjoy a ski vacation. (Mr. Sobeck isn\u2019t quite ready to leave his son unsupervised for a week.)<br /> <br /> One evening, Mr. Sobeck is called away by a business emergency. Gus encounters Brittany wandering the halls of the house. She\u2019s been drinking and is not entirely steady on her feet. Brittany sways against him and Gus catches her before she falls. Brittany clings to him and says he\u2019s very strong. And young. And handsome. And probably great in bed. If they\u2019re going to do it, they should do it now because once she marries his father it would be, you know, <i>wrong.</i> Said with a titter.<br /> <br /> Brittany is hot (in a hungry, slightly desperate way), but Gus has no desire for this particular complication in his life. He smiles a crocodile\u2019s smile and says he never horns in on his father\u2019s action. He waits until his dad is done with a woman. Then, maybe. Thoroughly spooked, Brittany pulls away from Gus. He takes the bottle and settles her on a couch to sleep it off\u2026 alone.<br /> <br /> JAEDA, meanwhile, has a plan for a very different sort of rendezvous. She wants to have a private talk with Leo Lefebvre\u2019s lion-demon Guide to learn more about Leo\u2019s home life and the ways it\u2019s messing up Leo. She does not want Leo in on the conversation, though. Jaeda wonders if she can drug Leo and then call out the demon. But first: Can she drug Leo?<br /> <br /> Jaeda snitches some ether from the school chemistry lab and tries knocking herself out. (She realizes this is dangerous \u2014 back in Los Angeles, she saw kids die from huffing. She makes sure that if she loses consciousness, the ether-soaked cloth falls away from her nose.) Jaeda finds that her Epic Stamina is too much for the ether: She gets a little dizzy, but nothing more. (I treated this as a poison that deals bashing damage. Jaeda\u2019s player consistently rolled over the ether\u2019s Toxicity, and thus suffered no damage and only half the Penalty.)<br /> <br /> So Jaeda looks for something stronger. Using her street skills, she buys some black market Rohypnol (the infamous \u201cdate rape\u201d drug). The dealer shakes his head in amazement. \u201cI wouldn\u2019t figure you would ever need this stuff. Or\u2026 what, it\u2019s been used on you so many times you addicted?\u201d Jaeda is tempted to hit him, but figures it would be imprudent to burn a contact.<br /> <br /> The \u201croofies\u201d don\u2019t work either. Again, they only make Jaeda a little dizzy and dopey. And she has reason to believe that Leo\u2019s Epic Stamina is higher than hers. (She and Gus have firmly forced themselves into the training sessions with Leo and Marshall. They\u2019ve seen that Leo flat-out never gets tired, no matter how grueling the exertion, and shrugs off punches that Gus and Marshall would find painful.) Jaeda concludes that her plan just can\u2019t be done, and shelves it \u2014 at least for now.<br /> <br /> HOLLY recognizes Barbara, not Delia, as her true rival for social dominance within the school, and so she makes an effort to learn what connections Barbara has built and how she uses them. This means she learns that Avalon High\u2019s chapter of Future Business Leaders of America has invited local businessman Suttung Vetterson to talk about running a small business in today\u2019s economy. The frost giant runs a local cold storage company. (Well, duh.)<br /> <br /> On the day of the talk, Barbara insists that Holly stay out of the room, as she does: Suttung might cancel the talk and leave if his sees a Scion in the room. Holly therefore listens from outside. Barbara, meanwhile, has several of her FBLA Followers keeping open cell phone lines to her so she can listen closely and text them questions to ask the frost giant.<br /> <br /> Suttung talks about business strategy. He warns against relying too much on other companies, especially for financing. (As he points out, many small businesses are failing because they depend on short-term loans to meet their weekly payroll and expenses, instead of maintaining cash reserves \u2014 and banks have become reluctant to make those loans.) \u201cAlways keep resources in reserve,\u201d Suttung stresses. \u201cNever let yourself become dependent on someone else\u2019s favor or risk more than you can afford to lose.\u201d When asked about competition, though, he also stresses the need for allies, from suppliers who have proven themselves reliable to bureaucrats who are willing to read regulations in a favorable way. He again stresses the value of patience and endurance, holding back and slowly wearing down the competition until they are too weak to fight back. <i>Then </i>crush them.<br /> <br /> All in all, Holly thinks the talk was very informative. And not just for the insight into Suttung\u2019s methods and personality.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T16:11:05-08:00","id":1024535,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 14: Bad Options (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Sometimes, You Cannot Win</u></b><br /> <br /> A few days later, Warren and Cole both stop by Stick It to talk to Jack. It\u2019s evening, not long before the mall is set to close. Bull, one of Jack\u2019s Followers, is also there, hoping to cadge free food.<br /> <br /> Cole wants to talk about Holly and Scions in general. Holly warned him that her life is dangerous \u2014 Exhibit A, how the lindwurm nearly killed her in one bite \u2014 and that as a completely non-supernatural mortal, Cole could get killed if he stays near her. So Cole asks Jack if he knows any way to get a power-up. Jack reluctantly says that he does, but they all involve selling yourself to alien forces that view you as a disposable tool. In fact, they might turn you into the sort of monster the Scions would need to kill.<br /> <br /> Warren chats with Jack about the offers he\u2019s received from colleges across the country. He\u2019s a senior, and lots of colleges want him to join their football programs next year. (These include some top names in college football: Ole Miss, Texas Aggies, Notre Dame\u2026) They discuss various strategies and pitfalls of college football, including the Sports Illustrated Curse: High school superstars who make the cover of <i>Sports Illustrated</i> tend not achieve great careers because of drugs, scandals, jail or other forms of self-destruction or just bad luck. After the big game against Etna, Warren appeared on the cover of <i>Sports Illustrated</i> with the caption: You Will Believe A Football Player Can Fly.<br /> <br /> Then three middle-aged men stalk up to the group. Two open their trenchcoats to show machine pistols held at the ready. The one in the lead growls, \u201cCome with us, Scion, or the mortals around you shall die. Your blood shall restore the Master.\u201d And he says it to Warren.<br /> <br /> Well, <i>that\u2019s</i> a form of the Sports Illustrated Curse no one expected.<br /> <br /> Of course, Jack isn\u2019t about to let some nutters shoot up the mall or take Warren for his blood to restore the Master. Whoever the Master is. Jack vaults over the Stick It counter to knock the guns from the two men\u2019s hands, and he does it so slickly that the guns flip through the air to land in the Stick It\u2019s deep fryer. (No, it doesn\u2019t cook off the ammunition. Boiling oil isn\u2019t hot enough.) Cole is right behind Jack and smacks one of the men with his Cobra. Just as quickly, Warren and Bull tackle the third man. The four boys subdue their three assailants and hustle them into the back room of the Stick It so quickly that hardly any shoppers even notice what happened. Bull takes over the Stick It counter while Jack calls the other Scions.<br /> <br /> Holly, Gus and Jaeda arrive shortly thereafter. They hit the three men with Scion Mind Tricks and find that, as Jack already suspected, the men were minions of a destroyed vampire. Destroyed by Scions, of course. In fact, the men momentarily mistake Gus for his uncle Gav, who had been one of the Scions. The three \u201cRenfields\u201d saved the vampire\u2019s ashes and had been looking for another Scion ever since, on the theory that divinely-empowered blood would resurrect the vampire. (Holly finds this not totally implausible, though only for some kinds of vampire.) The ashes are kept in the \u201cFane.\u201d (To the puzzled looks of the other Scions, Holly explains that a Fane is another name for a temple, used by the excessively pretentious.)<br /> <br /> The Scions agree they cannot leave this situation to drift. So, they leave Warren, Bull and Cole to guard the three minions while they go to the Fane.<br /> <br /> The Fane turns out to be a dowdy old suburban house. More precisely, it\u2019s a sub-basement decorated in Hammer Films Transylvania Classic, with a coffin in the center of the room, a crematory urn in the coffin and a winch over the coffin for hanging sacrifices. There\u2019s also ornate plasterwork and red and black drapes.<br /> <br /> Not being stupid, the Scions suspect traps. Jaeda searches the Fane (she\u2019s the one with Larceny) and finds hidden wires leading from the coffin to two of the plaster panels. Jack knocks away some of the plaster, and Jaeda chips away the rest to reveal the claymore mines set to go off if the urn is disturbed. This would be quite sufficient to blow Scion blood throughout the room and into the vampire ash from the shattered urn.<br /> <br /> Well, the Scions can\u2019t leave high explosives lying around; someone could get hurt. They retreat from the room, and Jack throws something to knock over the urn and trigger the mines, <i>sans </i>Scion blood. After that, they figure they\u2019d better leave quickly before the noise attracts the police. First, though, they collect the scattered ash with a Dustbuster. They make a quick trip to scatter the ash into the nearest running water, never to be retrieved.<br /> <br /> Back at the mall, the three mortal boys are glad to hand the minions back to the Scions. Warren and Bull leave. Cole sticks around while the Scions discuss what to do with the minions\u2026 because that is a problem. The three men were ready to kill to revive their vampire master. The Scions suspect they may have killed before, hoping that mortal blood would be enough.<br /> <br /> Holly asks her mysterious Guide if enthralled vampire minions can be deprogrammed. Her Guide says it\u2019s unlikely, given that the three stayed loyal for almost 30 years. In fact, the Guide guesses that only their enthrallment enabled the men to preserve a fa\u00e7ade of sanity all this time, and losing all hope of restoring the Master will break their minds completely.<br /> <br /> Holly then asks if there isn\u2019t something <i>someone </i>could do for the men. The Guide says that a broken mind is hard enough, but the vampire broke the minions\u2019 souls as well. It\u2019s the only way they could have stayed loyal so long. Even Gods would have difficulty fixing that sort of damage. At best, the men would have to be locked up for the rest of their lives, and their broken souls would make them sock puppets for other forces of evil.<br /> <br /> \u201cThis sucks,\u201d Holly says. Her Guide agrees. That\u2019s why such evil must be stopped. Because some damage can never be fixed.<br /> <br /> Sure enough: When confronted with proof that the Scions thwarted their plot and the Master isn\u2019t coming back, the three men go catatonic. The Scions argue a bit about what to do with them. The only choices they see are to deliver them to a mental hospital or kill them, and death not only looks more expedient as a way to prevent other Titan servants from using the men, it might even be a mercy. Cole looks sick. He asks Holly if she\u2019d think less of him if he left now and pretended that at the last moment the Scions thought of some brilliant way to make it come out all right. Holly says she would have thought less of him if he\u2019d insisted on staying. Cole leaves.<br /> <br /> The Scions load the comatose minions into the van the minions had intended to use to transport Warren, and drive to a remote location. Holly says she won\u2019t have other people doing her dirty work. She takes a knife and thrusts it into a minion\u2019s heart. Jaeda and Gus kill the other two. Gus summons his crocodile to dispose of the bodies.<br /> <br /> All in all, the Scions found it one of the worst days of their lives. Holly seems especially bummed. Gus reminds her that they are merely at the beginning of their power. Someday, Holly will be a God who will work miracles that astound even other Gods. Perhaps he doesn\u2019t realize that Holly is less broken up over the tragedy than over her inability to think of a solution.<br /> <br /> Daughter of Louhi, after all.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-12-30T16:12:33-08:00","id":1024538,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 14: Bad Options (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Sex and Violence</u></b><br /> <br /> As President\u2019s Week break approaches, the Scions make plans for their week off from school. On a car-hunting trip with Gary, Jaeda learns that the Vettersons will be out of town. They spend the week on a skiing vacation in the mountains, enjoying the snow. Gary wishes Jaeda could come along, but with his father hating Scions\u2026 Of course, it turns out to be the same ski lodge where Mr. Sobeck is getting married.<br /> <br /> The rival Band, it turns out, is also leaving town. Some of them are willing to talk to Jack, because everybody knows Jack is just a big goofball. The rival Scions are also going to the lodge on a\u2026 hunting trip. Jack manages to wheedle his way into the expedition, but he lets the others know about the rival Band\u2019s expedition.<br /> <br /> The PCs immediately figure out that their rivals probably intend to try killing the Vettersons. Frankly, they don\u2019t think their rivals can take the giants. After all, Gary and Reese practically defeated Gus when they were all Legend 2, and the Scions know that Suttung and Hyndla are even more formidable. They also know that if any of the Vettersons are attacked, or worse killed, it\u2019ll be war between the rest of the family and all the Scions. Suttung and Hyndla have only held back this long because they didn\u2019t want to throw away their comfortable lives.<br /> <br /> Mister Sobeck told Gus he could bring a friend along to keep him company at the lodge. Gus invites Jaeda. (The elder Sobeck smirks and says she\u2019s a good choice.) Holly invites Cole to join her for a ski trip as well, though she reserves rooms at a neighboring ski lodge instead of the one to which the Sobecks and Vettersons are going.<br /> <br /> Once at the lodge, Jack tries to talk Barbara out of attacking the Vettersons. He doesn\u2019t think that his Band could win a fight with the Vettersons without losses of their own \u2014 maybe at all \u2014 and the rival Band is not as skilled at combat and magic as they are. The giants are <i>tough. </i>Gary has shown he can survive a shot to the head, and he\u2019s probably not the only one of them!<br /> <br /> Barbara thinks they can do it. She has no intention of attacking the Vettersons as a group. Delia has foreseen times in quick succession when three of the four Vettersons will be alone. They will ambush each giant. Marshall will start by shooting each giant in the head. Before the giant can recover, Leo will engage to keep the giant busy. Barbara will fly down and attack with thermite charges prepared by Marshall\u2019s gun-shop mentor. Marshall and Barbara both think the plan has a good chance to work. And if they can eradicate a whole family of dangerous titanspawn, woohoo! The power of their Legend will grow!<br /> <br /> Jack retorts that Gary and Reese are changing in ways Holly can detect with her magic. The two young giants are no longer pure titanspawn. If attacked, though, they will stand by their parents and whatever change they are in the middle of will be ended.<br /> <br /> Barbara wavers. She says she has to think about it and talk to the other Scions. Marshall, she admits, is the one who\u2019s most set on killing the Vettersons. The others simply want to do something, well, <i>legendary.</i> But Jack points out that he and his friends have not gone hunting for enemies to kill. (Well, Dr. Sosa was an iffy case.) They\u2019ve simply allowed monsters and villains to come to them, or show that they were clear and present threats to human life, and <i>then</i> fought them.<br /> <br /> Barbara also lets drop that she knows all about Jaeda and Gary. Weeks ago, Delia recognized that Jaeda was going steady with someone. From there, it wasn\u2019t hard for the daughter of Apollo to figure out who it was. It is possible that Marshall has issues with losing the Atzl\u00e1nti girl of his dreams to a giant.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Jaeda has managed to find Gary without being seen by the other Vettersons. It\u2019s not hard: The lodge is a flurry of activity with all the caterers, decorators and wedding guests running around, preparing for the wedding the next day. Suttung Vetterson, of course, is none too happy to find the Sobecks at the lodge, but he does nothing but glower.<br /> <br /> They have a nice reunion. When Gus looks for Jaeda, he finds her in a back parlor with Gary. Actually, he hears Gary moaning, \u201cOh, Jaeda, there\u2019s only so much a man can stand\u2026\u201d followed shortly by \u201cThis website is fantastic. A Shelby-customized Ford Torino Super Snake, it\u2019s gorgeous!\u201d Which gives him the confidence to look in on them.<br /> <br /> Jaeda sits on Gary\u2019s lap in a big overstuffed chair. They have a laptop that displays a picture of a muscle car. They also seem very at ease with each other. As soon as Gary sees Gus enter, his posture shifts and he grins a look-at-me-with-your-date smirk, but Gus saw there\u2019s real affection between them, from Gary as well as Jaeda. It isn\u2019t just sex. Privately, later, Jaeda says she\u2019s sorry she kept it secret she was still dating Gary, but she was afraid he and the other Scions would be angry. Gus says he respects Jaeda\u2019s right to choose her boyfriend, he respects Gary, and is neither jealous nor angry. He can\u2019t imagine the others will mind, either, or feel it\u2019s any of their business. But he does tell her about the rival Band\u2019s plot to kill the Vettersons.<br /> <br /> Holly and Cole come over from their lodge to confer with the Band. Holly uses Mystery to find when and where their rivals plan to ambush the Vettersons. (Since the rivals already made these plans, it\u2019s valid information for Mystery.) Their rivals plan to attack on the next day \u2014 the day of the wedding \u2014 in the span of less than two hours.<br /> <br /> Once again, they see no good way out. Jaeda is adamant: She isn\u2019t betraying Gary. The Vettersons are all safe from her until one of them attacks her. The others aren\u2019t too keen on cold, premeditated murder of someone who never attacked them or any innocent mortals as far as they know. On the other hand, they agree that tipping off the Vettersons to the attacks will push Suttung to murderous counterattack and spark the conflict they want to avoid. They aren\u2019t too keen on fighting the rival Band, either. The discussion breaks up without resolution.<br /> <br /> At least, without resolution from three of the Scions. Gus goes looking for Marshall and finds him about to head out on skis. Gus knows it\u2019s the Scion of Huitzilopochtli\u2019s first time on skis, and he offers to show Marshall some of the best views and slopes. He takes Marshall past one of the ambush sites, and spots how Marshall examines the terrain for places to hide and shoot.<br /> <br /> Gus then casually mentions the most dangerous slope in the area, that only expert skiers may dare. It would be risky even for a Scion super-athlete if he wasn\u2019t experienced. Gus has skied it, of course\u2026 Sure enough, Marshall takes the challenge and demands to ski Suicide Slope right away. They both race down the slope. Marshall would have easily taken a Winter Olympics medal with his skiing (5 successes on the Dexterity + Athletics roll). Gus far surpasses him (10 successes \u2014 super-hot roll). Marshall is so elated by the rush of speed and adrenaline that he doesn\u2019t even feel jealous.<br /> <br /> Well, that won\u2019t do. Gus goads him, saying his skiing wasn\u2019t bad for a newbie, admittedly he\u2019ll never be as good as Gus. And trying Suicide Slope took more courage than Gus expected from a sniper who tries to shoot people in parking lots instead of facing them in honest, open combat. And so on. Marshall snarls, \u201cI shot the wrong one!\u201d \u2014 incidentally confirming Gus\u2019 suspicion that Marshall tried to kill Gary. Before long, Gus gets the reaction he wants: Marshall pulls a knife and attacks him.<br /> <br /> Marshall knows knife-fighting, but he\u2019s a novice on skis and he doesn\u2019t have Cat\u2019s Grace to let him fight in thigh-deep snow without impediment. Gus quickly grapples Marshall, and Gus is much stronger. He grips Marshall\u2019s arm and move it so the knife cuts a bloody line across Gus\u2019 face. Then he beats Marshall unconscious and beyond, breaking bones. Marshall gets a few punches of his own in, but the Scion of Huitzilopochtli never has a chance of winning.<br /> <br /> Gus then carries Marshall to the nearest aid station and gives the paramedics a story about how they tried Suicide Slope and it nearly killed them. Scion Mind Tricks ensure the paramedics believe the lie. Gus endures treatment for the cut on his face and a few bruises, then heads back to the lodge to tell the Scions that the problem is solved. Barbara, Delia and Leo can\u2019t spring their ambushes without Marshall; Gus counts on them to see reason.<br /> <br /> Barbara returns to the room where Jack waited for her once she talked to the rest of her Band. Bitterly, she tells him, \u201cWell played.\u201d Jack realizes that Barbara now believes his earnest attempt at persuasion was a ploy, and his sincerity was deceit that she failed to penetrate. Jack isn\u2019t sure what makes him feel worse: that Barbara thinks he\u2019s a lying bastard, or that she thinks he\u2019s actually a brilliant planner.<br /> <br /> Gus apologizes to Jack and the rest for taking action without consulting them, but he didn\u2019t want to force them \u2014 especially Jack \u2014 to choose or reject a course of action that even Gus admits was pretty slimy. But, well, Scion of Sobek, the leg-breaker of the Pesedjet who gets his hands dirty so others don\u2019t have to. There\u2019s no chance now of bringing the two Bands together, though, and Marshall will undoubtedly set out to kill Gus.<br /> <br /> The rival Scions leave early the next day. Barbara tells Jack that no doubt he will find alternate transportation home.<br /> <br /> The wedding happens on schedule. The Scions spot Hyndla watching from a doorway. She may be a giant, but she still likes a wedding. Reese shows up long enough to dance with Gus at the reception. Gary and Jaeda furtively text back and forth about when they can get away.<br /> <br /> That\u2019s when the woman on snowshoes comes in screaming that animals attacked her husband and dragged him into the snow.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Finally, A Nice Simple Monster</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions easily slip away in the uproar and put on their skis. Using Predatory Focus, they follow the woman\u2019s trail back to the attack site. There\u2019s a lot of blood melting a pit in the snow, with scraps of clothing and snowshoes. The largest remaining part of the victim is a foot still inside a boot. It seems that just as the woman said, a number of somethings jumped out of the snow and dragged the man down.<br /> <br /> The Scions follow the trail. It leads them to a dell full of deep snow. As they enter, four bizarre beasts leap from the snow and attack, one beast per Scion. The white-furred beasts have heads like weasels or wolverines, but much larger. They have no legs, though. Instead, they swim through the snow like fish. Fortunately, although the beasts take the Scions by surprise and bite them, their jaws are not quite powerful enough to bite through Holly\u2019s kevlar coat or the Epic Stamina of the others. (And the damage rolls sucked.) The Scions strike back when they get the chance, but the beasts\u2019 strategy is to dive back into the snow as soon as they get a chance, to leap out again from somewhere else in hopes of getting another surprise attack. Once the Scions are prepared, however, they can dish out a lot more damage than the beasts can. In less than 20 seconds, the four beasts are all dead.<br /> <br /> A bit to her own surprise, Holly has heard of these chimeric beasts. (Thank you Know-It-All.) They are <i>snow wassets,</i> whose only published description comes from a slim volume called <i>Fearsome Creatures of the Lumberwoods</i>, published in 1910. (You can find the whole book online.) As for what brought them out <i>here and now,</i> well, most likely the Fate-warping presence of eight Scions and four giants!<br /> <br /> As she examines the bodies with Unlidded Eye, Holly sees the fading power of the chimeric beasts concentrating in their pelts and their teeth. She figures out that the Scions have a choice of trophies: Take the pelts and turn them into garments that grant immunity to extreme cold; or take a tooth as a Relic that would enable its owner to channel powers of ice and cold (<i>i.e.,</i> the Frost Purview) \u2014 but not both. They take three pelts to turn into fur jackets, anticipating that they will be useful when Suttung eventually moves against them, and one tooth in case someone ever wants to learn Frost Boons.<br /> <br /> The rest of the week is uneventful. Nevertheless, the Scions can\u2019t fully enjoy their week in the mountains as they keep watching for Suttung Vetterson to drop an avalanche on them, or for more snow wassets to attack the people around them. All in all, they\u2019re fairly glad to go home at the end of the week and prepare for school.<br /> <br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-02T15:59:46-08:00","id":1025605,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 15: Obsession<br /> <br /> Late February and early March are a depressing time of year in western Washington. It\u2019s been cold, wet, dark and windy for <i>months.</i> Spring can\u2019t arrive soon enough.<br /> <br /> But not yet.<br /> <br /> (Gus wasn\u2019t along for this adventure. He was out of town. Dunno why\u2026 maybe the Pesedjet took him up on his offer to do \u201cjobs\u201d for the pantheon.)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Jaeda Versus Horndog Bob</u></b><br /> <br /> As always, the other Scions keep busy in their own ways.<br /> <br /> JACK learns that his father Lono expects him to develop greater leadership skills. His Guide challenges Jack to lead his Followers in some sort of group project. It doesn\u2019t matter what, so long as it takes at least a day: It has to be something greater than leading a bold expedition to Burger Trough for an all-you-can-eat special. Jack \u2014 possibly influenced by his Endurance Virtue \u2014 plans an overnight camping trip in the wet, cold Washington woods and manages to talk the Defensive Line into coming along.<br /> <br /> JAEDA talks to Leo about the problem of Landon, since Tlazolt\u00e9otl told her the Anuna were good with divine covenants and bindings. Leo replies that Landon sold his soul to the Titans and deserves what he gets. Jaeda points out that the Titans haven\u2019t collected yet, and what would Leo prefer: Landon bound to a pagan god of dubious morality, or Landon bound to a Titan of absolute, definite, no kidding evil? Leo passes on what his lion-demon Guide says: If Leo embraced his Anuna heritage more fully, he could learn to bind Landon in a covenant\u2026 to Nergal. Leo won\u2019t be able to forge covenants in his own name until he becomes a God. Leo suggests this is not much of an improvement for Landon, but Jaeda keeps it in mind as a last-ditch way to keep the young shinobi out of the Titans\u2019 hands.<br /> <br /> HOLLY has talked Cole, Landon and Jack\u2019s Follower Gnu (the smart one) into joining her in a junior vigilante squad, protecting people from muggers and minor supernatural threats and solving minor mysteries. (\u201cIt\u2019s really old Mrs. Feeny\u2019s nephew, trying to drive her crazy for her money!\u201d \u201cI would have gotten away with it too, if it weren\u2019t for you meddling kids!\u201d) She has her magic and her superhuman Intelligence and Dexterity; Landon has his shinobi powers; Cole and Gnu have skills and guts.<br /> <br /> Since no one could interest one of their divine parents in Landon\u2019s case, Holly decides she has only one force that might interfere with an attempt by the Black Bodhisattva to claim Landon for the Titans: She casts spells on him repeatedly to Fatebind him to her for life. Landon doesn\u2019t much like the notion, but he is willing to try just about anything to keep from becoming the Black Bodhisattva\u2019s slave. Now they just have to find out what role Landon is bound to assume, in relation to Holly, for the rest of his life\u2026<br /> <br /> All this activity means that Holly spends less time with Laine, Derek and other members of her coterie of mortal friends. Derek says that he and Laine missed her; they hoped she\u2019d come to more of the swim meets. The boys\u2019 swim team improved over the quarter: Some swimmers who seemed to have hit their limits suddenly improved. And the whole team will need to be at their best to compete with Etna. (Avalon\u2019s great rival never had a strong swimming program before, but this year\u2019s team is incredible.) Actually, Derek feels a little concerned about how much some of his teammates improved their speed. He suspects performance-enhancing drugs or\u2026 something.<br /> <br /> A few days later, Jaeda comes home from her shift working at Big Cups Espresso to find her would-be nemesis Bob the Thrall waiting for her. He wears a long overcoat. Jaeda says that if he flashes her, he\u2019ll regret it. Instead, Bob rants about how he and the other thralls will take their revenge for the killing of Bolvarg, because the fenrir gave them power and now they know how to use it. But Jaeda won\u2019t be the first one they hunt: No, first they\u2019ll kill the Scion who shot and killed Bolvarg. She\u2019ll be next, though, so enjoy life while she can! And Bob then throws off the coat, turns into a wolf and runs away \u2014 a bit to Jaeda\u2019s disappointment. (She looked forward to beating him up again. Or maybe getting a wolf-pelt rug to replace the Bolvarg pelt that burned with her trailer.)<br /> <br /> Jaeda remembers that Marshall shot down Bolvarg\u2019s throat at the same time she gutted the fenrir and Gene ran him over with a van, making them equally responsible for his death; but she guesses Marshall tells the story a little differently. Evidently Bob somehow heard that version. After a moment\u2019s thought, Jaeda sighs and phones Marshall to warn him that a pack of werewolves are after him. Marshall manages to thank her for the tip without sounding too ungracious, and he boasts that he will find the former thralls easy to kill.<br /> <br /> Then Jaeda phones Jack and Holly to keep them apprised. Neither of the two brainy Scions ever heard of fenrir thralls becoming werewolves. They figure Guibourg somehow enchanted Horndog Bob and the rest. It\u2019s moot, though. The next day, Marshall boasts to all the Scions about how he led the werewolves away from his home, shot and killed them all. Holly hopes that Jaeda helping Marshall will reduce the hostility between the two Bands.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Jack Versus His Mom\u2019s Date</u></b><br /> <br /> The same day begins innocently enough for Jack. His mother tells him he\u2019ll have to make his own dinner before his shift at Stick It begins, since she won\u2019t be home until later that night. Jack asks why. His mother blushes as she admits that she has a date. It\u2019s a man named George Skehum, whom she met through her work, and she hopes Jack will like him when they meet. Eventually. But not this night.<br /> <br /> Jack spends the day in distraction. (Doctor Demerit notices and comments on it.) After school, Jack drafts a convenient, confused kid to learn the exciting world of food service by taking his shift at Stick It. Jaeda, Holly and Cole help in tracking his mother to her dinner date with George at the local Olive Garden restaurant. Jaeda immediately notices George\u2019s classic 1967 Chevrolet Camaro. Holly spies on the couple with the Unlidded Eye. As expected, George is supernatural \u2014 but not, as the Scions half-expected, Coyote. (Why Coyote? Well, the well-built, handsome George is Native American, and \u201cSkehum\u201d sounds like \u201cScheme.\u201d Yeah, it\u2019s kind of dumb, but sometimes so is Coyote.)<br /> <br /> Instead, to the Unlidded Eye George appears as a sort of otter-man: a <i>kushtaka,</i> a race of lesser immortals from Pacific Northwestern myth. The Gods appointed kushtakas to save people who drown at sea by turning them into more kushtakas. Unfortunately, the kushtakas got over-zealous and took to drowning people to make sure they would be on hand to save them. While kushtakas are not totally evil, they can be pretty bad. Holly uses Mystery to learn that George the Kushtaka intends to marry Jack\u2019s mom, then turn her into a kushtaka.<br /> <br /> Jack\u2019s first thought is to break up the date and possibly attack George then and there. Jaeda thinks that\u2019s a great idea: They can kill George, and then take his car! Surely with their Social Fu they can transfer the registry to her\u2026 Instead, Jack has Cole jimmy the trunk of the Camaro so he can hide and ride along, able to jump out at a moment\u2019s notice.<br /> <br /> After dinner, George drives to a place with a view across Lake Washington to Seattle. Jack is forced to endure some lovey-dovey small talk and smoochy sounds. After a while, though, the two agree they should call it a night as they both have lesson plans to prepare for tomorrow. George drives Jack\u2019s mom back home (more smooching before they part; Jack grinds his teeth). Then George drives to his own home \u2014 and Jack pops the trunk to confront him.<br /> <br /> George reacts with normal outrage at this trespass. Jack sasses back, saying \u201cYeah, there otter be a law.\u201d If George catches the pun \u2014 and Jack\u2019s hint that he knows what George really is \u2014 the kushtaka chooses to ignore it. Once Jack lays out what he knows, though, George doesn\u2019t waste any more time on bluster. He also immediately twigs that Jack is a Scion.<br /> <br /> Jack lays it out bluntly: If George harms Jack\u2019s mother, Jack will kill him. There\u2019s no place that\u2019s far away enough to escape him. (Atua, you know. Great voyagers.) George protests that he wants to turn Jack\u2019s mom into a kushtaka because he fell in love with her the moment he saw her on a dive, and he wants her to be with him forever. As a kushtaka, she won\u2019t grow old! If Jack loves his mother, how could he deny her immortality? Jack is adamant: If George wants to court and marry his mother, the kushtaka must agree to live as a human from then on, never using any supernatural powers, and letting her live a normal, human life. George then throws Jack off-balance by promptly saying, \u201cOkay.\u201d They part, not liking each other one bit.<br /> <br /> Jack hurries back to Stick It. He finds $40 gone from the cash register and a store manikin taking his place at the counter. The exceptionally nearsighted (and dim) customer demanding service does not seem to have noticed.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-02T16:01:06-08:00","id":1025606,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 15: Obsession (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Holly Versus the Vettersons</u></b><br /> <br /> A few days later, Holly eats dinner while pondering how to get filthy rich. None of the Internet schemes she thought of so far seem promising. She decides that she\u2019ll simply have to study business administration, investment and the like, and just invest the money she already has to make it grow. Between her Scion super-genius and careful use of Mystery (and Prophecy, once she learns it), she figures she can assure herself a steady, and steadily-increasing, income.<br /> <br /> Holly hears a scratching on the roof and smells a peculiar smoky odor from the fireplace. She immediately calls Cole. A wave of weakness sweeps over her, though, and the phone drops from her hand: Holly finds herself too weak to move, stricken by a poison in the air! At the same time, she feels dizzy and sleepy: Her dinner was <i>also </i>poisoned.<br /> <br /> Holly realizes who blew poisoned smoke down the chimney when a window breaks and a pair of Skinwalkers leap into the room. Navaho legend says that Skinwalkers poison people with a magic dust that they blow into victims\u2019 faces or drop into fires. The Skinwalkers gloat a bit. The other shoe drops when Hyndla Vetterson tiptoes from the kitchen to stand right behind the Skinwalkers. She bangs their heads together, and the Skinwalkers fall unconscious. Hyndla then turns off Holly\u2019s cell phone. Holly falls unconscious about the time Hyndla dumps her in the Vetterson family SUV.<br /> <br /> Cole, of course, immediately contacts Jack and Jaeda and tells them about the alarmingly-cut off call. They hurry to Holly\u2019s house and find the Skinwalkers just waking up.<br /> <br /> Jack and Jaeda do not tolerate the Skinwalkers\u2019 attempts to bluff and wheedle. They say that Very Bad Things will happen to the Skinwalkers if they don\u2019t cooperate. The Skinwalkers explain that they used a hollow reed to escape from the Ghost Dance Country into the World. At first they thought they had a whole new world of mortals to terrorize and exploit, but the World has changed a lot since they left it more than 100 years ago. It\u2019s scary, they want to go home, but they can\u2019t re-open the portal Coyote made! They thought Holly could do the job for them since she is the witch among the Scions. The Scions say that no, actually Jaeda opens the nexus, but if the Skinwalkers ever want to see the Ghost Dance Country again, they will help rescue Holly. The Skinwalkers start by turning into coyotes to sniff around for scents. They report that Holly\u2019s abductor smelled <i>cold.</i> The Scions realize that the conflict they tried to avert in the previous episode is happening anyway: The Vettersons have attacked them. The Scions, Skinwalkers and Cole race to the Vettersons\u2019 house.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Holly wakes up in a basement kitchen, trussed up with duct tape. Hyndla has a cauldron on a stove. In it she mixes honey that bees made from graveyard flowers, rain water collected at the dark of the moon, and other such ingredients. The giantess cheerily explains that it\u2019s nothing personal. Gary is going to college next year, and she wants to get his grades up. So, she\u2019s making blood mead \u2014 the magical Mead of Wisdom from Norse myth. She doesn\u2019t have Kvasir\u2019s blood, but Holly\u2019s should serve to impart academic skills and knowledge. Mortal blood would do for a temporary endowment of wisdom, but Hyndla wants to do it right. After all, if you don\u2019t have time to do something right, when will you have time to do it over?<br /> <br /> And besides, for Suttung this <i>is </i>personal. The jotun <i>paterfamilias</i> stops by the basement to say that he\u2019s wanted to kill the Scions for some time. Starting with Holly makes sense because she could divine his plots against the other ones. His reasons? Suttung wants revenge. Decades ago, the Gods slaughtered his clan, leaving him as the only survivor of his family. Now he\u2019s going to kill the children of the Gods \u2014 any Gods. He doesn\u2019t know which Gods massacred his clan, so he holds all Gods responsible.<br /> <br /> Gary also stops by, briefly, to protest that he doesn\u2019t need magical mead of wisdom. He already has offers from colleges in Texas, and they don\u2019t make football players take classes for <i>real</i>. He\u2019ll have nerds to do his schoolwork! But Gary isn\u2019t willing to defy his parents outright or admit that he doesn\u2019t want to fight the Scions because he\u2019s dating one of them.<br /> <br /> All Holly\u2019s persuasion seems to be in vain. The older giants don\u2019t care that they\u2019ll have to fight all the other Scions; Suttung even says he has other plans in motion to deal with them. The two giants also scoff at Holly\u2019s claim that Gary and Reese are changing into something no longer purely frost giant that might end the war between Gods and giants. Holly decides that her dignity matters less than even the slightest chance of survival and starts screaming for help.<br /> <br /> Outside, the Scions make a nasty discovery. Jack, with his Atua sense for supernatural prohibitions, senses the Ward around the house. Sure enough, the Scions find they cannot enter. But it\u2019s only a Ward against Scions! Jack and Jaeda persuade the Skinwalkers to enter the house, try using their poison smoke powder against the giants (burn it and send the smoke into the house\u2019s excellent air conditioning system) and rescue Holly.<br /> <br /> Down in the basement, Holly smells the smoke and tries holding her breath. Unfortunately, Suttung and Hyndla have too much Stamina, Resistance and Epic Stamina for the Strength-draining supernatural poison to affect them. Suttung notes the presence of the Scions outside of the house, comments that he wishes he had his bazooka handy, and heads upstairs. The home\u2019s fire alarm sounds; Holly soon hears Suttung\u2019s roar, a crash, and the yipping of fleeing Skinwalkers.<br /> <br /> Holly has a final brainstorm. She asks Hyndla what information she could provide to ransom herself. After all, she can find out <i>anything.</i> Like\u2026 Where do other frost giants live? They wouldn\u2019t be alone in the World. Her children could find proper jotuns to marry. Hyndla wavers, but agrees to the deal. She returns Holly\u2019s magic mirror. While Suttung chases Skinwalkers, Holly invokes Mystery (this is a different subject than Kushtaka George and therefore is a different story) and finds that the nearest colony of frost giants lives in British Columbia. Honoring the deal, Hyndla cuts her loose.<br /> <br /> Outside, the Scions are surprised and delighted to see Holly stroll toward them, safe and sound. The Skinwalkers jump out windows to join them. Suttung is dumbfounded to see Holly free. She shrugs insouciantly and says, \u201cI have a pet shinobi.\u201d Everyone skedaddles.<br /> <br /> The Scions keep their word to the Skinwalkers. After collecting the third Skinwalker (stoned in the shapeshifter-witches\u2019 hideout \u2014 Skinwalkers are debauched, self-indulgent creatures), they hurry to the Avalon High parking lot. Holly confirms that the latent nexus point still exists. Jaeda stabs it with the First Flower, and it opens. The Skinwalkers jump in, eagerly, and are gone.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-02T16:02:39-08:00","id":1025607,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 15: Obsession (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Scions Versus Good Intentions</u></b><br /> <br /> Even though Hyndla Vetterson released Holly, Suttung mentioned plans to attack the Scions \u2014 and to owning a bazooka. The Scions fear the jotun might try to kill them by blowing up homes or cars. That might be enough to kill even the toughest among them; it would certainly kill their mortal parents.<br /> <br /> Holly\u2019s parents are safe on the East Coast. Jaeda\u2019s father knows the score: She has no trouble moving him to another motel. That leaves Jack. It\u2019s Friday, so he borrows money from Holly to buy his mother a weekend getaway at a mountain resort. Jack tells her that he confronted George about dating her but was ashamed of doing so, and he arranged this weekend to make it up to her. And beyond that, he doesn\u2019t want to know. Holly warns the rival Scions that Suttung might make a move soon and endures the resulting I-told-you-sos (mostly from Marshall).<br /> <br /> While they arrange all this, Derek Huckovich brings further concerns to Holly. His friend and teammate Alex Totenbush is trying to sell him on <i>something </i>that Alex says improved his swimming prowess and could help Derek as well, warning that Derek could fall behind the rest of the team and the Etna swim team \u2014 but Alex won\u2019t say what this \u201csomething\u201d <i>is.</i> He just says it\u2019s great, it\u2019s legal, and Derek won\u2019t regret it. Derek is suspicious.<br /> <br /> So Holly turns the Unlidded Eye on the swim team and finds that half of them \u2014 Alex and five others \u2014 are kushtakas. Well, Alex is a kushtaka. The other five kids seem halfway there. She also takes a quick look at the Etna swim team. They are all half-changed kushtakas, too!<br /> <br /> Between her encyclopedic knowledge and super-genius, Holly figures out that the transformation from mortal to kushtaka does not always happen at once. (In one well-known myth, a great shaman prevented his own full transformation into a kushtaka and even managed to regain his humanity. In another story, a man\u2019s kushtaka sister started enchanting his children into otter-folk, but reversed the spell at his insistence.) Holly deduces that the half-transformed kids were drowned and changed in swimming pools, but the change will only be complete once the kids enter salt water. Killing the kushtaka who changed them should break the enchantment. Unfortunately, the kids were genuinely drowned, and they will still be drowned when the enchantment reverses. Only immediate medical help can save their lives.<br /> <br /> Of the Scions, only Holly and Jaeda have any medical skill. What luck, though, that Holly recently gained the Instant Seminar Knack, so she can share her Medicine Ability with others\u2026<br /> <br /> The Scions debate what to do about George and how best to round up the half-changed kids. Holly remembers a favor her mother Louhi gave her in exchange for examining Bolvarg\u2019s collar (back in Episode Six). She pulls out the business card, phones the number, and orders a hit on George Skehum. She does think to add the proviso that the assassination must not harm anyone else. (Somewhere, Louhi smiles at this sign of her daughter\u2019s progress.)<br /> <br /> The Scions realize they don\u2019t know <i>when </i>George will die, and to save the Kushtaka Kids they need to have them all present at that exact moment. Holly grits her teeth and turns to the one person they know with Prophecy: Delia Bright. Delia agrees to perform the Prophecy in return for a favor to be named later. Holly agrees, and Delia gives a time down to the minute.<br /> <br /> The Scions use their Social Fu to bring all the Kushtaka Kids together, but apart from anyone else, when Zero Hour comes on Sunday. Holly coaches Jack\u2019s Followers, the Defensive Line, on lifesaving. Suddenly, the Etna swimmers fall over, drowning. The Scions and Followers swing into action. In the group of Avalon swimmers, though, nothing\u2019s happening.<br /> <br /> The Scions realize that George didn\u2019t change them. Well, of course not! Once they think to check, the Scions find that George was the Etna swim coach, and he wasn\u2019t about to help the rival school\u2019s team. <i>Alex</i> did. Alex had a friend on the Etna team who told him about how George magically made them into super-athlete kushtakas, and Alex wheedled George into changing him, too. Alex then went swimming in Puget Sound, completing his own transformation. And then he started changing his friends on the Avalon swim team.<br /> <br /> Alex is outraged by the Scions\u2019 outrage. He <i>helped </i>his friends. Being a kushtaka is great! They\u2019re <i>immortal </i>now. <i>And </i>better swimmers (thanks to Epic Dexterity). They can learn other magic powers, too. But he promises not to drown anyone else.<br /> <br /> Alex is a fibber, though. He manages to hustle his teammates away while the Scions are still dealing with the Etna team. They steal a van. Holly uses Ariadne\u2019s thread to follow them. It\u2019s a race to see whether the Scions catch up to the van before Alex reaches the seashore. Alex has a head start, but the Scions have Jaeda\u2019s driving skills and Jack\u2019s math genius to find the fastest routes. Jaeda forces the van off the road barely a quarter-mile from the shore. The van rolls, banging up Alex and the other kids, but the nascent kushtakas take off running.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s only one way left to save the five kids. Everyone chases Alex. Fortunately, both Jaeda and Holly have Lightning Sprinter. They delay Alex long enough for Jack to catch up and grab him.<br /> <br /> For all the harm Alex tried to cause, the Scions don\u2019t want to just kill him. (Holly especially feels bad about the people they couldn\u2019t save.) But they have a cunning \u2014 and very dangerous \u2014 plan.<br /> <br /> Jaeda has a Knack that lets her set limits to the damage she inflicts. (With Great Power, adapted from the Scion Wiki.) She slides the First Flower into Alex, bringing him exactly to his last Dying health level. The five swimmers fall down, drowning on dry land. And then Jaeda hits Alex with her new Health Boon, Anodyne/Affliction (Heal/Infect, renamed and slightly rewritten for clarity), turning the lethal levels into bashing. Alex is still unconscious (and with a few levels of lethal damage from bashing wraparound), but he isn\u2019t dying anymore. Jaeda sprints to the other five kids, using the same Boon on them in turn, saving their lives while Holly administers conventional first aid for drowning. (And spending all this Legend in a short time definitely Fatebinds the swim team to Jaeda in some way, not yet known to her.)<br /> <br /> The Scions hustle the five now-mortal kids to a hospital. Despite the ministrations of Holly and Jaeda, they did suffer drowning: They will need medical care and convalescence from the physical trauma. The Scions hit the emergency room staff with whatever Social Fu they can muster, so nobody thinks too hard about how five kids drowned in a traffic accident on land.<br /> <br /> Then the Scions wake up Alex and give him an ultimatum. If he <i>ever</i> tries turning <i>anyone </i>into a kushtaka again, they will kill him for real. Alex still insists that he was helping his friends, but he understands the Scions are deadly serious. (Thank you, Benefit of the Doubt.) He promises not to transform anyone, and the Scions feel sure of his sincerity, or at least of his fear.<br /> <br /> Jack\u2019s mom suffers an unhappy end to her weekend, though. She had a really nice day and a half with George. On the way back from the resort, though, George crashed his car. It burned. (Jaeda grieves at the loss of the Camaro.) The remains were so badly burned they almost didn\u2019t seem human.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Aftermath</u></b><br /> <br /> Jaeda hears from Gary. Hyndla convinced Suttung that finding more frost giants mattered more than killing Scions. Arranging a trip to the wilds of Canada will take about a week.<br /> <br /> Gary is bummed. He and Reese told their parents they\u2019d be fine back in Avalon, but Suttung and Hyndla believe the Scions will kill the younger giants the first chance they get. So, Gary must go to Canada too. Maybe no cell coverage! Definitely no Jaeda! \u201cThey\u2019re treating me like a <i>kid!</i>\u201d Not to mention that he needs to graduate properly in order to go to college. Jaeda doesn\u2019t like it either, and says she\u2019ll try to find some way for him and Reese to stay.<br /> <br /> (She also tells Gary how the Band stopped their rivals from their attempted ambush of the Vettersons at the ski lodge, and that Marshall probably still wants to murder the family. Gary immediately growls that he doesn\u2019t run from fights. If Marshall wants to throw down face to face like a <i>man,</i> Gary\u2019s ready any time! And he\u2019ll even let Marshall take a free shot at him with his rifle, not dodge or anything\u2026 if Marshall gives Gary a free swing with his axe afterward.)<br /> <br /> The Scions also worry that Suttung and Hyndla might use the week to launch further attacks. So, they arrange to meet the Vettersons under a flag of truce, bringing Barbara along to represent the other Band. They offer a deal: None of the Scions will attack any of the Vettersons, here or up in Canada, if the Vettersons agree not to attack any Scions. They promise that Gus is honorable and will abide by a treaty negotiated by the rest of his Band; Barbara says Delia has agreed to the treaty as well, and Leo doesn\u2019t care. Marshall will hate it, but Barbara says that she and Delia can control him.<br /> <br /> Suttung is suspicious, of course, but the Scions manage to convince him and Hyndla of their sincerity. (Barbara has Benefit of the Doubt: When she tells the truth she is believed, no matter how implausible the truth may be. And Hyndla has Takes One to Know One, which detects lies.)<br /> <br /> The Scions can see the wheels turning in Suttung\u2019s head as he realizes the Scions gave him a pledge to let him strike first. He and Hyndla agree to the treaty.<br /> <br /> The Scions spend the rest of the week looking over their shoulders, nervously waiting to see if Suttung attacks them. He doesn\u2019t. The next weekend, Suttung and Hyndla load up their extra-large SUV and drive off to Canada to search for the secret settlement of frost giants.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-02T16:03:39-08:00","id":1025609,"title":"","content":"<b>Research Note:</b> My source for kushtaka lore is <i>Shamans and Kushtakas: North Coast Tales of the Supernatural</i>, by Mary Giraudo Beck. I recommend it highly.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-03T07:49:55-08:00","id":1025939,"title":"","content":"I'm gonna have to side with the kushtaka on this one... What was so bad about making his friends immortal super stars?<br /> <br /> Greedy Scions want the glory all to themselves. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-04T15:44:54-08:00","id":1026725,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 16: Mictl\u00e1n A Go-Go<br /> <br /> March brings final exams for Winter Quarter, with the promise of Spring Break after that. It also brings statewide championships for the various sports. Avalon does poorly in boy\u2019s swim &amp; dive, since half the team is recuperating from a freak drowning accident. Only Alex Totenbush and Derek Huckovich do well: Alex because he\u2019s still a kushtaka, and Derek because he\u2019s a superb athlete who didn\u2019t fall for Alex\u2019s promises. Still, at least Avalon <i>has </i>a team: The entire Etna team withdraws from competition, for reasons unspecified. On the other hand, Gus and Gary compete to see who\u2019s the state wrestling champion. The two are perfectly matched. This time, Gary wins the title for Etna. (I flipped a coin, because they really <i>are </i>perfectly matched in all their relevant Traits.) Gus takes it sportingly. He tells Gary he hopes their competition continues until their final battle to see who\u2019s the last man standing at the end of the world. Gary agrees, that\u2019d be cool.<br /> <br /> The Scions also have some enduring concerns from their last adventure. Jaeda finds Holly\u2019s scheme to Fatebind Landon slightly unbelievable. She goes to Leo with her concern and asks to speak to his demon: Since bindings and covenants are a big Anuna thing, she figures the lion-demon is the closest thing they have to an expert. The lion-demon says that Landon\u2019s Fatebinding will not protect him from the Black Bodhisattva in any way. He\u2019ll just become a slave of Eternal Darkness and Evil with a Fatebinding.<br /> <br /> When Jaeda passes this discouraging news to Holly, she also brings up her concern about Marshall \u2014 concerns shared by Gus. The two Scions just can\u2019t believe that Marshall Byrd took on four werewolves by himself and won. They ask Holly to use Mystery to find what really happened. Jaeda suspects that Marshall actually lost the fight and is now a werewolf, while Gus wonders if Marshall made some kind of treacherous deal to save his life. Holly confirms, however, that Marshall told the truth and is not a werewolf. Marshall led them away from populated areas, then shot them from hiding. The other Scions point out that Gus only defeated Marshall so easily because Marshall is not primarily a hand-to-hand fighter and didn\u2019t have Cat\u2019s Grace to let him walk on the crust of snow. Any scorn for his abilities really is not fair.<br /> <br /> Gus also takes the opportunity to ask if Marshall wants to kill him (\u201cYes, someday\u201d) or intends to kill Gary within the next week (\u201cNo\u201d). Holly still has one question left in her use of Mystery, so the Scions ask the nature of the plans Suttung Vetterson had devised to kill them all. Holly receives the brief statement, \u201cCar bombing.\u201d After that, the Scions with vehicles all have Jaeda check them for bombs. None are found.<br /> <br /> In other matters, Jack pushes Jaeda to quit working at Big Cups Espresso and get a weekend job as an auto mechanic. She has the skills. It bothers Jack that she\u2019s taking the easy course as a bikini barista \u2014 and he feels the irony of giving such advice when he still works at Stick It.<br /> <br /> Jack also has a new motorcycle: a Ducati, an insanely fast Italian bike. A Ducati normally costs about $5000, but Jack got a reduced price on a used and damaged bike because the previous owner\u2019s next of kin wanted to get rid of it. Still, Holly wonders where Jack got the money. Not working at Stick It! Jack also yawns a lot in class and sometimes dozes off, something that hasn\u2019t happened since he received his Visitation and Epic Stamina. Holly vows to find out what <i>else </i>Jack does for money that keeps him from sleeping most nights.<br /> <br /> Jaeda spends a weekend camping in the woods as a vigil to search for her nagual. She has a cold, wet and miserable time, none of the wildlife shows any interest in her, and as she trudges back to Avalon some asshole in a Dodge Viper almost runs her down.<br /> <br /> Finally, the Scions receive the vests or jackets they had made from the pelts of the snow wassets they slew back in Episode 14. The person they hired to cure the pelts said he wasn\u2019t qualified to make clothing from them, so he passed the furs to a skilled tailor: Lucky Dragon Bespoke Tailor, who just opened at Hooverdale Mall. Gus and Holly, who have experience with such things, are immediately suspicious: Bespoke tailors don\u2019t take walk-in business. You call them and make appointments.<br /> <br /> Mister L\u00f3ng, the tailor, is an a comically stereotypical little Chinese man with a thick accent, but he knows his business. The new articles of Relic clothing fit perfectly. Mister L\u00f3ng tells the Scions to hire him again if they have any other <i>unusual </i>tailoring needs. Give velly good plice! And as Holly already expected before she activated the Unlidded Eye, the shop and Mr. L\u00f3ng are magical. The Scions feel sure he\u2019s really a dragon in human guise.<br /> <br /> The Scions search the mall for other unusual businesses. Sure enough, Holly sees the magic aura of another new business, Brawn\u2019s Gym. Like Scribal Depot, the gym in the mall is merely a front. The <i>real </i>gym in the back room is accessible from many different storefronts around the World. (You can\u2019t use it for travel in the manner of a Touchstone, though. Patrons can only leave through the storefront by which they entered. It\u2019s part of the magic that lets the gym be in several places at once.) The proprietor Mr. Braun, an immensely muscular middle-aged black man, readily admits to being a former Scion of Ogoun, now deceased. He became a Demigod before his death; unfortunately, as a minor Loa he can maintain corporeal form only in the gym.<br /> <br /> Gus gets a big grin when he sees the real gym in the back room. Braun promises that his equipment can give even the strongest God a good workout. He also has equipment and training programs for speed and agility, endurance and toughness, general athletics and all forms of combat. Its transdimensional nature makes it a good place to meet people from around the supernatural World, too. Of course it\u2019s enchanted to prevent fights. Gus immediately buys a membership for himself and pools his money with Holly to buy trial memberships for the other Scions. After they leave, Gus calls Gary to let his best rival know about the gym as well.<br /> <br /> Yes, the mall is getting weirder\u2026<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Ghosts \u2014 What, Again?</u></b><br /> <br /> That Friday, Jack nearly nods off while taking his final in World History. He rouses when the student next to him, a boy called Steve, leans over and whisper-asks him the answer to question 7. The teacher, Mr. Pol, notices and rather loudly reminds the class that there is no talking and any attempt at cheating results in an F. Steve says he\u2019s sorry, but he has to SIIIING! And he tosses aside his test and Scantron answer sheet and starts singing a mariachi song in Spanish. And <i>dancing.</i> The class is in an uproar.<br /> <br /> Jack immediately realizes that something unnatural is happening. (Gosh, those three dots of Epic Intelligence are useful.) He finishes his test in five seconds, tosses it to Mr. Pol and says he should take Steve to the school nurse. Mister Pol agrees that might be a good idea.<br /> <br /> Steve snaps out of his mariachi fit when Jack collars him. As expected, Steve does not remember what he did. He doesn\u2019t even know Spanish.<br /> <br /> Instead of taking Steve to the school nurse, Jack heads for American Studies, the Advanced Placement class that Holly takes in this period. It\u2019s taught by Mr. Douglas, a very distinguished-looking, elderly black man who also coaches the debate team. Jack knows Mr. Douglas since he turned out for debate this quarter. (It lets him exercise his burgeoning super-genius <i>and </i>play the fool by coming up with arguments and rebuttals that are absurd, or at least peculiar \u2014 but documented, which is what matters in formal debate.)<br /> <br /> \u201cSorry to interrupt,\u201d he tells Mr. Douglas, \u201cBut Holly is needed right away. And I\u2019ll give you <i>three reasons</i> before the end of class.\u201d Mister Douglas recognizes the debate formula. Amused, he excuses Holly and says he looks forward to the evidence Jack will cite.<br /> <br /> Holly examines Steve using the Unlidded eye. As Jack expected, she finds fading traces of ghostly possession. At that time, however, they hear another disturbance coming from the school gym. Jack tells Steve to return to Mr. Pol\u2019s class and provides a simple mnemonic for what boxes to tick off on the test.<br /> <br /> In the gym, Jack and Holly see a class of freshmen dancing in a conga line. Vice-Principal Stone, who is also one of the PE teachers, yells at them with such fury he looks like he\u2019s about to have a seizure. Jack quickly devises a lie that Stone will find plausible, at least coming from one of the school\u2019s star athletes: He says its all a hazing prank. Stone grins and says he understands, but the little maggots still need to do 20 push-ups as punishment! The conga line breaks and a few confused freshmen protest. Stone roars to make it <i>30 </i>push-ups!<br /> <br /> During this by-play, Holly sees that the freshmen were possessed, but it feels odd \u2014 as if just a few ghosts somehow possessed and controlled all the kids at once. Such ghosts would need to be quite powerful. The Unlidded Eye doesn\u2019t let her see ghosts, though, so this remains a theory.<br /> <br /> By then, the phone call from Derek Huckovich is almost expected. \u201cHey, did you know there ghosts in the school again?\u201d (Remember, Derek unexpectedly gained the power to see ghosts when the Scions regenerated his eyes.) Yes, they say, they noticed.<br /> <br /> And a minute later, the school intercoms crackle to life. Principal Flecker says he has an announcement for the school: \u201cEverybody MAMBO!\u201d<br /> <br /> After that, the rest of the school day is pretty much a loss. The Scions figure no one will miss them if the cut class for the rest of the day. But Jack does give Mr. Douglas his three reasons.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-04T15:46:09-08:00","id":1026728,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 16: Mictl\u00e1n A Go-Go (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Into the Underworld!</u></b><br /> <br /> The last plague of ghosts came from the McLann tomb. The current crop, though, seems darned peculiar for specters. It\u2019s the only lead they have on ghosts, though, so the Scions head for the tomb. They bring Derek along since he\u2019s the only one who can see ghosts. (Well, Leo can, but the rival Band sets itself the task of finding ghosts, commanding them to leave the people they possess, and smacking them around until they go back to the Underworld.)<br /> <br /> At least no one seems to have disturbed the magical mausoleum. Derek reports the ghost of Michael Balboa standing in the tomb doorway, waving to them. Unfortunately, Derek didn\u2019t get magical ears with which to hear ghosts, so Michael can\u2019t talk to them. The Scions settle for asking questions that Michael can answer with shakes or nods of his head. When that proves insufficient, Jaeda volunteers to let Michael possess her. (He\u2019s fellow Atzl\u00e1nti, so it doesn\u2019t seem as oogy to her.)<br /> <br /> Using Jaeda\u2019s voice, Michael explains that this is no jailbreak by specters. His divine father Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli <i>ordered </i>him to let this batch of ghosts free. The Atzl\u00e1nti God of the Dead was also humming dance tunes and sometimes taking little dance steps. Michael fears that his father has gone mad. He can\u2019t imagine how this happened or what anyone could do about it. He can only suggest that someone go to Mictl\u00e1n, the Underworld realm of the Atzl\u00e1nti, to investigate. No one is available but the Scions. And they\u2019d better do it as soon as possible.<br /> <br /> So how do they reach Mictl\u00e1n? Michael fills them in on the standard methods. Unfortunately, western Washington has a shortage of Aztec funerary monuments, the Day of the Dead is months away, and nobody wants to try having Jaeda (or worse, Marshall) cut out their hearts and hope it sends them to Mictl\u00e1n instead of killing them. Michael points out, however, that they have the Sword of Xibalba, specifically crafted to open portals to the Underworld. They mustn\u2019t use it near the tomb: That particular nexus was meant to open and never close. It\u2019s too dangerous. If they just go to some place with an affinity to any pantheon\u2019s death-realm, though, that might be enough for the sword to cut its way into the Underworld and detour them to Mictl\u00e1n. Gus remembers a graveyard that sports an actual pyramid, but the Band finally decides to use a Native American burial site. At least it has some loose cultural kinship to the Atzl\u00e1nti.<br /> <br /> Michael stresses, however, that the Sword of Xibalba is an extremely dangerous Relic. When they use it, specters will probably try to escape through their passage. He\u2019s not sure the sword can close the passage, either. Most of all, the Scions must not let any specters or other servants of the Titans take the sword, or they\u2019ll be able to cut their own passages from the Underworld anywhere and any time they want! \u201cYeah, important safety tip,\u201d the Band agrees.<br /> <br /> Given the risks, the Scions decide to bring the rival Band into the plan. Someone needs to stay at the burial mound and swat any escaping ghosts or monsters. Barbara accepts the logic of the plan. Leo, for once, wants to take the lead. This is <i>harrowing Hell.</i> Barbara and Delia exert themselves to convince him that he needs to stay behind. Marshall also wants to charge into the Underworld because it\u2019s a glorious adventure that could raise his Legend. Persuaded by the girls, Leo backs Barbara when she tells him to clobber Marshall if he doesn\u2019t stick to the plan.<br /> <br /> Finally, Holly recently learned the first stage of Runesinging, the Pantheon-Specific Purview of the Finnish Gods. She applied it to Prophecy, a Purview she wanted to learn but had no Relic with which to channel it. Given five minute in which to sing, though, she can use Prophecy without a Relic! Holly puts everything she has into the attempt (<i>i.e.,</i> makes this a Legendary Deed) as she asks Fate what difficulties they will face and what they can do to overcome them.<br /> <br /> As Holly sings, she slips into a prophetic trance. In her song, she says the Scions need to bring a dog, water bottles and jade beads. She further sings that they will find Tuonela, the Death Titan that is the special foe of the Finnish Gods, invading Mictl\u00e1n, and they will need to fight its creatures. At the end, Jaeda will know what to do. And on that ominous note, the trance ends.<br /> <br /> Understandably, the Scions wonder why they need these items. A little internet research shows that the Aztecs buried their dead with a sacrificed dog, a water-skin and a jade bead to help them pass the perils of Mictl\u00e1n. So, Holly finds a jewelry store where she can buy a jade bead for each Scion. They each carry a sport bottle of water. And the dog\u2026? Jack realizes that the picture of the special breed of dog looks a bit like his puppy Rocky, the former Stone Person transformed by Coyote. With deep misgivings, he decides to bring Rocky along.<br /> <br /> The Scions meet Saturday evening at the chosen burial mound. Jack drives up on his Ducati, clad in black leather with black sunglasses and a sword strapped across his back, looking totally Blade and feeling that life just doesn\u2019t get any better. He takes the opportunity to ask Barbara, \u201cIf I live through this, will you go on a date with me?\u201d Barbara, trapped, says yes. They figure Jaeda should use the Sword of Xibalba. She cuts herself to bleed on the sword, and it starts glowing the color of apocalypse. Jaeda stabs the blade down into the soil, and the mound splits in a deep chasm. Skeletons stick out from the earthen walls, clawing and wailing. A cold, stinking wind blows up from the depths. And the Scions jump in! As they fall, they feel cold, deathly presences flying up past them \u2014 and hear, faintly in the distance, a \u201cWhack!\u201d as Leo clubs the first specter back down into the chasm.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-04T15:47:29-08:00","id":1026730,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">EPISODE 16: Mictl\u00e1n A Go-Go (Continued)</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">This section of the adventure was based entirely on the treatment of Mictl\u00e1n given in <b>Scion: Demigod</b></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">. It\u2019s nice when I can make use of someone else\u2019s research and writing; it doesn\u2019t happen that often.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Act Three: We're Off To See The Death God</span></span></u></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The fall lasts several seconds, but the impact is lighter than they expected. The Scions find themselves in a dark landscape of rugged, barren mountains riddled with the mouths of caves. A stench of rot drifts from the mouth of the nearest cave.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">For a start, Jaeda bleeds on the Sword of Xibalba again and commands it to close the breach it made. The sword resists, but she overpowers it with her will and force of Legend, and the gap in the darkened sky closes.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">That's about the time a dozen hungry corpses shamble out of the nearby cave and attack. Gus summons his crocodile to join the fight, though the Creature fails to strike a single foe. (Just bad dice rolls.) Rocky growls fiercely but hides behind Jack.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jaeda starts by slashing at a hungry corpse with the Sword of Xibalba, hoping it will deal more damage than the First Flower. The sword bites deeply and it feels as if the sword gains power from being used thus. It feels right, in a deeply wrong sort of way. Jaeda realizes that feeding power to a sword designed to release deathly horrors on the World might not be a good idea. She sheathes the sword and draws the First Flower instead.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">At first, Holly prudently retreats from the fight because she isn't much of a brawler. She soon realizes, however, that the trick isn't so much doing damage to the hungry corpses, it's simply hitting them enough times to make them go down. Holly joins the fight and performs her first combat stunt: A series of Olympic-class acrobatic flips that ends with her stabbing the high heel of her extremely fashionable shoe through a hungry corpse's eye. \u201cOh, now I see why you like combat,\u201d she says as she collects Legend points for the stunt. \u201cIt makes you more powerful!\u201d</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus also performs several excellent stunts using the sturdy staff he brought along for just this purpose. For instance, he uses it to grapple two hungry corpses and pin them to a cliff for other Scions to attack; swings around it to sweep the legs out from under a few hungry corpses; grapples from behind and uses it to snap a hungry corpse's head off; and so on.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The fight takes a while, but only because hungry corpses take a lot of killing. Thanks to abundant and creative stunting, though, everyone ends the fight with more Legend points than when they began. They'll need it for the trials to come: a series of tests based on adherence to the Atzl\u00e1nti Virtues of Conviction, Courage, Duty and Loyalty.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The Scions hear a river in the distance. They recognize this as the first hurdle in the long and difficult journey to Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli. Gus takes the lead, starting across the fast-flowing, white-water river with the help of his crocodile. Crossing requires accumulating successes on a (Stamina + Loyalty) roll. Gus doesn't have any Loyalty, but he has plenty of Stamina and Epic Stamina. (The rules for Epic Attributes only specifically mention lack of an Ability as grounds for denying the use of Epic Attribute bonus successes. And it seemed unfair to deny use of the Epic Attribute for a dice pool where a character normally could not have the second required Trait.) Gus also has the help of a supernatural animal taking the place of the traditional dog. He crosses in a few hours. (Gee, the river didn't seem that wide.) He waves for them to follow him.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The other Scions start across the river with Rocky as their guide. The 90-pound puppy plunges happily into the river. The Scions have trouble staying together, but they soon realize they don't need to: Jack and Jaeda both have Loyalty ratings of 3, which attracts a xoloitzcuintle dog to them. Jack tells Rocky to help Holly (who has a Loyalty of only 1) while they cross with the help of the native dogs. Everyone gets across in time. By the end of the crossing, though, even Rocky is tired and willing to leave the water.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Next they trudge and clamber through the rugged cleft that is the Joined-Mountain Pass. Everyone gets very thirsty, and they are glad they brought the water bottles.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The pass brings them to the Obsidian Mountain. By now, the Scions realize they must tackle each challenge on their own and start up the mountain themselves. Here, the roll is (Strength + Conviction) to keep climbing despite the pain of being cut by the razor-sharp stones of the mountain. Gus scampers up the mountain quickly, being well endowed with both Traits. He even carries Rocky on his shoulders. Jack and Jaeda follow more slowly: Jack has plenty of Strength but no Conviction, while Jaeda has a dot of Conviction but lacks Epic Strength. Holly falls far behind, since her Strength is low and Conviction is not one of her pantheon Virtues. The Scion of Louhi grits her teeth, though, and focuses all her will on climbing the mountain. Between a Legendary Deed and spending all her Willpower, she finally makes it up the mountain despite the pain (and some really sucky dice rolls).</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Next comes the path of the Icy Knife-Winds that whisper of the traveler's betrayals. This challenge, however, is easy: Holly and Jack both remember the chant that silences the accusing winds and teach it to the other Scions. Jaeda is mortified that the Scions of Louhi and Lono know her mythology better than she does.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">After that, the Scions must pass through a valley while collecting the flags that represent their allegiances. The roll is (Perception + Duty). Unfortunately, only Jaeda has this Virtue. Once again, though, Gus sets a fast pace: He has no Duty, but he has Epic Perception! Jack and Jaeda follow more slowly, moving carefully and applying all their will to spotting flags. (This time, they spend Willpower.) Poor Holly, with neither Duty nor Epic Perception, and no Willpower points left, soon botches and finds herself back at the head of the valley. Unable to resist her principal Virtue of Intellect, she sits down and spends the rest of the day designing a perfect search pattern until she falls asleep.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">As she wanders through the forest of flags, though, Jaeda comes under attack. A huge serpent, its skin withered and maggots dripping from the shriveled remains of its eyes, crashes through the flags and strikes at her. Fortunately, although the mummy-snake is terribly strong and fast, Jaeda is quicker and more agile. The snake can't bite her, but she stabs it a few times. When the snake stops trying to bite her and tries to wrap around and squeeze her instead, Jaeda quickly positions the First Flower so the snake impales itself on the obsidian blade. The snake doesn't like that and quickly releases her. Another stab or two dispatches the unholy thing. As the slain horror shrivels in on itself, Jaeda remembers the tomb-spawn from back in Episode 10: Creatures of Tuonela explode into toxic dust when they die. She moves back before the snake explodes and suffers no harm. The snake leaves a hadseax-sized fang behind and Jaeda collects the Trophy. (Holly later revealed that the fang can strike immaterial ghosts.)</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Best of all, Jaeda finds that the thrashing snake cleared a path through the flags. She sees the exit from the valley and is the first one out! As an Atzl\u00e1nti, her Duty was to fight the invasion from Tuonela: It seems that defeating one of its creatures was enough to see her through.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus and Jack straggle in a few hours later. They camp and wait for Holly. Jack feels terrible, though, and the other two need to talk him out of punishing himself for leaving Holly to struggle through on her own. (Jack used up all his Willpower; his highest-rated Virtue is Endurance.)</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The next morning (though Mictl\u00e1n stays pretty dark all the time), Holly has recovered a Willpower point. She realizes two things: Her attempt to defeat the valley through pure logic was nuts; and she can apply her Legend in other ways than Legendary Deeds. Grimly, she starts through the valley again while her player applies Legend to re-roll any roll that fails. After several hours, Holly finally emerges from the valley, laden with flags that represent her Duties.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">After this, the Scions must traverse another ledge. As each one approaches, a voice demands, \u201cHow do you repay the Giver of Life?\u201d (It speaks Nahuatl, but Jaeda translates.) Jaeda replies, in true Atzl\u00e1nti fashion, \u201cWith blood,\u201d and cuts herself to drip a little on the ground. She does not try to avoid the flight of arrows that shoot out of the mist. They strike her but deal only a single level of lethal damage. On the other hand, she recovers her Willpower for her willing sacrifice. The other Scions receive the same question, but they try holding out the jade beads as \u201cpayment.\u201d It doesn't work, but neither do they try avoiding the arrows. Though everyone is wounded by the time they leave the ledge, they feel somewhat better for having their Willpower restored.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Next comes a trek through a jungle full of fierce birds and beasts that try ripping out everyone's heart. Here, the Scions realize why they needed the jade beads: Each attacking animal accepts a bead in place of a heart. A cottonmouth attacks Jaeda, and she sees a pattern emerging.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Finally, the Scions reach the rough stone arch that leads to the great pyramid of Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli. They hear howling approach. As they start across the bridge, four huge dogs come out of the jungle and charge after them. The dogs are clearly creatures of Tuonela, both rotted and withered, with scraps of skin hanging off their bones, dripping maggots as they run. Battle is joined!</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holly realizes that these are far more formidable opponents than the hungry corpses, and she retreats behind the other three Scions. It doesn't help. One hound jumps over the Scions to attack her. It bites, dealing considerable damage and poisoning her as well. Gus, however, attempts a daring ploy: He attacks three of the deathly hounds with a sweep attack and successfully knocks them off the bridge and into the mist-filled void below. The other Scions gang up on the remaining hound and destroy it, prudently moving away when it explodes into poisonous dust. Holly applies her Legend to resisting the poison, but this isn't her strong suit. Jaeda quickly applies a Health Boon to heal some of the damage from the bite, though, and so Holly avoids incapacitation. As the Scions look up from the aftermath of the fight, they find they have spontaneously moved to the other end of the bridge. Evidently, Mictl\u00e1n considered fighting the minions of the invading Titan to be a sufficient demonstration of Valor.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The doors of the pyramid-tomb open and the Scions enter the Windowless House of Mictl\u00e1n!</span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-04T15:48:32-08:00","id":1026732,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 16: Mictl\u00e1n A Go-Go (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: The Disco King of the Dead</u></b><br /> <br /> At the center of the pyramid, the Scions find the immense, vaulted throne room of Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli and his queen, Mictl\u00e1ncihuatl, seated back to back. The Scions announce themselves. The God of the Dead waits a long moment before he says, in a grating voice like stone on stone, \u201cEverybody \u2014 MAMBO!\u201d Spotlights converge on the mirror ball hanging above the thrones. A mariachi band starts playing. The dead seated in the tiers around the chamber rise and dance. And the Scions can see that the King of the Dead\u2019s throne room is very strange.<br /> <br /> A third of it is classic Aztec/Mayan stonework and carving. There\u2019s lots of skull and bone imagery. It\u2019s grotesque, but in a clean and formal way. The Scions who know their mythology remember that to the Aztecs, bones are symbols of latent life and the potential for resurrection.<br /> <br /> Another third is horrific. Aztec art shows blood and bones, but never rot. These carving show the zombie and mummy creatures of Tuonela along with maggots, worms and dripping entrails.<br /> <br /> The final third is a mixture of contemporary Day of the Dead imagery \u2014 dancing skeletons and the like \u2014 with stenciled Day-Glo flowers and other psychedelia. It looks like a cross between the Day of the Dead and a \u201860s discotheque. As the Scions watch, though, the disco imagery flows over and replaces Tuonela portions. The invading Titan doesn\u2019t retreat without a fight, though: The Scions also see the imagery of rot flowing over the classic Aztec decorations. They realize that on some metaphysical level they don\u2019t fully understand, this is the God of the Dead\u2019s way of fighting the Titan subversion of his realm.<br /> <br /> Jaeda realizes the prophecy that she would know what to do was optimistic. She kneels before Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli and asks what she should do. The god grates out, \u201cUse \u2014 the \u2014 Giver \u2014 of \u2014 Life. I \u2014 must \u2014 die!\u201d Jaeda remembers that for her pantheon, death is sacrifice that brings new life. And she carries the knife that sacrificed the entire pantheon to birth a new World. The Scions agree, Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli must have released the ghost plague as a lure to draw them, and the First Flower, to him. Jaeda drives the First Flower into Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli\u2019s robes where his belly should be, reaches up into his chest and pulls out his heart. At first the God\u2019s heart is a dry and shriveled thing, but in her hand it swells and reddens with blood.<br /> <br /> (\u201cAnd in the depths of Mictl\u00e1n, they say,\u201d Jack murmurs, \u201cMictl\u00e1ntecuhtli\u2019s heart grew <i>three sizes</i> that day.\u201d Later, he feels dirty.)<br /> <br /> The Lord and Lady of the Dead collapse in heaps of bones. Then the heart in Jaeda\u2019s hand blazes like the sun, momentarily blinding the Scions before it burns to ash. When the Scions\u2019 vision clears, they see the two Gods back on their thrones. The Tuonela imagery is gone from the chamber, as are the Disco Day of the Dead dancing skeletons and flowers.<br /> <br /> \u201cThank \u2014 you.\u201d Mictl\u00e1ntecuhtli says. \u201cYou \u2014 must \u2014 go,\u201d his other half says. \u201cI \u2014 must \u2014 purge \u2014 my \u2014 realm,\u201d the God continues. \u201cGo,\u201d Mictl\u00e1ncihuatl finishes, and points to a small door in the wall of the chamber.<br /> <br /> The Scions leave and find themselves in a room lit with fluorescent lights, looking on the ruins of a pyramid. They are in the museum that surrounds the ruins of the Templo Mayor in the heart of Mexico City.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Aftermath</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions hope their trek through the Underworld happened outside of the normal flow of time in the World. No such luck: They spent three days in the Underworld, and three days have passed in the World as well.<br /> <br /> First, the Scions call their parents. Jaeda\u2019s father Mateo knows the score about Scions, so she has no trouble explaining matters to him. Jaeda is actually pleased that her father worried about her. Holly\u2019s parents don\u2019t know her daily activities, so she merely says she headed down to Mexico for Spring Break and apologizes for not telling them earlier. Gus\u2019s father is upset at not knowing what happened to his son, but he readily accepts Gus\u2019 story about some s00per-hawt girls and an offer that had to be accepted right away. Jack has the most trouble: His mother was worried sick about him! She orders him to come home immediately and fumes that he\u2019s so grounded that \u201cYour <i>grandchildren</i> will still be grounded!\u201d Gus suggests that Jack should stay in Mexico \u2018cause how can he get in worse trouble, but Jack says he can\u2019t put his mother through any more worry and aggravation. He catches the next bus north. The Scions also think to call Barbara and let her know they\u2019re unharmed.<br /> <br /> The other Scions decide to make the most of the rest of the week. They head for famed resort town Cabo San Lucas. Thinking of Jaeda, Gus also phones Gary and invites him and Reese to join them. Holly phones Cole. The three fly down the next day to join the epic partying.<br /> <br /> Jaeda and Reese happily participate in wet t-shirt contests. Holly considers this beneath her dignity until Jaeda gets her drinking. Then she participates \u2014 and wins, thanks to the Evil Eye she casts on Jaeda.<br /> <br /> When the boys hold a wet boxer shorts contest, of course Gus and Gary can\u2019t resist competing. Once again, the two are evenly matched in physique, appearance and Epic Appearance. They differ in Charisma, Epic Appearance and Presence, but not by much. The contest comes down to contested (Charisma + Presence) rolls to see who\u2019s best at playing to the crowd. Gary channels his Rapacity to project that I-want-you-<i>now</i> vibe, while Gus channels his Conviction that he won\u2019t let anyone beat him. Gary got a semi-decent roll, but the roll for Gus was incredible (11 successes!) Gus is the clear crowd favorite as Best-Filled Basket. Though, he admitted later that the competition felt gay enough to make him uncomfortable after the fact.<br /> <br /> That\u2019s nothing, though, compared to the discomfort Gus feels later that evening when Reese comes on to him. She says he\u2019s like a frost giant: Big, blond and super-strong, as well as being gorgeous. Gus is severely tempted, but he regretfully turns her down. In the first place, Gary would try to kill him, and they\u2019ve agreed to leave their fight to the death for the End of the World. In the second place, Gus still considers Reese Gene\u2019s girl, and he won\u2019t poach unless he knows that Gene is really truly never coming back. Reese consoles herself with a football team from Texas.<br /> <br /> Only afterward, as the Scions travel back to Washington, do they realize that their quest through the Underworld has boosted their Legends to 4.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-06T15:49:04-08:00","id":1027880,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 17: MORTAL CLAY<br /> <br /> As the school year moves into April, the Scions deal with the consequences of the previous adventure and get on with their lives.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: School, Monsters and Other Distractions</u></b><br /> <br /> HOLLY AND BARBARA: April brings a rare sunny day. Out in the bleachers by the school athletic field, Holly briefs Barbara on the quest through Mictl\u00e1n and learns what the rival Band did at that time. Barbara says Marshall wanted to jump after the PCs, but she and Leo stopped him. A few specters escaped through the burial mound but got smacked back to the Underworld. A powerful mummy clad in a suit of jade plates (a way some Mayan kings were buried) also escaped and gave the Band a hell of a fight, but they managed to destroy it. They\u2019re pretty sure the jade suit is a Relic that protects the wearer from the deathly environment of Tuonela.<br /> <br /> Barbara also notes that she became more powerful after that little adventure. It seems her Legend is to Stick To The Plan. And to clean up the backsplash of the other Band\u2019s adventures. She almost manages not to sound bitter.<br /> <br /> The conversation soon segues to boys. Barbara admits she thinks Leo is hot. (And the hottest thing about him is that he doesn\u2019t know it.)<br /> <br /> About that time, Landon and another boy, Zach, come by with cameras. They are both in the school\u2019s photography club and want pictures of the school\u2019s two most overachieving girls for the yearbook. Zach mostly wants to take pictures of Holly, though, and treats the occasion as a fashion shoot: \u201cThe camera loves you, love it back!\u201d He\u2019d also like to sculpt her in clay. Preferably a nude study. Landon winces. And that\u2019s when Cole walks up behind Zach, spins him around, and punches him in the jaw.<br /> <br /> JACK\u2019S FOLLOWERS AND GUS: Jack is grounded, but he\u2019d planned to have Gus fight the Offensive Line (Bull, Moose, Bear, Ox and Gnu) as a way to show them what supernatural threats are really like in a way that won\u2019t get them killed. Gus agrees to the plan. Jack wants his friends to know that though they might be able to deal with cultists or get bystanders out of the way, most creatures of Legend are way out of their league.<br /> <br /> The five boys ambush Gus as he leaves one of his self-defense classes. At least, they try: Gus spots them. The boys try surrounding Gus so one of them can hit him from behind with a baseball bat; but Gus moves first, uses a stunt to gain surprise on them, and throws two in the open trunk of his car. Gnu, who leads the assault, throws a handful of salt in Gus\u2019 eyes; but Gus has Blind Fighting and is not handicapped at all. Gnu\u2019s second attempt, with a powerful flashbulb, fails for the same reason. Gus lets the boys hit him just to show they cannot hurt him, before catching a couple around the neck and carefully squeezing them unconscious. The others end up on the ground with Gus sitting on them. Gnu says \u201cuncle.\u201d<br /> <br /> That\u2019s when a creature about the size and shape of a black bear jumps out of the shadows and tries to savage Gus. Hey, this isn\u2019t part of the plan! The conscious boys drag their unconscious friends away while Gus tries to grapple the beast and tear it apart. While the beast can\u2019t escape Gus\u2019 grip, Gus can\u2019t break its bones and barely manages to tear its tough, rubbery skin. Hitting the thing doesn\u2019t work: In fact, such attacks bounce back at the attacker! Gus thinks of trying to burn the thing and directs Gnu to the flares in the trunk of his car. Gnu manages to jam the flare in the rip Gus made and inflicts a little damage. At last, Gus manages to tear a hole in the creature\u2019s skin and it <i>pops</i> like a balloon!<br /> <br /> THIS IS NOT YOUR ADVENTURE: Gus immediately calls Holly to ask what the hell attacked him. Holly recognizes it as a <i>gumberoo</i>, another one of the bizarre beasts from <i>Fearsome Creatures of the Lumberwoods.</i> She in turn alerts Barbara and finds that Delia just encountered a gumberoo as well. Delia, however, simply ran. Both groups of Scions try backtracking their gumberoos. The PCs eventually follow its trail back to a dolmen (a sort of megalithic construction, though this one is made of concrete) back in the hills. They find a Titan-corrupted dryad, several more gumberoos who serve her, some deluded cultists, and the rival Band already dealing with the situation. Delia holds the dryad with Serpent\u2019s Gaze and repeated Overt Orders while Barbara chops it up with her axe. Marshall leads his Army brat Followers in gunning down the gumberoos with incendiary bullets (<i>Fearsome Creatures </i>confirmed that fire is the bane of gumberoos), except for one that Leo has grappled and is choking to death. He isn\u2019t strong enough to rip it apart, but the weird chimera still needs to breathe. Jaeda is disappointed: Holly deduced that the gumberoo\u2019s skin could be made into lightweight Relic armor, and Jaeda hoped to bag one for herself. She can still ask Gus for the skin he collected, though.<br /> <br /> JACK IN THE LUNCHROOM: During lunch period the next day, the Offensive Line tells Jack about their farcical defeat by Gus. They took the lesson to heart: They cannot fight potent supernatural entities.<br /> <br /> Their deliberations are interrupted by a group of kids hassling another boy a few tables over. \u201cYou\u2019re in my seat, Bennie. Ooh, what\u2019s this you\u2019re lookin\u2019 at? Oops, I spilled my drink! Whaddaya have to say about that, Bennie? HAHAHA!\u201d Bennie, surrounded and outnumbered, can only manage a few vowel sounds as his face reddens.<br /> <br /> Jack decides he doesn\u2019t like this. He stands up, moseys over and suggests to the boys (a group of third-tier bullies who only pick on the weakest, least popular kids) that they should think of an elsewhere to be. The Offensive Line joins Jack. The bullies back off. From another table, Marshall guffaws scornfully that Jack is wasting his time, it\u2019s only Silent Bennie after all. \u201cOh, Marshall,\u201d Jack says, \u201cWe can always count on you to be the voice of compassion and stand up for the underdog.\u201d<br /> <br /> Whatever Bennie might say, he doesn\u2019t. He just stammers a few more vowel sounds, gathers up the book and papers he had around him, and hurries away. Jack sees that some of the loose papers were photocopies of something Egyptian \u2014 hieroglyphic text, funny pictures of people walking sideways, animal-headed gods, and the like. Bennie\u2019s book is a \u201cTeach Yourself Hieroglyphics\u201d text. Jack notes it as something that might get him another $100 worth of pizza from Gus.<br /> <br /> Jack doesn\u2019t get a chance to follow Bennie, though, because Vice-Principal Stone wants a word with him. Stone is displeased that Jack did not sign up for the WASLs. Avalon High gets money from the Federal government if enough students excelling at the Washington Assessment of Student Learning tests that take place later in April; and Dr. Demerit says that contrary to appearances, Jack is actually a brilliant student. Jack will therefore show his school spirit by taking the tests and doing very well on them. <i>Or else.</i> Jack agrees to do so.<br /> <br /> GUS IN THE WEIGHT ROOM: Earlier, Gus asked Landon to look for other kids who wanted to better themselves as Landon had; Landon wasn\u2019t sure exactly what Gus looked for, but he suggested that some of his fellow nerds might try working out if they felt sure the jocks wouldn\u2019t make fun of them. So, Gus started a \u201cfitness for non-jocks\u201d after school program with his personal guarantee that it would be a mockery-free zone. Jaeda gets involved, too, which increases the interest in the class. As Gus and Landon train their charges, various other kids come to ogle Jaeda as she teaches aerobics and Gus as he teaches weight training.<br /> <br /> Today, Zach also shows up, with Silent Bennie in tow. Zach accuses Landon of abandoning and betraying his fellow nerds. It was kind of cool when Landon learned judo, but to team up with uberjock Gus\u2026! There\u2019s an ugly edge behind the apparent raillery, and Gus puts his foot down. He promised an environment free of mockery and insults, and he keeps his word. Zach can sign up or get out. As Zach stalks off, Gus notices Bennie (whom Jack told him about earlier) and says he\u2019s welcome in the class, but Bennie hurries off after Zach.<br /> <br /> JAEDA, LANDON AND ANOTHER: After the class, Landon asks Jaeda if she\u2019d consent to some photos. While the yearbook will include a shot of her among the rest of Mr. Oldfield\u2019s auto shop class, he\u2019d also like to photograph her for an upcoming art show. He\u2019d really like to win something, and he thinks he\u2019d have a better chance with a stunningly beautiful girl in the pictures. Jaeda readily agrees. They quickly agree on some arty shots of her striding down a wet street at night, silhouetted against neon bar signs.<br /> <br /> They take the pictures that night over in Etna. Zach tags along despite his earlier tiff with Landon. In a rare show of self-preservation, Zach keeps quiet and does not ask Jaeda to pose nude. Landon thinks the shoot went well; he drives Jaeda back to her motel.<br /> <br /> A man steps out from a shadow as Landon\u2019s car pulls away. He is young, Native American, devastatingly handsome, dressed in black leather with black sunglasses at night. Jaeda recognizes those sunglasses: Smoking Mirrorshades, just like the ones she uses. The Relic sunglasses of Tezcatlipoca. Sure enough, the man takes off his sunglasses and Jaeda can see he has golden jaguar eyes.<br /> <br /> \u201cHello, Jaeda,\u201d Tezcatlipoca says. \u201cWe need to talk about your friend.\u201d<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-06T15:50:34-08:00","id":1027882,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 17: MORTAL CLAY (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Job Offers</u></b><br /> <br /> The Smoking Mirror knows that Landon is a shinobi. More than that, he\u2019s a <i>masterless </i>shinobi \u2014 something that hasn\u2019t happened in nearly a thousand years. That condition won\u2019t last long, though. Unless a God binds Landon to him, the Black Bodhisattva or some other agent of the Night Titan Mikaboshi will find Landon, give him a command, and bind him to the Titans forever. As Jaeda well knows.<br /> <br /> Tezcatlipoca wants to be that God. By claiming Landon, he can work a signing statement into the shinobi enchantment for the Atzl\u00e1nti, the way the Amatsukami did long ago. He wants Jaeda\u2019s help in convincing Landon to take his offer. While he expresses confidence in Jaeda\u2019s sense of duty to the Atzl\u00e1nti, he also promises unspecified rewards for success in recruiting Landon. Tezcatlipoca tells Jaeda to bring Landon to a certain hotel, one of the fanciest in Seattle, at 8 p.m. the next night. When Jaeda agrees to help him, Tezcatlipoca smiles, gets in his car \u2014 a black Jaguar (duh) \u2014 and drives away.<br /> <br /> As Fate would have it, the Smoking Mirror is not the only God interested in Landon. When Holly comes home from school that afternoon, she finds an embossed and gilded card on her dining room table, with a phone number on it. The number connects her to her mother Louhi. The Crone of Pohjola lays out the same proposal as Tezcatlipoca: This is a chance for her to start her own line of shinobi, bound to serve her. And the other Finnish Gods, of course, when she allows it. Holly must bring Landon to the <i>other </i>fanciest hotel in Seattle at 8 p.m. tomorrow night. Not being a fool, Holly agrees.<br /> <br /> The two Gods are not content with recruiting their own children. When Gus gets home, his father says a man in a black Jaguar stopped by the dealership to give him a card to give to Gus. The business card bears only the URL of a website, Tepeyollotl.com. Gus logs in after dinner. The screen shows various Central American motifs, especially jaguars, with an announcement that this is a members-only website. A box with a blinking cursor bears the instruction, \u201cTYPE YOUR NAME HERE.\u201d Gus does so. The screen clears to give a new message: \u201cTURN AROUND.\u201d Gus turns and finds Tezcatlipoca in his room.<br /> <br /> The Smoking Mirror goes through his spiel, spun to appeal to Gus. Not only is this a way to save Landon from the Titans, this is a chance for Landon to develop his skills and powers to the utmost. Gus says he will do his best to help Landon make the right decision, which Tezcatlipoca takes as an acceptable reply.<br /> <br /> At about the same time, Jack finds a middle-aged man in a $1000 suit standing in line at Stick It. The guy looks like a lawyer. Jack\u2019s superhuman intellect recognizes that this is not normal Stick It clientele. Sure enough, the man doesn\u2019t want to order a food-like substance on a stick; he wants to talk about a lucrative opportunity that could benefit both Jack and one of his friends. The man tells Jack to meet him at a certain exit from the mall when his shift ends, and hands Jack a business card with a folded $100 bill. The card identifies the man as Mark Hatto, an employee of Northland Investments.<br /> <br /> Jack immediately phones Holly. She confirms to Jack that Mr. Hatto undoubtedly works for Louhi. (Not only is Northland Investments the company of Louhi\u2019s current mortal persona, Mark Hatto\u2019s name recalls that of Markahattu, the Pohjolan who murdered the hero Lemminkainen by throwing a snake through the hero\u2019s heart. Neat tick, especially since Markahattu was blind. See the <i>Kalevala </i>for the full story.) Holly says Jack should take the offer Mr. Hatto will undoubtedly give. Sure enough, Mr. Hatto lays out the spiel about recruiting Landon for Louhi. He says Jack can earn a $10,000 finder\u2019s fee if Landon takes Louhi\u2019s offer. Hatto also suggests that the Crone of Pohjola looks for talented young people whatever their background, and that Jack might be interested in the summer internships available at Northland Investments.<br /> <br /> Gus and Jaeda also phone Holly to fill her in. Holly decides that rather than play phone tag, they should meet the next day.<br /> <br /> As the Scions compare stories the next day, they recognize that whatever they choose, two of them piss off a notably powerful and dangerous god of night and sorcery. (As Holly observes, \u201cNight Gods tend not to be very nice people.\u201d) Holly wants Landon to choose Louhi, as a matter of self-preservation (Holly\u2019s, not Landon\u2019s). Jaeda loyally wants Landon to sign up with the Atzl\u00e1nti. Whatever they think of the two Gods\u2019 offers, though, Gus and Jack want the choice to stay Landon\u2019s own. (Though Gus is annoyed that the Pesedjet aren\u2019t making an offer to Landon. He\u2019s tried to contact them for the last month, after all.) They also let Landon know that two Gods want to recruit him.<br /> <br /> The Scions decide they should take Landon to some neutral ground where neither God has an advantage, and where both might want to refrain from any smiting. Only one place nearby fills the bill: the home of Aristotle Faustus Dee. The old mystery-man apparently has experience with Gods but doesn\u2019t work for any of them anymore, and Louhi and Tezcatlipoca might not want to risk freeing the buried Titan horror through a temper tantrum.<br /> <br /> That evening, therefore, Gus, Jaeda, Holly and Landon pile into Gus\u2019 car and take Landon to Dee\u2019s, on the theory that it is easier to gain forgiveness than permission. (Jack participates by cell phone since he\u2019s still grounded and doesn\u2019t want to make things worse with his mom.) Dee is not especially happy at the plan, but he agrees this is probably the best way to obtain the best deal for Landon. Sure enough, at 8 p.m. Dee\u2019s doorbell rings and Dee tells his manservant to admit the two Gods. Dee greets Tezcatlipoca with due courtesy and formality. Turning to Louhi, he says, \u201cHello, Lou. It\u2019s been a long time.\u201d And to Holly\u2019s further surprise, Louhi calls Dee \u201cAri\u201d in her reply. But Tezcatlipoca does not seem put out by this hint of a past between them.<br /> <br /> The Gods make their pitches to Landon:<br /> <br /> Louhi promises wealth, power and the best trainers in the world. It\u2019ll be dangerous, of course, but Louhi offers rewards in proportion to the danger. She likes to think of Northland Investments as not just a business but a family, and she\u2019d like Landon to become part of that family. Literally. Heroes who meet the challenges she sets get to marry her daughters, and it seems Holly is already interested. After all, she talked Landon into accepting a life-long Fatebinding to her\u2026<br /> <br /> Tezcatlipoca doesn\u2019t talk about money; he talks about adventure. Landon will see the Worlds and perform deeds he never imagined. There will be treasures to loot, beautiful women to seduce and enemies to kill. The Smoking Mirror doesn\u2019t pretend it\u2019ll be nice. Nobody uses ninjas to send flowers or rescue puppies, and Landon should have known that when he agreed to become a shinobi. What\u2019s more, Tezcatlipoca doesn\u2019t pretend he is a benevolent god: He is the Enemy of Both Sides, bringer of chaos. But he says he is necessary to the World, and so will be the missions he assigns. Landon will live more intensely than he ever dreamed he could, and he can make a difference in the Worlds.<br /> <br /> Before making a choice, Landon asks the Scions their opinions. Holly and Jaeda, of course, support their pantheons. Gus sides with Tezcatlipoca. Jack waffles a bit, but finally sides with Tezcatlipoca as well. Even Holly finally admits that her advocacy for her mother is merely obedience born of self-interest. The Scions reach a consensus of sorts that while the Smoking Mirror and the Crone of Pohjola are both fairly evil, as mortals reckon such things, it\u2019s better to work for a God who admits it up front.<br /> <br /> Dee speaks up at this point and suggests that before Landon accepts either God\u2019s offer, he should try to get as much as possible for himself. Louhi and Tezcatlipoca both grant that Landon can finish high school before taking a full-time position, and he can stay in contact with his family after that. Still, Louhi can see which way the wind\u2019s blowing and does not seem surprised when Landon chooses Tezcatlipoca.<br /> <br /> Holly apologizes to Louhi for failing to recruit Landon, though she also grants that she could have tried harder and expects to suffer for her failure. Louhi says Holly will\u2026 learn from the experiences to come, and confirms Holly\u2019s suspicion that Cole will become one of those lessons. Louhi departs.<br /> <br /> Tezcatlipoca casts a spell to bind Landon to him and the Atzl\u00e1nti (a slight variation on the spell by which one God can adopt another God\u2019s Scion). While it involves blood, it isn\u2019t too dramatic: The Lord of Sorcerers seems slightly amused by the watching Scions\u2019 sense of anticlimax. As a result of choosing a pantheon, though, Landon\u2019s Legend rises to 2.<br /> <br /> As her reward for helping to recruit Landon, Tezcatlipoca gives Jaeda GPS coordinates for her nagual. (Yes, he knows about her search. He is the Night Wind: He knows <i>everything.</i> That\u2019s the legend, anyway.) When Jaeda goes there later, she finds an abandoned house and a 1970 Ford Torino Cobra under a tarp. It\u2019s rusty and on blocks, but looks like it could be repaired. Jaeda immediately recognizes it as her nagual \u2014 snake (as she suspected) and car in one.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-06T15:51:39-08:00","id":1027883,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 17: MORTAL CLAY (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Playing With Dolls</u></b><br /> <br /> The Gods dealt with, the Scions turn to the issue of Bennie. Everyone agrees they should investigate a student who teaches himself hieroglyphics. Gus volunteers to talk to Bennie.<br /> <br /> Before he gets a chance, though, Holly receives a chiding from Bobby Kilmer, her computer genius Follower. Bobby tells her it\u2019s not nice to string Cole along while also making out with some other boy. This is news to Holly. Bobby explains that he saw Holly going hot and heavy with a boy in a blue Toyota. It was definitely her \u2014 Holly\u2019s long, pale blonde hair especially is unmistakable \u2014 though he couldn\u2019t identify the boy. Holly insists that despite appearances, it wasn\u2019t her.<br /> <br /> Gus finds Bennie eating lunch in the library\u2019s back room instead of the cafeteria. The Scion decides this is a good chance to use his new Heku power, Bind the Heart. Sneaking up behind Bennie, he draws with his finger an inch over Bennie\u2019s back as he imposes the new Nature of Conformist: Bennie will feel most comfortable acting in support of someone else, and Gus plans to be that someone. Communication is still difficult at first, but Gus manages to set Bennie at ease enough to reduce his stammer. He also learns that Bennie doesn\u2019t stammer if he speaks in some other language \u2014 he already taught himself Latin and Greek, and now works on Ancient Egyptian.<br /> <br /> Bennie\u2019s father works as a conservator at the Burke Museum, part of the University of Washington. The museum staff recently found a hitherto-unknown Egyptian scroll in the backlog of uncatalogued material (some of which has been there for decades). Bennie thinks ancient Egypt is cool (Gus smiles and agrees) so he thought he\u2019d attempt his own translation. It seems to be a book of spells for creating <i>shabti,</i> the figurines that magically become servants in the Afterlife \u2014 or in this World. Bennie mentions a tale about a magician who brought a wax model of a crocodile to life to eat his unfaithful wife and her lover. The scroll claims to be written by a God, but which God is unclear to Bennie: Some images suggest the God Khnum, the potter-God who shapes the unborn in the mother\u2019s womb, but other images and references suggest the very ancient and obscure Creator-God Tatenen, raiser of the primeval mound that became the World, who is usually treated as an alternate name and form of Ptah. Gus wishes he knew more about his own pantheon. He suspects Tatenen is actually a Titan.<br /> <br /> Bennie insists that he never tried using the spells he\u2019s translated, though Zach thought it would be a lark. When Gus and Holly compare notes, though, it all falls together. Zach is a sculptor as well as a photographer. He\u2019s Bennie\u2019s only friend. He must have used the spells Bennie translated to make a <i>shabti </i>of Holly come to life and serve as his sex toy. Eww! But how can this possibly work? Bennie and Zach aren\u2019t Scions or other supernatural creatures\u2026 aren\u2019t they? And even if the scroll is a Relic, they didn\u2019t even have the real scroll, just a photocopy!<br /> <br /> Holly decides to confront Zach. The other Scions come along to give Zach a chance to make amends before Holly wreaks terrible vengeance upon him. (And Jaeda wants to make sure Zach doesn\u2019t make any copies of her.) At first Zach denies everything, but when pressed he confesses. He had a copy of the spells and \u201cborrowed\u201d the reproduction <i>uas </i>(an Egyptian priest-magician\u2019s staff) owned by Bennie\u2019s father. Some days ago, he animated a clay sculpture he did of a Welsh corgi. His parents won\u2019t let him have pets, so he found this very cool. Then, yeah, he sculpted a figure of Holly and brought it to life for, well, you know. He\u2019s sorry, okay? He <i>promises </i>he\u2019s sorry. If they can tell him how to be more sorry, he\u2019ll do it. But don\u2019t worry, his shabti both turned back into clay figurines at his command.<br /> <br /> The Scions say that for a start, Zach needs to destroy the figurines, burn his copies of the spells, and get rid of the <i>uas </i>rod. Then they\u2019ll see about stopping Holly from making him suffer for eternity. They leave the school. This is important enough to skip class.<br /> <br /> And out in the parking lot they hear a strangely deep-pitched yapping. A moment later a huge canine figure the size of a pony leaps at them and tries to bite Zach. Is it a fenrir? A dire wolf? No: It is a giant Nemean Welsh Corgi!<br /> <br /> The Scions battle the strangely cute menace. Holly decides not to waste spells and Legend unless she must. The Corgi is pretty tough (Nemean beasts are like that), but with three Scions attacking, it soon lies on the ground, its short little legs twitching as it dies and reverts to a small, smashed clay figure.<br /> <br /> Zach doesn\u2019t understand. When he told his shabti to stop living, they turned back into statues. Beyond that\u2026 \u201cCounterspell? What counterspell?\u201d As the Scions hurry to Zach\u2019s house, they speculate on whether they\u2019ll need to fight a 50-foot Holly.<br /> <br /> As they approach, Holly (using Unlidded Eye) sees powerful magic blasting from the basement of Zach\u2019s house. Fortunately, his parents are not home. The house itself is shifting on its foundations. There\u2019s also a big corgi-shaped hole in the wall.<br /> <br /> The Scions enter. Gus decides to take whatever\u2019s in the basement by surprise (and make a cool entrance) by smashing through the floor. The Scions drop down to find another Holly \u2014 only the shabti-Holly is dressed in Egyptian drag, with Tatenen\u2019s headdress of twisting horns and ostrich plumes. There are also 10 identical Egyptian warriors. The concrete basement is slowly shifting into the sanctum of an Egyptian temple, pushing the house apart as it changes. When she sees the Scions, the shabti-Holly taps her <i>uas</i> rod against the basement wall and concrete flows over it to form an eight-foot-long giant rod. Battle is joined!<br /> <br /> Shabti-Holly announces that she is, well, not Tatenen itself, but an emanation of that Titan. It nearly broke into the World almost fifty years ago; this time it shall succeed! Gus notes the timing: That was when his great-uncle Randolph was apparently active in the area as a Scion. He taunts Tatenen that another Scion of Sobek will defeat it.<br /> <br /> Holly starts by casting the Evil Eye on Tatenen, but the Titan-shabti knows this spell too and counters it. The shabti warriors are highly skilled, but the Scions have sufficient Epic Dexterity that the warriors only have a chance to hit by attacking in groups of five. Gus quickly uses Game Face to scare away a couple warriors so they can\u2019t exploit that tactic. The warriors manage to strike a few blows anyway, but by now everyone except Holly has Epic Stamina and Damage Conversion, so only Holly takes significant damage. (Though Jack had to spend a huge amount of Legend to resist harm. <i>Something</i> is Fatebound to him because of this fight.) Holly remembers it\u2019s <i>really stupid </i>for her to get in the middle of scrums like this, so she retreats to protect Zach. Good thing, too: There are a few more shabti warriors upstairs, chasing the boy. Holly activate Lightning Sprinter and grabs Zach, keeping him well ahead of the pursuing warriors.<br /> <br /> The shabti Tatenen also attacks the Scions with mighty swings of her huge concrete staff. Fortunately for the Scions, she can\u2019t hit any better than the warriors can: Each impact of the staff makes the house shake. Jack becomes her target after hurting her with his Shinobi Shuriken; he spends scads more Legend avoiding her blows. When Jaeda rips through Tatenen with the First Flower, the Titan-shabti gets smart and Overt Orders her to throw it out the hole in the basement ceiling. Gus and Jack are piling on as well, though, and Jaeda quickly retrieves the First Flower. Tatenen casts Evil Eye on Gus, but Gus counters it by Perfect Partnering, channeling Conviction (his whole \u201cnever give up, especially versus the Titans\u201d thing) and a Legendary Deed \u2014 and gets lucky. Gus strikes a mighty punch with his fenrir-fang cesti that smashes Tatenen back into a squashed clay figurine. All the shabti warriors crumble as well.<br /> <br /> Well, that wasn\u2019t so hard! Holly senses magic still coming from the huge concrete staff, so the Scions collect it along with Zach\u2019s copy of the shabti spell. They tell Zach to let this be a lesson never to mess with Dark Supernatural Powers for kicks, and leave him to explain to his parents how the house got wrecked.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Epilogue</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions defeated the immediate threat, but it bothers them a great deal that an ordinary boy could work powerful magic using only the words of a spell and a modern copy of a magic rod. It probably helped Tatenen that Zach made a copy of a Scion, but he shouldn\u2019t have been able to do magic at all! They can only conclude that the ambient Legend of the town is getting so high that supernatural power is spilling over to mortals.<br /> <br /> The Scions also decide that the Scroll of Tatenen is far too dangerous to leave in the hands of innocent archeologists. Jaeda steals the original from the museum, and does her best to destroy any scans or copies of the manuscript and text. One more doomsday Relic to go with the Sword of Xibalba\u2026<br /> <br /> The magic of Tatenen\u2019s giant staff actually comes from the normal-sized wooden replica staff inside it. The Scions crack open the concrete to find that the wooden made-in-Cairo staff has transformed into stone. It is now a Relic for channeling the Earth and Magic Purviews. It incidentally has the combat stats of a macana, for what that\u2019s worth.<br /> <br /> (Sorry \u2014 It is <i>not </i>a quauhol\u2026 a big honkin\u2019 mace like I first said. I didn\u2019t realize this created the combination of high-damage weapon + Warrior\u2019s Mana raising damage even further + Relic Piercing + Fight With Your Head reducing the target\u2019s soak even further, and that combination <i>just ain\u2019t gonna happen</i>. You\u2019ll have to settle for a Bashing weapon that\u2019s already Speed 4, +1 Accuracy.)<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-06T17:35:32-08:00","id":1027942,"title":"","content":"<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-size:24px\"><b>Nemean Corgi<br /> <br /> <img src=\"http://d21vu35cjx7sd4.cloudfront.net/dims3/MMAH/thumbnail/645x380/quality/90/?url=http%3A%2F%2Fs3.amazonaws.com%2Fassets.prod.vetstreet.com%2F04%2Ffc49b0a72b11e0a0d50050568d634f%2Ffile%2FCardigan-Welsh-Corgi-2-645mk062311.jpg\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/></b></span><br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-07T15:50:50-08:00","id":1028302,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 18: MODEL STUDENTS<br /> <br /> As usual, the Scions\u2019 lives keep them all busy. It\u2019s late April, the weather is getting warmer (when it isn\u2019t raining), and the end of the school year is in sight \u2014 which means some teachers start pushing the pace of class. Kids are also busy finishing various projects in their school-supported clubs, especially the students who need to fill a community service or activity requirement. For instance, Teens Against Tobacco Use has wallpapered the halls with earnest homemade posters about the dangers of smoking. Relationships require maintenance as always.<br /> <br /> Jack is out of town for this episode. His mother is spending a week as one of the divers on a marine biology research trip, and she brought Jack along. Jack protested that he could take care of himself for a week. His mother disagreed. She does not want to return to find, say, the house burned down. Jack finds the lack of faith in his judgment hurtful and insulting, but probably accurate considering his life as a Scion.<br /> <br /> Jaeda now has a gumberoo-leather trenchcoat as a gift from Gus. (Relic *, +1L/2B soak). She admits it\u2019s more practical (and wearable in public) than the catsuit she would have made.<br /> <br /> The most important news, though, may be that Suttung and Hyndla Vetterson are back from their trip to seek a colony of frost giants in British Columbia. They did not return alone\u2026<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: Giant Revelations</u></b><br /> <br /> GUS learns the score when he runs into Reese Vetterson at the mall. Two young giants accompany her. Both are about the same age as Reese and Gary, but they are obviously giants to Gus\u2019 eyes. Reese introduces them. Norfi, the boy, is a huge, lumpish bruiser, fully as large as Gary. Gunnlod, the girl, could charitably be described as big-boned and would look right at home in a Soviet women\u2019s shot-put team. Reese merely introduces Gus as a boy from another school that she and her brother know. Norfi and Gunnlod, she says, are visiting from Canada and this is their first ever visit to a shopping mall. Reese barely manages to avoid grinding her teeth as she talks.<br /> <br /> Gus perceives that Reese wants to vent. They go to Stick It and Gus springs for a platter of deep-fried, food-like substances on sticks. The two giants dig in. Norfi loves Stick It; Gunnlod sniffs that she could cook it better. Reese pulls Gus away from them and tells how the Vettersons have had two families of <i>hicks</i> as house-guests for the last week. The Canadian giants don\u2019t know about cell phones and Facebook. Heck, they don\u2019t know about showers and indoor plumbing. And Reese\u2019s parents expect her to coach them on fitting in? The worst, though, is that she suspects her parents want more than for her to have fellow giants as friends.<br /> <br /> At that time, Norfi finishes his food and wants more. The pimply-faced young clerk tries explaining that this was not an all-you-can-eat special and if Norfi wants more food, someone must pay for it. Norfi gets a look of stupid cunning and says Gus will pay for it all \u2014 now, and any other time Norfi comes to Stick It.<br /> <br /> Gus finds this excessively generous. Norfi tries intimidating Gus into going along. Bad idea! Gus challenges Norfi to arm-wrestle for it: If Norfi wins, Gus will pick up his tab forever. Norfi takes the bet and Gus beats him with ease. The outraged Norfi realizes that Gus is not mortal and attacks. Gus decides that wrecking the mall fighting this dumb lunk is a bad idea, so he simply grapples Norfi and holds him until he runs out of steam. Reese, meanwhile, lectures the two newcomers that this is the 21<sup>st</sup> century and the USA, people try to get along. The commotion (Norfi\u2019s kind of loud) attracts a mall cop, but he recognizes Gus the Local Sports Hero and is satisfied he has this troublesome kid under control. Reese drags Norfi and Gunnlod away \u2014 Norfi still glowering at Gus.<br /> <br /> JAEDA knows about the new giants in town from Gary\u2019s calls and text messages. Gary is not happy: The Canadian giants are more than a handful, and his parents keep him busy training them. Finally, Suttung decides to move them out of the house and into a motel \u2014 the cheapest motel he can find nearby. Naturally, it is Fated that this is the Motel 6 where Jaeda and her father live. Suttung and Jaeda see each other as he and Gary move the newcomers into their motel rooms. Suttung does a face-palm. Gary rolls his eyes. Then Suttung looks back and forth between Jaeda and the new giants and gets a nasty smile.<br /> <br /> It\u2019s still another day until Gary can devise an excuse to get away from his parents for a date. He and Jaeda park on a lonely Hooverdale side-street and make out. When they shift to talking about Jaeda\u2019s progress in fixing up her Torino, a figure steps out of the shadows. It\u2019s Kwame the suspected were-leopard. (And Jaeda suspects he was watching all along, the perv.)<br /> <br /> Kwame wondered why he wasn\u2019t seeing Gary so much any more, and followed him. Now he knows, and he\u2019s angry. Gary\u2019s doing a Scion? The enemy? And actually seems to love her? That is so wrong! But there\u2019s one way Gary can make things right\u2026<br /> <br /> Friends share. (And yes, Rapacity is Kwame\u2019s highest-rated Dark Virtue.)<br /> <br /> Jaeda watches Gary to see how he reacts. (Jaeda\u2019s gang background means that being passed around is not entirely a new concept to her, and she knows her mother Tlazolt\u00e9otl is not exactly big on monogamy.) Gary reacts by snarling, jumping out of his car, and ripping a small tree from the ground to use as a Kwame-swatter. Kwame backs away quickly. As Gary draws back to throw the tree like a javelin, Kwame blurts angrily, \u201cI don\u2019t know you, man!\u201d before running.<br /> <br /> HOLLY, meanwhile, plays Plucky Girl Detective with Cole and Landon. She figured out that surly Mr. Burton is hiding something nefarious in his machine shop, and she wants to find out what. The machine shop is locked up tight. Landon could use his mad shinobi skills to pick a lock and disarm alarms, but he just motions for the others to wait and shadow-walks inside. A moment later he unlocks a side door and bows them in. Holly sees a shuriken in the burglar alarm box\u2026 and Mr. Burton coming through a door, carrying a shotgun.<br /> <br /> Holly acts first, of course. She starts by kicking Burton in the stomach, but she fails to knock him out. Landon decides to intimidate Burton into submission by cutting his shotgun in half. Unfortunately, this merely results in Burton firing a sawed-off shotgun when Cole tackles him. The shot barely misses Cole. The three athletic teens quickly subdue Burton. The shotgun blast alerted neighbors, though, and the police will arrive soon. The three kids beat a hasty retreat.<br /> <br /> Holly chews out her partners with a mix of anger, fear and relief. She tells Landon that his stunt was worse than useless. De-shinobified, he hangs his head and apologizes that he\u2019s still new at this. Then Holly rounds on Cole and asks what he was thinking, charging a man with a shotgun. He could have been killed! Unabashed, Cole says he was thinking that if he didn\u2019t die, Holly would be so impressed (or relieved) that she would smother him with kisses. Holly admits that, well, yes, that\u2019s coming, but she\u2019s still upset and she doesn\u2019t want him to die.<br /> <br /> (As it happens, the newspapers the next day tell how the police responded to a call about a gunshot at Burton\u2019s machine shop. The cops found the marijuana-growing operation in a hidden room, which explained why the machine shop kept drawing power when the machines weren\u2019t running. Taken away to jail, Burton growled, \u201cI would have gotten away with it too, if it weren\u2019t for those meddling kids!\u201d But he can\u2019t identify Holly and Cole, and including a ninja is just crazy talk.)<br /> <br /> Holly decides to bite the bullet and use Prophecy: What will happen if she continues her relationship to Cole? And the words that emerge from her prophetic rune-song chill her to the bone. She foresees Cole hanging himself, because he and Holly finally have sex \u2014 and then Cole discovers that he is Holly\u2019s half-brother.<br /> <br /> Oh, crap. Cole is bound into the story of the legendary Finnish hero Kullervo, who also had poor impulse control, banged his long-lost sister without knowing who it was, and ended up hanging himself for it. How is it that Holly has a half-brother? She doesn\u2019t know yet.<br /> <br /> The scene ends with Holly pondering what to do next. But she won\u2019t simply tell Cole right away. No, the daughter of Louhi must ponder every angle on how she can use the situation\u2026<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-07T15:52:16-08:00","id":1028307,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 18: MODEL STUDENTS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Feet of Clay</u></b><br /> <br /> Gus finds he has more important things to think about than giants. Spring means football practice starts again and picking next year\u2019s team. The day after Gus encounters Norfi, Coach Reagan calls Gus into his office to discuss a very important choice.<br /> <br /> The team needs a captain. With Warren graduating, the two best players are Gus and Jack, by a wide margin. Jack is popular with the other players, but he lacks seriousness of purpose. Gus has focus, but he challenged Coach\u2019s authority and Coach does not easily forgive that. So Coach Reagan puts the question bluntly: Will Gus back Coach in leading the team? Gus says yes, but does not back down from his earlier deeds. He says that with him as captain, Coach Reagan will never <i>need </i>to call for a dirty play, no matter who they play against. Coach Reagan grudgingly accepts that and provisionally appoints Gus as next year\u2019s team captain.<br /> <br /> Which makes this a terrible day for Lieutenant Picinich to pull Gus from class and arrest him for assault and robbery.<br /> <br /> Um, what?<br /> <br /> At the police station, Picinich gloats. Gus has finally crossed the line and a Sobeck is going to get what he deserves. Robbing that Circuit City, taking game consoles, other high-end electronic gear, CDs and DVDs, punching and shoving anyone who got in his way \u2014 all caught on multiple security cameras \u2014 was the dumbest bit of arrogance Picinich<br /> has ever seen. All the money in the world won\u2019t keep Gus out of jail! But\u2026 Picinich might go easy on Gus if he informs on Jaeda. The cop clearly thinks Jaeda is the Avalon Slasher, or at least criminal in some way. Gus gives nothing.<br /> <br /> Gus calls the family lawyer, of course, and thinks furiously. The robbery took place when he was at the mall, working out at Brawn\u2019s Gym. Cameras must have caught him entering and leaving the mall, giving him an alibi. The lawyer points this out. Picinich counters with his belief that Gus somehow slipped out of the mall without being seen, sped to Circuit City, robbed it and sped back and re-entered the mall, again somehow avoiding cameras. He just needs time to prove it, and he can hold Gus for at least 24 hours while he looks for it.<br /> <br /> But that\u2019s not the worst. First thing, Gus tries to point out that he could not have robbed the store because he was at Brawn\u2019s Gym, using Benefit of the Doubt to make Picinich believe this true statement. Picinich hesitates in doubt \u2014 but then grits his teeth and ripostes with the theory that Gus somehow left the mall and returned while evading the cameras. Somehow, this mortal beat Gus\u2019 Epic Charisma Social Fu, and <i>that shouldn\u2019t happen</i>.<br /> <br /> By the end of the school day, everyone in school knows about the arrest. Holly and Landon discuss it as they leave school and head for their cars. The conversation is interrupted when a giant eagle swoops down, grabs Landon and tries to carry him off.<br /> <br /> At night, this would not be a problem. Landon would turn into a shinobi and teleport away. He can\u2019t do this during the day.<br /> <br /> Holly darts to her car and pulls out the Colt Peacemaker she now hides in it. Marshall, just emerging from school, also races to fight the monster. Unfortunately, they soon find that guns are useless against the Nemean monster, while Marshall isn\u2019t strong enough to grapple it. The eagle carries Landon away. Holly, however, has time to cast The Unlidded Eye to see that the eagle is really a powerful shabti creation, just like the Nemean Corgi the Scions fought in the previous adventure. She also casts Ariadne\u2019s Thread so she can follow the eagle wherever it goes. She finishes by calling an alert to all the other Scions. Jaeda and Marshall pile into Holly\u2019s Lotus and speed after the eagle.<br /> <br /> Barbara, meanwhile, decides the new threat makes it imperative to spring Gus from the police station. She shows up and uses Social Chameleon and Epic Socials to present herself as a lawyer with the paperwork to release Gus. The Scion of Shango works fast and gets Gus out the door before the buffaloed cops notice the legal papers she gave them are actually forms she grabbed at random from desks. Gus and Barbara drive after the others, guided by cell phone. Unfortunately, Holly, Jaeda and Marshall have a long head start.<br /> <br /> As Holly &amp; co. enter the not-that-nearby town of Monroe, the Ariadne\u2019s Thread link to the eagle ends. Most likely, she realizes, the eagle reverted back to a clay figurine. Feeling foolish, she re-casts Ariadne\u2019s Thread with Landon as the target, and regains the magical guidance. The spell leads them to a foreclosed house in Monroe with a window (and part of the wall) broken out. The three Scions decide that time is of the essence and they can\u2019t wait for Gus.<br /> <br /> (I deeply apologize for screwing up the adventure timing. On reflection, I should have arranged for <i>something </i>to slow the Scions or speed Gus and Barbara, so they all arrived together. Like, um, Barbara could have phoned Delia and had her Prophecy where the eagle would take Landon, so they could take a shortcut and get there quicker.)<br /> <br /> The three Scions charge in to find Landon held by two of several shabti warriors identical to those Tatenen made. They also find Zach, Bennie and two horse-sized Nemean leopards. Zach holds the stone <i>uas </i>staff the Scions had left in Jack\u2019s care. Holly can see he\u2019s changed: Zach is now a person of Legend, a witch empowered by the Titans, as Acacia had been.<br /> <br /> As if Zach weren\u2019t in enough trouble already, he looks at Holly in surprise and says, \u201cHoney? What are you doing \u2014 oh!\u201d Holly experiences new heights of icy rage as she realizes Zach not only disobeyed the Scions\u2019 earlier command about never using the spell again, he made another copy of her! Giving full reign to her Vengeance, she vows that Zach will die. But then Zach just points at the Scions and says, \u201cAttack!\u201d The warriors and the Nemean leopards do so.<br /> <br /> In most ways, the fight goes badly. The Scions lack most of their weapons and armor, and the Nemean leopards are powerful and fearless. Holly orders one shabti warrior to attack another, freeing Landon, and Landon grabs Bennie to try escaping the house. Marshall finds the huge leopards are no easier to clinch or harm by gun or K-BAR combat knife than the eagle was. Only Jaeda can harm them, and the beasts are too tough for the First Flower to kill them quickly. The shabti warriors can be slain, though.<br /> <br /> Holly focuses on Zach. She zigs and zags through the scrum to kick Zach in the stomach. Holly isn\u2019t strong enough to knock him out in one blow, but she inflicts enough damage that he retreats to the next room and grabs an eagle figurine. He taps it with the staff and commands it to come to life and carry him to safety. Zach also yells for Bennie to come on, he\u2019ll get him away too. Bennie yells back, \u201cNo way!\u201d and lets Landon pull him out of the house.<br /> <br /> Holly then switches to her gun, retrieved from Marshall, and shoots Zach in the gut, sending him unconscious. That doesn\u2019t stop the figurine from turning into a Nemean eagle, smashing out the wall of the house and taking flight, Zach held in its claws. Holly shoots Zach again, killing him. The eagle reverts to a clay statue. So do the two leopards. The remaining warriors, however, fight on. Zach falls to the lawn, crushing the eagle figurine. Holly follows and puts another bullet in his head, just to be sure.<br /> <br /> By the time Gus and Barbara arrive a few minutes later, the warriors are all destroyed and the Scions have pulled Zach\u2019s body out of open sight. There\u2019s nothing for Gus to do but call his crocodile to eat the body. The Scions then skedaddle before the commotion attracts attention. Going through Etna on the way home, they see some students from Teens Against Tobacco Use picketing a convenience store that sells cigarettes to minors. Lucky kids, to have no worse evil to worry about.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-07T15:53:29-08:00","id":1028308,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 18: MODEL STUDENTS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Confusion To\u2026 Well, Everybody</u></b><br /> <br /> Once the Scions are safely away, they wonder what the hell is going on? Bennie can fill in some of it. Guibourg abducted them both and told them to summon Tatenen again. They didn\u2019t have the scroll, but Guibourg compelled Bennie to remember the words. Guibourg tried using a new <i>uas </i>staff but that didn\u2019t work, so Guibourg had undisclosed agents steal the stone staff from Jack\u2019s house. Then he talked Zach into making a new shabti of Holly and animating it, calling Tatenen \u2014 well, an avatar of Tatenen \u2014 back into the World. It wasn\u2019t hard for Tatenen to seduce Zach into a pact, selling his soul to the Titan.<br /> <br /> Bennie adds that Guibourg captured him and Zach days ago, but one of the first things Guibourg had Zach do is create shabti doubles to take their place. Zach decided on his own to kidnap Landon just so he could brag about how he\u2019s become powerful and cool. Zach never knew that Landon is a shinobi. (Actually, Bennie still doesn\u2019t.)<br /> <br /> Holly decides it\u2019s time to use Mystery and find what\u2019s going on. In hopes that she can suss some pattern to all that\u2019s going on, she channels her Intellect, invokes a Legendary Deed and asks the inscrutable, all-knowing power of Fate:<br /> <br /> * <i>Is the Gus-shabti still active?</i> Yes.<br /> <br /> * <i>What are Tatenen\u2019s current intentions?</i> To crush the Scions.<br /> <br /> * <i>In all, what shabti-doubles did Zach create?</i> Zach himself, Bennie, Gus, Holly, and two other kids the Scions don\u2019t know. (Their names mean nothing to Holly).<br /> <br /> * <i>Which are still active?</i> All of them.<br /> <br /> * <i>How does Picinich resist Gus\u2019 Social-Fu?</i> His badge is enchanted.<br /> <br /> * <i>All this seems like a distraction. A distraction from what?</i> Nothing. False assumption.<br /> <br /> * <i>What are Suttung\u2019s intentions with bringing the new giants to town?</i> Matchmaking. To kill all the Scions, if he sees a chance.<br /> <br /> * <i>What is Tatenen\u2019s current base of operations?</i> Guibourg\u2019s house.<br /> <br /> Well, at least it doesn\u2019t look like everyone is working together in a Master Plan\u2026<br /> <br /> The Scions decide their next step should be to take the Relic badge away from Lt. Picinich. It\u2019s not hard to guess that Guibourg had Zach send out the shabti-Gus and protected Picinich as a way to harass Gus without physical violence, and maybe even force him to leave town. Gus declines to play along. He\u2019s going home, where he knows Picinich will arrest him again. He will play dumb, that as far as he knew his release was legitimate. And then tomorrow, when Picinich is no longer protected by his magic badge, Social Fu and the Sobecks\u2019 lawyer will get him out.<br /> <br /> The scenario plays out as Gus expects. Picinich is waiting at the Sobeck house, doing everything he legally can to show Gus\u2019 father how much he hates the Sobecks. He arrests Gus and takes him back to the police station lockup. The drunk-and-disorderly who shares the cell does not try to trouble the strapping football hero. That night, Landon slips into Picinich\u2019s home and steals his badge. It\u2019s easy for the young shinobi. The Scions don\u2019t worry much. Even if the cop wakes up and sees Landon, what\u2019s he going to do? Say that a ninja took his badge?<br /> <br /> The next morning, Gus appears before a judge for possible arraignment. While the Circuit City surveillance video is undeniable, the evidence that Gus couldn\u2019t have been there is pretty strong, too (and the judge finds Gus\u2019 testimony completely believable). There\u2019s also the fact that the police in Monroe found the stolen goods when they responded to a disturbance at an abandoned house. The judge orders Gus released while the cops investigate further. Gus is back at Avalon High before lunch period.<br /> <br /> Meanwhile, Jaeda and Holly decide they\u2019d better deal with all the shabti doubles they can find. Between classes, they find the shabti doubles of Bennie, Zach and the two other students, lure them out of sight from the other kids, and slit their throats, turning them back into clay. Only Bennie can immediately resume his life. If the two Scions regret adding to the roster of students who\u2019ve disappeared, they feel they cannot leave potential tools of Tatenen running loose.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-07T15:54:39-08:00","id":1028309,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 18: MODEL STUDENTS (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Zombies Against Tobacco Use</u></b><br /> <br /> While the two Scions hunt shabti kids, they encounter Alex Totenbush. The swim team\u2019s resident kushtaka glares at the anti-smoking posters in the halls. He\u2019s offended: Tobacco is a sacred herb, after all, even if ordinary smokers impiously abuse it. But the Scions find they have more important concerns.<br /> <br /> Holly uses the Unlidded Eye as the Scions search for and dispatch the shabti doubles \u2014 and sees that dozens and dozens of students seem to be imbued with some dark force like nothing she\u2019s seen before. As she studies the students passing in the halls, a girl asks her to join Teens Against Tobacco Use. All the leading kids should set a good example for the rest in health, as well as beauty and fashion! It\u2019s Vanessa, the Fashionista who tried to humiliate Mannie Salkind and Holly, and on whom Holly took terrible revenge. And she too is possessed by the strange force. Vanessa reaches for Holly\u2019s hand, so earnest is her appeal\u2026 Holly recoils, intuitively certain that she must not let the Fashionista touch her, and makes an excuse to be elsewhere.<br /> <br /> Holly immediately suspects some form of contagious curse or possession. So, who is Patient Zero? Her first suspicion is Mr. Sycamore, the Health teacher who runs Teens Against Tobacco Use and is a notorious projective hypochondriac. (Every class he teaches, he diagnoses two or three students with Hodgkins lymphoma, cystic fibrosis or some other lethal disease. He has never once been right.) When Holly peeks in on his class she sees that he is infected, but in the same manner as the kids she\u2019s seen. So, Holly finds her pet computer genius Bobby Kilmer and has him search the school records for TATU.<br /> <br /> As she already suspected, very few kids stay in the club for long. They participate for one quarter to fulfill the school\u2019s community service requirement, then quit. In the last week, though, membership jumped so that almost 10% of the student body belongs to the club. And one student, Sally Wright, stands out for having belonged to TATU since the start of the school year. Holly phones all the other Scions to say they have a humungous new problem.<br /> <br /> Jaeda, Gus and Holly get together and head for the class where Sally Wright should be. Jaeda first stops at her locker to get her pack of clove cigarettes, just to be obnoxious to TATU. They find Sally, but also the rival Band and a bunch of other students. \u201cJoin us,\u201d they say. \u201cIt\u2019s the right thing to do.\u201d \u201cYou\u2019ll feel much better,\u201d says a smiling Leo. They advance on the Scions in friendly menace.<br /> <br /> Just because she feels pissed by all this earnest zombiedom, Jaeda pulls out a cigarette and lights it. The possessed rival Scions recoil like movie vampires from a cross. \u201cTake them!\u201d hisses Sally from the midst of the mob.<br /> <br /> The rival Scions are nowhere near the power of the PCs, but they do their best to grapple. Barbara and Delia try using Overt Order to make the Scions stand still, but the Scions have every reason to believe that\u2019s a form of suicide \u2014 it\u2019s an unacceptable order. On the other hand, the Scions don\u2019t want to crush a bunch of innocent possessed kids. All the while, teachers are emerging from classrooms to demand what\u2019s going on and order the kids back to their classes. Possessed students grab them, and a few seconds later they join the mob.<br /> <br /> Jaeda, however, thinks she knows the answer. She lets Leo grab her so they stand face to face. \u201cSorry, Leo,\u201d she says, and leans forward to kiss him on the mouth\u2026 and blow smoke into his lungs. Leo coughs, releases her, staggers and asks, \u201cWhat\u2019s going on?\u201d<br /> <br /> Holly catches on at once. She takes a deep drag from Jaeda\u2019s cigarette to immunize herself from the horde. Then she runs over the heads of the massed TATU zombies to race through the school, calling out to the un-possessed kids, \u201cSmoke \u2018em if you got \u2018em! YOUR LIVES DEPEND ON IT!\u201d A pulled fire alarm increases the confusion and gets the remaining kids out of the school and away from the zombie horde.<br /> <br /> In short order, the Scions all have cigarettes from Jaeda. The rival Scions are freed and join in corralling the zombies. The Scions advance slowly, blowing smoke on the kids one by one to free them from the curse. Eventually, they reach Sally. The teachers are freed, too.<br /> <br /> Holly questions Sally about how she became the vector for possession. Sally turns out to be an intensely earnest girl who found A Purpose In Life through TATU. During Spring Break, her family visited the park around Mount Saint Helens. Somehow, <i>something</i> got into her. Sally felt it growing stronger over the weeks, until she knew she could share it, and her dedication, with others.<br /> <br /> With her incredible erudition and super-genius, Holly finds this actually rings a bell. Pacific Northwest tribes had a legend about evil spirits called Seatco that dwelled in Spirit Lake. (In fact, the lake was named for them.) The Seatco were shapeshifters who lured people into the lake, then killed them and turned them into more Seatco. Only, the eruption of Mount Saint Helens about 30 years ago destroyed Spirit Lake. Maybe, somehow, the Seatco became whatever possessed Sally. At least, Holly doesn\u2019t have any better explanation.<br /> <br /> That\u2019s when Gary calls Jaeda. Things have gone crazy at Etna High. Crazier. A lot of the students seem, well, possessed! The principal \u2014 whom the Scions know to be Guibourg \u2014 has barricaded himself in his office. The Scions say a variety of bad words as they remember that Mr. Sycamore teaches Health and runs the TATU chapter at Etna as well as Avalon High.<br /> <br /> The Scions leave Holly (now out of Legend) and the rival Band to finish exorcising the Avalon student body. Holly suggests that saying the whole thing was guerilla theater might make a good cover story. In a few minutes, Gus and Jaeda are at Etna. Reese is among the possessed. Gus can\u2019t resist sweeping her into a kiss to blow smoke into her lungs. Gary glowers. \u201cHey man,\u201d Gus says. \u201cYou\u2019re in no position to complain about a Scion kissing a giant.\u201d Gary snarls that he\u2019s in every position to object about <i>Gus </i>kissing <i>his sister</i>. Gus shrugs, smiles and says that as far as he\u2019s concerned, Reese is still Gene Blackforest\u2019s girl, and he doesn\u2019t poach.<br /> <br /> The task is easier at Etna than Scion: A lot more kids there smoke. At last, the Scions break down the door to Guibourg\u2019s office. It is the first time they have seen their nemesis. He is a tall, distinguished-looking man in late middle age, the very image of a Dedicated Educator. He\u2019s also dressed in a ceremonial robe and setting a variety of weird and grisly relics around a circle chalked on his office floor, clearly about to call up some dire entity from beyond. The Scions immediately snap pictures on their phones.<br /> <br /> Guibourg hastily cleans up the evidence and locks his magical tools in a cabinet. He doesn\u2019t exactly thank the Scions, but he compliments them on their quick thinking. Jaeda shrugs in elaborate nonchalance. It was only an infestation of mutant Native American lake specters, after all. It was kind of <i>obvious</i> how to deal with them. Guibourg smiles thinly and suggests they should return to their classes in Avalon. The Scions do so, satisfied that they have saved Avalon from political correctness run amok.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Epilogue</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions find no real explanation for the TATU possession. They guess it\u2019s just another symptom of the force of Legend growing in the area.<br /> <br /> Tatenen is still on the loose. The Scions haven\u2019t found the Gus-shabti, either. At least they know Tatenen is staying near Guibourg. She seems unable to animate powerful shabti, since she had Zach animate the Nemean beasts. At a guess, though, Zach was never really the source of the power that animated the powerful shabti: His use of the spell was just a sort of beacon or conduit for the real Tatenen, and the real Titan cannot channel its full power through a mere shabti copy of itself. Apparently Guibourg couldn\u2019t take over the job either, and Zach was bound as the only person who could release Tatenen\u2019s power into the World. With him dead and the rod that was originally used to invoke the power back in the Scions\u2019 hands, the shabti Tatenen should not be able to create anything more powerful than the shabti warriors. They hope! The Scions place the rod in a safe-deposit box, just as they did with the Scroll of Tatenen and the Sword of Xibalba.<br /> <br /> The Scions debate whether to spread the pictures of Guibourg in Satanic Sorcerer drag as a way to get him disgraced and fired. For now, they decide it probably wouldn\u2019t work. Guibourg is no slouch at mental and social manipulation. Anyway, he could discredit the pictures with one word: \u201cPhotoshop.\u201d<br /> <br /> The experience with shabti creatures helps Gus learn the Shabti Boon (Heku *** from my rewrite of the Purview.)<br /> <br /> The Scions agree to send the Scroll of Tatenen to Louhi as a way to make sure Tatenen can\u2019t get it and use it to create mightier shabti creatures.<br /> <br /> For some time, Jaeda has felt bad that the only way she seems able to help people is by killing stuff. She especially wants to help Leo stop hating himself. Leo\u2019s Nature is Wretch: He loathes himself, thinks he deserves to be treated like crap, and recovers Willpower when people do so. Based on a talk with her mother, Jaeda decides to try using Social Fu to (in game terms) change Leo\u2019s Nature. She plans to keep hitting him with Blessing of Importance and Lasting Impression to make him feel good about himself, with Willpower points as the game-terms reward for doing so. Jaeda hopes that by, in effect, simulating him having a different Nature she can make it so.<br /> <br /> Finally, Gus wonders about Picinich\u2019s beef with his family. All his father can say is that brother Gav had wild adventures when the two of them were young, and sometimes it got them both in trouble. At that time, Picinich was a young cop in Avalon. Picinich could never make anything stick against Gav, though. Apparently, he still holds a grudge. Gus, however, suspects that something more personal lies behind the cop\u2019s hatred. What did Gav <i>do </i>to the guy?<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-08T16:02:11-08:00","id":1028727,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 19: Bad Neighbors, Part One<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: An Interesting Evening</u></b><br /> <br /> JACK: While eating lunch in the cafeteria, Jack notices Barbara frequently checking her watch. Naturally, he butts in and asks why. Barbara is evasive, but after checking her watch one last time she accosts Vice-Principal Stone about some bit of student government business that they need to take care of. She leaves with him, Jack trailing behind. As they pass a janitor\u2019s closet, they hear as small scream. Stone opens the closet and sees a tearful Delia Bright, clothes in disarray, and an English teacher named Mr. Renfrew with his hands on her. \u201cHe forced me \u2014 He threatened \u2014\u201c Delia sobs, and glares at Renfrew. The teacher backs away, spluttering incoherently. Stone grabs Renfrew and drags him away, in front of dozens of witnesses.<br /> <br /> Jack asks what that was about. Barbara explains that Renfrew is a witch that Guibourg recruited and initiated into the service of the Titans. He\u2019d been playing nasty magical and mind games with his students and their parents. Barbara and Delia worked out a sting. Delia \u2014 suddenly dry-eyed \u2014 produces Renfrew\u2019s Relic, a key chain bearing a small, black glass egg. Barbara says it might not be enough to put Renfrew in jail as a sexual predator, but he\u2019s out of the school and his capacity for mischief is reduced by loss of his Relic. So, does Jack still want that date she promised him? She\u2019ll drive.<br /> <br /> To end his grounding, Jack must promise his mother that Barbara is a good girl. Responsible, in student government, top student, etc. He dresses up nice. His mother grudgingly gives permission. So naturally, Barbara turns up at the Thompsons\u2019 door wearing a gumberoo-leather catsuit and a studded leather collar, with a riding crop in her hand. She archly tells Ms. Thompson not to worry, she knows how to deal with naughty boys. Jack hurries to the car before his mother recovers the power of speech.<br /> <br /> For their date, Jack decided to take Barbara to Seattle\u2019s Basque restaurant, one of only five or six such restaurants in the US. (Yes, it\u2019s a real place. It\u2019s a converted home with several small dining rooms.) To exercise his burgeoning super-genius, Jack learned Basque because it was one of the most difficult languages in the World, and by the Gods, he wants to get some use from it! The staff are so pleased that Jack speaks Basque that they ignore Barbara\u2019s garb and give the couple a private dining room. Jack\u2019s wits and savoir-faire are put to the test as Barbara plays with her food, asks embarrassing questions, pinches the waiter\u2019s butt, and generally does her worst to make this the Date From Hell. When the dinner\u2019s over, the staff asks Jack to please come again. <i>Alone</i>.<br /> <br /> On the way home, Barbara stops at a mini-mart for refreshments. Naturally, there\u2019s trouble \u2014 but she and Jack expected there would be, so they both brought their Relics. Inside, one of the town\u2019s new frost giants is losing his temper with the store clerk, who is trying to get his shotgun from under the counter. The adult giant (the aptly-named Jokull) tried to buy beer and food using (stolen) plastic, but he doesn\u2019t know which card to use, or how, and is about to trash the store. Jack decides he doesn\u2019t really want a fight, though. He pays for Jokull\u2019s groceries, chivvies him out of the mini-mart, and tells the clerk that it\u2019s <i>really dumb </i>to antagonize someone that big over beer, chips and cocktail pepperoni.<br /> <br /> Back home, Jack\u2019s mom has recovered the power of speech. Jack needs all his powers of persuasion to convince his mother that he and Barbara went to a costume party \u2014 he dressed up as an accountant! She barely believes him. All in all, Jack thinks it was a pretty good date.<br /> <br /> JAEDA was also in the cafeteria that day, but she left early because Leo wanted to talk to her in private. When they\u2019re alone, he nerves himself and asks Jaeda if she needs help getting away from Gary. He knows she\u2019s dated him, but he also noticed how she smiles at him and seems to care about him. And he cares about her, too. In fact, he\u2026 (GULP) loves her. And if Gary is threatening her in any way to make her stay with him, he\u2019ll do anything to help her get free.<br /> <br /> Jaeda recognizes that her attempts to alter Leo\u2019s personality using Blessing of Importance and Lasting Impression have not worked the way she hoped. She also recognizes that Leo has just tossed his fragile, spun-glass heart to her, and the slightest fumble will crush it, and him. She replies that she does care about him as a friend, and if she hadn\u2019t started dating Gary she\u2019d be proud to become more than friends \u2014 but she loves Gary (Jaeda realizes this is true; it\u2019s the first time she\u2019s said this even to herself), and she can\u2019t be true to Leo if she\u2019s false to Gary. Jaeda pushes all her Epic Charisma into her declaration, plus channeling Loyalty <i>and </i>applying a Legendary Deed: This matters <i>immensely</i> to her. Leo smiles, says he\u2019s lucky to have such a good and honest friend, and leaves feeling better about himself than, likely, ever.<br /> <br /> After school, Jaeda works her shift at Big Cups and returns home to the Motel 6. She, her father and the town\u2019s new giants (the Eismanns and the Hrimsteds) are now the only residents. Maybe the giants\u2019 barbecue pit and the beginnings of a crude blacksmith\u2019s forge scare away new guests. The Vettersons\u2019 big SUV is in the parking lot, though, and Jaeda hears loud, angry voices coming from the Eismann\u2019s suite. She sneaks closer to hear Suttung Vetterson chewing out the Eismanns \u2014 something about paying for their damages, and more to keep the matter quiet, and this is not why he brought them from Canada! He stomps out with a parting snarl of \u201cGet a job!\u201d and slams the door so hard it breaks. He notices Jaeda but just grumbles that at least she works.<br /> <br /> The Eismanns call in their neighbor Starkad Hrimsted (the blacksmith) to fix the door. Jaeda tries to sneak close enough to eavesdrop on the giants talking without being seen, but does not notice the Hrimsted\u2019s daughter Gunnlod approaching. (A <i>botched </i>Perception roll.) Gunnlod dresses Jaeda down for eavesdropping and warns her to stay away.<br /> <br /> HOLLY ends her lunch hour by heading for the signup sheet for the school\u2019s talent show, due to take place that Friday. She intends to win it. At the signup sheet, though, she encounters Delia Bright \u2014 fresh from getting Renfrew fired \u2014 who also intends to enter and win. Delia has a favor to ask. In fact, she\u2019s cashing in the favor Holly owes her for a bit of Prophecy several episodes back. Delia asks Holly not to enter. Holly smiles and says of course, anything for a friend. And silently vows revenge!<br /> <br /> As soon as possible, she meets Jack and drafts him for her plan: to meet all the genuinely talented performers in school and convince them <i>not</i> to enter. Delia will win, but her victory will be <i>meaningless.</i> What glory in flaunting divine talent, when there\u2019s no competition in the first place? Jack agrees to help, sensing that to refuse could be bad for him.<br /> <br /> After school, Holly decides to take a look at Guibourg\u2019s house. So that any of the warlock\u2019s minions who might be watching won\u2019t spot her, she disguises herself using her new Illusion Boon, Stolen Face (channeled through Santa Troll\u2019s heart \u2014 obtained in Episode 12). She has the Unlidded Eye running while she strolls past the ordinary-looking, two-story house. As she expects, Guibourg\u2019s house carries a Ward to keep out Scions.<br /> <br /> GUS has a less than pleasant day. It starts with Coach Reagan expressing his concern about Gus\u2019 recent arrest. Coach warns that even for a player of Gus\u2019 caliber, <i>repeated </i>arrests could be too much of a scandal and jeopardize his becoming team captain.<br /> <br /> Later, Gus sees Marshall blow up at Barbara. The Scion of Huitzilopochtli growls that he\u2019s sick of taking orders from civilians and girls, and stomps off. Gus tries talking to Marshall, and Marshall adds that the other Scions disgust him for tolerating the Vettersons. In fact, he calls Gus a traitor for attacking him in order to stop the rival Band\u2019s attack. And now there are six more giants in town! Way to go!<br /> <br /> Finally, Gus\u2019 Fashionista-assigned girlfriend Lindsey says she feels neglected. Gus needs to take her on a nice, high-visibility date as a way to flaunt their status to the other students. Gus considers himself still honor-bound by the pact between Bands (and their respective social sets) that Holly and Barbara worked out, so he agrees to take Lindsey to the Public Pool two nights hence. But these are mere irritations.<br /> <br /> At about 3 a.m., Gus awakens to pounding on the Sobecks\u2019 front door. Looking out the window, he sees cop cars. Approaching the door, he hears Lieutenant Picinich demanding that someone open the door or he\u2019ll break it down.<br /> <br /> Gus makes sure he reaches the door before his father, and opens it. Picinich screams for Gus to get down on his knees with his hands behind his head, he\u2019s under arrest! His gun is drawn. Picinich raves that Gus has finally done himself in, he has <i>pictures </i>of Gus trashing Picinich\u2019s car less than an hour ago! Gus protests that he was home asleep the whole time. Picinich sneers that Gus could have snuck out past the house\u2019s burglar alarms, reached the cop\u2019s home, smash up the car with a baseball bat and returned in time to slip back inside with no one else the wiser.<br /> <br /> Gus decides he\u2019s had enough of this harassment. He glares at the cop and hits him with Game Face. No longer protected by a talisman, Picinich drops his gun, gives a strangled cry, and runs out the door. The cops he brought as backup complete the arrest, though they permit Gus a few minutes to get dressed.<br /> <br /> At the police station, Gus sees the pictures Picinich took. Sure enough, the pictures look just like him, beating on Picinich\u2019s car. They put Gus in the lockup with a street person who wakes up and makes sure to stay as far from Gus as possible.<br /> <br /> Gus\u2019 father joins him at the station and says he thinks he knows why Picinich hates the family so much. It was more than the way Gus\u2019 uncle Gav kept getting in trouble, but Picinich could never pin anything on him. Picinich had a fianc\u00e9e. A week before the wedding, the young cop caught his fianc\u00e9e in bed with the underage Gav. She dumped Picinich and left town a week or so later (though not with Gav; Gus\u2019 father doesn\u2019t know what became of her, or even remember her name). It was enough of a scandal that it played a part in Gav\u2019s decision to join the Marines instead of finishing high school. Gav is long dead, but Gus looks an awful lot like him. Gus assures his father that despite what the pictures suggest, he did not trash Picinich\u2019s car. Rather, he suggests that Picinich is losing his mind.<br /> <br /> Gus also gets an unexpected visit from Guibourg. The warlock principal of Etna High has a warning for Gus: Leave town. In fact, Guibourg wants all the Scions to leave town. Guibourg knows he lacks the power to kill Gus \u2014 but he can make life hard for all the Scions. He can also do very bad things to the mortals that the Scions care about. Gus defies the warlock.<br /> <br /> Through heroic effort, the Sobeck family\u2019s lawyer manages to get Gus in front of a judge the next morning. Since Picinich is still hiding in terror somewhere, Gus and the family lawyer have no trouble getting Gus free on bail, but it\u2019s expensive and the charges still stand: Gus doesn\u2019t have the Social Fu to make a judge flat-out ignore evidence and ignore the law, no matter how sympathetic the judge feels. Gus is released in time for the second half of the school day, but there will be a trial.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-08T16:03:41-08:00","id":1028729,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 19: Bad Neighbors, Part One (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: Additional Shocking Revelations</u></b><br /> <br /> The next day, the Scions agree they must do something about Guibourg, the shabti double of Gus, and Tatenen \u2014 but what?<br /> <br /> The realization that at least two Scions have doubles running around prompts Jack to reevaluate Barbara\u2019s challenging behavior the night before, and her subdued manner and avoidance of him today. He asks Holly to check Barbara using the Unlidded Eye, and Holly confirms that it\u2019s really Barbara, but she also finds fading traces of magic on the Scion of Chango. It feels\u2026 almost Pesedjet. Holly remembers Gus telling her that Heku includes a power to alter people\u2019s personalities, but of course Gus would never do such a thing for mischief. Tatenen might, however, and the Titan-shabti already showed she had the Heku power to create low-power shabti of her own. Hurrying, Holly finds similar traces of magic on Marshall but not on any other Scions. In particular, not on Leo. Jack tries talking to Marshall about this, but the Scion of Huitzilopochtli refuses to concede that he said or did anything he did not really mean. Barbara, however, quickly remembers a meeting with Holly that the real Holly did not attend.<br /> <br /> Immediately after school, Holly turns to the power of Mystery to learn more about the warlock\u2019s capabilities and plans. As usual, Holly channels Intellect and makes this a Legendary Deed. She learns:<br /> <br /> * <i>What connection or coordination is there between Guibourg and Suttung Vetterson? </i>None. They are working separately.<br /> <br /> * <i>What is Guibourg\u2019s current objective?</i> Recruiting and initiating witches and warlocks to serve the Titans.<br /> <br /> * <i>How many has Guibourg initiated? </i>Ten. Uh-oh\u2026<br /> <br /> * <i>Which Titan does Guibourg personally serve?</i> Darkness. (The Scions guess he and the Black Bodhisattva are buddies.)<br /> <br /> * <i>What creature is summoned by Guibourg\u2019s death? </i>A Gyuki \u2014 another monstrous creature of the Darkness Titan.<br /> <br /> * <i>Where is Tatenen\u2019s current base of operations?</i> Guibourg\u2019s house.<br /> <br /> * <i>What is the current location of Gus\u2019 shabti double? </i>Guibourg\u2019s house.<br /> <br /> * <i>What defenses are on Guibourg\u2019s house, besides wards and Tatenen\u2019s shabti warriors? </i>Baka \u2014 a spirit of darkness from Voodoo legends. It looks like a black dog with horns.<br /> <br /> * <i>What is Barbara\u2019s favorite music?</i> \u2018Weird Al\u2019 Yankovic. (Jack slipped this question into the list and Holly permitted it.)<br /> <br /> * <i>What made Tatenen leave Guibourg\u2019s house?</i> Tatenen\u2019s use of Bind the Heart on Barbara feels improvisational to the Scions \u2014 something done on the spur of the moment because she saw a chance to make trouble, rather than a deep-laid plan. So what was her real goal at the time? Sure enough, Tatenen\u2019s primary goal was\u2026 seducing Cole Thomasun.<br /> <br /> Oh, crap.<br /> <br /> The Scions agree that for a start, they must save Cole from Holly\u2019s imposter before he does something they will all regret. Then they must do something about Tatenen before she uses imposters to ruin all their lives. But how can they attack Tatenen when the Titan-shabti can hide behind Guibourg\u2019s ward? Well, they know someone who\u2019s pretty damn tough but isn\u2019t a Scion\u2026<br /> <br /> A few quick calls bring Gary Vetterson and Cole to a meeting in Hooverdale with Gus and Jaeda. (The Scions choose the ruins of the unfinished big-box store where they fought djinn back in Episode 9.) Cole is surprised that the girl who finally said \u2018yes\u2019 and gave him the best evening of his life wasn\u2019t really Holly. And he\u2019d been so hopeful, too, when \u201cHolly\u201d said she\u2019d found a way for him to power up and become part of the Big Game of Scions and Titans, she just needed a day or two to pull together what she needed\u2026 Finally, he feels ill when the Scions reveal that somehow \u2014 they aren\u2019t sure of the details, but they know it to be true \u2014 Cole is Holly\u2019s half-brother. Fate was trying to make him replay the legend of Kullervo, who had sex with his long-lost sister and killed himself. (And Louhi knew it all along, the bitch. That\u2019s probably what she meant about Cole being a lesson for Holly.)<br /> <br /> Gary, on the other hand, likes the idea of invading his school principal\u2019s house to commit mayhem. Gus asking him to kill his double is even better! Gary, Gus, Jaeda and Cole take Gary\u2019s car to Guibourg\u2019s house. They swing by the Vetterson house so Gary can get his axe. Gary parks a few blocks away from Guibourg\u2019s so The Car won\u2019t be seen anywhere nearby to implicate him in the coming violence. Everyone agrees they\u2019d better do the assault immediately: Holly is tracking Guibourg using Ariadne\u2019s Thread, so she knows he isn\u2019t home yet (in fact he\u2019s getting groceries \u2014 even diabolical servants of the Titans need to eat), but he will come home soon.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-08T16:05:06-08:00","id":1028730,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 19: Bad Neighbors, Part One (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: BOOM</u></b><br /> <br /> Gary, Gus and Jaeda soon reach Guibourg\u2019s house. Just to be sure, Gus tries haymakering the ward with all his might, while Jaeda stabs with the First Flower. Their Legend does not exceed Guibourg\u2019s, and so they can\u2019t push through. Gary, however, swaggers through and breaks down Guibourg\u2019s front door. Cole follows \u2014 he can\u2019t fight Titan-shabti and demon spirits, but he thinks he can keep the ordinary shabti warriors off Gary\u2019s back.<br /> <br /> Sounds of a ruckus come from the house. Gus\u2019 severed head comes smashing through a window, turning to clay as it falls. A few seconds later, however, Gary comes crashing through the wall of the house to slam onto the lawn. \u201cBitch is strong,\u201d he says, but flashes Jaeda a reckless fighting grin and charges back into the house. The Scions realize that this Tatenen may be just as tough as the one they faced before, and call Holly and Jack to join them as soon as possible.<br /> <br /> Jack and Holly jump in her Lotus. Jack has the best Epic Attributes and skills to drive the car, so Holly lets him take the wheel. (Anyway, if Fate intends to wreck her Lotus because of Jack, it won\u2019t matter if he drives or not.) Jack channels his Atuan Loyalty and applies a Legendary Deed to the task of reaching his friends as quickly as possible. The nimble sports car soon goes beyond two-wheel turns to skipping over the roofs of other cars at 120 miles per hour.<br /> <br /> Back at Guibourg\u2019s house, Cole suddenly jumps out a window, screaming, \u201cBomb! Hit the dirt!\u201d And the house blows up. (Wait a minute, Mystery didn\u2019t say anything about explosives\u2026) The shock wave knocks Cole unconscious. Gus and Jaeda take a few levels of bashing damage too. Gus is annoyed at Cole\u2019s warning: If the blast came as a total surprise, he could have applied Solipsistic Well-Being. Gary, however, didn\u2019t hear the warning. As the smoke clears, he stands amid the wreckage of the house, unharmed except his Etna letter jacket is torn to shreds. But Tatenen is also unharmed. She\u2019s just that tough. As the Scions watch, she reaches into the stump of the house\u2019s chimney and pulls out a huge tetsubo made of bricks and mortar. On the other hand, the destruction of the house also apparently means the end of the ward!<br /> <br /> Tatenen takes a swat or two with the massive brick staff, but its huge size can\u2019t make up for the Scions\u2019 greater agility and skill at combat. Gus, Gary and Jaeda close to wolf-pack the Titan avatar. Unfortunately, Gary only manages to strike her once, and his great strength and his axe are still not enough to harm her. Even with their Piercing fenrir-fang cesti and dagger, and coordinating using Perfect Partner, Gus and Jaeda also find it difficult to land solid blows on Tatenen.<br /> <br /> The battle comes to a head when Jaeda finally lands a solid blow, though Tatenen instantly converts the damage from lethal to bashing. Tatenen tosses aside her useless tetsubo and simply grabs for Jaeda. The Titan-shabti\u2019s vast strength enables her to clinch Jaeda, and only sheer luck prevents her from squeezing the Scion unconscious at once. On the other hand, Jaeda manages to position the First Flower so Tatenen impales herself on the blade.<br /> <br /> Gus realizes he has mere seconds in which to stop Tatenen from crushing and killing Jaeda. He grabs Tatenen and strains with all his might against the Titan-shabti\u2019s greater strength. He actually manages to immobilize Tatenen long enough for Jaeda to twist around and push the First Flower into Tatenen\u2019s body. Holly\u2019s double falls unconscious, but not dead. Gary delivers the coup de grace. At which time, Holly and Jack arrive.<br /> <br /> The Scions linger just long enough for Jaeda to heal Cole into consciousness. Then they skedaddle as lots of sirens approach. With any luck, no one caught a clear view of the battle.<br /> <br /> On the one hand, the Scions are glad they finally destroyed Tatenen. With any luck, there are no more doubles running around causing trouble. On the other hand, their struggle with Guibourg has escalated in a big way. And the day isn\u2019t over.<br /> <br /> TO BE CONTINUED<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T05:58:01-08:00","id":1028993,"title":"","content":"<b>&quot;MY HOUSE!!&quot;</b>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T15:27:06-08:00","id":1029290,"title":"","content":"Yes, it's not a good day for Guibourg.<br /> <br /> A worse one is coming.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T15:27:57-08:00","id":1029291,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">EPISODE 20: Bad Neighbors, Part Two</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">In which the only successful Perception roll came because the character had Epic Perception.</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Act One: Explosions Get Noticed</span></span></u></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">As the Scions hurry away from the smoking rubble of Guibourg\u2019s house, they wonder what the hell is going on. In particular, why would Guibourg blow up his own house? Holly suggests it might be to cover up something that would interest the cops even more than a massive explosion, such as the bodies of his sacrificial victims. Still, they don\u2019t really know and Holly can\u2019t find out because she already used her one use of Mystery.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">The Scions agree, however, that they need to kill Guibourg soon. After all, he\u2019s alive because he serves the Titans in some way that\u2019s worse than the powerful monster that his death would release into the World. But the Scions won\u2019t attack him right now: Some of them are wounded; most are low on Legend.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">First, they contact Aristotle Faustus Dee. He calls them to his house in Rowanfield because some things are not safe to discuss over cell phones. He agrees that a crisis point approaches: The higher the ambient Legend rises, the mightier a servant monster the Titans can send to Earth when Guibourg dies. The ambient Legend is already dangerously high, what with the Scions, three families of giants and a bunch of Titan-serving witches and warlocks in the area, plus miscellaneous creatures. A Gyuki is pretty bad \u2014 a big strong monster that can stalk you in your dreams, then pop out to attack you in the real world \u2014 but worse things exist in the depths of Ultimate Darkness.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Dee also shows the Scions that a video of the explosion and their fight has already appeared on YouTube. It\u2019s going viral. This is bad: The video can increase mortal belief in the supernatural, which makes it easier for the supernatural to manifest. Dee will do what he can to cover up this evidence of the supernatural (just like he did with crop circles years ago), but the Scions need to work on this as well.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">That evening, Holly thinks of another angle. Guibourg threatened Gus with retribution against the Scions\u2019 friends and families. Is he already taking action? Such as by replacing relatives with shabti doubles, laying curses or holding hostages? Has he <i>already done so</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">, and one of the Scions is already working against the others? She phones the other Scions (including those in the rival Band) and lets them join her paranoia-fest.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Then she rousts Landon, and together they sneak into the homes of everyone they know while Holly spies on them using Unlidded Eye. She doesn\u2019t spot any magic at work except the lingering traces from the Scions themselves. Holly has a nasty shock when they swing past Landon\u2019s house and she sees terrible forces of Darkness swirling around it. Then she realizes she was looking past Landon and mistaking his Shinobi power for something in the house. (Yes, the player rolled a botch.)</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Meanwhile, the Scions agree they should get their mortal relatives out of town as quickly as possible. This is easier for some Scions than others. Jaeda has no trouble: Her father knows about Gods and Titans. Too late, though, she realizes her request undermines the pride her father developed from helping to support her. Jaeda tries to undo some of the damage to her father\u2019s self-esteem by thanking him for all his help and asking him to leave some of the money he\u2019d earned behind for her so she could buy things like, oh, food. But she sees he still feels pretty useless as he leaves. Scions with strong Epic Socials are also confident they can talk their parents into taking sudden vacations. And Jack wishes he\u2019d put more effort in that area.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Before talking to his mother, Jack goes to the garage. Before his Ducati he lays out a shot of whiskey and an unfiltered cigarette in a long black holder \u2014 ceremonial invocations of Hunter S. Thompson, since Lono took the man\u2019s guise when he fathered Jack. \u201cDad,\u201d he says, \u201cIf there\u2019s ever a time for you to hear a prayer and respond, it\u2019s now. I want Mom to hear the truth from you, not me.\u201d</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Nothing happens. Jack bites the bullet and tells his mom the truth about being the son of Lono. Naturally, Jack\u2019s mother thinks he\u2019s on drugs or having a psychotic break. Jack picks up the refrigerator with one hand; then his mom thinks <i>she\u2019s</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> having the psychotic break. At last, Jack convinces her that whatever the truth may be, he has superhuman powers and the World is not what she thought it was. His mother is not happy that Jack\u2019s been keeping secrets from her, but she leaves a message for the college where she teaches that she\u2019ll be gone for a few days because of a family emergency, and she drives away. Jack wonders if she\u2019ll come back.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jack decides that he\u2019ll spend the rest of the night trying to find Guibourg\u2019s witch acolytes using the power of math. It\u2019ll be just like on <i>Numbers</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">: He\u2019ll correlate every bit of weirdness or crime that could be evidence of Titan cultist activity and mathematically deduce where said cultists live. Dee has already promised to send him all the files he\u2019s collected that might help in this project. Jack will need to reconstruct entire fields of math (he still has only one dot), but how hard could that be for a divinely-empowered genius?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">When Gus heads home, he encounters George Delisle \u2014 father of the late Sheena the medusa \u2014 leaving the house. Delisle says he\u2019d come to talk to Mr. Sobeck about buying a new car. He doesn\u2019t ask about Sheena or even show that he remembers Gus very well. Gus recognizes that Delisle is lying about the car (thank you Takes One to Know One) and pays close attention to Delisle\u2019s body language and shadings of vocal tone (<i>i.e.,</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> does a Read Motivation). He realizes that Delisle feels venomous hatred for him, though he hides it well. Gus phones Holly the next chance he gets and tells her to add Delisle to her list of Persons Of Interest. He also tells his father to watch out around Delisle: He gets a weird vibe from the man.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">So Holly swings by the Delisle house, still using Unlidded Eye, and thinks she sees Doomsday happening. She speed-dials all the other Scions and tells them it\u2019s going down <i>now</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">, the Titan is breaking through! She invokes Prophecy to see what form the imminent Apocalypse shall take, though, and receives nothing except the sound of ice clinking in a glass and pouring liquid. And there is nothing supernatural about Delisle\u2019s house at all. (Yes, the player rolled </span><i><span style=\"color:#000000\">another</span></i><span style=\"color:#000000\"> botch on the Perception roll. A </span><i><span style=\"color:#000000\">bad</span></i><span style=\"color:#000000\"> botch.) Holly calls everyone back and tells them it was a drill to see how fast they could mobilize.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Having spent a few hours on this, Holly swings by Jack\u2019s to see how his attempt at data mining is going. She finds him naked except for a beer hat loaded with Red Bull, writing equations on the walls with dry-erase marker. Holly wishes she could go blind. Nevertheless, Jack insists he\u2019s making progress. When he drops to the floor to scrawl more math, Holly recovers her wits long enough to take pictures with her phone. Jack stoned on crystal math should be good for later embarrassment and/or blackmail.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jaeda finds she is too wound up to sleep after all this excitement and false alarms, so she breaks into the Avalon High auto shop to work on her Torino. She calls Gary, and he joins her. Together, they get the car running, if not well: Whatever comes, the Band at least has one more source of transportation. Afterward, they return to Jaeda\u2019s Motel 6 for celebratory sex. They do not notice Gunnlod watching them enter the motel room together.</span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-09T15:31:42-08:00","id":1029293,"title":"","content":"Technical difficulties with the next installment. (I just noticed that when I was splitting the text into chunks, part of the last paragraph got lost.) I'll pick it up tomorrow. Sorry.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T14:38:56-08:00","id":1029764,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">EPISODE 20: Bad Neighbors, Part Two (Continued)</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Act Two: Meetings and Revelations</span></span></u></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">To everyone\u2019s relief (and slight disappointment), though, the night passes without further eruptions of terror and violence. The next morning, everyone must go to school, same as normal. Jack, however, comes to school wearing nice slacks, a white shirt, sport jacket and a tie, with his hair neatly combed. He stared into the heart of Math\u2026 <i>and it stared back\u2026</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> but he insists he has found four locations where Guibourg\u2019s witches live! (Thank you Epic Intelligence, a Legendary Deed and using temporary insanity as a stunt.)</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Just to be sure, the Scions have Dr. Demerit check Jack\u2019s reconstruction of geographic profiling theory. Demerit notes a few places where Jack could have shown his work more thoroughly, but it seems sound overall. \u201cClearly, I have been going too easy on you,\u201d he pronounces. \u201cYou are ready for assignments of greater difficulty.\u201d</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Naturally, the other kids are buzzing about Etna High Principal Borgen\u2019s house blowing up. It was on the evening news. What\u2019s more, a video of the explosion appeared on YouTube! Though shot from a distance, the telephoto video clearly shows the house with the Scions standing outside, though fortunately they all thought to wear ski masks. Then Gus\u2019 head flies out a window. Then the house blows up, leaving a recognizable Gary Vetterson (the explosion blasted off his mask) and Holly Frost (Tatenen still using her appearance). The beautiful blond girl then shapes a giant club from the remains of the chimney, swings it around without effort, fights the other people, and falls. The last shot is of Gary swinging an axe down at a figure that can no longer be clearly seen because other people are in the way. The Scions grin and insist they don\u2019t know a thing, but it\u2019s amazing what you can do with Photoshop, isn\u2019t it?</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">And naturally, Lieutenant Picinich wants to ask the Scions what they know about all this. After all, Holly and Gary are both known associates of Gus Sobeck. Gus\u2019 head is on the video. Gus insists that he did not cause the explosion and repeats the Photoshop suggestion. Besides, he\u2019s still alive and not beheaded. Picinich remains hostile. Gus tries to make peace with Picinich by saying he knows that the cop looks at him and sees Gav, and what his uncle did to Picinich is unforgivable, but <i>he\u2019s not Gav</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">. At being reminded of the greatest trauma of his life, Picinich snarls for Gus to get the hell out of the room.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">When he talks to Jaeda, Picinich says he thinks the knife that the masked girl uses against the Holly double looks familiar \u2014 like it was obsidian, not metal. (Actually, obsidian and metal reflect light in such similar ways they are nigh-impossible to tell apart on video, but Jaeda can tell the cop is fishing based on other suspicions.) Jaeda agrees to let Picinich borrow the First Flower \u201cfor tests\u201d to see how similar metal and obsidian look on video. To this end she fetches the knife from her locker under cover of collecting her jacket and books (using Subtle Knife so Picinich doesn\u2019t see it), then releasing the illusion while she pretends to pull it from a drawer at the hotel. Picinich gives Jaeda a receipt and promises to return the knife soon.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jaeda concludes by asking if the cops have made any progress in finding who burned and crushed her mobile home. Picinich says the case has gone cold. Jaeda bitterly says that of course, some glints of light on an obviously faked video matter more than the destruction of a Latino\u2019s home. It\u2019s not as if an <i>Anglo\u2019s</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> home were destroyed. And she stalks out. Her anger is quite genuine even though she knows the physical evidence adds up to impossibility, the motive is beyond the cops\u2019 comprehension, and the perp became a pile of mud months ago: She\u2019s a teen.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Jack likewise suggests a hoax, since so much on the video is clearly impossible. Further, he notes that YouTube takes great care in recording who uploads videos, and whoever made and loaded the video must have known the house would explode\u2026 See the connection? Jack also suggests where to look for the camera, based on the angle of the view and the Power of Math. Picinich starts getting irritated at having kids tell him how to do police work when an imposing-looking black man steps into the conference room where he conducts his interviews. The man (who looks a lot like the character of Broyles from <i>Fringe</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\">) introduces himself as Special Agent Anthony Cross of the FBI, and says Picinich can go now. The explosion is domestic terrorism, so now the experts will handle the investigation. Cross seems amused by Picinich\u2019s ill-concealed resentment at having the Feds push him aside.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Cross and Jack do a bit of verbal dancing as Cross suggests that Jack and his friends are involved and that Cross knows who and what they are. Finally Cross comes out and says it: He knows Jack is a Scion, one of several. Cross is a Scion too. Applying Know-It-All, Jack figures out Cross\u2019 father must be Legba, a.k.a. St. Anthony, God of the Crossroads.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Cross confirms the Scions\u2019 suspicions and makes them worse. The video is another ploy to encourage belief in the supernatural and disbelief in a sensible world in which Gods and monsters are just stories. Cross knows that Guibourg didn\u2019t blow up the house and plant the cameras: Tatenen did. (Perhaps she was dissatisfied with Guibourg\u2019s slow plan to recruit witches.) If the ambient Legend rises high enough, Guibourg\u2019s death might even allow a Titan into the World. It\u2019s also possible that Avalon, Etna and Hooverdale might drop out of the ordinary World completely to become a mythical Terra Incognita. The Gods will not allow either to happen. If the region\u2019s concentration of Legend does not drop, and the video cannot be thoroughly debunked and disbelieved, the Gods will take drastic action to cover it all up. Minds will be broken. Then Cross vanishes from the room. Jack passes this happy thought to the other Scions.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holly is not left out. She receives a text from her mother saying her jet will land on a certain runway at a certain time after school. Holly is to be there. Not feeling suicidal, Holly obeys. The jet arrives on time. Louhi says that she will give Holly a chance to assuage her mother\u2019s disappointment at losing Landon. The Crone of Pohjola knows about Avalon\u2019s spiking Legend and Guibourg\u2019s deadman-switch summoning. She has built her plan around them. Louhi intends to push the Legend high enough that Guibourg\u2019s death releases a Titan Avatar of far greater power than the shabti Tatenen. She will be on hand to capture the avatar and pull, so the entire Greater Titan comes through \u2014 and into her binding spell. The Gods dare not kill the Titans, or even their avatars, because of the havoc this causes in the World, so the Titans must be bound instead. Louhi considers this a rare and near-ideal chance to bind a Titan and remove it from the battle against the Gods.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">To further this plan, Holly must place two dozen mystic resonators about Avalon, Etna and Hooverdale that will augment Louhi\u2019s binding spell. Holly must also do whatever she can to increase ambient Legend and belief in the supernatural. As a sweetener, and reward for passing along the Scroll of Tatenen, Louhi gives her daughter another card with the phone number for her assassination service. (Remember, Holly used the last one to kill George Skehum the kushtaka.) Holly smiles dutifully and agrees.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Meanwhile, the other Scions get on with their lives. This is a regularly scheduled day for working out and sparring, now conducted at Brawn\u2019s Gym at the mall. Leo\u2019s father Nergal sent him a set of swords (which he had Holly store at her house so Leo wouldn\u2019t need to explain them to his parents). His demon is nagging him to train with swords. Jaeda agrees to spar with Leo using bokken. Gus, Jack and Barbara are also there.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"font-family:Times New Roman\"><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Several minutes later, the other Scions notice that something strange is happening with Jaeda\u2019s and Leo\u2019s sparring. Leo inherits great natural combat talent from Nergal, but he\u2019s always seemed somewhat stiff and awkward. Not anymore. Sparring with someone he now accepts as a friend, he moves with feline grace. Gus is immediately suspicious that Leo isn\u2019t Leo anymore, but the gym manager Braun says no, Leo\u2019s finally feeling at ease with himself. As the others watch, Leo starts using Perfect Partner to match Jaeda\u2019s moves. Jaeda responds by using Perfect Partner too, and the sparring becomes a fantastic duel-dance. Jack hears Barbara murmur, \u201cI <i>want</i></span></span><span style=\"font-size:12px\"><span style=\"color:#000000\"> him,\u201d and broods that he is never going to get a cool girlfriend. Jaeda notices how Barbara watches Leo and directs his attention to her. That was a mistake. Leo immediately feels embarrassed and self-conscious. The spell breaks; he stumbles, and the sparring match ends.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak</span></span></span></span><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T14:40:16-08:00","id":1029765,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">EPISODE 20: Bad Neighbors, Part Two (Continued)</span></span><br /> <br /> <b><u><span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Act Three: Night Out</span></span></u></b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">When Jaeda finally returns to the Motel 6, she finds a package waiting for her holding the First Flower. The cops shouldn\u2019t just drop off returned evidence like that, but the Atzl\u00e1nti\u2019s most powerful Relic wants to return to its owner.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">That evening, Gus take his Fashionista-assigned girlfriend Lindsey on her promised date at the Public Pool. Who should be there but Gary and Reese, grimly enduring a double date with Gunnlod and Norfi. Jaeda also goes to the Public Pool. She\u2019s low on Legend (though she already used Maguey Sting once), and her plan to get more needs an audience. The moment Gunnlod sees Jaeda, though, the young giantess glares at her with such venomous hatred that Jaeda realizes Gunnlod knows about her and Gary. Norfi scowls at Gus.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Neither Gunnlod nor Norfi can dance worth a darn. Norfi gets frustrated and tired of trying. The country boy also can\u2019t handle the light, noise and crowd. While he stalks off to get sodas at the bar, Gus slides over to Reese. Reese tells Gus she must have died, because this is Hell.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Norfi loses it first. The Scions don\u2019t know what sets him off, but he slugs someone. The slugged teen flies through the air and falls into the mosh pit that used to be the pool, unconscious and with broken ribs. Gus tells Lindsey to leave the nightclub and call 911, but she hangs around instead in hopes of seeing Gus do something macho like beating up Norfi.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus, however, is mindful of the need to avoid flaunting Legend. He does his best to calm Norfi and get him out of the club.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Meanwhile, Jaeda finds a partier with a hip flask and easily borrows it. She lodges it in her cleavage and does the Limbo so the booze pours out into her mouth, as a way to invoke Raise Your Glass. Jaws drop.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">It\u2019s too much for Gary\u2019s Rapacity. When Jaeda flips back upright, it\u2019s into his arms. He growls, \u201cHottest. Girlfriend. Ever!\u201d and dips her into a mighty kiss. When Jaeda can breathe again she responds, \u201cAnd you\u2019re the hunkiest boyfriend ever. But not the brightest. Excuse me, but Gunnlod\u2019s about to try to kill me!\u201d Sure enough, the enraged young giantess takes a swing at Jaeda while screaming that Gary is hers, Jaeda is no good for Gary, she probably can\u2019t cook or sew at all! (Jaeda concedes the giantess is correct.) Matters go downhill from there. Jaeda gets Gary out and running for his car. His plan is sex on the hood, but finally spends Willpower and hops in the driver\u2019s seat instead when Gunnlod comes running out of the nightclub waving a section of metal guardrail as a six-foot club. Gary starts to peel out of the parking lot, but Gunnlod screams for him to <i>Stop!</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> and he does \u2014 she knows Overt Order. This gives her time to catch up, so Gary and Jaeda have to fight her to protect The Car.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Norfi then tries to grab Reese and drag her out of the club. Reese hauls off and slugs him. Norfi, possibly bound by Fate into the stupidest possible macho clich\u00e9, grins and says he likes a girl with spirit! He makes another grab for Reese and holds her: Reese is strong, but Norfi is much stronger.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Gus comes to Reese\u2019s rescue. Norfi soon shifts his attention to fighting Gus. The Scion leads Norfi out of The Public Pool, springs to the roof, and taunts him into following; Gus is sure that Gary and Jaeda can deal with Gunnlod. He is quite correct. In short order, the enraged young giants are unconscious.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">What to do with them? After getting to know Reese and Gary, the two Scions feel reluctant to kill Gunnlod and Norfi out of hand. That would also result in all-out war with the two young giants\u2019 parents. Everyone agrees to pass the buck. Gary and Reese drive home with the unconscious giants piled in the back seat. Let Suttung decide what to do with them!</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Meanwhile, Holly takes a long drive in the country. Louhi gave her a lot to think about \u2014 and Holly is utterly certain that her mother did not tell her everything. Eventually, she turns to the only Scion in the area who approaches her intelligence, Jack. The two map out the locations where Louhi wants the resonators placed and see how what Louhi said fits with their own knowledge of the supernatural. Jack also applies the Power of Math. They reach horrible conclusions:</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">First, Louhi gave Holly only a third of the resonators that the plan requires. Some other agent must be planting additional devices\u2026 and probably watching Holly.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">More importantly, Louhi left out that her plan would result in <i>both</i></span><span style=\"color:#000000\"> of the outcomes that Cross predicted. When Louhi uses Guibourg\u2019s death to pull the Darkness Titan into the World, Avalon and Etna will implode and drop out of the mortal World. The towns will become the new Tartarus \u2014 and the people will be trapped along with the Titan. And the Scions, by the way.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Well, crap.</span></span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:&amp;quot\"><span style=\"color:#000000\">Holly can see the logic of the plan. A single Titan can fight an entire pantheon. Removing one from the war in the Overworld is huge. The Gods could easily consider the loss of 10,000 (or so) mortals acceptable collateral damage. In the end, though, Holly decides that she is not her mother. This plan must not come to fruition.</span></span><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-10T14:41:48-08:00","id":1029768,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 20: Bad Neighbors, Part Two (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Four: Unlikely Rescue</u></b><br /> <br /> The Scions spend hours discussing what Holly has learned, but how can they stop Louhi\u2019s plan? The only way they can see is to kill Guibourg themselves and try to cope with the Gyuki when it comes. Not that they have any idea how to fight a monster that\u2019s tougher than the Black Bodhisattva and ducks in and out of dreams\u2026 Gus is quietly amused by the horror of the Band\u2019s super-brains as they realize Gus\u2019 original plan of killing Guibourg and taking their chances with whatever is summoned might be the best course after all.<br /> <br /> By the wee hours of the morning, the Scions all feel pretty low. Which is when a Scion\u2019s phone rings. It\u2019s Reese saying the Vettersons are under attack, HELP!<br /> <br /> The Scions charge to the rescue. Arriving at the Vetterson house, they find Reese hiding a block away and the side door to the house smashed in. Reese tells them it\u2019s the giants from British Columbia. The ungrateful bastards charged in, taking them by surprise. She forced herself to flee when she realized they couldn\u2019t win. Now, Reese doesn\u2019t even know if Gary and her parents are still alive.<br /> <br /> Oh. What do the giants want? They want the Vetterson\u2019s treasure. The Vettersons are rich, so the plan is to take their gold and hightail it back to Canada. Only the stupid lumps have never heard of <i>bank accounts</i>.<br /> <br /> As they enter, the Scions hear some giants ripping up the house in a different room. They tiptoe to the logical place to keep prisoners: The Vettersons\u2019 soundproofed basement. Reese sneaks off to find Gary\u2019s axe and the controls for the house\u2019s sprinkler system (water, so the other Vettersons can heal themselves), and maybe do an ambush of her own if she finds Norfi or Gunnlod. Jack signals Jaeda to go after whoever\u2019s tearing up the house. Jaeda does so, but is chagrined to realize that Jack had not volunteered to join her.<br /> <br /> Hearing noises of talk and torture from the basement, the Scions decide to make an entrance. Gus knocks down the basement door and rides it like a surfboard down the stairs, Jack and Holly following behind. Down below, they find Suttung, Hyndla and Gary, much battered and chained up with fetters too massive even for them to break. They are attended by the three male giants: Norfi, his father Jokull, and the giants\u2019 blacksmith Starkad. Starkad is torturing the bound and gagged Hyndla to make Suttung talk. Norfi is torturing Gary with a blowtorch, just because.<br /> <br /> Battle is joined. Jack starts by throwing his Shinobi Shuriken at Starkad, wounding the giant but not by much. Gus pounds on Jokull and nearly rips his face off with a clinch from behind. A powerful blow from Starkad\u2019s hammer nearly knocks out Gus, but Self-Healing puts him right as rain. Then Gus takes on Starkad, clinching the giant blacksmith from behind and pinning him with his own huge hammer. It\u2019s easier because Holly has cast Evil Eye on Starkad. Jaeda engages Norfi. Holly quickly thinks to cut Hyndla\u2019s gag as well: Both of the adult Vettersons know Overt Order (the reason the Canadian giants always kept three in the room and only one Vetterson able to talk). But the Vettersons are gravely wounded and need a moment to collect their wits.<br /> <br /> Seeing the odds, the Scions call for Jaeda to join them. She does so \u2014 but the female giants follow her, one by one. Still, Gus, Jack and Jaeda are now incredibly skilled combatants, and Holly keeps Overt Ordering one giant to attack another, and catching them with Serpent\u2019s Gaze. Norfi manages a lucky attack with the blowtorch, but he soon falls. Jack knows that he is nearly impossible to hit when he combines Untouchable Opponent with Fight With Your Head, so he draws the giants\u2019 attention to himself: He makes a big production about drawing his katana, hands shaking as if the blade carried terrible power, while intoning a tongue-mangling invocation (\u201cMary had a little lamb; its fleece was white as snow\u2026\u201d \u2014 in Basque). He play-acts like the sword moves by itself and drags him along, as a stunt and to unnerve the giants even further. (And of course he imbues the blade with Warrior\u2019s Mana.)<br /> <br /> When Suttung and Hyndla start Overt Ordering giants to attack each other, drop their weapons, etc., and Reese joins the fight with Gary\u2019s axe in hand, the battle ends soon after. Gus delivers the final blow to Norfi.<br /> <br /> Jaeda helps release Gary. Suttung glares as he sees the way they look at each other, but is too tired and thrashed to object, even after healing himself with water and Legend.<br /> <br /> What to do about the wounded, unconscious Canadian giants? Nobody really wants to leave them alive. The Scions and Reese just haul them to the bathtub one by one and start chopping.<br /> <br /> Jack realizes that he\u2019s made a good beginning at turning his ordinary sword legendary. Using it to fight giants is legendary, right? Someday, maybe he can have enough legendary deeds associated with it that a God can turn it into a Relic! But a legendary blade needs a name. Considering his play-acting in the fight, he decides that \u201cLamb of Lono\u201d is unfortunately appropriate. It sounds cooler in Basque.<br /> <br /> The Scions have a talk with the elder Vettersons. Gary is firm: He shall continue dating Jaeda. Suttung doesn\u2019t like it, but he owes the Scions his life and the lives of his family. The truce will continue.<br /> <br /> About this time, the Scions notice Reese is gone. So is one of the slain giants\u2019 hearts. Holly is out of Legend, so Hyndla casts Ariadne\u2019s Thread to find her daughter. The Scions hadn\u2019t tried collecting eitr before because they thought it would be deeply rude to do so in front of the Vettersons, but with the prospect of fresh trouble Jaeda quickly collects the last three doses from the melting hearts.<br /> <br /> The Scions and Vettersons find Reese a brisk five minute\u2019s jog away, in a clearing in some woodland. She has just squeezed the last eitr out of the heart while calling, \u201cAcacia! Acacia! Acacia!\u201d<br /> <br /> And Acacia rises out of the ground, whole again \u2014 remember how the Tomb-Spawn ripped out of her, back in Episode 10? \u2014 but the Scions know she is not alive.<br /> <br /> The crazy Goth witch smiles a crazy Goth smile at seeing Reese and the Scions. Then she sees the faint light in the East from the approaching dawn. Acacia promises them the fun is just beginning. Then she dissolves into mist and vanishes.<br /> <br /> Reese explains afterward that while she carried away the dying Acacia, her friend told her that supernatural blood might be able to bring her back. (She\u2019d been full of eitr and other stuff when she died, it was a very supernatural death anyway\u2026 worth a shot, anyway.) Reese finally had a chance. She missed her friend. Sure, Acacia was nuts, but she was fun to hang with and she was a friend. Reese doesn\u2019t abandon her friends.<br /> <br /> So, the Scions eliminated two families of giants (and without any mortals seeing them do so). On the other hand, they can now add a vampire to their list of problems. And they still have Gus\u2019 court hearing and the talent show to look forward to.<br /> <br /> The fun just never stops at Scion High!<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T06:05:04-08:00","id":1030017,"title":"","content":"<i>They should just get Al Simmons to deal with Guiborg. He's dealt with shit like this before.</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T16:02:27-08:00","id":1030294,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1010387-scion-high-campaign-chronicle-repost?p=1030017#post1030017\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>They should just get Al Simmons to deal with Guiborg. He's dealt with shit like this before.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>?<br /> <br /> &lt;checks Google&gt;<br /> <br /> A baseball player?<br /> <br /> &lt;looks further down the list of results&gt;<br /> <br /> Oh, the <i>other</i> Al Simmons. That makes more sense.<br /> <br /> Probably not possible, though. The studio that makes <i>Scion High</i> would need to buy rights to the character, and the suits likely wouldn't spring for the money for a Scion High/Spawn crossover. :-)<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T16:04:24-08:00","id":1030296,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 21: Goodbye, Avalon<br /> <br /> In which the saga of Scion High ends a little earlier than expected.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act One: A Busy Friday Night</u></b><br /> <br /> Fighting the Canadian giants and other business left the Scions almost exhausted of Legend and Willpower, but they feel better by Friday. Good thing too, because that\u2019s the day of the school talent show!<br /> <br /> Delia\u2019s attempt to win by blocking Holly from entering deeply offended Holly, who sought revenge by making her victory blatantly hollow. Unfortunately, Jack (not knowing this) challenged Gus to appear in the talent show. They quickly signed up for the show. Gus, as always, plans to do his best. His idea is to flaunt his athletic prowess with a display of parkour moves, general acrobatics and martial arts, topping it all by smashing a cinder block. But blocking Holly\u2019s revenge triggers her Vengeance all over again\u2026 Holly schemes to sabotage Gus by supplementing his music track with a softer second track of Right Said Fred\u2019s \u201cToo Sexy For My Shirt.\u201d Realizing that Gus has no sense of humor where his reputation is concerned, though, and not wanting to blow up the Band at this moment of crisis, Holly grits her teeth and quashes the impulse.<br /> <br /> Early Friday, Jaeda also has a brainstorm. What if she and Leo reprised their spectacular sparring match for the talent show? When she suggests this to Leo, he gets a trapped, miserable expression. He\u2019d like to do it because it would make her happy, but he can\u2019t. He just can\u2019t. This disappoints Barbara, who very much wants to see Leo strut his stuff (preferably shirtless to flaunt the ripped physique he\u2019s developed with his Epic Strength, Dexterity and Stamina). She suggests that Gus could deal with Leo\u2019s stage fright by using Bind the Heart on him. Leo\u2019s a little creeped out by the thought of someone magically altering his personality, but for Jaeda\u2026 He accepts. Gus agrees and gives Leo a new Nature so he enjoys showing off. Then Barbara uses Social Fu to put Leo and Jaeda on the program even though registration was supposed to have closed days ago.<br /> <br /> The discussion of altering Natures leads Jack to a revelation. Jaeda has worked to change Leo\u2019s Nature; but he thinks she\u2019s changing her own. She isn\u2019t as needy and clingy as she was at the start of the school year, more about caring for others. (In game terms, her Nature is changing from Addict to Caregiver.) Jaeda is surprised, but she hopes Jack is right.<br /> <br /> The Scions are the last acts on the program. Jack goes first. He comes out on stage dressed in Shakespearean doublet and hose and declaims in a Richard III squawk, \u201cNow\u2026\u201d and pauses. And pauses some more. Then whispers loudly, \u201cLine?\u201d A hook pulls him off stage.<br /> <br /> Gus then does his acrobatics/martial arts routine. In his competitive zeal, he strives to make it a Legendary Deed. Between that and his Epic Dexterity, he does <i>unbelievably </i>well. (19 successes! Yes, more successes than dice.) Jaws drop.<br /> <br /> Next come Jaeda and Leo, announced as \u201cThe Lady and the Tiger.\u201d Jaeda and Leo wear tights, with a bikini top for Jaeda and some stripes painted in Leo\u2019s hair and across the lion-demon tattoo displayed on his bare back. Perfect Partner and an accelerating, rhythmic piece of music turns the bokken duel into a dance, and then Jaeda\u2019s Epic Appearance and Presence turn the dance into a seduction. The duel-dance ends with them pressed together, crossed bokken between them, looking like they are about to kiss. Jaeda and Leo also turn their performances into Legendary Deeds. The result is not quite so unbelievable, depending on how you parse the result (17 successes for Jaeda\u2019s Appearance + Melee roll, 14 for Leo\u2019s Dexterity + Melee roll to complement her), but they too receive a standing ovation, and a hundred camera phones record the performance.<br /> <br /> Delia ends the show with a display of cheerleading to accompany the school song. She actually has Art (Cheerleading), as well as Epic Dexterity, Epic Appearance and Center of Attention. She also Deeds her performance, for 12 successes. The last act in a talent show enjoys an advantage as the freshest in people\u2019s minds; talent show tradition is to give first prize to a girl who sings; and \u201cSchool Spirit\u201d is always a winner\u2026 but not after Gus, Leo and Jaeda.<br /> <br /> The judges, blasted by three such performances, are still ready to give Gus the prize. Looking over at Jaeda, though, Gus sees how much this contest means to her. And, the player revealed, Gus has been in love with Jaeda for quite some time. (Gus said, \u201cI do love you, Jaeda,\u201d after the fight with the giants, but she smiled and misunderstood the way he loves her.) Gus plucks the first prize announcement from the presenter\u2019s hand and names Jaeda and Leo the winners. The student audience is happy enough, and the two Scions take a bow to thunderous applause. Once they leave the stage, Barbara grabs Leo and hauls him off to a broom closet for smoochies.<br /> <br /> Immediately after school, though, Gus has his court hearing. Before heading for the courthouse, he creates a shabti of himself and sends it out to rob the same Circuit City that was robbed before by the double created by the late Zach. He doesn\u2019t try using Social Knacks on the judge, prosecutor or Picinich; just sits quietly and lets the family lawyer do the talking. The judge sets a trial date; Picinich gloats that Gus will get what he deserves; Gus smiles slightly.<br /> <br /> By the next day, the cops and prosecutor have the store video footage of Gus causing trouble at the Circuit City while Gus was in a courtroom in full view of a judge. Picinich sputters that Gus has an accomplice made up to look like him. The prosecutor avers that that may be, but the trial goes on indefinite hold for further investigation. Picinich\u2019s captain orders him to take a long vacation.<br /> <br /> Holly, meanwhile, copes with the unstable emotions of her pyromaniac minion, Maynard Salkind. Since the Gyuki is a creature of shadow, bright, hot incendiaries are the obvious weapon to use against it, just as they were against the Lucifuge. Maynard can build such devices. By Friday, however, Maynard is an emotional wreck. While Holly was busy working Social Fu damage control and fighting giants, Vanessa the Fashionista unexpectedly decided to apologize to Maynard for setting him up for humiliation with Holly (back in episode 11). She apologized so much that she gave him a happy. Then she was disgusted with him and told him so, eloquently. Holly catches up to Maynard not far from Vanessa\u2019s home in Rowanfield. He\u2019s wearing an incendiary suicide vest and is not ready to listen to reason, so she catches him with Serpent\u2019s Gaze and disarms him. Then she exercises all her skills at calculated, manipulative niceness to talk him down. It isn\u2019t easy. Still, she now has a supply of the incendiaries she wanted\u2026<br /> <br /> Jaeda also has a not-so-pleasant coda to her talent show triumph. During her shift at Big Cups, a Native American man in a black Subaru drives up to the coffee stand. He wears an all-black suit with a black shirt and black tie. The man says for her to pass the word that if Louhi attempts her plan, the Manitou will take it as an act of war. See, it won\u2019t just be Avalon and Etna that disappear: The towns and the Titan will crash down into the Ghost Dance Country, since Coyote was careless enough to create a weak point leading to the pocket world the Manitou created for the tribes and their spirits. <i>That</i> will become the new Tartarus. The black-clad figure concludes by warning Jaeda that Louhi isn\u2019t the only God who can steal the sun. Jaeda promises the God \u2014 whom she correctly recognizes as Raven \u2014 that she will do her best to pass his message to Louhi and other Gods.<br /> <br /> For his part, Jack works another depressing shift at Stick It. Special Agent Cross stops by to update him on the campaign to debunk the video of the Scions\u2019 fight against Tatenen. A number of Gods have joined Holly and Barbara in using Rumor Mill to spread the \u201cIt\u2019s Just Photoshop\u201d meme. Unfortunately, parties unknown (but probably including Guibourg) are using Rumor Mill to spread a contrary memes that It\u2019s Real and proves that Otherworldly Forces Are Among Us. As for the town\u2019s ambient Legend, killing a bunch of giants helped lower it \u2014 but the spectacular displays at the talent show raised it again.<br /> <br /> (Jack, incidentally, wonders if Agent Cross is real or if he\u2019s going nuts as various genius mathematicians have done. After all, <i>none of the other Scions have seen Agent Cross.</i>)<br /> <br /> The Scions decide they had better try convincing Louhi her plan is unworkable. But how? They guess that since Holly is not the only agent placing Louhi\u2019s mystic resonators, someone else is probably keeping an eye on her and the town. To draw out that agent, they start collecting the resonators Holly already placed, and from the locations Jack deduced.<br /> <br /> The Scions soon notice that not all the resonators they collect (disks of wood, metal and bone inscribed with runes) are the ones Holly placed. On two of them, some of the runes are drawn wrong and are, in fact, decals covering another symbol: a stylized canine head. A coyote?<br /> <br /> Before too long, a Nordic-looking man accosts the Scions and warns them to stop interfering with Louhi\u2019s plan. The Scions point out that Coyote has interfered already, and they pass along Raven\u2019s warning.<br /> <br /> That done, the Scions round out the evening by framing Guibourg. The big problem with debunking the Tatenen video is that a house really blew up, and a huge brick tetsubo was left on site. You can\u2019t explain away a massive explosion as Photoshop.<br /> <br /> So they come up with a story that \u201cPrincipal Borgen\u201d is involved in shady financial dealings and blew up his own house to collect the insurance money. He had the \u201cfake\u201d video created as a distraction to send the cops on a wild goose chase. Holly, Barbara and Gus know enough about business to know what information and fake documents they need for a hoax that will survive examination for at least a little while. Jack makes sure the numbers are correct. Bobby Kilmer, Holly\u2019s hacker minion, breaks the video down into separate tracks and fakes up CGI rough drafts of Tatenen-Holly swinging the brick tetsubo.<br /> <br /> In the wee hours of the morning, Landon breaks into Etna High with a jump drive loaded with digital fakery. Gus creates a squad of shabti warriors to protect Landon. As the Scions expected, Guibourg warded his office against Scion entry. He also warded it against shinobi entry: Oops, he knows about Landon. However, he did not ward it against <i>shabti </i>entry. Landon send a shabti in with the jump drive and talks the animate statue through the process of loading the false data into Guibourg\u2019s computer.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T16:06:14-08:00","id":1030298,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 21: Goodbye, Avalon (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Two: A Busy Saturday As Well</u></b><br /> <br /> That Saturday, Holly disguises herself using Stolen Face and goes to the cops. She (looking like a he) says she\u2019s the hacker who created the video for \u201cPrincipal Borgen,\u201d but Borgen stiffed her on payment and she didn\u2019t know the plan involved high explosives. She lays on all her Epic Manipulation and Presence to convince the cops, but it really isn\u2019t that hard: The cops want to believe insurance fraud, because that\u2019s something they can understand. It will take time for the cops to investigate, find the rest of the frame in Guibourg\u2019s office and arrest him, but the Scions feel confident they have deflected the cops, deflated the video and made life difficult for their nemesis. Sure, Guibourg can probably Social Fu his way out of a trial, but it will tie him up for a while.<br /> <br /> The Scions also ponder what to do about Acacia. Holly is almost certain that Acacia put the whammy on Vanessa, possibly as a way to remove Maynard from the Scions\u2019 assets. (Though they remember that Guibourg is also skilled at subtly implanting suggestions.) Jaeda suggests they simply delegate. Holly has another card with which to order an assassination; call the number and put a hit on Acacia! The other Scions think that sounds pretty good, though they\u2019ll need to wait a few days for results.<br /> <br /> That afternoon, however, the Scions learn that Guibourg is not done with them. They receive a call from Gary saying that they now have much worse than the Tatenen video to worry about.<br /> <br /> Various journalists have pestered Gary about his appearance in the Tatenen video. He\u2019s done his best to play dumb (not hard for a giant) and deny any involvement. This afternoon, though, a sniper shot him in the head with a high-powered rifle. Which he survived using Solipsistic Well-Being, just as he did back in episode 10. <i>On camera</i>. It\u2019ll be on the evening news.<br /> <br /> The Scions decide they cannot wait any longer. They need to deal with Guibourg and his witches and warlocks <i>now</i>, before he does anything more to expose the supernatural. The Band calls up the other Scions and brings them in. Over the past week, Jack has used the magic-sensing aspect of Sense Tapu to identify the four witches in the locations he found using math, so the Scions can start by taking them out. The Band decides to attack Guibourg, while the other Scions hunt witches. For this, even Marshall agrees to work with the others again. Leo, however, accompanies the PCs: He wants to confront Guibourg more than anything. Landon agrees to accompany the other Scions to make up for Leo.<br /> <br /> Since his house blew up, Guibourg is living at a Holiday Inn with police protection. The Scions hatch a plan to remove the cops: Holly will use Stolen Face to disguise herself as a cop and say she\u2019s part of the relief shift. Then she\u2019ll just Social Fu the two cops out of the room. Gus, Jaeda and Leo will wait in a nearby hall. Jack, meanwhile, will lurk outside with Maynard\u2019s suicide vest and a shabti monkey created by Gus. When Jack gets the signal from Holly, he will throw the vest through the window into Guibourg\u2019s room; the monkey will pull the string to detonate the vest and blow up Guibourg! Since the vest is primarily incendiary, not high explosive, it should not harm people in nearby rooms.<br /> <br /> That much of the plan works. The cops answer Holly\u2019s knock at the door. She points down the hall and says something cop-like, such as \u201cHe\u2019s got a gun! After him!\u201d and her massive Presence and Epic Manipulation make the cops join her in running down the hall. Jack throws the vest, which detonates on cue; Gus and Leo dash into the burning room to finish Guibourg, trusting their Epic Stamina to protect them from the flames. Jack, who has just learned Wind\u2019s Freedom, starts running through the air up to the burning hotel room.<br /> <br /> There\u2019s someone on the floor, already burning to the point of being unrecognizable. (Failed Perception + Investigation rolls for Gus and Leo.) Just as Holly reaches the elevator with the two cops, though, she realizes that the clever and devious warlock certainly prepared for the Scions\u2019 inevitable attack \u2014 and the best way to escape it was to be one of the guards they would surely try to rescue first. Sure enough, the Unlidded Eye reveals that one of the cops is actually Guibourg using a cunning (but non-magical) disguise! Holly shouts a warning to the other Scions. Guibourg Overt Orders Holly to flee, while he activates Lightning Sprinter and dashes in the other direction.<br /> <br /> A mad chase through the hotel ensues as the Scions try to catch up with or intercept Guibourg. Just as they close on him in a stairwell, though, a wisp of fog coalesces into Acacia. As Guibourg races past, the young vampire reaches out her arm, nails extending into claws, and rips out his throat. Guibourg has already used Deus Ex Machina to escape death once in this scene, and the Storyteller doesn\u2019t give him a second opportunity. Acacia smiles at the stunned Scions and says the fun is only just beginning. Then she turns back into mist.<br /> <br /> Well. Cops and hotel security are running around, fire alarms are blaring, and their nemesis is bleeding to death on the stairs. Leo crushes Guibourg\u2019s skull just to be sure \u2014 and Holly feels a flash of powerful magic as a signal passes from Guibourg to a place outside the World, and an answer from that Otherwhere. Then they grab the body and run.<br /> <br /> The Scions then call the other Band to find how they\u2019re doing at hunting witches. Landon answers: The other Band is down. Marshall\u2019s army brat posse suddenly turned on him, snatched away his Relic rifle/shotgun/bayonet combo and shot and stabbed him. They also shot Delia. Barbara took to the sky but was clubbed unconscious by a flying skinhead with a baseball bat. Landon is hurt too, but he and Marshall managed to incapacitate the (unnaturally strong and tough) army brats before Marshall was clubbed like Barbara and lost consciousness. The flying skinhead retreated before Landon\u2019s shinobi shuriken barrage, and Landon is now taking the Scions to the hospital.<br /> <br /> The Scions quickly rendezvous with Landon. Jaeda uses Health to heal some of the damage to each, saving their lives, but they are still thrashed. Leo blames himself for going after Guibourg instead of staying with his team, but done is done. Marshall will heal within a day or two, but Delia and Barbara still need medical attention; Landon resumes his trip to the hospital.<br /> <br /> Well, crap. Holly decides that it\u2019s finally time to invoke Mystery and find out who\u2019s doing what and why. She learns:<br /> <br /> \u2022 <i>What was the flash of magic?</i> Summoning.<br /> \u2022 <i>What was summoned: </i>A Gyuki. Only, it didn\u2019t appear right at Guibourg\u2019s body.<br /> \u2022 <i>Who does Acacia work for?</i> Tuoni, primary avatar of the Death-Titan Tuonela.<br /> \u2022 <i>Who does Acacia work with? </i>Technically, Guibourg. Until she killed him.<br /> \u2022 <i>What\u2019s the plan for Guibourg\u2019s witches?</i> Currently, kill Scions.<br /> \u2022 <i>Who made Marshall\u2019s crew turn on him?</i> Acacia.<br /> \u2022 <i>Name of a witch not yet identified? </i>Reggie Storm.<br /> \u2022 <i>Name of another witch not yet identified?</i> Ray Soliz. Leo puts his head in his hands. Ray leads the Youth Group at Glory of God Ministries, his church.<br /> \u2022 <i>What is Acacia\u2019s plan?</i> Kill the Scions, humorously.<br /> \u2022 <i>What is Acacia\u2019s lair?</i> &lt;Fzzzz.&gt; Something is blocking this information.<br /> \u2022 <i>What does Delisle plan to do to the Sobeck family?</i> Feed gorgon blood to Gus\u2019 father.<br /> <br /> At that final answer, Gus <i>freaks</i>. It isn\u2019t hard to figure out Delisle\u2019s plan: Turn Gus\u2019 father into a medusa so Gus will be forced to kill him. Gus immediately calls his father. His father says they are just pulling up to Delisle\u2019s house \u2014 they were invited to a party. Gus pleads with his father to turn the car around and leave, NOW, this is the most important thing he has ever asked. The other Scions are flummoxed: They\u2019ve never heard tough-guy Gus sound this scared. But the elder Sobeck agrees. Gus further convinces his father to take his stepmother out of town for the night. It doesn\u2019t matter where, just far away!<br /> <br /> The Scions decide to continue their Night of Long Knives. First they go to the home of Ray Soliz. They find the young Evangelical accompanied by his parents and ten members of the church youth group. Leo demands to know why Ray betrayed God and Christ to join the Titans. Ray responds that he found God, and His name is Aten. Now, KNEEL TO ATEN! Leo cannot resist the Overt Order, but Holly and Jaeda catch him under the arms so he does not actually drop to the floor. Battle is joined; Ray sends his young acolytes charging toward the Scions, shouting \u201cSubmit to Aten!\u201d and exploding when they come within reach. Their suicidal explosions of sunfire, though, are too weak to harm the Scions. Outnumbered 5 to 1, Ray does not live long. His parents try to defend him, announcing their willingness to die for the Prophet of Aten, but they are not cultists yet \u2014 merely Social Fu\u2019d into madness. The Scions merely knock them out. Leo closes his eyes and whispers, \u201cForgive them.\u201d<br /> <br /> As the Scions approach the home of Reggie Storm, this young warlock attacks them first. He is the flying skinhead with the baseball bat, bound to the Air-Titan avatar Aegir. Reggie swoops down and nearly knocks Holly\u2019s head off\u2026 but just barely misses (thank you defensive do-over!) Again, he is vastly outnumbered and Jack can fly. He too does not live long.<br /> <br /> The Scions end their sweep through Avalon and Etna with a stop at George Delisle\u2019s house. He might not be a witch, but Gus vows to kill him for the sadistic threat to his family. The party is over; the house is empty. They race to the Sobeck residence. It too is empty, but Gus knows his parents got away. George Delisle, however, is also gone.<br /> <br /> Where did Delisle obtain gorgon blood? Holly checks the sample the Scions obtained from Dr. Sosa, that she had weaponized into a dart. As she expected, it\u2019s gone \u2014 replaced with an inert duplicate. It would not have been difficult to steal if someone knew it was there.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T16:07:44-08:00","id":1030300,"title":"","content":"EPISODE 21: Goodbye, Avalon (Continued)<br /> <br /> <b><u>Act Three: Early Graduation</u></b><br /> <br /> The next day, the story of Gary Vetterson surviving a hole blown through his head is washed away by a media frenzy over the firebombing of a Holiday Inn and the spasm of home invasions that left almost 20 people dead or disappeared. Most of them were students or faculty at Etna High, including Principal Borgen. This is too much, coming on top of earlier waves of disappearances: The school district superintendent closes Etna High for the rest of the school year.<br /> <br /> Avalon High, however, is still open and the Scions go to school as usual. At homeroom, they receive word that their parents want to talk to them during lunch hour. The meeting takes place at the same conference room where they met each other months before. Sure enough, everyone\u2019s divine parents are there \u2014 even Louhi (and the other Gods stay well away from her). Lono, however, does not look like Hunter S. Thompson. This time he looks like, well, a Polynesian warrior-king in a business suit.<br /> <br /> Huitzilopochtli, Sobek and Nergal congratulate the Scions on their murderous rampage. The Aztec War-God grudgingly admits that Jaeda became quite a warrior even though she\u2019s a girl. To Jaeda\u2019s gratitude, Tlazolt\u00e9otl points out that Jaeda has done far more, and greater, deeds than killing things. The other Gods are more subdued. Louhi stays coldly inscrutable.<br /> <br /> The Gods tell Gus, Holly, Jack and Jaeda that they cannot continue at Avalon High. They are on the verge of becoming Demigods; it is time to put away childish things and join their pantheons as special agents in the World. It is also too dangerous to keep such a concentration of Legend in one place for long! Louhi notes, in a tone of icy disapproval, that Holly will <i>not </i>become part of her organization, but Vainamoinen expressed an interest in becoming her patron.<br /> <br /> From the Gods\u2019 point of view, the Scions have no unfinished business. Louhi\u2019s assassination service will deal with Acacia. The Gyuki is a powerful and dangerous foe \u2014 now one of the most powerful titanspawn loose in the World \u2014 but it left town. Someone has to find and kill it, but not the Scions, here, now.<br /> <br /> The Scions do ask if they have to become Demigods. They learn that no, they don\u2019t. They can choose to remain at their current Legend. The Scions also ask if they can stay a Band. Not if they become Demigods; they will be too valuable to their individual pantheons.<br /> <br /> The Gods grant the Scions a recess to discuss their options. By the end of lunch period, the Scions make their choice.<br /> <br /> Gus will go with Sobek. He loves the thought of joining his true father as a hatchet-man for the Pesedjet, using his strength and skill to the utmost. That evening, he says goodbye to his mortal father and explains the truth about Scions and the family\u2019s long involvement with Sobek. His father is shocked, of course, and sad to see his son go, but admits that a great deal of family history now becomes clear. Gus also celebrates his immanent ascension to Demigod by belatedly accepting Reese\u2019s past propositions to him \u2014 to Gary\u2019s anger, but the giant hasn\u2019t a moral leg to stand on.<br /> <br /> Jaeda, Jack and Holly agree they cannot stay in Avalon. They remember Barbara\u2019s bitter comment that her Legend seems to be that of backing their plans and cleaning up their messes. If they stay, they will overshadow the other Scions and might prevent them from developing powerful Legends of their own, and maybe learning to become a real Band. The three also agree that their ruthless killing of the witches marked a conclusive break from childhood. They can\u2019t continue being kids when they have acted as soldiers \u2014 or assassins. Jack and Jaeda have effectively said goodbye to their mortal parents already.<br /> <br /> But they do not want to join their parents, either. Unlike Gus, the three Scions feel they are still discovering who they are. Jaeda has been a fighter, a lover and an unexpectedly successful counselor, but she is still getting over the psychological effects of her gang years. Jack knows he cannot continue being the Goofy Slacker Dude, but he isn\u2019t sure what he should become. A warrior chief, as his father hopes? A mathematician, which has proven surprisingly useful? Can you be a goofy warrior mathematician? And Holly has fought the manipulative and vindictive arrogance she inherited from her mother, but she knows she has not conquered it. Most of all, none of them want to become mere echoes of their divine parents, reliving Legends not their own.<br /> <br /> So they will leave Avalon, traveling to discover the wonders of the World and their place within it. Maybe they can hunt the Gyuki. And if Gary wants to come along \u2014 come on, there\u2019s no way he can just go to Texas and become a football star \u2014 that would make Jaeda very happy. But that remains to be seen.<br /> <br /> The Scions stay in Avalon for the rest of the week, wrapping up their mortal affairs and finishing the cover-up of supernatural activities. Then they leave.<br /> <br /> Goodbye, Avalon. Roll credits. Play wistful, semi-relevant classic folk-rock song:<br /> <br /> <i>Those were the days, my friend,<br /> We thought they'd never end,<br /> We'd sing and dance forever and a day.<br /> We'd live the life we choose,<br /> We'd fight and never lose,<br /> For we were young and sure to have our way\u2026</i><br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T16:24:26-08:00","id":1030307,"title":"","content":"And that was Scion High.<br /> <br /> IIRC, a standard TV season has 22 episodes. I wrapped it up in 21 for two reasons. First, all the subplots seemed to be tied up at that point. More importantly, I just couldn't go one any longer, Scion High was the best campaign I ever ran, but it was also exhausting. Some of this was wrestling with the loosey-goosey Scion rules. More of it was just the work of juggling all the characters and subplots. (It didn't help that I came to Scion High immediately after finishing a lengthy D&amp;D 3rd ed. campaign, which was a fair bit of work as well.) The effort broke me as a GM. I've barely been able to run anything since.<br /> <br /> (I might add that running an Exalted campaign broke one of my group's other GMs. He couldn't run anything for years, either. He's back in form now, though, so there's hope for me too. But we are not running any more White Wolf/Onyx Path games unless we know the game mechanics are <i>rigorous, clear and not too complicated.</i> At our ages, we are no longer willing to wrestle with balky rules. Especially considering what games cost nowadays, we expect designers to do their goddamn job and not make us do it for them.)<br /> <br /> But I have one question for people who've read through this Chronicle. If Scion High was a TV show, then a .pdf of the chronicle would be the series DVD. And DVDs need extras. The campaign pr\u00e9cis, house rules and so forth would provide some extras. What else would people like to see?<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-11T18:18:31-08:00","id":1030366,"title":"","content":"That was probably a good way to end it off. I understand that 1E's mechanics break down considerably at Demigod level.<br /> <br /> Dex Monkeys are the only effective class, and anybody who tries to play a different roll is going to rage-quit. :P","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 01-12-2017, 01:45 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T15:14:25-08:00","id":1030819,"title":"","content":"Yeah, we thought the mechanics were showing the strain even at Legend 4.<br /> <br /> For one thing, by Legend 4 there is no reason to buy more than one dot of a new Ability. You'll get more suxx through Epic Attributes, channeled Virtues and Deeds than you will by raising the Ability rating.<br /> <br /> Incidentally, we all thought it was pretty funny that as the game went on, many of the times when the Scions pulled out everything to maximize dice rolls -- Legendary Deed, channeled Virtues, maybe even a stunt -- it was for noncombat activities that, objectively, might not matter much. Stuff like Gus' contests with Gary, the talent show, or Jack trying to figure things out through the Power of Math. But then, those often were the parts of the stories that the players thought were the most fun and character-defining. That's where their Legends developed.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-12T22:21:54-08:00","id":1031011,"title":"","content":"Gets even more tedious as one of the Dodekatheon, who's PSP is specifically designed to boost your Ability scores. :P<br /> <br /> <br /> By that point, your level of performance encroaches upon the realm of cartoon shenanigans. Somebody hands you three tooth picks, a bubble wand and some gum; you hand them back a tactical nuke.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T02:33:16-08:00","id":1031508,"title":"","content":"Man, I remember reading this whole thing back on the old WW forums. I'm vaguely teary-eyed and nostalgic getting to reread the whole thing after all these years. What a monster of a campaign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1012-sorcerymon","name":"Sorcerymon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1012_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"15"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-01-14T16:10:45-08:00","id":1031864,"title":"","content":"Yeah, I also enjoyed revisiting this group of imaginary friends. Like I said, it was the hardest campaign I ran, and it burned me out -- but it was totally worth it. I'm glad other people have enjoyed it, too.<br /> <br /> Dean Shomshak","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1175-dshomshak","name":"DShomshak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"197"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}